November 2025 (admin) 
Artificial Intelligence (AI) Policy 
1. COMPLIANCE OBLIGATION SUPPORTED 
Tertiary Education Quality and Standards Agency Act 2011 (Cth) 
2. PURPOSE 
Curtin University aims to be a world leader in the use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) within tertiary 
education and research. Guided by our core values of Integrity, Respect, Courage, 
Excellence and Impact, the University encourages the ethical integration and use of AI across all 
facets of the institution. This includes leveraging opportunities for learning and teaching, research, 
staff development, the operations of University administration and student experience. 
In this context, the purpose of this Policy is to adopt a set of principles to guide the procurement, 
development, deployment, use, management and improvement of AI systems at the University. 
AI technology and the use of AI systems will continue to evolve and will be increasingly regulated. 
As such, this policy and any associated procedures will be reviewed regularly to consider 
technological advances, current and emerging issues, regulatory standards and best practice 
guidance. 
3. POLICY STATEMENT 
3.1. The use and development of AI systems in a safe and secure manner is consistent with the 
University’s commitment to innovation, productivity, enhancing student and employee 
experience and leveraging leading-edge technology. The University acknowledges AI’s 
potential to: 
(a) 
enhance the student experience;  
(b) 
contribute to academic excellence; 
(c) 
enhance research methods and outcomes;  
(d) 
improve employee experience and University operations; and 
(e) 
equip students with skills and knowledge for AI-augmented workplaces of the future.  
3.2. Alongside these opportunities there are risks with respect to a range of factors, including the 
pace of technological change relative to governance, misuse of personal or confidential 
information, exploitation of intellectual property and Indigenous data, exclusion of minority 
communities, harm to the environment, misinformation, and the reliability and accuracy of AI 
systems. The use of AI systems in a university setting also presents challenges in relation to 
academic integrity.   
3.3. In all cases, human oversight, judgement and intervention are vital to ensure the potential 
offered by AI systems is realised in an ethical, safe and responsible manner. 
3.4. Accordingly, the University adopts the below principles, to guide (as relevant) the 
procurement, development, deployment, use, management and improvement of AI systems 
for teaching, learning, research and operations at the University (AI Guidance Principles), 
mapped to Curtin’s 2030 Strategic Vision of making a difference to People, Planet and 
Partnerships.  
3.5. The AI Guidance Principles are adapted with adjustments to the University’s context from the 
principles in the Western Australian Government AI Policy. 
3.6. AI Guidance Principles 
To the extent possible and as relevant to the activity or use-case, the University community 
participating in University business or activities involving AI systems should: 
Principle 1: Human, societal and environmental wellbeing 
 
Develop, deploy and use AI systems in a way which 
benefits individuals, society and/or the environment. 
Assess and account for the broader impacts, positive and 
negative, throughout the AI system’s lifecycle. 
