Part 2 – Reporting and preliminary assessment 
8. 
Reporting 
8.2 
If a Staﬀ Member reasonably suspects that a Student has engaged in Student Misconduct, that person 
must ensure that it is reported to the Oﬃce of the Academic Registrar as soon as reasonably 
practicable. 
8.3 
If any other person reasonably suspects that a Student has engaged in Student Misconduct, that 
person may report it to the Oﬃce of the Academic Registrar. 
9. 
Allegations 
An allegation of Student Misconduct may consist of one or more instances of Student Misconduct.  A 
decision maker may consider these instances concurrently or separately at their discretion. 
10. 
Preliminary Investigation 
10.1 When a report of suspected Student Misconduct is received, an Inquiry Oﬃcer will carry out a 
preliminary investigation and prepare a report, which must be provided to the Authorised Oﬃcer.  
10.2 The report will describe the circumstances of the case and may include recommendations and 
material that will assist the Authorised Oﬃcer in making their determination. 
11. 
Preliminary assessment  
11.1 When an allegation of suspected Student Misconduct is received, the Authorised Oﬃcer must 
consider the allegation and preliminary report and must do one of the following:   
(a) 
If the Authorised Oﬃcer reasonably believes there are insuﬃcient grounds to warrant further 
investigation, they must dismiss the matter. 
(b) 
If the Authorised Oﬃcer reasonably believes it is appropriate to deal with the matter as a 
Warning, they must issue the student with a Warning. 
(c) 
If the Authorised Oﬃcer reasonably believes there are suﬃcient grounds to warrant further 
investigation, they must either:  
(i) 
commence the Pathway 1 Process; or  
(ii) 
commence the Pathway 2 Process where the circumstances of the matter indicate that 
a Category 2 Penalty may be imposed. 
Part 3 – Pathway 1 Process 
12. 
Pathway 1 – Authorised Oﬃcer Determinations 
12.1 The Authorised Oﬃcer must provide the Student with: 
(a) 
written notice of: 
(i) 
the substance of each allegation of Student Misconduct; and 
