September 2026 
Cyber Security & Information & Communication Technology (ICT) 
Appropriate Use Policy 
1. 
COMPLIANCE OBLIGATION SUPPORTED 
 
Cyber Security Act 2024 (Cth)  
 
Security of Critical Infrastructure Act 2018 (Cth)  
 
Western Australia’s Cyber Security Policy 
2. 
PURPOSE 
To establish the University’s cyber security risk management approach and appropriate use of ICT 
assets to ensure our University community is provided with a cyber-resilient and reliable digital 
experience.  
 
3. 
POLICY STATEMENT 
3.1. Cyber Security Risk Management 
3.1.1. The University manages cyber security risks in alignment with its defined risk appetite. 
3.1.2. Anyone in the University community who is responsible for ICT assets must regularly 
assess cyber security risks related to those assets. 
3.1.3. The University will take all reasonable steps to continually improve its cyber security 
capabilities to govern, identify, protect, detect, respond to, and recover from cyber 
security threats and incidents. 
3.2. Cyber-Aware Culture 
3.2.1. The University will promote a strong security culture through education and awareness 
programs to improve cyber resilience. 
3.3. Appropriate use of ICT Assets 
3.3.1. ICT assets must only be used for authorised purposes, such as official University 
business, teaching, research. Limited incidental personal use is permitted as needed. 
3.4. Responsibilities 
3.4.1. These positions are responsible for implementation of the policy in their work areas: 
a) Executive Managers; 
b) Line Managers; 
c) Chief Information Officer; and 
d) Chief Information Security Officer. 
4. 
SCOPE OF POLICY 
This Policy applies to all members of the University community (which includes Council members, 
students, employees, University Associates, Curtin controlled entities, and all persons participating 
in University business or activities, including whether as a visitor, adjunct appointee, service 
provider, or contractor) in any location or campus within Australia and offshore. 
5. 
DEFINITIONS  
(Note: Commonly defined terms are in the Curtin Common Definitions. Any defined terms below are specific 
to this document) 
ICT assets 
Any information, communications technology or audio-visual service, equipment or facility owned 
leased or contracted by the University that hosts, stores, transmits, or presents digital information 
assets for the business and purpose of the University. This may include, but is not limited to: 
• 
Software applications. 
• 
Physical and virtual hardware. 
• 
Email, messaging and collaboration applications. 
