Higher Degree by Research (HDR) Appeals and Complaints Procedures 
1. 
COMPLIANCE OBLIGATION SUPPORTED 
Higher Degree by Research (HDR) Appeals and Complaints Policy 
2. 
PROCEDURAL DETAILS 
2.1. 
An applicant or student who is notified that a decision to revoke, cancel or refuse enrolment 
has been made, will have 15 working days from the date of the notification to appeal.  
2.2. 
A student attending an interview associated with an appeal may be accompanied by a support 
person.  
2.3. 
Any student that is not satisfied with the result or conduct of any formal internal appeal 
process may file a complaint with the Western Australia Ombudsman..  
2.4. 
Appeals against cancellation of enrolment 
2.4.1. An appeal against the cancellation of enrolment will only be permitted on the grounds 
of procedural non-compliance. 
2.4.2. A student may appeal against cancellation of enrolment within 15 working days of the 
date of the notification that the recommendation for the cancellation of enrolment has 
been upheld. The appeal will be in writing and will not be more than 10 pages (not 
including supporting documents). The appeal will be addressed to the Associate 
Deputy Vice-Chancellor Research (ADVCR) (or delegate).  
2.4.3. Upon receipt of an appeal, the ADVCR (or delegate) will: 
(a) convey the submissions of the student to the relevant Director of Graduate 
Research of the Enrolling School and the principal supervisor (where 
appropriate); and 
(b) determine if a prima facie case exists for reconsideration of the student’s 
cancellation of enrolment.  
2.4.4.  If the ADVCR (or delegate) determines that a prima facie case does not exist, the 
student’s enrolment cancellation will be upheld. The student will be notified of the 
decision to uphold enrolment cancellation by the Graduate Research School (GRS). 
2.4.5. If the ADVCR (or delegate) determines that a prima facie case does exist they will 
convene an Appeals Committee comprising: 
(a) two Directors of Graduate Research, neither of which is to be the Director of 
Graduate Research of the student’s Enrolling School, or a person who has 
been, at any time, the student’s supervisor or Thesis Chair; and  
(b) a Higher Degree by Research (HDR) student nominated by the Curtin Student 
Guild, who must not be the appellant and who is free from conflict of interest 
with any of the parties involved in the matter.  
2.4.6. The Appeals Committee will resolve either to uphold the cancelled enrolment or to 
permit the student to resume their course under any conditions determined by the 
Committee.  
2.4.7. The decision of the Appeals Committee will be final. The decision will normally be 
made within 30 working days of the appeal committee being formed by the ADVCR (or 
delegate). 
2.5. 
Appeals against classification of thesis  
2.5.1. A student whose thesis has been classified as failed may appeal within 15 working 
days of the date of the letter of notification of the failed classification. An appeal will be 
in writing, in no more than 10 pages (not including supporting documents). The appeal 
will be addressed to the ADVCR (or delegate). An appeal will be permitted only on one 
or both of the following grounds: 
