(a) procedural irregularities in the examination of the thesis or in the conduct of 
any examination which forms part of the determination of the result. In this 
case it will be necessary for the student to demonstrate that an aspect(s) of 
the examination process was not appropriately followed and that this caused, 
or was likely to have substantially contributed to, the failed classification via 
documented evidence; or 
(b) documented evidence of bias on the part of one or more of the examiners.  
2.5.2. Upon receipt of an appeal, the ADVCR (or delegate) will: 
(a) convey the submissions of the student to the Director of Graduate Research of 
the Enrolling School and the principal supervisor (where appropriate); and 
(b) determine if a prima facie case exists for reconsideration of the classification of 
the thesis. 
2.5.3. If the ADVCR (or delegate) determines that a prima facie case does not exist, the 
student’s thesis classification will be upheld. The student will be notified of the 
decision to uphold the thesis classification by the Graduate Research School (GRS). 
2.5.4. If the ADVCR (or delegate) determines that a prima facie case does exist they will 
convene an Appeals Committee comprising: 
(a) two Directors of Graduate Research neither of which is to be the Director of 
Graduate Research of the student’s Enrolling School, or a person who has 
been, at any time, the student’s supervisor or Thesis Chair; and  
(b) a Higher Degree by Research (HDR) student nominated by the  Curtin 
Student Guild, who must not be the appellant and who is free from conflict of 
interest with any of the parties involved in the matter. 
2.5.5. On hearing the appeal, the Appeals Committee will resolve that: 
(a) the student be permitted to resubmit the thesis for examination by an 
additional examiner(s) in accordance with the Higher Degree by Research 
(HDR) Thesis Examinations Procedures, who will examine the thesis and 
submit a written recommendation under the same conditions as the original 
examiner(s); or 
(b) the thesis and examiners’ reports will be sent to an adjudicator in accordance 
with the Higher Degree by Research (HDR) Thesis Examination Procedures; 
or 
(c) the failed classification will be upheld.  
2.5.6. The decision of the Appeals Committee will be final. The decision will normally be 
made within 30 working days of the committee being formed. The GRS will notify the 
student of the Committee’s decision. 
2.6. 
Appeals against Admission or Enrolment Revocation, Termination or Refusal 
2.6.1. A student who has their offer of admission or enrolment revoked, terminated or 
refused by the Vice Chancellor as per section 2.8 of the Higher Degree by Research 
(HDR) Admission and Enrolment Procedures may appeal within 15 working days of 
receiving the letter of notification.  
2.6.2. An appeal will only be permitted on the grounds of procedural irregularity in the 
revocation, termination or refusal process. 
2.6.3. An appeal will be in writing; in no more than 10 pages (not including supporting 
documents) The appeal will be addressed to the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Research 
(DVCR). 
2.6.4. Upon receipt of an appeal, the DVCR will:  
(a) convey the submissions of the student to the relevant Director of Graduate 
Research of the Enrolling School and the principal supervisor (where 
appropriate); and  
(b) determine if a prima facie case exists for reconsideration of the revocation, 
termination or refusal of the student’s admission or enrolment.   
 
