2.6.5.  If the DVCR determines that a prima facie case does not exist, the student’s 
enrolment cancellation will be upheld. The student will be notified of the decision to 
uphold enrolment cancellation by the Graduate Research School (GRS).  
 
2.6.6. If the DVCR determines that a prima facie case does exist, they will convene an 
Appeals Committee comprising:  
 
(a) two Directors of Graduate Research, neither of which is to be the Director of 
Graduate Research of the student’s Enrolling School, or a person who has 
been, at any time, the student’s supervisor or Thesis Chair; and   
 
(b) a Higher Degree by Research (HDR) student nominated by the Curtin Student 
Guild, who will not be the appellant and who is free from conflict of interest 
with any of the parties involved in the matter.   
 
2.6.7. The Appeals Committee will resolve either to uphold the revocation, termination or 
refusal or to permit the student to resume their course under any conditions 
determined by the Committee.   
 
2.6.8. The decision of the Appeals Committee will be final. The decision will normally be 
made within 30 working days of the date of the submission of the appeal committee 
being formed by the DVCR. 
2.7. 
Complaint processes 
2.7.1. A student who has a complaint regarding supervision or other matters affecting 
progression in their course is to report, in the first instance, to their Thesis Chair, 
Director of Graduate Research, or Faculty Dean of Research.  
2.7.2. Notwithstanding the above, all complaints are to be considered in accordance with the 
University Complaints Procedures.  
3. 
RESPONSIBILITIES 
Responsibilities are as set out in Section 2.  
4. 
SCOPE OF PROCEDURES 
These procedures apply to all staff, students and HDR supervisors involved with HDR courses at 
Australian and global campuses. 
