November 2026 (Admin) 
b) Individuals will consider accessibility, diversity and inclusion at the point of creation of data 
and information to ensure that appropriate language and formats are used. 
c) Individuals will only collect the minimum amount of Personal Information necessary from 
students, research participants, community members and others to undertake the relevant 
University activity for which they are collecting the information.   
d) Data and information that belongs to an Archival series (such as executive committee 
papers) will be managed as Archival from the point of creation. Refer to University 
Archives - Archival Series. 
e) The University and individual areas will comply with the Privacy Procedures with regards 
to the creation, collection and capture of personal information. This may include 
completing a Privacy Impact Assessment (PIA). Refer to the PIA checklist to determine 
when a PIA is required. In the case of researchers collecting and/or using personal and 
sensitive personal information, the need to complete a PIA is identified as part of the 
Research Data Management Plan (RDMP) process.  
3.3  
CLASSIFY 
a) Data and information will be classified as per the Information Security Classifications (refer 
to the descriptions and examples in Appendix A) unless it sits outside of the 
Classifications as outlined in the Information Management Policy.  Refer to the Information 
Security Classification Toolkit for further guidance on how to determine and apply the 
Classifications. 
b) Where applicable and feasible, data and information will be labelled with the relevant 
classification at the point of creation. See Information Security Classification Toolkit.  
c) In the case of data and information held in Business Information Systems and other DTS 
supported systems where the data and information are an aggregation, and it is not 
feasible to classify each element separately, then a classification is to be applied at the 
system or aggregation level. 
d) The Information Security Classification of data and information will be reviewed if the 
sensitivity and/or value of data and information changes during the lifecycle.  Where the 
current classification is no longer appropriate then individuals and areas will ensure the 
data and information will be reclassified.  
e) Where appropriate files and folders are to be given titles that reflect the content and 
context of the data and information they contain (Refer to the Document Naming 
Guidelines). Where feasible, systems will store data about the information it holds such as 
descriptive or technical metadata that can be used to make decisions about how to handle 
the data and information it stores.  Where data and information are of historical or Archival 
value additional metadata will need to be captured as determined by the University 
Archivist – see Metadata Guide. 
3.4 
STORE 
a) Wherever possible, individuals and areas are to ensure that data and Information in digital 
form will be stored in Curtin supported systems, unless there is a contractual or technical 
requirement to use alternative storage. Data in hard copy form will be stored in locations 
appropriate to its Information Security Classification. Refer to the Privacy Procedures and 
Where to Store Information.   
b) When non-Curtin supported systems are used, the risks of using alternative storage are to 
be considered and mitigated wherever possible. 
3.5 
USE, SHARING AND DISCLOSURE 
a) Data and information will be handled according to its sensitivity as determined by its 
Information Security Classification (see Information Security Classification Toolkit). 
b) The University, through the FOI Coordinator, is to comply with requirements to release 
information under the Freedom of Information Act 1992 (WA), with the exception of Global 
campuses who must comply with any local requirements in their country with regards to 
freedom of information.  
c) The University community will comply with the Privacy Procedures and/or Disclosure of 
Personal Information Procedures with regards to using, sharing or disclosing any personal 
or sensitive information to any individual, entity or other third party under contract, 
collaboration, licence, project or agreement or other relevant situation.  
