November 2026 (Admin) 
3.6 
RE-USE and MAINTAIN 
a) Any real or suspected loss, theft or unauthorised access of personal data or information 
will be reported in accordance with the Information Breach Policy.  Global campuses will 
abide by any local requirements in their country for management and reporting of data 
breaches. 
b) Processes will be put in place, and followed, so that data and information, along with its 
associated metadata, remains discoverable, accessible and useable by those authorised 
to access it, over its lifecycle regardless of the repository or system within which it is 
stored. 
c) The data and information managed by Individual Areas will be included in their business 
continuity and disaster recovery planning. 
d) Data and information will be retained and maintained for the appropriate minimum time as 
determined by the relevant Retention and Disposal Authority regardless of when it was 
last used and regardless of format. 
3.7  
ARCHIVE and PRESERVE 
a) Hard copy data and information of historical or Archival value will be identified, retained, 
preserved and, where feasible, transferred to the custody of CIMA as soon as practicable 
after the Area is no longer actively using it. 
b) Relevant Areas will ensure that digital data and information of historical or Archival value 
will be identified, retained and preserved by undertaking digital preservation actions such 
as providing appropriate preservation metadata and moving digital content to newer file 
formats or repositories.  
3.8  
DELETE or DESTROY   
a) Individuals and Areas will take reasonable steps to avoid retaining data and information 
beyond its minimum retention period as set out in the Retention and Disposal Authorities, 
unless there is a University, regulatory or legal reason to retain it and the risks of over 
retaining the information have been assessed. (Refer to the Information Handling 
Guideline.) 
b) With the exception of the items covered in 3.8(c), approval will be sought from CIMA prior 
to destruction or deletion of data and information (see Disposal of Information).   
c) Certain types of low value, low risk data and information that do not require CIMA 
approval before deletion or destruction, are to be deleted or destroyed as soon as they 
are no longer needed, such as: 
o 
low value routine transitory messages giving minor instructions of a routine 
instructional nature that are used to further some activity,  
o 
exact duplicate files 
o 
Personal Information collected for proof of an individual’s identity  
o 
other types of information as set out in Disposal of Information. 
d) Hard copy data and information that has been digitised will only be destroyed if it meets 
certain conditions (see Scanning Documents).  If conditions are not met, it cannot be 
destroyed. 
3.9  
In addition to the responsibilities above the following responsibilities apply. 
Global campus staff will: 
a) Abide by Freedom of Information requirements that relate to their jurisdiction. 
b) Abide by privacy requirements that relate to their jurisdiction. 
c) In lieu of clause 3.5 (b), abide by any requirements in their country for management and 
reporting of data breaches. 
Managers (e.g., Heads of School/ Area/ Organisational Area) will: 
a) Foster and support a culture that promotes good information management practices. 
b) Ensure that all individuals in their area are informed of, the University’s Information Management 
policies and procedures. 
