November 2026 (Admin) 
 
Complete a Privacy Impact Assessment prior to commencement of any project, system roll 
out, collaboration, licence or agreement which will use, disclose or share personal or 
sensitive information. 
4. SCOPE OF PROCEDURES 
This policy applies to the University Community, including Council members, students, staff, 
University Associates, Curtin controlled entities, and all persons participating in University 
business or activities, including whether as a visitor, adjunct appointee, service provider, 
contractor or volunteer who manages Curtin data and information. 
5. 
DEFINITIONS 
(Note: Commonly defined terms are located in the Curtin Common Definitions. Any defined terms below are 
specific to this document) 
 
Accessibility 
Accessibility refers to the design and provision of products, services, environments, and 
information in a way that ensures people with disabilities can access and use them effectively, 
independently, and with dignity. It is a vital aspect of promoting diversity, equity, and inclusion in 
society.  In the context of this Procedure, accessibility can also refer to the availability of 
information in the sense that authorised users can access information or systems whenever they 
need it, without interruption or delay.   
 
Archives 
Data and information, in any format, that is preserved permanently because of its enduring 
significance and value. Archives provide evidence of university functions, programs, issues and 
associated decisions that are sufficiently critical, important or memorable that they warrant 
permanent retention. They may relate to social, economic, political, environmental, national 
security, historical, cultural, scientific and legal impact.   
 
Business Information System 
Systems that capture and manage information about an organisation’s functions and activities to 
support the goals and purpose of the organisation, such as: 
• 
Financial Management 
• 
Human Resource Management 
• 
Student Management 
• 
Customer Relationship Management 
 
Business Information System Owner 
The individual authorised by the University with responsibility for the overall procurement, 
development, integration, modification, operation, maintenance and retirement of an information 
system. Examples include the business owner of Student One or Finance One. 
 
Data  
Facts, statistics, or information that are collected for analysis, reference, decision-making and to 
gain knowledge and insights. It serves as the building blocks for information and knowledge. This 
definition includes Research Data, Confidential Data and Sensitive Data as defined in the 
Research Data and Primary Materials Policy. 
 
Information  
Any communication or representation of knowledge such as facts, data or opinions in any 
medium or form. This definition includes, but is not limited to, documents, emails, webpages, 
images, audio visual files, social media posts, chat messages, building plans, and other types of 
knowledge captured in digital or hard copy format. 
 
Information Lifecycle 
The stages through which information passes, from creation or collection through to destruction, 
deletion or Archival preservation.  Stages include Creation, collection and capture; Classification; 
Storage; Use and sharing; Reusing and maintaining; Archiving and preserving; Deleting and 
destroying. 
 
 
 
 
