Privacy impact assessments
A privacy impact assessment is a process used to find out whether a project, function or activity
may have an impact on the privacy of individuals. If you are starting a project, function or activity
which will involve personal information of individuals or which changes the way we already use
personal information, then you might need to conduct a privacy impact assessment before you
start.
To consider whether a privacy impact assessment is needed, please complete our privacy impact
assessment checklist and forward a copy to privacy@curtin.edu.au
Privacy impact assessment advice sheet
Privacy impact assessment checklist
Request a privacy impact assessment
Managing an information breach
An information breach can be any unauthorised access to or disclosure of personal and sensitive
information. It can also be the loss of a Curtin device which holds or has access to personal
information or sensitive personal information.
In the event of an actual or suspected information breach, even if it is not deliberate or malicious,
refer to the information management procedure and email privacy@curtin.edu.au
Understanding an information breach advice sheet
Information breach policy and procedure
Report a privacy or information breach
Privacy and artificial intelligence
Curtin encourages the safe, responsible and ethical use of AI systems in accordance with the
Artificial Intelligence Policy and S.E.C.U.R.E. framework.
The Privacy and Responsible Information Sharing Act WA includes principles which outline how we
are permitted to collect, use and share personal information. This includes the use of any kind of
automated decision making (a system which makes decisions without input from any human).
Automated decision-making (ADM) also includes a person who makes a decision by referring to
information which is wholly generated by an ADM system or by AI (referred to as materially-assisted
automated decision making).
Materially assisted ADM is permitted at Curtin using university approved tools, systems and
processes where human oversight is assured. A Privacy Impact Assessment is used to seek this
approval. If your area is using any AI system or ADM process, you are responsible for ensuring
individuals are given all the relevant information.
Individuals must be made aware if a decision that impacts them was in any way assisted by an ADM
process, they must be given sufficient information about how the ADM works in a way they can
reasonably understand and they must be permitted to request a review of that decision. Any review
is to be carried out by a person, without further reference to any ADM process.
Individuals who believe an ADM decision has impacted them may raise their concerns using the
Privacy Complaint process described on this page.
Privacy Advice – Automated Decision-Making and Privacy
Privacy and AI advice sheet
OIC WA advice on automated decision-making
Curtin’s privacy framework
At Curtin, we publish our privacy Statement, privacy values and privacy management framework to
ensure all members of the Curtin community are aware of their rights and obligations.
