Privacy values
Privacy management plan
Other external resources
Further information can be found from the West Australian Office of the Information Commissioner,
or by referring to the Privacy and Responsible Information Sharing Act (WA (2024).
WA Office of the Information Commissioner
OAIC privacy management framework
Privacy and Responsible Information Sharing Act (WA) 2024
Frequently asked questions
Can we share information about a student’s results or progress with a parent or verify qualifications
for a potential employer?
The WA PRIS Act protects an individual regardless of age and any person who is considered to have
the capacity to give and understand consent must be consulted.
In general, the university will not provide information about its students to parents or other third
parties external to Curtin without the students’ consent, unless we are compelled to do so by law.
Students may submit their consent using the Disclosure of Information and Proxy Authorisation
form.
Awards can be verified by searching online or submitting a request form.
What should I do if I accidentally shared personal information with the wrong person?
It is important that all staff at the university understand that our Information Breach requirements
are not intended to assign blame. Human error accounts for one third of all notified information
breaches. Taking a punitive approach may create barriers to reporting and will impact on the
effectiveness of our security and other controls. You’re encouraged to seek advice and contact the
Privacy Officer for support.
Curtin encourages the use of Curtin owned email for university business including students and
employees. This will help us to manage our security and assist in mitigation for accidents such as
this.
Whether you have accidentally emailed the wrong person in error or clicked on a suspicious link or
if you are concerned someone might have accessed your Curtin owned device without consent, you
should always report an incident to your line manager in the first instance and then lodge an
Information Breach incident with the Privacy Officer so that action can be taken quickly to resolve
the matter.
I want the University to delete personal information about me, what should I do?
There is no general right to be forgotten under Australian or WA Privacy law and any deletion of
personal information held within university information is governed by the Information Management
Policy and cannot be done without proper approval.
In certain circumstances, we may be able to obfuscate or remove certain identifying or contact
information.
Contact the Privacy Officer at privacy@curtin.edu.au or
InformationManagementandArchives@curtin.edu.au for advice.
I’m worried that someone has used my personal information in a way that I am not happy with.
What can I do?
If you have any questions or concerns about your privacy at Curtin, you can contact the Privacy
Officer for support and advice.
You can email Privacy@curtin.edu.au or phone 9266 3965, or write anonymously to Curtin
University, Privacy Officer, GPO Box U1987, Perth WA 6845.
You may also lodge a Privacy Incident via our online portal.
