 Information Management Policy 
1. 
COMPLIANCE OBLIGATION SUPPORTED 
State Records Act (WA) 2000 
2. 
PURPOSE 
To establish the framework and principles for effective data and information management that 
supports the business functions and activities of Curtin University. 
3. 
POLICY STATEMENT 
3.1. 
The University will establish and maintain data and information management practices that 
result in appropriate and ongoing management of these assets to support decision making, 
fulfil compliance obligations, demonstrate accountability, reduce risk, progress Curtin’s 
strategic priorities and meet stakeholder expectations. 
3.2. 
Management of University data and information will be based upon the following principles: 
 
The University will recognise data and information as a valuable asset that 
supports University functions and activities 
 
Data and information created or received during the course of Curtin activities, 
including Curtin activities contracted out to third parties, is vested in the University 
and remains under University control unless: 
a) There is a written agreement in place between the University and the other 
party stating that the data and information is owned by other parties, or; 
b) The data or information constitutes Intellectual Property that is vested in an 
individual or community as per the University’s Intellectual Property Policy. 
 
Data and information will be discoverable and reliable through: 
a) Managing data and information throughout its lifecycle  
b) Appropriate creation, capture, classification, organisation, storage, retention 
and disposal of data and information 
c) Practices and systems that support the integrity, findability and trustworthiness 
of data and information 
d) Practices and systems that support the right data and information being 
available to those authorised to access it when they need it in accessible 
formats consistent with the principles of Universal Design. 
 
The University will protect and secure data and information from unauthorised 
access, alteration, deletion or misuse.  This includes: 
a) Assigning the following Information Security Classifications to our data and 
information: 
• 
Public 
• 
Internal 
• 
Sensitive 
• Sensitive – Legal 
• Sensitive – Personal  
• 
Restricted 
• Restricted - Personal 
b) Managing data and information in accordance with its Information Security 
Classification and/or in accordance with contractual obligations should they 
specify handling requirements.  
