c) Taking steps to meet privacy and responsible handling obligations with 
regards to the collection, use, storage, access to and disposal of data and 
information. 
d) Adhering to contractual obligations with regards to the management of data 
and information that sits outside of our Information Security Classifications, 
such as data and information from government agencies which require the 
application of the Australian Government’s Protective Security Policy 
Framework (PSPF) for data and information assessed as Security Classified. 
 
The University will retain and dispose of data and information in accordance with 
the applicable Retention and Disposal Authorities, legal requirements, business 
need and risk appetite. 
 
Responsibilities and accountabilities are outlined in the Information Management 
Procedures. 
4. 
SCOPE OF POLICY 
This policy applies to the University Community, including Council members, students, staff, 
University Associates, Curtin controlled entities, and all persons participating in University 
business or activities, including whether as a visitor, adjunct appointee, service provider, 
contractor or volunteer who manages Curtin data and information. 
5. 
DEFINITIONS  
(Note: Commonly defined terms are located in the Curtin Common Definitions. Any defined terms below are 
specific to this document) 
 
Data 
Facts, figures, observations and experiences that are collected for analysis, reference, decision-
making and to gain knowledge. Data acts as the building blocks for information and knowledge. 
This definition includes Research Data, Confidential Data and Sensitive Data as defined in the 
Research Data and Primary Materials Policy. 
 
Information 
Any communication or representation of knowledge such as facts, data or opinions in any medium 
or form. This definition includes, but is not limited to, documents, emails, webpages, images, 
audio visual files, social media posts, chat messages, building plans and other types of 
knowledge captured in digital or hard copy format.   
 
Information Lifecycle 
The stages through which information passes, from creation or collection through to destruction, 
deletion or Archival preservation.  Stages include Creation, collection and capture; Classification; 
Storage; Use and sharing; Reusing and maintaining; Archiving and preserving; Deleting and 
destroying. 
 
 
 
 
 
 
