Cyber Security Procedures 
1. COMPLIANCE OBLIGATION SUPPORTED 
Cyber Security and Information & Communication Technology (ICT) Appropriate Use Policy 
2. PROCEDURAL DETAILS 
2.1. Cyber security is key to enabling University Learning and Teaching, Research and Curtin 
operations. ICT asset users at all levels of the University have specific responsibilities for the 
management and handling of information and ICT assets. This is essential for achieving, 
maintaining, and enhancing the University’s competitive advantage, brand and reputation, and 
legal and regulatory compliance.  
This document outlines the cyber security responsibilities for members of the University 
community in any location or campus, whether in or outside of Australia. 
2.2. The specific cyber security responsibilities of ICT asset users are dependent on their role 
within the University and are outlined in these Procedures. 
2.3. Executive Managers 
 Executive Managers will champion and support the University’s cyber security strategy 
and programs to reduce Curtin’s cyber risk to agreed risk appetite levels. 
 Executive Managers will allocate sufficient resources and budget, commensurate with 
the evolving risk of cyber security threats to the University to continually mature 
Curtin’s cyber security capabilities.  
 Executive managers will promote cyber security culture across Curtin to reduce the 
risk of cyber impact to Curtin and promote positive behaviours and engagement. 
2.4. Chief Information Officer (CIO) 
 The Chief Information Officer (CIO) will ensure the effective and efficient delivery of 
ICT services to the University that is consistent with the University’s cyber security risk 
appetite and other legislative requirements. 
 The CIO will allocate sufficient resources and support for the management of cyber 
security, including the protection of ICT assets.  
2.5. Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) 
 The CISO, or their nominated delegate, will approve and direct all cyber security 
related matters. This includes but is not limited to: 
(a) Cyber security policy development and management 
(b) Cyber security governance and compliance 
(c) Cyber security awareness initiatives 
(d) Cyber security incident response management 
(e) Cyber security investigations 
(f) 
Cyber security threats and vulnerability management.  
 The CISO will: 
(a) Act as the University’s representative when dealing with law enforcement 
agencies, other universities, and government agencies with respect to cyber 
security matters. 
(b) Assist Executive Management in defining the scope and support required for the 
University’s cyber security Program. 
(c) Assist the Chief Information Officer and Executive Management in defining the 
University’s cyber security threat landscape and cyber security risk appetite. 
 The CISO will establish and maintain cyber security standards that relevant ICT assets 
will adhere to and with authorise exceptions, where sufficient justification is provided.  
 
