1 
Intellectual Property Policy 
 
1. 
COMPLIANCE OBLIGATION SUPPORTED 
Curtin University Act 1966 (WA) 
Copyright Act 1968 (Cth) 
Trade Marks Act 1995 (Cth) 
Patents Act 1990 (Cth) 
Designs Act 2003 (Cth) 
Plant Breeders’ Rights Act 1994 (Cth) 
Circuit Layouts Act 1989 (Cth) 
 
2. 
PURPOSE 
Curtin seeks to foster a culture which values and rewards technology transfer and entrepreneurial 
endeavour; promotes collaboration between the University, staff and industry; and maximises the 
benefits to Australia from publicly funded research. Curtin encourages staff to actively engage in 
commercialisation of intellectual property (IP) and be rewarded for that activity.  
The Intellectual Property Policy and Procedures facilitate good practice and clarity of ownership 
when considering identification, protection, management and commercialisation of IP. 
 
3. 
POLICY STATEMENT 
3.1 
Ownership of IP by employees  
3.1.1 Unless otherwise agreed in writing, the University owns all IP developed, created, 
authored or otherwise contributed to by an employee:  
(a) 
in their course of duties; or 
(b) 
pursuant to a University project,  
 
that is not artistic work, scholarly work or indigenous cultural and intellectual property 
(ICIP). This ownership is not impacted by whether or not the employee’s involvement 
was outside of work hours or used resources other than University resources. 
3.1.2 If an employee develops, creates or authors IP that is not related in any way to their 
course of duties or a University project and has not used any University resources in 
developing, creating or authoring that IP, then the employee owns that IP. 
3.2 
Ownership of IP developed, created or authored by students 
A student owns IP developed, created or authored by them during their period of admission 
at the University, except where the IP arises from participation in a University Project, in 
which case the University will own the IP, unless otherwise agreed in writing with the student. 
3.3 
Scholarly work and artistic work 
The University does not assert ownership over the IP in scholarly work or artistic work. 
3.4 
Indigenous cultural and intellectual property 
3.4.1 The University acknowledges that Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities 
are the custodians of their ICIP and have an interest in protecting their ICIP from 
exploitation that is contrary to their customary laws. 
3.4.2 The University aims to give appropriate recognition, protection and respect to ICIP and 
its owners by ensuring that: 
(a) 
ICIP is used for a purpose permitted by the owners of the ICIP with informed 
consent and, if required, continued consent of its owners; 
(b) 
the ownership of ICIP made available for use in research and University 
