August 2025 (Admin) 
Purchasing Procedures 
1. 
COMPLIANCE OBLIGATION SUPPORTED 
Procurement and Purchasing  Policy 
2. 
PROCEDURAL DETAILS 
These procedures, together with the Procurement Procedures, support the Procurement and 
Purchasing Policy. 
The Purchasing Procedures are intended to describe the process by which goods and services are 
ordered, paid for and recorded by the University. These procedures apply to all purchasing 
regardless of funding. Templates, training material and reference guides are also available; see 
sections 6 and 7 of this document. 
The Procurement Procedures describe the decision making process as to what to buy, from whom 
and at what price. 
End to End Procurement Process 
(Requirements, vendor selection, Pricing, 
Terms and Conditions etc) 
Purchasing Procedures (Goods and Services Order, 
Approved, paid for and recorded). This document.
 
2.1. 
Implementation Principles 
• Expenses must be incurred for University business purposes only. 
• All expenses must be recorded in the University financial records. 
• The purchase approver is ultimately accountable for the purchases made within their 
area. 
• All documentation should comply with relevant regulations including tax regulations. 
• No netting of revenues and expenses is permitted. In other words, goods or services 
purchased must be accounted for in full while any offsetting revenue should be accounted 
for separately. 
• Consistent with the University’s Code of Conduct, sharing of personal information is 
strictly prohibited, in particular staff ID and password information for corporate cards and 
other financial systems or web sites used for purchasing. 
• Each transaction must be specifically approved prior to order (see table in Section 2.2.2 
on permitted ordering methods). 
2.2. 
Overview of procedures 
This section covers the ordering, receiving (confirmation of delivery) and payment for all goods 
and services purchased by the University. 
Excluded from these procedures are employee payments through the payroll system and 
payments to students for tuition refunds or scholarships. Separate procedures apply in each of 
these circumstances. Other exceptional cases may be approved in writing by the Chief 
Financial Officer. 
Prior to purchasing, certain procurement procedures should be followed, such as a market 
assessment and pricing comparison: refer to the Procurement Procedures for further 
information. 
2.2.1. 
Permitted ordering methods 
Ordering of goods or services commits the University to spending and must only be 
performed within an individual’s approved delegation: refer to the Register of 
Delegations. 
A purchase order is a formal request to buy a specific item for a specific price with 
specific terms and conditions. Current methods include: 
1. Using catalogued products within Ci-Anywhere, accessible from the Staff Portal 
web page, to generate a purchase order (see section 2.5.2) 
