2.6. 
Peer review 
2.6.1. 
Participants in peer review will: 
a) 
Be fair, rigorous and timely in their review; 
b) 
Act in confidence and not disclose the content or outcome of any process in 
which they are involved;  
c) 
Declare all conflicts of interest, not permit personal prejudice to influence the 
peer review process, and not introduce considerations that are not relevant to 
the review criteria; 
d) 
Not take undue or calculated advantage of knowledge obtained during the 
peer review process; 
e) 
Ensure that they are informed about, and comply with, the criteria to be 
applied; 
f) 
Not conduct a review for which one lack appropriate expertise; and 
g) 
Give proper consideration to research that challenges or changes accepted 
ways of thinking. 
2.7. 
Dissemination of research outputs 
2.7.1. 
In the dissemination of research data, researchers will: 
(a) disseminate research data as described in the Data Management Plan and the 
Research Data and Primary Materials Policy. 
(b) consider the potential unintended consequences of research for individuals or 
communities prior to publication and dissemination, and will take steps to mitigate 
this. Unintended consequences may include the research data being misused 
and cause harm to humans, animals, plant health, the environment and national 
security. 
2.7.2. 
In the dissemination of research outputs, researchers will: 
(a) share research outputs responsibly, transparently, and ethically, and will avoid 
‘predatory publishing/publications’ in the dissemination of their research. 
(b) provide a suitable summary of the research findings to research participants 
where appropriate. 
(c) take all reasonable steps to accurately and properly report research outputs. 
(d) will correct misleading and inaccurate statements about their work as soon as 
possible after they become aware of them. 
2.8. 
FAIR and open access to knowledge 
2.8.1. 
Researchers will disseminate research outputs as open access on publication where 
possible, through appropriate outlets, including Curtin’s institutional repository, and 
within the restrictions of confidential information, rights to use intellectual property, and 
personally and culturally sensitive information. 
2.8.2. 
Fragmented publication, plagiarism, self-plagiarism and under-reporting of research 
are not permitted. Duplicate publications are not acceptable, unless appropriate 
citation is made in the later publication to the earlier one, and where the editor or 
publisher is explicitly informed. 
2.8.3. 
Researchers are required to connect Open Researcher and Contributor ID (ORCID) to 
the institutional research publication system (i.e. Elements), and to include their 
ORCID in all of their published research outputs. 
2.8.4. 
Researchers are responsible for recording their research outputs in the institutional 
publications management system (i.e. Elements), and depositing a copyright 
compliant version of their output into Curtin’s institutional repository. 
2.9. 
Restrictions on dissemination of research outputs 
2.9.1. 
Researchers will take account of any restrictions on publication imposed by legislation 
and sanctions, the sponsor, contractual arrangements, and any restrictions relating to 
