ve"", ""L044"": ""accessibility needs. Ensure that:"", ""L045"": ""Gen-AI tools used are secure (e.g. they do not train on or share content)"", ""L046"": ""Outputs are not shared publicly (e.g. posted on the open internet)"", ""L047"": ""Use does not conflict with material that is commercially available (e.g. if material in an"", ""L048"": ""accessible format is commercially available, that item should be used instead of making local""}","via the licensing terms of the source of the material
If the researcher is the copyright owner
If the researcher obtains permission to use the material.
Researchers must ensure that:
Gen-AI tools used are secure (e.g. they do not train on or share content)
Outputs are not shared publicly (e.g. posted on the open internet)
Use aligns with Curtin’s academic integrity guidance
Use with Library database material does not breach licensing terms.
Copyright Act: the Fair Dealing for Research and Study Exception
The Copyright Act allows copyrighted material (e.g. images and textual material) to be used
for research and study purposes, providing the amount used is “fair”. This “fair” amount
generally consists of:
10% of the number of words, or
one chapter, if the work is divided into chapters.
one article (in its entirety) from an issue of a periodical.
an image (in its entirety) from the internet or another published source.
There is some flexibility with these amounts, depending on the circumstances. If the use of
material needed exceeds these limits, additional uses can be assessed based on a set of
criteria. For more information, see the Copyright Council’s guide to the Research and Study
exception.
Library commercial licenses
Many of the library’s commercial licenses, which provide access to journal articles and eBooks,
have some flexibility regarding use with Gen-AI. Researchers may generally use the library’s
commercially licensed material within secure Gen-AI tools as long as use falls within the same
criteria as in the Copyright Act’s research and study exception. There are some databases
which have expressly forbidden any use of their materials within Gen-AI, and we have listed
these in the Library Database Material section, below.
Individual licenses and permissions
Researchers can also use copyrighted material with Gen-AI as long as they obtain consent
from the copyright holder, or if they themselves are the copyright owner. Some database
companies provide Text and Data Mining licenses to individual researchers so they can extract
large amounts of text for analysis. If researchers are embarking on research projects which
involve copyrighted material and artificial intelligence, obtaining necessary copyright
permissions should be part of their research plan. Contact the Library for support with
obtaining permissions.
Material that has a Creative Commons license displayed may allow more flexibility of use with
Gen-AI, although this can depend on the terms of the license. CC Licensed materials always
require attribution for any use.
People with disabilities (staff or students)
Anyone who has a disability can use copyrighted material with Gen-AI or AI powered tools for
accessibility purposes to support personal study and learning. This includes using
Curtin-owned teaching and learning material (such as lecture slides, lecture recordings,
captions or transcripts). Curtin staff can also use this provision to assist students who have
accessibility needs. Ensure that:
Gen-AI tools used are secure (e.g. they do not train on or share content)
Outputs are not shared publicly (e.g. posted on the open internet)
Use does not conflict with material that is commercially available (e.g. if material in an
accessible format is commercially available, that item should be used instead of making local
