ECU,A014,ECU Developing AI Literacy Framework,41,41,"L001, L002, L003, L004, L005, L006, L007, L008, L009, L010, L011, L012, L013, L014, L015, L016, L017, L018, L019, L020, L021, L022, L023, L024, L025, L026, L027, L028, L029, L030, L031, L032","{""L001"": """", ""L002"": ""Home Initiatives  Articles Subscribe Contact"", ""L003"": ""  Developing AI Literacy in Higher Education: A Framework Search"", ""L004"": ""Curriculum Transformation"", ""L005"": ""Educational Experience"", ""L006"": ""Curriculum Transformation"", ""L007"": ""ECU Employability Team"", ""L008"": ""Developing AI Literacy in Higher"", ""L009"": ""Education: A Framework"", ""L010"": ""Written by Dr Megan Ellyard | 02 Jul 2026"", ""L011"": ""Learning for an AI World"", ""L012"": ""A practical framework for teaching and learning with AI"", ""L013"": ""Artificial intelligence (AI) is already part of how students learn. Whether we design for it or not, students are using"", ""L014"": ""AI tools to explore ideas, draft responses, and make sense of complex information. When left unguided, however,"", ""L015"": ""students risk using AI in ways that bypass learning or compromise the integrity of their work."", ""L016"": ""When students are explicitly taught how to use AI responsibly and given opportunity to practice, they develop more"", ""L017"": ""responsible AI habits (Sullivan et al., 2024; Susanto, 2026). Therefore, it is important to build opportunities for"", ""L018"": ""hands-on practice using AI in the curriculum."", ""L019"": ""AI-integrated learning is when AI is incorporated into the learning process in ways that help students reach a"", ""L020"": ""learning outcome. The goal does not change; students still need to know, understand or be able to do something"", ""L021"": ""they could not do before. When used well, AI can boost learning efficiency and comprehension (Araújo & Aguiar,"", ""L022"": ""2023; Crompton & Burke, 2024), expand and deepen thinking (Baltà-Salvador et al., 2025; Rahman et al., 2025),"", ""L023"": ""provide personalised feedback (Bearman et al., 2024; Crompton & Burke, 2024), foster evaluative judgement"", ""L024"": ""(Bearman et al., 2024), build student confidence (Adamakis & Rachiotis, 2025; Crompton & Burke, 2024; Naznin et"", ""L025"": ""al., 2025; Rahman et al., 2025), make learning more inclusive (Crompton & Burke, 2024; Trust et al., 2023; Rahman"", ""L026"": ""et al., 2025, Jafry & Vorstermans, 2025) or more engaging (Adamakis & Rachiotis, 2025; Crompton & Burke, 2024;"", ""L027"": ""Hon, 2026). In all instances, AI should enrich the learning process, not replace it."", ""L028"": ""From this perspective, telling students that they can or cannot use AI in a task is insufficient; it does not role-model"", ""L029"": ""the variety of responsible ways students may leverage AI tools either during their studies or future workplaces."", ""L030"": ""Such rules-based approaches are also difficult to enforce, arguably introducing more problems than they solve"", ""L031"": ""(Corbin, Dawson, & Liu, 2025; Dawson et al., 2024). Instead, curriculum needs to reform to create space for"", ""L032"": ""students to experience how AI can be used responsibly to meet a learning goal, in contexts where its value is""}","Learning for an AI World
A practical framework for teaching and learning with AI
Artificial intelligence (AI) is already part of how students learn. Whether we design for it or not, students are using
AI tools to explore ideas, draft responses, and make sense of complex information. When left unguided, however,
students risk using AI in ways that bypass learning or compromise the integrity of their work.
When students are explicitly taught how to use AI responsibly and given opportunity to practice, they develop more
responsible AI habits (Sullivan et al., 2024; Susanto, 2026). Therefore, it is important to build opportunities for
hands-on practice using AI in the curriculum.
AI-integrated learning is when AI is incorporated into the learning process in ways that help students reach a
learning outcome. The goal does not change; students still need to know, understand or be able to do something
they could not do before. When used well, AI can boost learning efficiency and comprehension (Araújo & Aguiar,
2023; Crompton & Burke, 2024), expand and deepen thinking (Baltà-Salvador et al., 2025; Rahman et al., 2025),
provide personalised feedback (Bearman et al., 2024; Crompton & Burke, 2024), foster evaluative judgement
(Bearman et al., 2024), build student confidence (Adamakis & Rachiotis, 2025; Crompton & Burke, 2024; Naznin et
al., 2025; Rahman et al., 2025), make learning more inclusive (Crompton & Burke, 2024;  Trust et al., 2023; Rahman
et al., 2025, Jafry & Vorstermans, 2025) or more engaging (Adamakis & Rachiotis, 2025; Crompton & Burke, 2024;
Hon, 2026). In all instances, AI should enrich the learning process, not replace it.
From this perspective, telling students that they can or cannot use AI in a task is insufficient; it does not role-model
the variety of responsible ways students may leverage AI tools either during their studies or future workplaces.
Such rules-based approaches are also difficult to enforce, arguably introducing more problems than they solve
(Corbin, Dawson, & Liu, 2025; Dawson et al., 2024). Instead, curriculum needs to reform to create space for
students to experience how AI can be used responsibly to meet a learning goal, in contexts where its value is
