Title: Academic Integrity
Policy Owner: Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Students and Education)
Keywords: Academic breach, academic misconduct, scholarship
Reference: P
Intent
Organisational Scope
Definitions
Policy Content
Accountabilities and Responsibilities
Related Documents
Contact Information
Approval History
INTENT
This policy sets out the University’s commitment to academic integrity in all aspects of
learning, teaching and research in accordance with the requirements of the Higher Education
Standards Framework (Threshold Standards) 2021, Australian Code for the Responsible
Conduct of Research 2018, and the Australian Qualifications Framework.
The principles underpinning the framework for promoting and assuring academic integrity are
consistent with ECU’s values of:
Integrity – being ethical, honest and fair;
Respect – considering the opinions and values of others;
Rational inquiry – motivated by evidence and reasoning;
Personal excellence – demonstrating the highest personal and professional standards;
Courage – we are bold and resolute in our thinking and actions in pursuit of our goals.
ORGANISATIONAL SCOPE
This policy applies to all Students and Staff irrespective of delivery location and mode of study.
This policy is to be read in conjunction with the University Statute No. 22 Student Conduct, the
Academic Misconduct Rules (Students), and the Conducting Research With Integrity Policy.
While academic integrity, in its broad sense, encompasses research integrity (as defined in the
TEQSA Guidance note: Academic and research integrity), responsible research conduct is
articulated in dedicated legislation, codes of conduct and guidelines. Accordingly, this policy is
to be read in conjunction with the University’s Research Integrity Framework and its policies
relating to responsible research and research integrity.
DEFINITIONS
The University Glossary applies to this policy.
POLICT CONTENT
Principles
A culture of academic integrity, consistent with the University’s values, is fundamental to the
University’s operations and reputation. Academic integrity involves upholding ethical
standards in all aspects of academic work, including learning, teaching, Scholarship, and
research. At its core, academic integrity calls for a commitment to the fundamental values of
honesty, trust, fairness, respect, responsibility, and courage, even in the face of adversity.
