Misconduct as prescribed by the University and where disclosure of such records is required by
applicable accreditation, legal or regulatory requirements, ensure the disclosure is in
accordance with those requirements; and
undertake training to support education, detection, and response to academic integrity
matters.
Each Unit Coordinator is required to promote academic integrity through supporting an
educational approach in the design and delivery of Unit curriculum, assessment, and feedback
aligned to the Academic Quality and Standards of the University, including:
when Courses and Units are developed or reviewed, integrating the use of enabling
technologies, learning and assessment experiences that allow Students to develop and
demonstrate good academic practices as they progress through a Course or Unit;
ensuring that Students are aware of and demonstrate acknowledgement and referencing
practices that apply to each Assessment; and
reporting on assessment practices and outcomes to their relevant Assessment Panel or Unit
Review Committee, as applicable in accordance with the Assessment Panel Procedures.
Each Associate Dean (Teaching and Learning), or the person performing a substantially
equivalent role within the relevant School or Teaching Area, is required to:
oversee the development and implementation of appropriate academic integrity resources,
education, and training activities in their School or Teaching Area in collaboration with the
Centre for Learning and Teaching;
analyse data and reports on Academic Breach and Academic Misconduct to identify trends and
areas of concern; and
advise relevant School Committees, Unit Review Committees, Executive Deans, Associate
Deans and Course Coordinators, on interventions and educational strategies that are currently
in place or planned to address these issues.
Each Associate Dean (Research), or the person performing a substantially equivalent role
within the relevant School or Teaching Area, in collaboration with the Library and the Centre
for Learning and Teaching, in relation to research Students, is required to:
oversee the development and implementation of appropriate academic integrity education in
their School or Teaching Area in collaboration with the Centre for Learning and Teaching;
analyse data and reports on Academic Breach and Academic Misconduct to identify trends and
areas of concern; and
advise relevant School Committees and the Dean, Graduate Research, and the relevant
Executive Dean, and Associate Deans on interventions and educational strategies that are
currently in place or planned to address these issues.
The Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Research), Associate Deans (Research), Dean, Graduate
Research, Research Integrity Advisors and others, will be accountable for responsible research
