ist in the"", ""L044"": ""development of actions to address such concerns;"", ""L045"": ""provide institutional leadership in relation to academic integrity;"", ""L046"": ""facilitate professional learning for academic integrity decision makers; and"", ""L047"": ""support a consistent, whole-of-University approach to promoting academic integrity principles""}","and research integrity at the University.
The Director, Student Administration, is required to report to Associate Deans on trends
relating to allegations and findings of Academic Misconduct in Central Examinations.
The Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Students and Education) Or Pro-Vice-Chancellor (Learning and
Teaching) will (through the Library, the Centre for Learning and Teaching, or otherwise):
provide academic integrity support and training for Students and Teaching Staff;
provide advice and resources for Students and Teaching Staff which address academic
integrity and acknowledgment conventions, including through the University website;
provide Teaching Staff and Students with access to enabling technologies, and exemplars;
have systematic, mature, internal processes for quality assurance and the maintenance of high
academic standards and academic integrity;
regularly review policies and procedures against national and international benchmarks to
encourage best practice in promoting academic integrity and dealing with Academic Breach
and Academic Misconduct;
work with collaborative partners, including third party providers or work integrated learning
opportunities, to encourage them to:
uphold and promote the principles and practices of academic integrity;
maintain policies and procedures in respect of academic integrity that are congruent,
compatible, and as equally robust as the University’s rules and policies; and
enable their Teaching Staff to act in a manner consistent with the University’s academic
integrity rules and policies;
collaborate with other higher education providers, to strengthen the University’s culture of
academic integrity, and to adopt best practices;
provide an annual consolidated report on academic integrity within the University, to each Unit
Review Committee and to the Academic Board; and
maintain and secure confidential records of academic integrity allegations, breaches,
investigations and outcomes, in accordance with the University’s Privacy Policy, Records
Management Policy and any other applicable law.
Each School will respond to the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Students and Education)’s annual
report, for submission to Academic Board, addressing the following in relation to their School
or Teaching Area:
data and trends relating to concerns, allegations and findings of Academic Breach and
Academic Misconduct for Students;
the manner in which allegations were dealt with;
the steps taken to promote academic integrity and to minimise Academic Breach and
Academic Misconduct; and
action plans to continuously improve academic integrity.
The Manager, Educational Integrity is required to:
assist the University to ensure that allegations of an Academic Breach and Academic
Misconduct are responded to in a fair, consistent, transparent, and timely manner across all
Schools and Teaching Areas;
facilitate the University’s continuous improvement of academic integrity procedures; and
use data to identify academic integrity concerns at a University level and assist in the
development of actions to address such concerns;
provide institutional leadership in relation to academic integrity;
facilitate professional learning for academic integrity decision makers; and
support a consistent, whole-of-University approach to promoting academic integrity principles
