Title: Privacy Impact Assessment Procedure
Procedure Owner: Director, Strategic and Governance Services
Reference: P
This procedure supports the University to operationalise the Privacy Policy and must be
complied with to ensure the University complies with its obligations under the Privacy &
Responsible Information Sharing Act 2024 (WA).
Intent
Organisational Scope
Definitions
Procedures Content
Accountabilities and Responsibilities
Related Documents
Contact Information
Approval History
INTENT
This procedure ensures that privacy risks are properly identified and addressed whenever the
University undertakes high privacy impact initiatives involving new or changed practices for
handling of personal information.
The Privacy & Responsible Information Sharing Act 2024 (WA) (PRIS Act) requires the
University to conduct a Privacy Impact Assessment (PIA) for all high privacy impact initiatives.
Staff should consider whether a PIA should be completed for an initiative on a case-by-case
basis.
Even if a PIA is not required, it may still be appropriate to seek advice from the Privacy or
Enterprise Risk teams about aspects of the initiative.
ORGANISATIONAL SCOPE
This procedure applies to all University staff.
DEFINITIONS
The University Glossary and the following definitions apply to this procedure:
Term:
Definition:
Privacy Impact Assessment
Or PIA, as defined in PRIS Act is a risk assessment which identifies and considers privacy risks
in detail and set out recommendations to manage the privacy risk/s.
Privacy Threshold Assessment
Or PTA, is preliminary evaluation using a triaging method to identify if an initiative involves
Personal Information and if further Privacy Impact Assessment, Risk and/or Compliance
support will be required. The PTA outcome can resolve privacy due diligence concerns or result
in the requirement of a full Privacy Impact Assessment.
Initiatives
Any planned actions, program or projects undertaken to achieve specific goals or drive
improvement within the University. An initiative may include the following activities:
Policy proposal
