uire reporting elsewhere, such as to a Project Steering Committee."", ""L046"": ""Compliance Monitoring & Reporting"", ""L047"": ""The Privacy Officer or delegate will monitor and review submitted PTA to ensure appropriate""}","New or amended programs, activities, systems or databases
New methods or procedures for information handling, student support or administrative
services
Data migration, analytics or automation
New systems, business processes or programs of work, product or service
Research, data collection, data sharing
A new procurement activity or Contracted Service Provider, and
Enhancement or upgrades of systems, software or data bases.
PROCEDURE CONTENT
Scope
A Privacy Impact Assessment (PIA) is an important risk management and governance tool used
to identify, assess and mitigate privacy risks associated with an initiative involving personal
information. Conducting a PIA ensures personal information is collected, used, stored, shared
and disposed of in a lawful, transparent and secure manner. It supports compliance with ECU’s
Privacy Policy, legislative and regulatory obligations, strengthens and promotes privacy by
design principles through information management lifecycle.
The purpose of a PIA is to proactively evaluate how an initiative may affect the privacy of
individuals and to implement appropriate controls before privacy risks materialise. The PIA will
assist initiatives in identifying potential privacy harms, improving decision-making, building
stakeholder trust and demonstrating due diligence in how ECU manages and handles personal
information.
The PIA will ensure privacy considerations are embedded early in the planning and operational
activities for all initiatives - projects, programs, systems, or changes in processes that:
Collect, use, store, disclose, or transfer personal information;
Introduce new or significantly changed ways of handling personal information;
Are likely to have a high rated privacy risk or significant impact on individuals’ privacy.
Step 1 – Privacy Threshold Assessment
For every initiative involving new or changed ways of handling personal information, the
Project Officer or Manager must conduct a Privacy Threshold Assessment (PTA).
The PTA is in the format of a short questionnaire to triage if further detailed Privacy Impact
Assessment is required.
Depending on the outcome of the PTA, some assessments will end at this point. Some will
proceed to Step 2 Conduct a PIA.
Step 2 – Conduct a Privacy Impact Assessment
The PIA is comprised of a number of questions to guide Staff through a systematic assessment
of the initiative.
The Project Officer or Manager are responsible for initiating and completing the PIA.
The PIA must be approved by the Project Sponsor.
The PIA must be submitted to Privacy team (Privacy@ECU).
The Privacy Officer or delegate will assess the PIA.
Recommendations to mitigate or manage the privacy risks identified will need to be tracked in
a relevant risk register by the Project Officer or Manager.
Following completion of the PIA, any residual ‘High’ risks identified in the PIA must be entered
in a Risk Register and reported to the Project Sponsor. Depending on the nature of the iniative,
it may require reporting elsewhere, such as to a Project Steering Committee.
Compliance Monitoring & Reporting
The Privacy Officer or delegate will monitor and review submitted PTA to ensure appropriate
