Policy Title: Conducting Research with Integrity
Policy Owner: Deputy Vice Chancellor (Research)
Keywords: research, governance, Australian Code, ethics, collaboration, participants, data
Policy Code: P
Intent
Organisational Scope
Definitions
Policy Content
Accountabilities and Responsibilities
Related Documents
Contact Information
Approval History
INTENT:
The purpose of this policy is to establish parameters that underpin Edith Cowan University’s
(ECU or the University) commitment to Research being conducted responsibly, ethically,
safely, and with integrity. It is informed by the Australian Code for the Responsible Conduct of
Research (the Code).
ORGANISATIONAL SCOPE:
This policy applies to all members of the University Community involved in a Research activity
that could reasonably be believed to be associated with or endorsed by the University. This
includes Students, Staff, (including Adjunct and Honorary appointments), Visiting Academic
Appointments and anyone undertaking a University Research related supervisory role. It also
includes Research that is both externally funded and funded from within the University.
DEFINITIONS:
The University Glossary and the following definitions apply to this policy.
Term:
Definition:
Data
For the purpose of this policy Data is defined in accordance with the National Statement on
Ethical Conduct in Human Research (2007) Updated 2018.
Data is intended to refer to bits of information in their raw form and includes the term
‘information’ which is used to refer to Data that have been interpreted, analysed, or
contextualised. Data and information may include but not be limited to:
What people say in interviews, focus groups, questionnaires/surveys, personal histories, and
biographies;
Images, audio recordings and other audio-visual material;
Records generated for administrative purposes (e.g. billing, service provision) or as required by
legislation (e.g. disease notification);
Digital information generated directly by the use of mobile devices and the internet;
Physical specimens or artefacts;
Information generated by analysis of existing personal information (from clinical,
organisational, social, observational or other sources);
