ied during the"", ""L041"": ""ethics review are in place."", ""L042"": ""With a view to embedding Research integrity into institutional culture, all Researchers must"", ""L043"": ""engage with the ECU approach to Research integrity professional development as appropriate"", ""L044"": ""to career stage."", ""L045"": ""The University will take reasonable steps to align with Research-related aspects of the Federal"", ""L046"": ""Government’s Guidelines to Counter Foreign Interference in the Australian University Sector"", ""L047"": ""and to protect the University’s Research environment from reasonably avoidable risk."", ""L048"": ""The University will comply with applicable legislative requirements when interacting with all"", ""L049"": ""foreign individuals and entities. Researchers must be aware of their responsibilities with"", ""L050"": ""respect to International Compliance Reviews and Consolidated List Reviews.""}","As informed by ECU’s Framework for the Productive and Ethical Use of Artificial Intelligence,
and the Ethical Use of Enterprise Data Policy, research should be conducted in alignment with
the Guidelines for Responsible Use of generative Artificial Intelligence (AI) in Research.
Safety and Risk
The University will comply with applicable legislation when undertaking Research, including
when partnering with other entities in joint Research projects.
Researchers must take reasonable and practicable steps to ensure they are aware of their
responsibilities with respect to the applicable Occupational Safety and Health legislation and
governing regulations, including regulatory requirements for Research involving radiation,
biosafety, and hazardous substances.
To the extent reasonable and required, the University will ensure work health and safety
advice is available to Researchers through the University’s Work Health and Safety
Management System. This will include information on work health and safety advisors, safety
and health representatives, local and University Work Health and Safety Committees, and any
other relevant committees, such as the Radiation Biosafety Hazardous Substances Committee
and Persons Conducting Business or Undertakings.
To the extent reasonable and practicable, the University will establish, maintain, review, and
improve processes and practices that allow for the identification, assessment, evaluation,
treatment, and review, of Research related risks. Consideration will be given to the University’s
legal, regulatory, and ethical requirements, as well as the impact and impost on Researchers
and other parties involved in the interface between risk and Research.
Research Governance
The University will put in place facilities and processes for the safe and secure storage and
management of Research Data and primary materials, including for the appropriate
generation, collection, access, use, analysis, disclosure, storage, retention, disposal, sharing
and re-use of data and information.
Research must be conducted in alignment with the University’s Research Data Management
Guidelines.
All Research activity conducted by Staff and Students, must be recorded in the University’s
Research Ethics Management System prior to the commencement of Research activities. The
process of recording the Research will determine the ethical review requirements and, where
data is being collected or received, will include completion of a mandatory Research Data
Management Plan. This clause applies to all Research within the University and includes
proposals for Research that will be undertaken as part of an Honours, Masters by Coursework
or other Higher Degree teaching and learning stream where Research may not be the primary
focus.
Research must not commence until the required Work Health Safety risk process has been
undertaken and approved, and:
formal advice is provided that ethics approval is not required; or
until ethics approval has been received and all governance requirements identified during the
ethics review are in place.
With a view to embedding Research integrity into institutional culture, all Researchers must
engage with the ECU approach to Research integrity professional development as appropriate
to career stage.
The University will take reasonable steps to align with Research-related aspects of the Federal
Government’s Guidelines to Counter Foreign Interference in the Australian University Sector
and to protect the University’s Research environment from reasonably avoidable risk.
The University will comply with applicable legislative requirements when interacting with all
foreign individuals and entities. Researchers must be aware of their responsibilities with
respect to International Compliance Reviews and Consolidated List Reviews.
