Research related funding and grants
The University encourages Researchers to actively seek Research funding from external
sources including formal grant bodies, corporations, charities, not-for-profits, Commonwealth,
State and local governments, noting that some funding sources, such as philanthropies, may
come within scope of the University’s fundraising activities, and compliance with the
University’s fundraising policy, and the requirements of the Office of Development and Alumni
Relations, must be adhered to.
Should it be reasonably suspected that a funding source may not align with the values of the
University, or may represent a risk, including reputational, to the University, the Deputy
Vice-Chancellor (Research) has the right to decide whether the funding source will be accepted
or continued with.
To the extent of their authority, and within the parameters of the University’s financial
delegations, the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Research) is authorised to approve the use of
University funds to support Research. Selection of Research for funding allocations of this
nature is at the discretion of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Research) or their nominee.
Dispersal of Research related funding and grants must occur in accordance with the delegated
authority, Research agreements and the Research Grants and Funding Management
Guidelines.
All formal applications to external funding agencies or potential sponsors for Research funding,
and all legal and other communications, including submission of progress and final reports,
must be conducted through Research Services.
Researchers should consult with Finance and Business Services Centre (FBSC) about financial
considerations before submitting funding applications and prior to signing of Research
agreements. FBSC will manage all financial aspects of grants, including acquittals and
statements. Disclosure of any financial information for any purpose will only take place with
the approval of FBSC.
Research grants, funding, awards and agreements, will be managed in accordance with the
University’s Research Grants and Funding Management Guidelines, having due consideration
of the costs of the work to the University, the recovery of these costs, and other important
issues, such as the ownership of intellectual property, equipment, and project assets; ethics
requirements; liability; confidentiality; risk management (including safety and health); and
legislative compliance.
Research Collaborations
The University supports the establishment of Research Collaborations as an avenue for
expanding the University’s Research impact and engagement and maximising connections
with strategic partner institutions.
The University will only enter into Research Collaborations with organisations and entities that
uphold the Code, align with the National Health and Medical Research Council Collaborative
Research Guide, or demonstrate alignment with comparable Codes or instruments of
governance that are reasonably believed to require Research to be conducted with integrity.
The University shall not enter into Research Collaborations in breach of applicable sanctions
laws, including the Autonomous Sanctions Act 2011 (Cth). The University shall not engage in a
Research Collaboration with a foreign individual or entity until relevant International
Compliance Reviews and a DFAT Consolidated List Review have been completed, and the
results of those reviews are deemed acceptable by the University.
The Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Research) has authority to direct that a Research Collaboration is
not entered into, or is not to be continued, if it is reasonably suspected that:
the collaboration may compromise the reputation of the University;
the collaboration may negatively impact the University’s ability to conduct Research in
accordance with the Code;
