accordance with this policy and associated operational documents; or
any other reason exists which may impact the integrity or viability of the University’s Research
environment.
Research Institutes and Centres
The University will establish Strategic and School-based Research entities with the intention of:
providing a strong Research environment;
investing in world-class Research;
creating a lasting impact for the community
leveraging the University’s Research strengths; and
enhancing the University’s Research reputation.
The Vice-Chancellor holds the authority to endorse the establishment or renewal of an ECU
Strategic Research Institute or Centre including the structure, principles and governance
framework under which it shall operate.
The relevant Executive Dean holds the authority to endorse the establishment or renewal of a
School-based Research Centre including the structure, principles and governance framework
under which it shall operate.
The Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Research) will decide performance outcomes and outputs that
must be achieved by ECU Strategic Research Institutes and Centres in order to retain their
status as an Institute or Centre.
Breaches of the Australian Code for the Responsible Conduct of Research
The University will take reasonable steps to ensure Researchers are informed of actions that
constitute breaches of the Code, and, to the extent reasonable and practicable, will provide
information, advice, and guidance to assist Researchers in adhering to the principles of the
Code.
Without any limitation to the reporting procedures applicable under the Academic Misconduct
Rules and the Fraud and Misconduct Prevent and Management policy, should a suspected
breach of the Code be identified, all Researchers have a responsibility to report the suspected
breach using the University’s Research Concerns or Complaints Lodgement form.
Should a suspected breach of the Code, or any other conduct of a Researcher, cause concern
that misconduct may have occurred, the matter will be investigated as per the University’s
Research Misconduct Guidelines, and any required action will be taken in accordance with
relevant legislation, Industrial Instruments, and University Statutes, Rules, policies, and
operational processes.
The University will address allegations of Research or Academic Misconduct as follows:
Allegations relating to Researchers who are a Student of the University, including HDR
Students, will be investigated and responded to in accordance with the Academic Misconduct
Rules (Students) and any other relevant Statues, Rules, policies and operational documents;
Subject to any applicable notification obligations under the Corruption, Crime and Misconduct
Act 2003 (WA) and the Fraud and Misconduct Prevent and Management policy, allegations
relating to Researchers who are a University member of Staff will be investigated in
accordance with the Research Misconduct procedures, and, where Research Misconduct or
other misconduct or serious misconduct as defined by the Industrial Instrument is reasonably
believed to have occurred, it will be responded to in accordance with the relevant industrial
instrument, and any other relevant Statues, Rules, policies and operational documents.
Allegations relating to Researchers who are considered both a Student and a member of Staff
will be examined by the Director Research Services and the Director Human Resources Service
Centre to determine the situation in which the allegation arose, and the incident will be
managed in the first instance according to whether it related to the person’s role with the
University as a Student or as a member of Staff.
