Attachment 1: Assessment, Examination and Moderation Procedures
Ensuring effectiveness of assessment
Effective assessment comprises Valid Assessment, Reliable Assessment and Fair Assessment.
Effective assessment design must include consideration of the following key elements in order
to certify achievement:
Learning Outcomes - Learning Outcomes must be Valid, measurable, and aligned to the
purpose of the Course and/or Unit.
Assessment Type - Assessment Types (selected in CAPS) must be an appropriate format and
genre for Students to demonstrate achievement in relation to the Learning Outcomes being
assessed. Generally, a variety of Assessment Types will be required across a Course to ensure
all Learning Outcomes are assessed.
Assessment Task - Assessment Tasks must be developmentally appropriate to the stage of the
Course, be conceived in meaningful relationship to each other and to assessment in related
Units, and must clearly describe what Students are required to do.
Evaluation/marking system - Marking criteria must be explicitly aligned with Learning
Outcomes being assessed and must align with Benchmarked Standards of performance.
Moderation - Moderation procedures must ensure Reliable judgement in relation to
Benchmarked Standards during all phases of the assessment cycle.
Student Identity Verification - The identity of the Student completing the task (authorship)
must be able to be verified.
Information for Students
The assessment regime, including Assessment Types and weighting, as listed in CAPS and as
shown in the Unit outline for that Teaching Period, cannot be varied after the CAPS publication
deadline.
Assessment information for Students must be detailed in a designated location in the Learning
Management System (LMS) and include details of:
the Assessment Type;
the Assessment Task requirements;
the relevant Learning Outcome(s);
the weighting;
