iolicy Title: Data Governance
Policy Owner: Chief Information Officer
Keywords: critical element
Policy Code: P
Intent
Organisational Scope
Definitions
Policy Content
Accountabilities and Responsibilities
Related Documents
Contact Information
Approval History
INTENT
The purpose of this policy is to define the principles and parameters that govern the
University’s Critical Data.
The policy recognises the University’s increasing dependence on Critical Data to enable
strategic decisions, improve teaching quality, student satisfaction, research outcomes, and
operational efficiencies. It addresses the vital role that Critical Data plays in ensuring the
university has confidence in the accuracy, availability, integrity, confidentiality, and suitability
of the Data it uses for informed insights and decision making.
The policy positions the University to better manage inefficiencies and exposure to risks that
can, and do, arise when Critical Data is not well governed.
ORGANISATIONAL SCOPE
This policy applies to all Staff, contractors, and consultants that have the capacity to impact
the integrity of the University’s Critical Data.
Only Critical Data, as defined by this policy, is within scope.
DEFINITIONS
The University Glossary and the following definitions apply to this policy:
Term:
Definition:
Business Process Owner
Means a role within a business function that creates, manages, and executes business
processes in order to achieve outcomes associated with their business function.
Critical Data
Means Data that has been analysed and assessed by the Chief Data Officer or delegate and
the relevant Business Process Owner and has been deemed to be of strategic importance to
the business processes of the University, including those relating to compliance, reporting to
government, and used in key operational and strategic decision making.
Data
Means all the structured and semi structured data that resides within the University’s
enterprise systems that emanates from the execution of business processes.
Data Element
