governance committees or"", ""L047"": ""working groups to identify and agree on a workable solution to achieving compliance or"", ""L048"": ""approving an exception.""}","Means an atomic unit of Data, datum or piece of information that has very specific meaning,
relevance, or semantics.
Data Governance Stakeholders
Means stakeholders that have been identified by Information Technology Governance
Committee as responsible for collaborating with the Chief Data Officer or their delegate in the
implementation of the University’s Data governance policy and Standards.
Master Data
Means the consistent and uniform set of identifiers that describes the core entities of the
University including Student, Staff, suppliers, chart of accounts, products, etc.
Strategic Asset
For the purpose of this policy means, Critical Data that, has meaning and usefulness across the
institution as a whole, as well being of intrinsic value based on the insights that can be derived
from it.
POLICY CONTENT
General Principles
The University will treat Critical Data as a Strategic Asset and contemplate it in accordance
with the University’s Information Management Principles, as referenced in the Information
Security and Information Technology policy.
The University will treat Critical Data in accordance with the Council-approved Risk appetite
statement.
To the extent relevant and required, the University’s Information Security and Information
Technology, Privacy, and Records Management policies apply to Data governance.
The quality of Critical Data must be considered when implementing new systems and Data
Governance Stakeholders must adhere to Data quality standards.
Data Governance will be centrally co-ordinated and overseen by the Chief Data Officer and any
delegate.
The Chief Data Officer or their delegate will develop and maintain guidelines, procedures,
processes and informing documents that allow this policy to be consistently operationalised.
Compliance with these associated documents is required. Reviews and updates will be
co-ordinated by the Chief Data Officer or their delegate and will be undertaken collaboratively
with impacted Data Governance Stakeholders. Approvals to any required changes to the
guidelines, procedures, processes and informing documents will be the responsibility of the
Chief Information Officer.
The suite of guidelines, procedures, processes, and informing documents developed to assist
in ensuring this policy is consistently operationalised will include:
processes for identifying Critical Data;
Data Governance roles;
responsibilities of key Data Governance Stakeholders;
processes for defining Critical Data quality standards;
glossary of Data Governance terms;
approved Master Data lists; and
the process for reviewing and responding to exception requests.
Where it is reasonably believed that an operational guideline, procedure, or process associated
with Data governance and Critical Data cannot be complied with, the concern must be raised
with the Chief Data Officer. The Chief Data Officer or their delegate will work collaboratively
with the impacted Data Governance Stakeholders and any relevant governance committees or
working groups to identify and agree on a workable solution to achieving compliance or
approving an exception.
