ate support and reasonable adjustments, having""}","Complainant
A person who makes a Complaint.
Concern
An expression of worry or dissatisfaction about a University decision, action, omission,
behaviour, service, or process where the person seeks clarification, discussion, or Local
Resolution rather than management through the formal complaints process.
Enquiry
A request for information, clarification, advice, guidance, or an explanation about a University
decision, process, service, policy, procedure, or requirement where the person is not
expressing dissatisfaction or seeking a formal response or resolution.
Feedback
A comment, suggestion, compliment, observation or opinion provided about the University’s
services, programs, processes, facilities or operations for the purpose of recognition,
improvement or consideration, where the person is not seeking a formal response or
resolution.
Local Resolution
An informal or early process undertaken by the relevant School, Service Centre or business
area to clarify, address or resolve a Concern without commencing the formal complaints
process.
Respondent
A person whose decision, action, omission or conduct is the subject of a Complaint. A
Complaint about a University service, system or process need not have an individual
Respondent.
Retaliation
Any adverse action, threat, intimidation, discrimination, or other detrimental treatment taken
against a person because they made or were believed to have made a Complaint, or
participated in a Complaint process.
POLICY CONTENT
Principles
The University will manage Complaints in accordance with ECU’s values of integrity, respect,
rational inquiry, personal excellence and courage, and the following principles:
Accessibility: Complaints processes are free, clearly communicated, and available to all
persons within the scope of this Policy.
Continuous improvement: Information and outcomes arising from Complaints inform service
and system improvements.
Cultural safety and inclusion: Complaints processes are culturally safe, inclusive, accessible
and responsive to diverse needs and experiences.
Fairness: Complaints are handled in accordance with procedural fairness and without bias.
Person-centred and trauma-informed practice: Complaints are managed with care in a manner
that promotes choice, agency and participation while minimising the risk of re-traumatisation.
Safety: People will be supported to raise, respond to and participate in Complaints without
Retaliation, victimisation or unreasonable disadvantage.
Timeliness: Complaints are handled without unnecessary delay.
Transparency: Processes, expectations and outcomes are communicated clearly.
The University recognises that raising or participating in a Complaint may be difficult, including
for people who have experienced discrimination, marginalisation, trauma, systemic barriers or
exclusion from institutional processes. The University will take reasonable steps to reduce
barriers to participation and provide appropriate support and reasonable adjustments, having
