L043"": ""University will consider the request and take reasonable steps to protect the Complainant’s"", ""L044"": ""identity and personal information, having regard to the Complainant’s wishes, the nature of"", ""L045"": ""the Complaint, applicable legal obligations and the requirements of procedural fairness."", ""L046"": ""Requests for confidentiality may limit the University's ability to assess, investigate or resolve"", ""L047"": ""the matter."", ""L048"": ""A Complaint may be made anonymously. Anonymous Complaints will be assessed on their"", ""L049"": ""merits, having regard to the seriousness of the allegations, the information and evidence""}","regard to each person’s circumstances and the requirements of procedural fairness.
The University will support effective Complaint management by ensuring that persons
performing Complaint management functions have appropriate authority, capability, training
and support, having regard to their roles. Training will include, where relevant to those roles,
complaint management, trauma-informed and person-centred practice, cultural safety, support
for culturally and racially marginalised persons, and administrative decision-making.
Non-Retaliation
The University is committed to ensuring that Complaints may be raised safely and without fear
of Retaliation.
No person will be subjected to Retaliation for: making a Complaint; seeking advice, advocacy
(including from the National Student Ombudsman where applicable) or support; participating
in a Complaint process; or providing information in connection with a Complaint.
Any conduct that may constitute Retaliation will be treated seriously and may result in
disciplinary or other action under relevant University frameworks.
This section does not prevent the University from taking reasonable and proportionate action
for independently established misconduct or another legitimate reason. Such action must not
be taken because the person made or participated in a Complaint.
Enquiries, Feedback and Local Resolution of Concerns
Individuals may make Enquiries, provide Feedback, or seek Local Resolution of Concerns with
the relevant School, Service Centre or business area.
A person is not required to attempt Local Resolution before making a Complaint. Local
Resolution may be encouraged or offered where it is safe, appropriate and consistent with the
person’s circumstances and procedural fairness.
The University will have regard to the substance of the matter and the outcome sought when
determining whether it is an Enquiry, Feedback, Concern or Complaint. Where the appropriate
pathway is unclear, the University will consult the person and inform them how the matter will
be managed.
A Concern may be treated as a Complaint where it is formally lodged through the University’s
complaints process or referred into that process by a School, Service Centre or business area.
Where a Concern is referred into the formal complaints process, the person will be informed of
the referral and how the matter will be managed.
Enquiries, Feedback and Concerns are not subject to the formal assessment, investigation and
outcome requirements that apply to Complaints, but may be recorded, referred or used to
inform improvements to the University’s services, systems and processes.
Making a Complaint
A Complaint may be lodged using any of the following methods:
the online form available through the University’s Complaints Resolution and Integrity
webpage;
by email to complaints@ecu.edu.au;
by telephone on (08) 6304 2199; or
with assistance or in an alternative format upon request.
A Complainant may request that their identity or other information be kept confidential. The
University will consider the request and take reasonable steps to protect the Complainant’s
identity and personal information, having regard to the Complainant’s wishes, the nature of
the Complaint, applicable legal obligations and the requirements of procedural fairness.
Requests for confidentiality may limit the University's ability to assess, investigate or resolve
the matter.
A Complaint may be made anonymously. Anonymous Complaints will be assessed on their
merits, having regard to the seriousness of the allegations, the information and evidence
