ision‑makers are impartial and free from conflicts of interest;"", ""L044"": ""considering all relevant evidence; and"", ""L045"": ""making evidence-based findings and giving reasons for them."", ""L046"": ""Outcomes"", ""L047"": ""Upon finalisation of a Complaint:"", ""L048"": ""the University will initiate any action required promptly; and""}","available, and whether the matter can be fairly investigated.
Anonymity may limit the University's ability to investigate, resolve, or determine a Complaint.
The University may decline to investigate, or discontinue an investigation, where insufficient
information is available or procedural fairness cannot be maintained.
Assessment and Investigation
When a Complaint is formally lodged or referred into the University’s formal complaints
process:
the University will commence assessment within 10 working days;
the Complaint will be triaged to facilitate early resolution where appropriate and proportionate
to risk and complexity;
an investigation will be conducted where appropriate; and
the Complainant will be provided with plain English guidance about the complaints process,
the scope of the matters to be considered, the handling of personal information, typical
outcomes, the expected timeline for resolution, factors that may foreseeably and reasonably
cause delays, and available pathways for escalation.
Multiple Complaints that are sufficiently similar may be consolidated and managed as a single
Complaint.
Complaints will ordinarily be finalised within 30 working days where practicable. Where this is
not practicable, the Complainant will be informed of the delay and provided with an expected
timeframe for resolution.
The University will provide Complainants with updates on the progress of a Complaint at
reasonable intervals and at key stages of the process, including where there are no
substantive developments.
The University may, at any stage, seek to resolve a Complaint through informal resolution,
facilitated discussion, mediation or another appropriate dispute resolution process. In
determining whether such a process is appropriate, the University will consider the nature and
seriousness of the matter, any safety concern, power imbalance, or conflict of interest, the
views of the persons involved, and the requirements of procedural fairness. Participation in a
consensual dispute resolution process is voluntary.
The use of such a process does not prevent the University from taking other action where
required by law, University policy, safety considerations or the seriousness of the matter.
The University may take reasonable and proportionate interim action where necessary to
protect safety or wellbeing, prevent Retaliation, preserve evidence, maintain the integrity of a
process or support participation in University activities. Interim action is precautionary, does
not constitute a finding and will be reviewed as appropriate.
Effective, Consistent and Fair
The University will ensure that Complaints are managed effectively, consistently and in
accordance with procedural fairness, including:
minimising unnecessary transfers of Complaint casework between Staff to support continuity
and timeliness;
identifying and sharing key information that could lead to an adverse decision;
providing affected persons with a reasonable opportunity to respond;
ensuring investigators and decision‑makers are impartial and free from conflicts of interest;
considering all relevant evidence; and
making evidence-based findings and giving reasons for them.
Outcomes
Upon finalisation of a Complaint:
the University will initiate any action required promptly; and
