ncluding the National Student Ombudsman and the Tertiary"", ""L047"": ""Education Quality and Standards Agency.""}","the Complainant will receive a written outcome.
Each written outcome for a Complaint will include:
findings (including material findings of fact);
reasons for those findings, addressing the key issues raised in the Complaint where relevant;
any actions taken or proposed; and
confirmation that the written outcome constitutes the University’s final substantive response
under this Policy, together with information about any external complaint, review or escalation
avenues available to the Complainant.
External Review
A prospective, current or former Student who is dissatisfied with the University’s handling or
outcome of a Complaint may make an external complaint to the National Student
Ombudsman, where the person and matter are within its jurisdiction.
A member of the public will be informed of any external complaint, review or escalation
avenues relevant to their Complaint.
Nothing in this Policy prevents a person from contacting an external body at any time.
Support and Participation
Persons involved in a Complaint process may:
seek independent advice or advocacy;
be accompanied by a support person of their choice, including a legally qualified person, a
peer supporter, an advocate, or a personal support person; and
access internal or external support services. Where appropriate, the University will facilitate
referral to suitable support services.
Gender-Based Violence, including Sexual Assault and Sexual Harassment
Complaints involving gender-based violence, including sexual assault or sexual harassment,
will be managed using person-centred and trauma-informed practices and in accordance with
relevant policies, including the Gender-based Violence Prevention and Response Policy. These
practices will prioritise safety, trust, choice, collaboration, and empowerment, and aim to
minimise the risk of re-traumatisation.
Confidentiality
A person authorised to manage or investigate a Complaint on behalf of the University may
make a reasonable, necessary and proportionate confidentiality direction for the purposes of
protecting personal information, ensuring procedural fairness and maintaining the integrity of
an investigation or Complaint process.
A confidentiality direction is intended to protect privacy, support procedural fairness, maintain
the integrity of the Complaint process and minimise the risk of harm to persons involved. It is
not intended to prevent individuals from discussing their experiences or accessing support.
A confidentiality direction:
will be communicated clearly and in plain English;
will be limited in scope and duration; and
does not prevent a person from discussing their underlying experience.
Notwithstanding any confidentiality direction, a person involved in a Complaint process may
disclose information to:
a support person of their choice, including a legally qualified person;
a health or counselling professional;
a legal practitioner for the purpose of obtaining legal advice; or
an external agency or regulator, including the National Student Ombudsman and the Tertiary
Education Quality and Standards Agency.
