Overarching Regulations 
Student Integrity Regulations  
Policy 
1. 
The University aims to create and maintain a culture of academic integrity that: 
1.1 makes explicit to students the norms, structures and practices of the wider 
academic culture of which student academic integrity forms a part 
1.2 promotes academic integrity by supporting students to use, generate and 
communicate information and ideas in an honest and responsible manner 
1.3 ensures that student academic integrity is supported through curriculum 
and assessment design and their periodic review  
1.4 undertakes that all academic staff are trained in educating students about 
academic integrity 
1.5 provides students with timely opportunities to learn and practise the 
principles and techniques needed to maintain academic integrity. 
2. 
The University expects that students will: 
2.1 individually and collectively engage in learning activities honestly and 
responsibly, and act with respect and fairness in all forms of assessment, 
groupwork and placement 
2.2 equip themselves with the knowledge and skills needed to meet the 
University’s academic integrity standards 
2.3 take responsibility for their actions, utilise the University’s support systems 
as required and avoid acts of academic misconduct. 
3. 
Academic Staff will: 
3.1 be fair, consistent and timely in their dealings with students in managing 
academic misconduct 
3.2 be guided by the decision-making principles set out in the Student Integrity 
Regulations. 
4. 
Failure to meet the University’s academic integrity standards constitutes 
academic misconduct.  
5. 
Academic misconduct includes any unfair, unauthorised or dishonest behaviour 
through which a student intends or is likely to gain advantage.  
6. 
Instances of academic misconduct include: 
6.1 Plagiarism 
6.1.1. directly copying any material without acknowledging the source, 
including sentences, paragraphs, computer code, tables, graphs, 
images and diagrams from electronic or print resources including 
websites, articles, books and reports 
