Purpose and Scope 
These Regulations give effect to Statute No. 26 – Student Misconduct. They must be 
read in conjunction with the Academic Freedom and Freedom of Speech Regulations 
which prevail over these Regulations. 
Regulations 
1. 
Any defined terms below are specific to this document. 
The following words have the following meanings: 
1.1. “Gender-based Violence” is defined in the Gender-based Violence 
Prevention and Response Policy. 
1.2. “Misconduct” means any behaviour on the part of a student that:  
1.2.1. impairs the reasonable freedom of the University community to 
pursue and participate in academic, community or other University 
activities, or in the life of the University  
1.2.2. contravenes the provisions of the Murdoch University Act 1973, 
any statute, rule or by-law of the university, or a university code of 
conduct, policy, procedure or set of guidelines 
1.2.3. contravenes any lawful direction by a member of the staff of the 
University  
1.2.4. contravenes any written law of the Commonwealth of Australia, a 
State or Territory of Australia where the written law relates to an 
activity associated with the student being a student of the 
university.  
1.3. “Student” means a person enrolled in a unit or course offered by the 
University at the time the alleged misconduct occurred.  
1.4. “Outcome” means the education, support, conditions or penalties imposed 
on students following the determination of a case of student misconduct.  
1.5. “Suspension” means suspension of the student’s enrolment in a course 
for one or more teaching periods to a maximum equivalent of one year, 
including suspension of current enrolment in all units and/or removal or 
placing conditions on the student’s use of or attendance at facilities, 
Student Integrity Regulations 
  
