1.6. “Exclusion” means that the student is forbidden to enter or remain on any 
or all university physical environments or any specified part of a university 
physical environment and/or to use or access university facilities or any 
specified university facilities for a period of time.  
1.7. “Expulsion” means that the student is expelled from the university 
including cancellation of the student’s current enrolment and removal of 
access to future enrolment for any period of time.  
1.8. “university Community” means university staff, students, volunteers, 
consultants, contractors and their staff, members of university boards and 
committees, subsidiaries of the university and their staff, visitors to 
university grounds, university guests, university function attendees, and 
any other people or organisations appointed or engaged by the University 
to perform duties or functions on its behalf.  
2. 
These Regulations specify:  
2.1. the behaviour for which a student may be disciplined by the University. 
2.2. the procedure for dealing with allegations of Misconduct made against a 
student. 
2.3. the outcomes that may be imposed upon a student if any allegation of 
Misconduct is upheld. 
2.4. the capacity for an appeal against any finding of Misconduct against a 
student.  
3. 
Immediate Exclusion  
3.1. A security officer or other person authorised by the Vice Chancellor may, 
without a hearing, exclude a student from any or all University physical 
environments if they are of the reasonable opinion that doing so is 
necessary to protect other people, property or to prevent serious 
disruption to university activities. 
3.2. A person authorised by the Vice Chancellor may, without a hearing, 
exclude a student from use of university information technology facilities if 
they are of the reasonable opinion that doing so is necessary to protect 
other people, University information and communication systems or 
prevent serious disruption to university activities.  
4. 
Decision-Making  
4.1. Decision-makers will take account of the following principles:  
4.1.1. the process will be procedurally fair and just 
4.1.2. the rights and interests of all university members, visitors and the 
University’s partners and stakeholders are to be respected, as 
well as those of individual students 
4.1.3. investigations will be impartial and timely 
