5.3 There may also be use/disclosure for reasons of law, order, enforcement 
and safety in the following circumstances: 
5.3.1 
If required or authorised by law or court/tribunal order; 
5.3.2 
When it is unreasonable or impracticable to obtain consent and the 
University reasonably believes it is necessary to prevent or lessen 
a serious threat to the life, health or safety of any person; or public 
health or public safety. 
5.3.3 
If the University has reason to suspect that unlawful activity or 
misconduct of a serious nature that relates to the University’s 
functions or activities has been, is being, or may be engaged in, 
and the University reasonably believes that it is a necessary part of 
its investigation and action in relation to the matter to disclose it, 
including in the reporting of its concerns to relevant persons or 
authorities; 
5.3.4 
If it is reasonably necessary either to establish, exercise or defend 
a legal (or equitable) claim, or for the purposes of a confidential 
alternative dispute resolution process; 
5.3.5 
When the University believes that the use or disclosure is 
reasonably necessary for one or more enforcement related 
activities conducted by an enforcement body (e.g. the police), in 
which case a written note will be kept with details of the disclosure. 
5.4 There may also be use/disclosure for health-related situations -. Health 
information may be disclosed for purposes other than the primary purpose 
of collection as outlined in clauses 5.2 and 5.3, and in the following 
circumstances: 
5.4.1 
Research 
5.4.1.1 Disclosure is necessary for research or for compiling and 
analysing statistics relevant to public health or safety;  
5.4.1.2 It is impracticable to obtain consent;  
5.4.1.3 The research is conducted in accordance with all 
applicable guidelines; or  
5.4.1.4 The University reasonably believes the recipient will not 
disclose the information or any personal information 
derived from health information. 
5.4.2 
Incapacity to give consent 
5.4.2.1 If a health service has been provided; and 
5.4.2.2 The recipient is a responsible person for the individual; and 
5.4.2.3 The individual is physically or legally incapable of giving 
consent or cannot communicate consent; and 
5.4.2.4 The carer providing the health service for the University is 
satisfied that the disclosure is necessary for appropriate 
care or treatment, or is made for compassionate reasons; 
and 
