4.2 If personal information is not provided in full, or not kept up to date, then 
there may be serious consequences, including (by way of example only): 
4.2.1 
the withdrawal of an offer to a student of a place at the University; 
or 
4.2.2 
if admitted to the University, the cancellation of a student’s 
enrolment; or 
4.2.3 
an adverse impact on the student/staff record. 
4.3 Upon request, and subject to applicable exceptions, the University will 
provide   access to the personal information it holds about an individual 
within 30 days, at no cost. Personal information may be reviewed and 
depending on its nature, may be updated or corrected in accordance with 
relevant procedures. This applies, for example, to information held on 
Callista (MyInfo) for students and Workday for staff.  Access may be denied 
to information where this may impact the privacy of other individuals or 
involve the disclosure of trade secrets, confidential information, ongoing 
research data or information about the business, commercial or financial 
affairs of the University; or where the request is frivolous or vexatious; or 
the information is subject to legal professional privilege. 
4.4 If any personal information held by the University is inaccurate, out of date, 
incomplete, irrelevant, or misleading, the University will take reasonable 
steps to correct it. If a correction is refused, an individual will receive a 
written explanation, and upon request, a statement disputing the 
information will be attached to the record. 
4.5 Unless impracticable or unlawful, the University will notify any third party to 
whom personal information was previously disclosed of a correction, if 
requested. 
 
5.  
Use and disclosure of personal information 
5.1 The University will not use or disclose personal information other than for 
the primary purpose for which it was collected. 
5.2 The University may disclose information in the following specific situations: 
5.2.1 
Authorised Contractors: To contractors engaged by the University, 
such as IT service providers, where required to fulfil contractual 
obligations. 
5.2.2 
Academic Verification: In response to a legitimate request from 
another university regarding a student’s academic record and 
disciplinary history, provided reasonable steps are taken to confirm 
the legitimacy of the request. 
5.2.3 
Graduate Register: Publication of graduate details, including name, 
conferral date, and award title, on the University’s Graduate 
Register to enable third parties to verify qualifications. 
5.2.4 
Surveys: For the purpose of conducting student and graduate 
surveys, either directly by the University or through an authorised 
third party. Any third party engaged will be required to comply with 
