6.  
Automated Decision-Making 
6.1 The University may use automated systems or tools to assist in decision 
making processes. These may include analysing personal information to 
support administrative, educational, research, or service-related functions. 
6.2 Where an automated decision-making process is used, the University will: 
6.2.1 
ensure the use of personal information complies with the PRIS Act, 
including the IPPs; 
6.2.2 
take reasonable steps to ensure that any automated decision is fair, 
accurate, and based on reliable and up to date information; 
6.2.3 
provide individuals with information about the use of automated 
decision making where it forms a substantial part of a decision that 
affects them; 
6.2.4 
ensure that a meaningful human review is available upon request 
where an automated process has materially influenced an 
outcome; and 
6.2.5 
maintain appropriate oversight, auditability, and safeguards to 
prevent unlawful discrimination, bias, or adverse impacts arising 
from automated processes. 
6.3 Biometric information will not be used for automated decision making that 
has a significant effect on an individual’s rights or access to the university 
services without appropriate human oversight. 
6.4 Automated decision making will not be used as the sole basis for decisions 
that may significantly affect an individual’s rights, access to services, or 
opportunities without appropriate human involvement. 
 
7.  
Disclosure to overseas recipients 
7.1 If the University is likely to disclose personal information to an overseas 
recipient, reasonable steps will be taken to notify the individual at the time 
of collection and, where practicable, specify the countries in which such 
recipients are likely to be located. 
