Panel, in accordance with the Graduate Research Degrees Procedure. 
51.1. Significant changes to the research program may require changes to the 
Supervisory Panel.  
51.2. In the case of significant changes to the research program, the candidature 
must be confirmed again, without extension to candidature. 
Progress Reports  
52. During the period of confirmed candidature, the Supervisory Panel and the 
candidate must jointly provide the Dean with Progress Reports (Annual and 
Interim) in accordance with the Graduate Research Degrees Procedure. 
53. Failure to submit the Progress Reports may result in termination of candidature. 
54. After reviewing the candidate’s Progress Report, the Dean must: 
54.1. confirm the candidate’s continued enrolment; 
54.2. place any conditions on the continuation of the candidature; 
54.3. in consultation with the Supervisory Panel, transfer the candidate’s 
enrolment to another degree; or 
54.4. terminate the candidate’s enrolment on the basis of unsatisfactory 
progress. 
55. Before making a referral under Regulation 54.4, the Dean may require a 
candidate to provide additional reports to ascertain what progress the candidate 
is making and/or to assist the candidate in completing within the enrolment period 
set out in Regulation 17. 
56. If the candidature is terminated for unsatisfactory progress, from the date of 
termination, a candidate will not be permitted to enrol in a new graduate research 
degree for a period of not less than fifty per cent of the maximum enrolment 
period. 
Variations to Candidature 
57. A candidate may transfer between full-time and part-time candidature, subject to 
the Dean’s approval on the recommendation of the Principal Supervisor and 
endorsement by the PGRC.  
57.1. Candidates who are scholarship recipients will be required to check their 
scholarship conditions before applying to transfer between full time and 
part-time enrolment.  
57.2. The candidate must report to the Dean any significant changes in other 
commitments outside the research program that are likely to hinder 
satisfactory progress. 
