63.1. The research undertaken by the candidate while enrolled for the Master of 
Philosophy must be continued for the Doctoral Degree (Research) or 
modified to meet the requirements for the Doctoral Degree (Research). 
63.2. Transfers may take place no earlier than at the point of Confirmation of 
Candidature and, other than in special circumstances, no later than 18 
months (three years for part-time candidates) after initial enrolment. 
63.3. Transfers must take place without a break between candidature for the 
Master of Philosophy and the Doctoral Degree (Research) subject to any 
approved period of leave. 
63.4. The same enrolment period for a Doctoral Degree (Research) as outlined 
in Regulation 17 applies, with the time completed as a Master of Philosophy 
candidate deducted from the enrolment as a Doctoral Degree (Research) 
candidate. 
64. Candidates enrolled for a Doctoral Degree (Research) may elect to transfer to a 
Master of Philosophy for academic or personal reasons, subject to Regulation 
12, in accordance with the Graduate Research Degrees Procedure. 
64.1. Transfers may take place at the point of Confirmation of Candidature in the 
Doctoral Degree (Research), subject to Regulation 17.  
64.2. The same enrolment period for a Master of Philosophy as outlined in 
Regulation 17 applies, with the time completed as a Doctoral Degree 
(Research) candidate deducted from the enrolment as a Master of 
Philosophy candidate. 
Termination of Candidature 
65. On the termination of candidature, candidates will immediately lose their right to 
access the University’s resources and facilities, including supervisory support. 
66. Candidates seeking to apply for re-admission into a graduate research degree 
following termination will be assessed by the Dean, subject to Regulation 12 and 
the cause of termination. 
66.1. Candidates will not usually be admitted into any graduate research degree 
if they have been terminated for misconduct or unsatisfactory progress. 
Thesis Submission 
67. The thesis shall be based on research supervised during the enrolment period at 
Murdoch University, as outlined in Regulation 17, with the following learning 
outcomes: 
67.1. the Doctoral Degree (Research) must make a significant and original 
contribution to knowledge and demonstrate the learning outcomes at the 
relevant Australian Qualifications Framework level;  
67.2. the Doctoral Degree (Professional) must make a significant and original 
contribution to knowledge in the context of professional practice, and 
demonstrate the learning outcomes at the relevant Australian Qualifications 
Framework level; and 
