67.3. the Masters Degree (Research) must apply an advanced body of 
knowledge in a range of contexts for research and scholarship and as a 
pathway for further learning and demonstrate the learning outcomes at the 
relevant Australian Qualifications Framework level.  
68. At the time of thesis submission, the candidate must be enrolled, subject to 
Regulation 18. 
69. At least two months before the thesis submission, the candidate must submit the 
title of the thesis, together with a draft abstract of approximately 300 words, to 
the Supervisors, PGRC, and Graduate Research Office as notification of the 
intention to submit. 
70. The candidate’s thesis content, in accordance with the Graduate Research 
Degrees Procedure : 
70.1. must include a written declaration by the candidate to verify, among other 
things, authorship and originality, and a substantial portion can be attributed 
to the candidate’s contribution at a minimum of 75 per cent, subject to 
Regulations 67 and 70.2;  
70.2. may describe work done in conjunction with the Principal Supervisor and/or 
other persons, provided that the candidate’s personal share of at least fifty 
per cent in their contribution to any publication included in the thesis is 
clearly stated. 
70.2.1. Statements relating to author contributions of publications must be 
certified by the Principal Supervisor and/or other persons, as 
outlined in the Graduate Research Degrees Procedure;  
70.3. (except for a resubmitted thesis) the thesis contains as its main content 
work which has not been previously submitted for a degree at any 
university;  
70.4. must conform to scholarly standards of presentation and format for 
examination, citation, word length and referencing for the School, as set out 
in the Graduate Research Degrees Procedure; 
70.5. must conform to the University’s requirements in regards to the structure of 
the thesis, as outlined in the Graduate Research Degrees Procedure; and 
70.6. any required approvals. 
71. The Principal Supervisor must provide a statement confirming the candidate’s 
thesis content meets the requirements set out in Regulation 70 and 
recommending the thesis for examination, in accordance with the Graduate 
Research Degrees Procedure. 
72. Except in extraordinary circumstances, a thesis will not be examined if the 
Principal Supervisor, in consultation with the Supervisory Panel, or PGRC in the 
absence of an agreement in the Supervisory Panel, does not declare that the 
thesis is complete and suitable for examination, in accordance with Regulation 
69. 
72.1. Further to Regulation 72, a thesis will not be examined if the Principal 
Supervisor or candidate have not met the requirements for submission as 
set out in the Graduate Research Degrees Procedure.  
