Suppliers should review their contracts, agreements, and purchase orders with the 
University. These may include additional obligations or higher standards than those 
set out in this Code.  
3 Sustainability  
The University seeks to be a leader in education, research and operations that support 
a more sustainable world.  
The University expects suppliers to operate in a way that manages environmental 
impacts linked to the goods and services they provide. At a minimum, suppliers are 
expected to comply with all relevant environmental laws and regulations.  
Suppliers are encouraged to:  
• Integrate sustainability practices into business decisions and operations.  
• Reduce environmental impacts associated with energy use, emissions, water 
use, waste and hazardous substances.  
• Support more sustainable production and supply practices, including reduced 
packaging and increased use of recyclable or recycled materials where 
practicable.  
The University expects suppliers to demonstrate ongoing improvements in 
sustainability performance where their activities present material environment 
impacts.  
4 Equity, Diversity and Inclusion  
The University aims to foster a safe, inclusive and respectful community where all 
people are treated fairly and with dignity.  
Suppliers are expected to operate ethical workplaces and comply with all relevant 
equal opportunity and anti-discrimination laws.  
Suppliers should:  
• Provide workplaces free from discrimination, harassment and bullying.  
• Support fair and transparent recruitment, development and promotion 
practices.  
• Foster inclusive and respectful working environments.  
• Take reasonable steps to support staff wellbeing and participation.  
• Take reasonable steps to promote the above principles within their own supply 
chains.  
