Purpose and Scope 
This code is a public statement of the ethical principles, values and behaviours 
expected of members of Murdoch University.  
This code applies to all staff and students. 
 
Objectives:  
The Code of Ethics is based on three fundamental ethical principles: 
• The Principle of Justice 
• The Principle of Respect 
• The Principle of Responsible Care 
Code 
Justice  
1. 
Justice requires that people are fairly treated and not discriminated against, 
abused, or exploited. Justice seeks both equal opportunities and fair shares of 
social benefits for individuals, with equitable outcomes for disadvantaged groups. 
It is based on obedience to applicable laws, while recognising the right to criticise 
those laws or the policies that underlie them. 
Respect  
2. 
Respect requires that people’s rights are honoured and defended and that they 
are actively empowered to claim their rights. Respect for other people is a 
fundamental requirement of a just community. Individual members of a 
community must accept their social responsibilities to behave honestly and with 
integrity, to tolerate and respect others and to cooperate with other members of 
the community and other communities. 
Responsible Care 
3. 
Responsible care is a primary moral obligation to do good and not to do harm to 
others. Responsible care entails tolerance and respect for the rights of others. It 
requires that fundamental human rights are protected and that the diversity of all 
cultures and peoples is respected. Those who are strong enough to assert their 
rights have a responsibility to protect others, especially those who are dependent 
on them. All stakeholders are responsible for the efficient stewardship of assets, 
resources, and the environment. 
Code of Ethics 
