Gender-based Violence during the course of their previous employment, 
or otherwise in a legal process.  Any declaration made and any risks 
arising must be taken into consideration in determining suitability to hold 
the position. 
The CNC is briefed on the outcomes of the meeting with the potential 
members of Senate. A recommendation is made to Senate to co-opt or to 
recommend the candidate to the Governor (through the Office of the Minister 
for Education). 
Once the candidate has been selected, vetted and recommended, upon 
approval the candidate is then appointed. 
At the time of being invited to join, or nominating for election to the Senate, 
the University Secretary gives prospective Senator’s information about what 
is involved in being a Senator.  
3. Induction: 
Murdoch University provides a structured induction program for new Senate 
members, informed by the Code of Governance Principles and Practice for 
Australia’s Public Universities and relevant guidance and resources of the 
Australian Institute of Company Directors (AICD). The program is coordinated by 
the University Secretary’s Office. 
The program is designed to provide new members with an understanding of the 
University, its governance arrangements and operating environment, the role and 
responsibilities of Senate, and the expectations of individual Senate members. 
Induction is tailored to each member, having regard to their existing skills and 
experience as reflected in the Senate Skills Matrix, their Senate Committee 
appointments and any identified induction needs. 
The program is undertaken progressively, commencing before the member’s first 
Senate meeting and continuing through their initial period of membership. It 
comprises: 
• 
core governance orientation; 
• 
core leadership briefings; 
• 
a Senate buddy; 
