Copyright in a work is (usually) owned by the original author/creator of that work. Some
exceptions and considerations include:
copyright in works created by an employee (usually) belong to the employer
a work is automatically protected by copyright as soon as it is given 'material expression' –
that means a work has been created
copyright does not protect ideas or facts.
The copyright owner’s rights
The copyright owner of a work has the exclusive right to: copy, publish, and perform it;
communicate it online; translate or adapt it; and to assign, transfer, or sell ownership of the
work to someone else.
Regardless of who has ownership of the copyright in a work, its protection endures for the life
of the author plus 70 years.
How can I avoid infringing Copyright?
