Page 3 of 8 
Effective Date: 1/1/2026 
1 OBJECTS OF THE UNIVERSITY 
1.1 
The University's Objects are defined in Section 5 of its Act of Parliament: 
 
The Objects of the University are: 
(a) the provision of university education, within a context of Catholic faith and values; and 
(b) the provision of an excellent standard of - 
i. 
teaching, scholarship and research 
ii. 
training for the professions, and 
iii. 
pastoral care for its students. 
2 PURPOSE 
2.1 
This policy sets out the principles for assessment at the University of Notre Dame Australia 
(the University). Assessment is underpinned by best practice principles to engage students in 
assessment and assure student learning and to maintain standards of student achievement 
and uphold standards of academic integrity. 
3 SCOPE 
3.1 
This Policy applies to all forms of assessment in Higher Education coursework awards, the 
coursework component of Higher Degree by Research (HDR) awards, non-award courses, and 
pathway programs. 
3.2 
This Policy does not apply to Research Degrees without a coursework component, or to 
Vocational Education and Training courses.  
3.3 
The Policy: Academic Integrity and Procedure: Academic Integrity states the University’s 
requirements for ensuring academic integrity of students’ assessment work. 
3.4 
The Procedure: Assessment and Examinations states the University’s requirements for 
assessment in higher education coursework and enabling programs and courses, including 
research component courses of bachelor (honours), and masters by coursework programs. 
3.5 
The Procedure: Assessment and Learning Flexibility states the University’s requirements for 
the provision of reasonable adjustments and other types of flexibility (including special 
consideration) to support students to succeed in their studies. 
4 PRINCIPLES 
4.1 
The purpose of assessment is to both engage students in the learning process and give 
assurance of student learning. This is accomplished by providing students with varied, reliable 
and meaningful opportunities to demonstrate their achievement of learning outcomes and 
their ability to reflect on and make evaluative judgements about their achievements and 
those of others.  
4.2 
Assessment at the University is informed by research and scholarship, contemporary 
disciplinary standards, and evidence-based good practice, and is underpinned by the following 
principles. 
