4.3 
Principle 1: Assessment is considered programmatically as part of the educational design of 
programs to provide a holistic assessment regime that is constructively aligned and integrated 
horizontally and vertically and acknowledges progress over time. 
4.3.1 A programmatic approach incorporates processes for review and improvement of 
curricula and assessment to provide assurance of learning and contribute to assurance 
of qualifications awarded.  
4.3.2 Assessing student performance through integration and alignment of intended learning 
outcomes, horizontally in each level of study and vertically through progressive levels, 
enables students to understand the connection between program learning outcomes 
and assessment, and to progressively demonstrate acquisition of the University’s 
Graduate Attributes and professional competencies. 
4.3.3 A programmatic approach enables the strategic embedding of increasing assessment 
complexity across a program of study to support the University in making valid and 
reliable judgements about student progression and attainment. 
4.4 
Principle 2: Assessment is grounded in integrity, ethical scholarship and disciplinary standards 
and practices. 
4.4.1 Integrity and ethics are fundamental to good scholarship and are located within and in 
relation to disciplinary discourse, standards and practices. They point to the ethical 
requirements that students must meet in future employment and civic life. 
4.5 
Principle 3: Assessment is a developmental activity which, alongside feedback and timely 
support, helps student transition into scholarly practices that frame how students learn and 
what they achieve by actively engaging them in meaningful (productive, purposeful and 
authentic) learning. 
4.5.1 Students come to their studies with varied preparedness and understanding of 
expectations and require support to acquire and develop the skills they need for 
successful study, learning and assessment. This is particularly the case in early levels of 
study where assessment should build capability and confidence. 
4.6 
Principle 4: Assessment is authentic and represents an appropriate challenge that simulates 
the types of tasks, problems and issues students will encounter in their future professional, 
personal and civic lives, supporting the development of the knowledge, skills and dispositions 
for students to flourish. 
4.6.1 Authenticity in assessment improves student engagement and helps students to 
contextualise their learning and demonstrate the integration of knowledge, skills, and 
dispositions in ways relevant to their discipline(s). 
4.6.2 When assessment tasks engage students in learning and represent significant learning 
activities in themselves, they have the capacity to create relevance and meaning for 
student learning. 
4.6.3 Engaging students in complex and open-ended situations provides a rich frame for 
learning that requires higher-order thinking, deep understanding and sophisticated 
skills development, preparing students for future work, personal and civic lives. 
4.7 
Principle 5: Assessment is inclusive and encompasses diverse and differentiated methods and 
types of assessment, including both individual and groupwork, that act as fair and equitable 
mechanisms for students to demonstrate learning and performance in context. 
4.7.1 Providing diverse, complementary and integrated assessment tasks and assessment 
pathways within a program provides choice and ensures all students are supported to 
develop the full range of Graduate Attributes and demonstrate their learning and 
