7.2.4 Assessment criteria describe the specific elements of a student’s performance that align 
to the course intended learning outcomes.  
7.2.5 Assessment design encompasses a whole of program coordinated approach with 
integration horizontally and vertically, with assessments aligned clearly to learning 
outcomes and the time required commensurate with volume of learning and AQF Level. 
7.2.6 Assessment as learning occurs when students are their own evaluators. Students 
monitor their own learning, as questions and use a range of strategies to decide what 
they know and can do, and how to use this information for future learning. 
7.2.7 Assurance of learning is a systematic process for determining and revising program 
learning goals; designing, delivering, and improving program curricula to achieve 
learning goals; and demonstrating that program learning goals have been met. 
7.2.8 Authenticity in assessment refers to applying knowledge and skills in context, with a 
degree of realism, appropriate cognitive or skills-based challenges and the development 
of evaluative judgements. 
7.2.9 Authentic assessment directs students to deal with complex problems, issues, 
challenges, tasks that reflect what occurs in real world settings or to reflect on what 
occurs in real world settings. To be authentic, the assessment task needs to mirror 
external work environments or require the exercise of disciplinary / professionally 
relevant capabilities. 
7.2.10 Constructive alignment means the deliberate linking of intended learning as stated 
learning outcomes, teaching and learning activities and assessment tasks (including 
their associated assessment criteria and rubrics). 
7.2.11 Course has the same meaning as in the Policy: Programs and Courses.  
7.2.12 Course outline has the same meaning as in the Policy: Program and Course Information.  
7.2.13 Evaluative judgement means making a decision about the quality of work of self and 
others based on developing an understanding of quality, applying this to appraise the 
quality of one’s own and others’ work, and to carry this capability into future work, 
including in complex and unsupervised environments. 
7.2.14 Feedback means providing students with a range of constructive written and verbal 
information (or demonstrated tasks) to assist students in their learning, by 
acknowledging achievement, explaining results / performance and enabling students to 
develop evaluative judgement to assess the quality of their work and that of others.  
Ideally this should assist students to improve work in subsequent tasks. Feedback should 
be positive, respectful, inform students of their performance against specific 
assessment criteria, address misunderstanding, and guide future learning and improved 
performance.  
7.2.15 Grade means a code expressed as one, two or three letters used to record the outcome 
of a student’s enrolment in a course: either their level of academic achievement in the 
course or the outcome of an administrative process in circumstances where they did 
not complete it. Grades are recorded on a student’s official academic transcript. 
7.2.16 Mark means the numerical representation of a summative assessment of performance.  
7.2.17 Moderation of assessment refers to quality assurance processes and activities such as 
peer review that aim to assure the consistency or comparability, appropriateness, 
fairness, and comprehensibility of an assessment task, and the related criteria and 
standards. Moderation applies to many aspects of assessment such as the choice of 
assessment task and its design, assessment instructions, marking guides, rubrics, 
marking / grading and feedback, calibration of markers (interrata). It serves to assure 
