OVERVIEW OF ASSESSMENT AND LEARNING FLEXIBILITY 
3.1 
The University provides reasonable flexibility in various forms to support students to succeed 
in their studies across a range of individual circumstances. 
3.2 
The following table shows the types of flexibility that may be available to support students in 
various circumstances. Detailed requirements in relation to each type of flexibility are then 
stated in the other sections below. 
3.3 
In applying the provisions for reasonable adjustments for assessment arrangements in this 
Procedure, the University will ensure it meets any assessment requirements and mandatory 
or inherent requirements for professional practice that are set by professional accrediting 
agencies for the professional accreditation of programs and/or registration of graduates for 
practice. 
 
Circumstance 
Type of flexibility 
The student has a disability, injury or medical 
condition that is a permanent condition, episodic 
or of extended duration. 
Reasonable adjustments for disability (learning access 
plan). 
The student is a primary carer for someone with 
a disability, a medical condition (including a 
terminal or chronic illness), a mental health 
condition, or who is frail and aged. 
Reasonable adjustments for disability (learning access 
plan). 
The student has a short-term injury or medical 
condition which requires an adjustment to exam 
conditions. 
Reasonable adjustments for disability (learning access 
plan). 
The student has unexpected circumstances that 
will prevent them from submitting assessment 
work by the due date. 
Extension of time (for an extension up to 7 calendar days) 
Special consideration (for an extension greater than 7 
calendar days) – for special consideration, the student will 
need to show that the circumstances are exceptional. 
Exceptional circumstances impair the student’s 
performance in or prevent them from attempting 
an assessment task (other than a final exam) that 
requires them to do something on a scheduled 
date. 
Special consideration. 
Reasonable adjustments for disability through a learning 
access plan may also include adjustments to accommodate 
short-term exacerbation of a student’s health condition. 
Exceptional circumstances prevent the student 
from sitting a final exam. 
A deferred exam or irregularly scheduled exam. 
Exceptional circumstances prevent the student 
from attending scheduled classes or learning 
activities where attendance is required to pass 
the course. 
Special consideration. 
Reasonable adjustments for disability through a learning 
access plan may also include adjustments to allow 
attendance requirements to be met. 
A student is registered with the University as an 
elite athlete and needs to meet training or 
sporting commitments. 
The student can arrange assessment and learning 
adjustments directly with the student administration 
officer or elite athlete contact for their school and with 
course coordinators. 
For a final exam, a deferred exam or irregularly scheduled 
exam. 
