Circumstance 
Type of flexibility 
An Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander student 
needs to meet a cultural commitment of which 
the timing is outside their control. 
The student can arrange assessment and learning 
adjustments directly with the program coordinator and 
course coordinators. 
For a final exam, a deferred exam or irregularly scheduled 
exam. 
The student has: 
• narrowly failed a course (with an overall 
course mark in the range 45–491), or 
• gained an overall mark in a course of 50% or 
above but received a FN (fail non-attempt) 
grade because they did not achieve the 
required mark in a hurdle assessment task. 
Supplementary assessment tasks. 
(Where program requirements allow, a board of 
examiners may grant supplementary assessment tasks. 
Students cannot apply for supplementary assessment 
tasks.) 
The student has very narrowly failed (with an 
overall course mark in the range 48–492) a 
course which, if they enrol in it to repeat it, will 
delay their graduation. 
Conceded pass 
(A student cannot apply for this, but a board of examiners 
has discretion to grant it, unless the program requirements 
document rules it out.) 
 
4 
EXCEPTIONAL CIRCUMSTANCES 
4.1 
Some types of assessment flexibility described in this procedure (deferred exams, irregularly 
scheduled exams, special consideration) are available to students who are experiencing 
exceptional circumstances. 
4.2 
Exceptional circumstances are circumstances beyond the student’s control that significantly 
impair their ability to undertake an assessment task or prevent them from undertaking it, such 
as: 
4.2.1 
illness 
4.2.2 
physical impairment or incapacitation resulting from an accident 
4.2.3 
misadventure or trauma such as a natural disaster or being the victim of a crime 
4.2.4 
compassionate circumstances such as death or serious injury of an immediate family 
member or close friend 
4.2.5 
hardship such as financial adversity or unexpected loss of employment 
4.2.6 
family breakdown or domestic disturbance 
4.2.7 
unavoidable commitments such as: 
4.2.7.1 
a religious commitment 
4.2.7.2 
a sporting commitment as a state or national representative 
4.2.7.3 
emergency service or military service 
4.2.7.4 
jury duty or having to attend court, a tribunal or a hearing 
4.2.7.5 
an unexpected work commitment such as being instructed to work on a 
day or at a time when the student does not normally have to work 
4.2.7.6 
a compulsory professional experience placement 
4.2.8 
for an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander student, a cultural commitment, or  
 
1 Exceptions to eligibility for supplementary assessment tasks are prescribed in section 12.  
2 Exceptions to eligibility for a conceded pass are prescribed in section 13.  
