6 
ASSESSMENT AND LEARNING ADJUSTMENTS FOR ELITE ATHLETES 
6.1 
The University is a member of the Elite Athlete Friendly Education Network and accordingly 
provides some study and assessment flexibility to a student who is an elite athlete, elite coach, 
or elite sporting official to enable them to succeed in their studies while meeting their sporting 
and training commitments. 
6.2 
To be eligible for assessment and learning adjustments as an elite athlete, a student must: 
6.2.1 
demonstrate to the satisfaction of the elite athlete program administrator that they 
are a state or national sporting representative or a coach or sporting official at that 
level, so that 
6.2.2 
they can be added to the University’s register of students who are elite athletes. 
6.3 
The elite athlete program administrator will publish on a web page the current criteria for a 
student to qualify as an elite athlete. 
6.4 
The elite athlete program administrator will advise the school that manages the student’s 
program that the student is registered with the University as an elite athlete. 
6.5 
Course coordinators will provide the student with adjustments to learning activities and/or 
assessment arrangements to enable the student to meet their elite sporting commitments, 
provided that: 
6.5.1 
the student provides satisfactory evidence of each elite sporting commitment, and 
6.5.2 
the commitments will not require the student to be absent from so much of the 
relevant course that the student will be unlikely to achieve the course learning 
outcomes. 
6.6 
The adjustments may take the form of: 
6.6.1 
an extension of time to submit assessment work 
6.6.2 
a deferred assessment task, and/or 
6.6.3 
an alternative assessment task that assesses the same learning outcomes. 
6.7 
Where the student needs a deferred or irregularly scheduled exam so they can meet a 
sporting commitment, they must apply for it in accordance with (as relevant) the section on 
deferred examinations or irregularly scheduled examinations below. 
6.8 
Where a student who is an elite athlete will be absent for so much of a teaching period that 
they are unlikely to achieve the course learning outcomes: 
6.8.1 
it may be unreasonable for the student to expect adjustments, and so 
6.8.2 
the student should, before the relevant census date, apply for leave of absence for 
the teaching period. 
7 
ASSESSMENT AND LEARNING ADJUSTMENTS TO ENABLE ABORIGINAL 
AND TORRES STRAIT ISLANDER STUDENTS TO MEET CULTURAL 
COMMITMENTS 
7.1 
The University will support students who are Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islanders to meet a 
cultural commitment where the timing of the commitment is outside the student’s control. 
7.2 
Such a student may reasonably request to be absent from study to meet cultural 
commitments for up to 10 days a year. 
