8 
EXTENSIONS OF TIME TO SUBMIT WORK 
8.1 
Staff who hold the following roles may approve a request from a student to have a short 
extension of time (up to seven calendar days) on the deadline to submit work for an 
assessment task (other than a final exam): a head of school, deputy head of school, program 
coordinator, discipline coordinator, campus student adviser, course coordinator and, in the 
School of Medicine, the National Head of Assessment. 
8.1.1 
The head of each school will decide which staff holding these positions in the school 
will decide extension requests from students in courses managed by the school. 
8.1.2 
In the case of a course coordinator, they may only approve an extension for a student 
enrolled in a course they coordinate. 
8.1.3 
A student must request such an extension in writing, and the decision-maker will 
inform the student of their decision in writing. 
8.1.4 
The decision-maker may require a student who requests such an extension to: 
8.1.4.1 
use the University’s form for requesting such extensions, and/or 
8.1.4.2 
provide supporting documentation of the circumstances that make the 
extension necessary.  
8.1.4.3 
Where, however, the student has a learning access plan that provides for 
them to receive extensions, the student should not be asked to provide 
other supporting documentation for an extension request. 
8.1.5 
The student must submit the request for the extension before the deadline to submit 
the work. 
8.1.6 
Where a student needs to request an extension after the deadline for submission of 
the assessment work, they must apply for special consideration. 
8.2 
A respect officer or a counsellor may confirm without disclosing any details that the student 
has experienced exceptional circumstances as set out in clause 4.2. In this case, their 
confirmation will be sufficient evidence to grant an extension to submit work. 
8.3 
Where a student needs an extension longer than seven calendar days on an assessment 
submission deadline, they must apply for special consideration. 
