9.8 
An application for special consideration submitted later than three working days after the 
assessment deadline or date of assessment will not normally be considered. 
9.8.1 
However, a head of school has discretion to consider a late application where the 
student provides satisfactory evidence of unusual circumstances that warrant 
compassion, and which explain their delay in applying, and the student is otherwise 
performing well in their studies. 
9.9 
A respect officer or counsellor may confirm without disclosing any details that the student has 
experienced exceptional circumstances as set out in clause 4.2. In this case, their confirmation 
will be sufficient evidence to grant special consideration. 
Cultural and religious commitments 
9.10 
Where a student applies for special consideration on the basis of a compulsory cultural or 
religious commitment, they must provide supporting evidence from the relevant community 
body including contact details of a representative of the body. 
9.11 
For adjustments to enable an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander student to meet a cultural 
commitment, see section 7. 
Special consideration outcomes 
9.12 
The decision-maker who has decided the outcome of a student’s special consideration 
application will email the student their decision and copy the email to Student Administration 
(student.admin@nd.edu.au), who will add the outcome to the student’s file. 
9.13 
Where the decision-maker grants a student special consideration, this may take the form of: 
9.13.1 an extension of time to submit an assessment task 
9.13.2 an offer of a rescheduled time to complete an assessment task 
9.13.3 a recommendation that the student be granted a deferred exam (see section 10) or 
an irregularly scheduled exam (see section 11) 
9.13.4 an opportunity to resubmit assessment work for which the student has received a 
failing mark or, where the assessment task is a hurdle assessment tasks, a mark below 
the minimum mark required to pass the course (only the head of school can grant 
this outcome) 
9.13.5 an alternative assessment task, or 
9.13.6 extra time to complete hours required for a work integrated learning activity such as 
a practical placement, clinical placement or internship. 
9.13.7 Clause 9.3 states the circumstances under which only the head of school can grant 
these outcomes. 
9.13.8 To maintain academic integrity, a student should not normally be granted an 
extension of time to submit work beyond the date when other students’ marked 
work for the same assessment task will be returned. Where this would be the case: 
9.13.8.1 preferably the student should be granted an alternative assessment task 
instead of an extension, but 
9.13.8.2 to avoid disadvantaging a student who is already experiencing adverse 
circumstances, it may be necessary in some cases to grant the extension. 
9.14 
Where a student is permitted to resubmit assessment work as a special consideration 
outcome, there is no restriction on the range of pass marks the student may receive. 
9.15 
A student may appeal the outcome of their special consideration application in accordance 
