10.8 
A counsellor or respect officer may confirm without disclosing any details that a student has 
experienced exceptional circumstances as set out in clause 4.2. In this case, their confirmation 
will be sufficient evidence to grant a deferred exam. 
10.9 
In deciding the outcome of a student’s application for a deferred exam, any recommendation 
from the school that manages the relevant course will be considered. 
10.10 The National Manager, Student Services and Graduations may deny an application for a 
deferred exam where: 
10.10.1 the evidence provided by the student does not substantiate exceptional 
circumstances 
10.10.2 there is a record of the student applying for deferred exams that is not supported by 
evidence of enduring exceptional circumstances 
10.10.3 the student’s results in other assessment tasks for the course mean that a fail grade 
is certain, or 
10.10.4 to do so would be contrary to University policy. 
10.11 Where the student is granted a deferred exam: 
10.11.1 the student must attend the deferred exam at the date, time and place notified to 
them, and 
10.11.2 if the student sits the deferred exam, they will be eligible to receive a grade from the 
normal range of grades for the course. 
10.12 Where a student is prevented, by exceptional circumstances (as defined in section 4), from 
sitting a deferred exam or an exam granted as a supplementary assessment, they will not 
normally be granted a further opportunity to sit the exam. 
10.12.1 To request a further opportunity to sit a deferred exam, the student must apply in 
writing to Student Administration (via student.admin@nd.edu.au) within three 
working days after the date on which the deferred exam or the supplementary exam 
was scheduled. 
10.12.2 The student may be granted a further opportunity to sit a deferred exam, based on 
written evidence of exceptional circumstances that warrant compassion. 
10.12.3 The date of the further deferred exam will be no later than six months after the date 
of the original final exam. 
10.13 A student may appeal the decision to deny them a deferred exam, or to deny them a further 
opportunity to sit a deferred exam, in accordance with the Policy: Student Appeals. 
11 
IRREGULARLY SCHEDULED EXAMINATIONS 
11.1 
An irregularly scheduled exam is a final exam that is held an earlier time than that scheduled 
for the final exam it replaces. 
11.2 
To be eligible to be considered for an irregularly scheduled exam, the student must either: 
11.2.1 be prevented from sitting the final exam by exceptional circumstances as defined in 
section 4 
11.2.2 be registered with the University as an elite athlete, and have an elite sporting or 
coaching commitment at the time of the exam, or 
11.2.3 be an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander and have a cultural commitment of which 
the timing is outside their control, at the time of the exam. 
11.2.4 Irregularly scheduled examinations will normally not be available as a replacement 
