for final exams in the Doctor of Medicine Program.  
11.3 
To apply for an irregularly scheduled exam, a student must: 
11.3.1 use the prescribed online application form following the instructions on the form, 
and 
11.3.2 submit their application within five working days of the release of the exam timetable 
to students.  
11.4 
Where the student needs the exam because of exceptional circumstances, they must: 
11.4.1 attach to their application independent documentary evidence of the circumstances 
that have caused them to need an irregularly scheduled exam (clause 10.7 states 
what constitutes this evidence for deferred or irregularly scheduled exams), or 
11.4.2 provide an explanation of the circumstances and why they cannot yet provide 
documentation of them. 
11.4.3 Where the student cannot provide the documentation with their application, they 
must supply it as soon as possible, and within five working days of the date of the 
release of the exam timetable (unless the National Manager, Student Services and 
Graduations permits an extension of time). 
11.5 
Where the student needs the irregularly scheduled exam because of an elite sporting or 
training commitment, they must attach to their application evidence that they are registered 
with the University as an elite athlete, and independent supporting documentation of the 
commitment. 
11.6 
Where the student is an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander student and needs the irregularly 
scheduled exam to meet a cultural commitment, they must attach to their application written 
confirmation of the commitment from an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander staff member or 
the relevant community organisation. 
11.7 
In deciding the outcome of a student’s application for an irregularly scheduled exam, the 
National Manager, Student Services and Graduations will consider any recommendation from 
the school that manages the relevant course. 
11.8 
The National Manager, Student Services and Graduations may, as an outcome of a student’s 
request for an irregularly scheduled exam: 
11.8.1 grant the student an irregularly scheduled exam 
11.8.2 grant the student a deferred exam instead, or 
11.8.3 deny the student an irregularly scheduled or a deferred exam. 
11.9 
Student Administration will manage and schedule irregularly scheduled exams. 
11.10 A student may appeal a decision to not grant an irregularly scheduled exam (including to not 
grant a deferred exam instead), in accordance with the Policy: Student Appeals. 
12 
SUPPLEMENTARY ASSESSMENT 
12.1 
A supplementary assessment is a further opportunity to demonstrate competency by 
undertaking a different assessment with the same learning outcomes. 
12.1.1 A student will only be considered for a supplementary assessment if they have 
attempted all assessment tasks for the course. 
12.1.2 A student may be granted a further opportunity to sit a final exam as a 
supplementary assessment task. 
