assessment across the programs they coordinate in accordance with the requirements set out 
in this section. 
3.3 
Assessment is constructively aligned and mapped to course learning outcomes and program 
learning outcomes, as well as the applicable qualification specifications in the Australian 
Qualifications Framework and disciplinary and/or professional standards. 
3.3.1 The connection between program learning outcomes, course learning outcomes, 
learning activities, the associated assessments and their criteria is clearly communicated 
to students, and to staff who teach in the program. 
3.3.1.1 Curriculum mapping is maintained for all programs to document the alignment 
of assessment tasks, course learning outcomes and program learning outcomes 
and confirm that all learning outcomes are taught and assessed. 
3.3.2 Course coordinators will ensure course outlines include an assessment rubric for each 
assessment task that:  
3.3.2.1 clearly communicates the requirements, criteria and expected standards of 
performance for assessment work, and  
3.3.2.2 aligns assessment criteria to the course learning outcome/s being assessed, 
and 
3.3.2.3 may state discipline-specific and/or task-specific versions of each expected 
level of achievement. 
3.3.2.4 This requirement does not apply to tests with multiple-choice questions, 
extended matching questions or short answer questions. 
3.3.2.5 The Procedure: Program and Course Information specifies the assessment 
information that must be included in course outlines. 
3.3.3 Diverse assessment tasks are scaffolded, strategically integrated and triangulated across 
the program curriculum to ensure any limitations of a single method or mode of 
assessment, in relation to accessibility and assurance of learning, are offset by the 
strengths of other methods and types  
3.3.3.1 Each course incorporates a minimum of three different types or methods of 
assessment (For example, method-written / type-essay, report, case study, 
creative writing, script, case notes, business plan, reflection.) 
3.3.3.2 Exceptions to clause 3.3.3.1 are permitted where the course learning outcomes 
include inherent requirements for accreditation to practice. 
3.3.4 Assessment design reflects a logical and progressive increase in challenge, complexity, 
depth and autonomy of learning and acquisition of knowledge and capabilities as 
students move through each level of their program of study.  
3.3.4.1 Assessment tasks are well scaffolded across courses and programs so that 
learners progressively develop new knowledge and skills in manageable 
sequences. 
3.3.4.2 Assessments within courses have meaningful relationships to each other, and 
to assessment in related courses. Wherever possible each assessment task 
should contribute to or inform the subsequent summative task. 
3.3.4.3 Mandatory or hurdle assessment tasks are strategically embedded to assess 
the attainment of inherent requirements, or knowledge and capabilities 
required for progression to higher levels of the program.  
3.3.4.4 Assessment in elective courses within a program, provides opportunity for 
disciplinary depth, breadth or higher levels of mastery. 
3.4 
Assessment and feedback are designed to support student understanding and development of 
