integrity, ethical practices and the awareness and skills required for upholding academic 
integrity. 
3.4.1 Assessment requirements clearly set out expectations and conditions regarding 
academic integrity and ethical scholarship to students. This includes clear statements 
regarding: 
3.4.1.1 own work, individual or group work 
3.4.1.2 legitimate cooperation 
3.4.1.3 referencing 
3.4.1.4 authorised and unauthorised use of assistive technologies (e.g. text editing 
tools, generative artificial intelligence, calculators, phones, websites) 
3.4.1.5 authorised materials (in examinations) 
3.4.1.6 (in early-level courses) exemplars of expected outcomes, and 
3.4.1.7 available support and resources, within the course, and the broader University. 
3.5 
Assessment integrity for programs and courses is maintained through an assurance of learning 
process which includes:  
3.5.1 constructive alignment of assessment to program and course learning outcomes  
3.5.2 triangulation of assessment methods 
3.5.3 mandatory or hurdle assessment tasks 
3.5.4 ensuring assessment methods and types are effective in confirming student levels of 
achievement of learning outcomes 
3.5.5 moderation to ensure consistency of marking judgements across markers and student 
cohorts and confirm that grades awarded accurately reflect levels of student 
attainment, and 
3.5.6 ensuring sufficient coverage of the course and program learning outcomes in secure or 
invigilated assessments. 
3.6 
Assessment is designed as a developmental activity which, alongside feedback, and timely 
support, helps students transition to university study, progress through their studies and 
transition to employment or further studies and is designed to: 
3.6.1 frame how students learn and what they achieve by actively engaging them in 
productive, purposeful and authentic learning 
3.6.2 support students to connect previous life and learning experiences to the discipline area 
3.6.3 specifically consider equity groups and other cohorts whose transition to university may 
represent greater challenges 
3.6.4 provide an orientation to university assessment methods and standards, and scaffold 
the development of knowledge, skills and dispositions required to complete assessment 
tasks as an integral part of the assessment process 
3.6.5 enable students to progressively and realistically build their capabilities including 
making independent judgements about the quality of their work and the work of others 
3.6.6 support students to understand assessment task requirements through engagement 
with assessment criteria and rubrics, exemplars demonstrating expectations and 
performance levels, and guidance about time management, and 
3.6.7 include early and formative assessments to provide mechanisms to identify students at 
risk and proactively provide additional support. 
3.6.8 All first level undergraduate courses and foundational postgraduate courses must 
include a formative or low-stakes task that is not onerous and enables students to 
receive meaningful feedback before census date.  
3.7 
Assessment is designed to have authenticity, simulating or representing contemporary 
