practices and challenges in the discipline, creating purpose and engaging students in 
meaningful ways with disciplines, professions and interdisciplinary perspectives.  
3.7.1 Authenticity in assessment tasks should have the effect to elevate assessment as 
learning. 
3.7.2 Assessment reflects current developments in the discipline and is based on clearly 
established standards and contemporary and emergent practices. 
3.7.3 Assessment types and weightings are aligned with course and program learning 
outcomes, the University’s Graduate Attributes, qualification specifications in the 
Australian Qualifications Framework, and disciplinary standards. 
3.7.4 Assessment methods in all levels should enable active and collaborative learning and 
assessment practices. 
3.8 
Assessment requirements, both within a course and across a program, must be achievable with 
reasonable effort and sustainable for students and staff  
3.8.1 The timing of assessment tasks allows reasonable time for task completion, marking and 
providing feedback.  
3.8.1.1 Due dates are coordinated across each level of study to minimise concentration 
of student and staff workload.  
3.8.1.2 Particular attention is paid to assessment patterns in first level courses to 
ensure appropriateness of assessment type, method, mode and challenge, and 
staggering of due dates to enable students to better manage their time. 
3.8.2 The number and weighting of assessment tasks in courses is commensurate with the 
course volume of learning.  
3.8.3 Courses up to 50 units of credit will normally meet the following requirements: 
3.8.3.1 a minimum of one authentic task per course (Ideally all tasks should include 
authenticity.) 
3.8.3.2 a minimum of three, and maximum of four assessments per course 
3.8.3.3 a minimum of 10 percent weighting for any assessment task, and 
3.8.3.4 a maximum 50 percent weighting for any assessment task including 
examinations. 
3.8.3.5 For courses with higher credit point values, and / or longer duration, there may 
be proportional variation to the requirements above.  
3.8.4 Variations to clause 3.8.3 may be approved where: 
3.8.4.1 the task is a requirement for professional accreditation of the program, or 
3.8.4.2 there is a clear rationale that does not conflict with policy objectives. 
3.8.5 Assessment tasks should only be broken into a series of sub-tasks if they are: 
3.8.5.1 complex tasks that involve developmental progression through stages that 
provide students with milestones and opportunities for feedback, or 
3.8.5.2 regular quizzes that provide feedback to students at meaningful stages of the 
course, for example, students’ competency in relation to a module or topic(s). 
3.8.6 Quizzes should be weighted at maximum of 10 percent for a single quiz style assessment 
and up to 20 percent weighting for a series of quizzes.  
3.8.6.1  Quizzes should be limited to earlier levels of a program and represent 
authentic engagement and interactive responses. 
3.9 
Assessment is designed to be inclusive, flexible, and accessible so all students have 
opportunities to succeed regardless of their backgrounds or learning preferences. 
3.9.1 Wherever practical, potential barriers to access or participation in assessment are 
removed or reduced to minimise the need for students to seek adjustments, including: 
