3.9.1.1 through application of Universal Design for Learning principles to provide 
learners alternative ways of acquiring information and knowledge, and 
demonstrating what they know  
3.9.1.2 providing learning resources in accessible formats, and 
3.9.1.3 ensuring accessibility in digital assessment environments for people with 
disabilities through application of the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines. 
3.9.2 The Procedure: Assessment and Learning Flexibility states requirements for: 
3.9.2.1 reasonable adjustments to assessment arrangements for students with a 
disability or long-term physical or mental health condition 
3.9.2.2 adjustments to assessment arrangements for students who are elite athletes 
and who need to meet sporting and training commitments 
3.9.2.3 adjustments to assessment arrangements for Aboriginal or Torres Strait 
Islander students who need to meet cultural commitments 
3.9.2.4 adjustments to assessment arrangements (extensions, special consideration, 
deferred exams, irregularly scheduled exams) for students in general who are 
experiencing exceptional circumstances, 
3.9.2.5 granting a student a supplementary assessment task or 
3.9.2.6 granting a student a conceded pass. 
3.10 Curriculum and Quality will work with associate deans, learning and teaching, faculty academic 
integrity officers and program coordinators (or their equivalent) to coordinate and facilitate 
assessment design and review processes in faculties and schools, including: 
3.10.1 identifying risk and developing strategies for assessment effectiveness to uphold 
academic integrity 
3.10.2 monitoring assessment methods in relation to vulnerability and risk 
3.10.3 programmatic assessment review processes, and 
3.10.4 supporting scholarship in assessment design to assure academic integrity. 
3.11 Innovation and Design will provide guidance, training and other support for staff in schools, 
faculties and professional support areas, including for: 
3.11.1 technology enabled assessment (best practice, new and emerging learning technologies 
and tools) 
3.11.2 application of the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines, and 
3.11.3 provision of assessment requirements and support information for students and 
teaching staff in the University’s Learning Management System (LMS). 
4 
CONSISTENCY OF ASSESSMENT TASKS ACROSS OFFERINGS OF A 
COURSE 
4.1 
Assessment tasks will normally be the same across all offerings of a course in the same teaching 
period. 
4.1.1 Where it is necessary for a course to have different assessment tasks in different 
offerings, for example intensive mode versus a full semester, on campus versus online 
delivery, or for state-based accreditation, the assessment tasks will: 
4.1.1.1 have the same level of complexity and challenge, addressing the same course 
learning outcomes 
4.1.1.2 involve similar learning experiences 
4.1.1.3 have the same weighting towards the overall course mark, and 
4.1.1.4 assess the same course learning outcomes within any teaching period 
