(semester or full year). 
5 
FEEDBACK ON ASSESSMENT 
5.1 
Program coordinators (or their equivalent) will ensure the assessment design for the program 
they coordinate provides multiple planned opportunities for meaningful feedback, including 
feed forward and early feedback, to ensure students can effectively apply it to future learning 
and assessment tasks and staff can proactively identify and support students at risk. 
5.1.1 Students are made aware of the forms of feedback available to them in each course, 
when these will be made available and when and how to best act on them. 
5.2 
Course coordinators will collaborate with other staff who teach in courses to ensure students 
receive useful, timely and consistent feedback on their assessment work. 
5.2.1 Feedback to students on their work for each assessment task (other than a final exam 
or other assessment task scheduled at the end of the teaching period) will enable them 
to understand: 
5.2.1.1 their current level of performance against the assessment criteria and in 
relation to the course learning outcomes to which the task is aligned, and 
5.2.1.2 how to improve their performance in future assessment tasks. 
5.2.2 Assessment feedback should enable students to develop evaluative judgement, using 
methods such as: 
5.2.2.1 exemplars, model answers and/or explanations of what answers were 
expected 
5.2.2.2 discussion of assessment tasks 
5.2.2.3 constructive comments on students’ presentations 
5.2.2.4 written feedback on drafts 
5.2.2.5 activities in which students self-assess or reflect on their learning 
5.2.2.6 practice quizzes 
5.2.2.7 facilitated peer feedback on a draft or on the work submitted. 
5.2.3 Staff will provide feedback on students’ assessment work in time for students to 
improve their performance in later assessment tasks. 
5.2.3.1 Staff will normally mark and return students’ assessment work within: 
• three weeks following the date by which students had to submit work for, 
perform or sit the assessment task, or 
• where the deadline or sitting date is near the end of the teaching period, in 
time for final grades to be provided for approval by the relevant board of 
examiners. 
5.2.3.2 However, where students need feedback on an assessment task so they can 
improve their performance in a later assessment task, the feedback on the first 
task will normally be provided by, at latest, one week before the deadline for 
submission of, or date of sitting, the second task. 
5.2.3.3 Where a student is granted an extension of time to submit assessment work, 
there will be an equivalent extension of time for the marker to return the 
marked work to the student. 
6 
GROUP ASSESSMENT TASKS, PEER MARKING AND SELF-MARKING 
6.1 
Assessment is designed to provide opportunity for student agency, choice and peer to peer 
