engagement and group or team-based assessments form an integral part of assessment 
regimes to provide opportunities for students to collaborate with and learn from each other. 
6.2 
The proportion of groupwork assessment in a program should be representative of the nature 
of work in the discipline or field.  
6.2.1 Assessment tasks requiring group work will be designed to include activities to support 
team dynamics and ensure that: 
6.2.1.1 the contribution of each individual students in the group member can be able 
be identified  
6.2.1.2 students in the group are each assigned a fair share of the workload, and 
6.2.1.3 there is a process to resolve issues in the group such as under-performing 
members. 
6.2.2 Group marks, peer marks and/or self-marks can in total contribute no more than 30 
percent of the overall course mark. 
6.2.2.1 Peer marks and/or self-marks can in total contribute no more than 20 percent 
of the overall course mark, and no more than 10 percent each. 
6.2.2.2 Accordingly, at least 70 percent of students’ overall course mark will be an 
individual mark decided by teaching staff. 
6.2.3 Exceptions to clause 6.2.2 may be approved by the Faculty Learning and Teaching 
Committee (or an equivalent committee), for example for courses with more practical 
or project-based assessments, provided group work marks in the compulsory courses 
across the program in total does not exceed 30 percent of overall course marks. 
7 
DESIGN OF EXAMINATIONS AND TESTS 
7.1 
Examinations and tests, should, as far as possible, be based on authentic assessment principles 
and require students to demonstrate higher-order thinking and the integration of knowledge 
and skills. 
7.2 
The design of examination or test papers should ensure: 
7.2.1 the duration of the exam is consistent with percent weighting or contribution to the 
final mark for the course and the credit value of the course 
7.2.2 the proportion of questions that are common versus distinct for each examination or 
test provide adequate similarities for comparative purposes and adequate 
differentiation to minimise the risk of academic misconduct 
7.2.3 the number of unique versions of each exam paper, which should support multiple 
sittings in each given study period, minimises the opportunity for unfair advantage and 
include distinct versions for each scheduled sitting, and for supplementary and deferred 
exams 
7.2.4 exam or test questions are kept secure until the commencement of the exam or test, 
and 
7.2.5 the rotation period for exam and test questions appropriate for the duration of the 
program and allows papers to be refreshed each teaching period. 
7.3 
The design of exams and tests should be moderated to ensure:  
7.3.1 they are appropriately aligned with the course content, learning activities and learning 
outcomes 
7.3.2  are fair and provide students with the opportunity to demonstrate the extent of their 
learning 
7.3.3 the questions, content and instructions are written clearly and as far as practicable, in 
