10.1 Students enrolled in a course must, as relevant, submit, perform or complete each assessment 
task detailed in the course outline, to pass the course. 
10.2 Assessment of participation 
10.2.1 Participation in a course cannot be an assessable task on its own. 
10.2.1.1 However, participation can be assessed as an aspect of an assessment task, 
provided that the marking rubric for the assessment task details criteria for 
assessing standards of participation in the task. 
10.3 Hurdle assessment tasks 
10.3.1 A course may include a hurdle assessment task that requires a specified level of 
performance. Students enrolled in the course must pass, or achieve a specified mark 
higher than a pass, to continue to the next assessment activity or to pass the course. 
10.3.2 A hurdle assessment task may be used: 
10.3.2.1 to meet a specific requirement of professional accreditation of the program 
and/or professional registration of graduates of the program 
10.3.2.2 to ensure students will be able to undertake a subsequent learning activity 
safely, or 
10.3.2.3 for another reason, with the approval of the Faculty Learning and Teaching 
Committee (or an equivalent committee). 
10.3.3 Students can be required to attend a proportion of classes for a course as a hurdle 
requirement, but such a requirement cannot contribute to the overall course mark. 
10.3.4 A student will receive a fail due to non-completion (FN) grade even if their overall mark 
for the course is 50 percent or greater, if they do not (as relevant): 
10.3.4.1 attempt an assessment task for the course 
10.3.4.2 meet an attendance hurdle requirement of the course 
10.3.4.3 pass a hurdle assessment task of the course that requires a pass 
10.3.4.4 gain at least the required minimum mark in a hurdle assessment task of the 
course that specifies a minimum required mark higher than a pass, and/or 
10.3.4.5 achieve a minimum level of results, in a subset or subsets of assessment tasks 
for the course, that is required to pass the course. 
11 SUBMISSION OF ASSESSMENT WORK 
11.1 Due dates and submission of assessment 
11.1.1 Due dates for assessment tasks should fall within the teaching period (which includes 
the exam period). 
11.1.2 Where an assessment task requires students to submit written or digitally recorded 
work, submission will be online via the LMS; no other hard copy or email submission will 
be required. 
11.1.2.1 The Faculty Learning and Teaching Committee (or an equivalent committee) 
may approve an exception to this requirement. 
11.1.3 When a student submits assessment work they will provide a declaration that the work 
is their own, either: 
11.1.3.1 by the process for recording such a declaration as part of the standard online 
assessment work submission process, or 
11.1.3.2 where the assessment work is to be submitted in hard copy or by email, by 
completing the University assignment cover sheet and including this in the 
submission. 
