13.6.9 An exam invigilator’s allegation of a breach of academic integrity or misconduct by a 
student in an exam will be handled in accordance with the Policy: Academic Integrity or, 
where applicable, the student misconduct section of the General Regulations. 
13.6.10 Students are not permitted to use, during a final exam, materials such as a dictionary, 
textbook, notes they have prepared, calculator or mobile electronic device, except 
where: 
13.6.10.1 a school or an individual course coordinator permits students to use materials 
during a final exam as documented in the course outline.  
13.6.10.2 In these situations, the course outline and the examinations timetable will 
specify the materials students can use in the exam. 
13.6.11 A student experiences a technical issue that prevents them from completing online 
exam may apply for a deferred exam in accordance with the Procedure: Assessment and 
Learning Flexibility. 
13.6.11.1 In such a circumstance, the application for a deferred exam must include 
supporting documentation in the form of screenshots or photos of the 
problems encountered while trying to complete the exam. 
14 MARKING AND GRADING 
14.1 Assessment of student learning is based on judgements made against predetermined 
assessment criteria and performance standards that are constructively aligned to course 
learning outcomes. 
14.1.1 Assessment is criterion-referenced and standards-based rather than norm-referenced. 
Accordingly, there is no requirement for final grades or results to reflect a prescribed 
distribution. 
14.1.2 A mark or grade is awarded to students for all summative assessment. A mark is a 
numerical indicator, and a grade is a descriptive indicator, of a student’s achievement 
in an assessment task 
14.2 Course coordinators will work collaboratively with other staff teaching and assessing in the 
course to ensure:  
14.2.1 they clearly understand the nature of scaffolding and feedback required for each 
assessment task  
14.2.2 have the capability to provide meaningful and timely feedback, and  
14.2.3 to reach a calibrated understanding of the assessment tasks and marking rubrics. 
14.3 Program coordinators (or their equivalent) will monitor moderation of marking for courses in 
the programs they coordinate. 
14.3.1 The marking of all assessment tasks, including examinations, must be moderated to 
ensure consistency of marking judgements are consistent across markers and cohorts. 
14.4 Course coordinators will ensure that, in a course they coordinate, the following moderation 
activities occur before the work is returned to students or marks are released to them. 
14.4.1 Before marking commences markers will meet to discuss examples of student work of 
different standards, to agree on how these should be marked. 
14.4.2 While marking students’ work for the task, each marker will compare their marking of 
earlier and later pieces of work to ensure they are applying the assessment criteria 
