14.4.3 Where a course is offered on more than one campus in the same teaching period, 
moderation of marking will include cross-campus moderation. 
14.4.4 Where the mark for a student’s assessment work is in the Fail range of 45 to 49 percent 
or the borderline High Distinction range of 78 to 83 percent it must be double marked 
in any of the following circumstances. 
14.4.4.1 The course has a credit value up to 50 units of credit and is either an enabling 
course, a level 1000 course, or a level 5000 course and the assessment task is 
weighted at 20 percent or higher. 
14.4.4.2 The course has a credit value up to 50 units of credit and is, either an 
undergraduate course at level 2000 or above, or a postgraduate course at 
level 6000 or above, and the assessment task is weighted at 40 percent or 
higher 
14.4.4.3 For the first iteration of new assessment methods and types within a course.  
14.4.4.4 The course is being offered for the first time. 
14.4.4.5 The assessment has been marked by a staff member who is teaching the 
course for the first time. 
14.4.5 Clause 14.4.4 sets out minimum requirements for double marking of assessment work. 
14.4.5.1 Schools may elect to double mark all student work with a Fail and High 
Distinction mark or assign a lower assessment task weighting as the threshold 
for double marking.  
14.4.5.2 For courses with a credit value higher than 50 units of credit, the threshold 
assessment task weighting may be proportionately adjusted. 
14.4.6 Where there is more than one marker, before the work and marks are released to 
students compare the markers’ distribution of marks, and if their distributions are 
significantly different, reassess the work and adjust marks to achieve consistency. 
14.4.7 Where students’ assessment work, such as a reflective journal, may include confidential 
personal information, the school will ensure that the assessment task instructions 
explain that another staff member than the marker may view the information for 
purposes of assessment moderation, but will keep the information in confidence. 
14.4.7.1 In these cases, the school should consider de-identifying the samples of work 
and assessment work in certain mark ranges for marking moderation. 
14.4.8 Markers must not scale marks to achieve a normal distribution: this is not appropriate 
in the University’s criterion-referenced assessment framework. 
14.5 Where a program or course accredited by the University is delivered via a third-party 
arrangement with another provider, the moderation of marking will be completed by the 
course coordinator under the supervision of the program coordinator (or their equivalent).   
14.6 Section 11 in the Procedure: Program and Course Quality describes the role of boards of 
examiners in monitoring grade distributions and requiring explanations of unusual grade 
distributions. 
