15.1.2 the marker should give this discussion a high priority. 
15.1.3 Where, however, the marker has not held the discussion with the student, and the 
deadline for a written request is approaching, the student should consider submitting a 
written request for remarking. 
15.2 A student may submit a written request for re-marking of their work for an assessment task on 
the following grounds: 
15.2.1 there has been a breach of a regulation, policy or procedure that has disadvantaged the 
student in undertaking the assessment task or in the marking of their work, or 
15.2.2 the course teaching team has not followed the process for the assessment task and/or 
has not applied the marking criteria stated in the course outline. 
15.3 The student must submit the request for the re-marking in writing to the to the decision-maker 
nominated by the head of school, providing evidence to substantiate one of the grounds in 
clause 15.2, no later than 10 working days after the return of the marked assessment work. 
15.3.1 A head of school may nominate one or more of the following decision-makers to decide 
whether to grant students’ request for re-marking: the deputy head of school, program 
coordinators, course coordinators and in the School of Medicine, the Head of Clinical 
and Head of Preclinical. 
15.4 On receiving the student’s request, the decision-maker will review the student’s assessment 
work and, within five working days, advise the student in writing of the decision to allow or not 
allow the re-marking. 
15.5 If the decision-maker allows the re-marking, to ensure it is objective, they will: 
15.5.1 wherever practicable, assign the work for re-marking to a different marker from the 
staff member who marked it originally, or 
15.5.2 where the marking requires specialist knowledge that only the original marker 
possesses, the relevant program coordinator (or their equivalent) will:  
15.5.2.1 choose a co-marker to collaborate with the original marker in remarking, and 
15.5.2.2 decide the mark resulting from the re-marking on the advice of both markers. 
15.6 The new mark resulting from remarking may be the same as, higher than or lower than the 
original mark. 
15.7 If the decision-maker does not allow the re-marking, the student may appeal the decision in 
accordance with the Policy: Student Appeals. 
16 CONFIDENTIALITY OF ASSESSMENT WORK, FEEDBACK, MARKS AND 
GRADES 
16.1 Marks, grades, assessment feedback and students’ assessment work must be kept in 
confidence by staff who mark students’ work, support learning and teaching or approve 
students’ results. 
16.2 A student’s assessment work, marks and grades can only be shared with or disclosed to another 
person where: 
16.2.1.1 the person needs to know the mark or grade for the purposes of managing the 
student’s request for assessment flexibility, marking in the course, quality 
assurance of marking or approval of grades, or 
16.2.1.2 the student authorises the disclosure. 
16.2.1.3 However, a program coordinator may share de-identified examples of 
assessment work with staff of another similar program, at this or another 
tertiary education institution, for the purpose of benchmarking assessment 
