24.1 This section applies to courses in bachelor (honours) programs and masters by coursework 
programs that include a supervised research component requiring students to submit a thesis 
or dissertation for examination. 
24.1.1 This section does not apply to courses with a research component that includes learning 
activities that are supervised and assessed entirely by the course coordinator and/or 
other staff teaching in the course. 
24.1.2 The Procedure: Higher Degrees by Research Candidature states requirements for thesis 
examination in higher degree by research programs. 
24.2 For each student enrolled in a course with a supervised research component, the head of the 
school that manages the program, in consultation with (as relevant) the honours coordinator 
or course coordinator: 
24.2.1 will appoint a principal supervisor to supervise the student’s work on their dissertation 
or project, and 
24.2.2 may appoint an associate supervisor for the same purpose. 
24.3 A student will conduct their research for a thesis or dissertation in accordance with the 
University’s policies and procedures on the responsible conduct of research. 
24.3.1 The student’s supervisor(s) will advise the student and monitor their research to ensure 
this. 
24.3.2 The student will undertake the University’s online training module in research ethics for 
higher degree by research students. 
24.4 A student’s research thesis or dissertation will be normally have at least two readers. 
24.4.1 The school that manages the bachelor (honours) program or masters by coursework 
program will provide the readers with the criteria for examination. 
24.4.2 At least one of the readers (an examiner) will produce a formal examination report to 
the head of the school that manages the course. 
24.4.3 The other reader will be an assessor of the paper and will confer with the examiner on 
the production of the final report. 
24.4.4 Neither the principal supervisor nor any co-supervisor or associate supervisor will 
normally be appointed as examiner. 
24.4.5 A supervisor may be an assessor. 
24.4.6 A school may pay an external examiner an honorarium. 
24.5 The student’s mark will be the average of the reader’s recommended percentage marks, unless 
these vary by more than 10 percent. 
24.5.1 Where the readers’ recommended marks vary by more than 10 percent: 
24.5.1.1 the head of the school that manages the program will appoint a third reader, 
and 
24.5.1.2 the student’s mark will be the average of the two readers out of the three 
whose marks are closest to one another. 
24.6 A student may ask the executive dean of the faculty that manages the program in which the 
student is enrolled, for permission to resubmit their thesis or dissertation, on condition that 
the student provides evidence that a reader: 
24.6.1  substantially misinterpreted the work, or 
24.6.2 was prejudiced or biased in their assessment. 
24.6.3 If the executive dean approves the student’s request, they will appoint a new reader to 
examine the work. 
