25.1 Quality assurance of assessment practices  
25.1.1 Quality assurance of assessment practices is embedded in the University’s processes for 
program and course design, approval and review in accordance with the principles in 
the Policy: Assessment in Higher Education and Enabling Coursework Programs and the 
requirements in this Procedure. 
25.1.1.1 Schools will follow a team-based approach to assessment design and review to 
ensure whole-of-program oversight and integration that provides a coherent 
experience for students. 
25.1.1.2 Assessment modes, methods and types are modified to address known and 
emerging risks to academic integrity, including in relation to the use of assistive 
technologies. 
25.1.1.3 Assessments are refreshed and renewed for each study period to ensure 
currency and improve assessment security by making assessment distinctive, 
differentiated and varied for each study period. 
25.1.1.4 Academic peer review of assessment and rubrics at the end of each study 
period and forms part of regular refresh cycles, comprehensive program review 
and curriculum development processes.  
25.1.1.5 Internal and external benchmarking occurs across programs as part of 
continuous improvement and program review cycles. 
25.2 Faculty level quality assurance of assessment 
25.2.1 Each faculty will have a documented assessment quality assurance process, approved 
by the University Academic Standards and Quality Committee, that specifies 
requirements for: 
25.2.1.1 assuring the quality of assessment design in programs, including benchmarking 
and /or external referencing 
25.2.1.2 reviewing course assessment modes, methods, types, weighting, and student 
performance / grades, and 
25.2.1.3 moderation of marking, including documenting and reporting on the methods 
used. 
25.2.1.4 Each faculty will provide an annual report on the outcomes of their assessment 
quality assurance process to the University Learning and Teaching Committee. 
25.3 The Procedure: Program and Course Quality states further requirements for quality assurance 
of assessment, including: 
25.3.1 peer review and benchmarking of assessment design at program level against discipline 
and/or professional standards 
25.3.2 peer review and benchmarking of the design and marking of assessment tasks for 
courses at all levels from introductory to advanced 
25.3.3 board of examiners review of assessment, and 
25.3.4 course level review of assessment at the end of each teaching period by the program 
coordinator (or their equivalent), course coordinator and other staff who teach in a 
course. 
25.4 Approval of assessment in programs and courses 
25.4.1 The Procedure: Program and Course Life Cycle states the University’s requirements for 
approving: 
25.4.1.1 assessment for new programs and courses 
25.4.1.2 changes to assessment requirements for course, and 
25.4.1.3 changes to assessment tasks in course outlines, in exceptional circumstances, 
