25.4.2 Further to clause 25.4.1, proposals for new courses or for changes to assessment for 
existing courses must be accompanied by the following information: 
25.4.2.1 assessment criteria and their alignment to course learning outcomes, program 
learning outcomes, the University’s Graduate Attributes, and their relationship 
to the relevant qualification specifications in the Australian Qualifications 
Framework 
25.4.2.2 relationship to other assessment tasks in the course, the level, and the program 
25.4.2.3 indicative weighting, and the mode, method or type of assessment, and 
25.4.2.4 whether the assessment: 
• is a mandatory or hurdle assessment 
• has associated mandatory requirements 
• offers a mastery path, or  
• forms part of a work integrated learning or community service placement. 
25.4.3 Where a program or program component is subject to professional accreditation 
requirements that are associated with assessment, proposed changes to assessment 
with potential implications for accreditation must be discussed with the professional 
body and confirmed or, where required, approved in writing.  
25.4.3.1 This confirmation must be included with any proposed assessment changes 
submitted as part of the University’s course approval process.  
25.5 Changes to assessment through the Quality Assurance of Course Outlines process  
25.5.1 The following changes to assessment tasks can be approved, subject to meeting the 
requirements of this Procedure, in advance of each study period through the Quality 
Assurance of Course Outlines process:  
25.5.1.1 variations to the distribution of weighting of assessment tasks in a course up 
to 10 percent for each task 
25.5.1.2 revisions to the wording of the criteria in an assessment rubric that does not 
substantively change the criteria or what is being assessed 
25.5.1.3 a change of assessment type within the same assessment method 
25.5.1.4 changes to an assessment task, type or method to address student feedback, 
or to include options for student choice, and 
25.5.1.5 changes to an assessment task, type or method to address an identified risk 
to academic standards or integrity, or a risk to students. 
25.5.2 Each faculty will monitor and record changes to assessment made through Quality 
Assurance of Course Outlines process and report to the University Learning and 
Teaching Committee each semester. 
25.5.3 Major changes to assessment should be considered programmatically and occur as part 
of Comprehensive Program Reviews or strategic initiatives of the University. 
25.6 Comprehensive program reviews 
25.6.1 Assessment design is to be reviewed programmatically as part of the self-assessment 
and program improvement plan within the comprehensive program review cycle. 
25.6.2 Comprehensive program review should consider assessment in relation to this 
Procedure and give particular focus to integrity, authenticity appropriate challenge, and 
assurance of learning.  
25.6.3 The Procedure: Program and Course Quality states the University’s requirements for 
comprehensive program reviews. 
