in the Procedure: Assessment and Examinations 
3.4.3.6 ensure all staff teaching or supervising students in their course are aware of 
the requirements of this procedure, and 
3.4.3.7 ensure they are accessible to students who are seeking support or advice 
about maintaining academic integrity 
3.4.4 All students are expected to: 
3.4.4.1 Complete the online academic integrity training modules for students 
3.4.4.2 ensure they understand how to comply with the University’s expectations for 
academic integrity 
3.4.4.3 ensure they only submit original work for assessment 
3.4.4.4 use correct citation practices and follow specified referencing requirements 
3.4.4.5 take steps to ensure other students don’t copy their work, and 
3.4.4.6 seek assistance either from the course coordinator or other available academic 
support. 
4 
CLASSIFYING BREACHES OF ACADEMIC INTEGRITY BY STUDENTS 
4.1 
Breaches of academic integrity by students are classified in four levels based on the type and 
severity of the breach, to guide staff in considering: 
4.1.1 when they are carrying out the initial investigation of a suspected breach, and whether 
it can be handled by the course coordinator or needs to be handled as an allegation of 
student misconduct, or 
4.1.2 when they are deciding the penalty for a breach of academic integrity in accordance 
with the student misconduct section of the General Regulations, what penalty is 
appropriate. 
4.2 
Level one breach 
4.2.1 A level one breach of academic integrity: 
4.2.1.1 does not involve cheating or fraudulent behaviour, and 
4.2.1.2 is poor academic practice and results from inexperience or a lack of knowledge 
of academic integrity principles and their application. 
4.2.2 A level one breach is not considered student misconduct and is handled by the course 
coordinator. 
4.2.3 Level one breaches are documented in accordance with Section 7 of this procedure. 
4.3 
Level two breach 
4.3.1 A level two breach of academic integrity: 
4.3.1.1 does not involve cheating or fraudulent behaviour and 
4.3.1.2 does not have the effect that a substantial or central part of an assessment task 
designed to assess program learning outcome/s lacks integrity; but 
