academic integrity principles and their application. 
4.3.2 Repeated level one breaches will be classified as a level two breach. 
4.4 
Level three breach 
4.4.1 A level three breach of academic integrity may involve any of the following: 
4.4.1.1 any form of cheating, including contract cheating 
4.4.1.2 fabrication or falsification of data or other information, or 
4.4.1.3 an intentional breach which has the effect that a substantial or central 
portion of an assessment task designed to assess program learning outcome/s 
lacks integrity; but which 
4.4.1.4 is unlikely to have included committing an offence that might lead to legal 
proceedings. 
4.4.2 Repeated level three breaches will be classified as a level four breach. 
4.5 
Level four breach 
4.5.1 A level four breach of academic integrity may involve any of the following: 
4.5.1.1 is likely to have included committing an offence that might lead to legal 
proceedings 
4.5.1.2 providing a false document or misrepresenting information, or 
4.5.1.3 involves wilfully concealing or facilitating a breach of academic integrity by 
others. 
4.5.2 Any breach of academic integrity, where the student has previously been suspended 
for a breach of academic integrity, is classified as a level four breach. 
4.6 
Appendix 1 provides further guidance for classifying the level of severity of an academic 
integrity breach and for determining an appropriate response, including the penalty for a 
breach within the disciplinary powers of the head of school, school disciplinary committee or 
University disciplinary committee. (in accordance with section 8 of the General Regulations). 
5 
INVESTIGATING A SUSPECTED BREACH OF ACADEMIC INTEGRITY BY A 
STUDENT 
5.1 
If a staff member or student believes a student has breached academic integrity, they will 
notify the course coordinator (if they are not themselves the course coordinator). 
5.1.1 Suspected breaches of academic integrity by students will be investigated in a way 
that ensures: 
5.1.1.1 the student is provided enough details of the allegation to enable them to 
understand and properly consider it 
5.1.1.2 the student has an opportunity to respond to the allegation, either verbally or 
in writing, and is allowed enough time to do so 
5.1.1.3 the confidentiality of all parties is respected, and 
5.1.1.4 a decision is made promptly and without bias. 
5.1.2 Investigations will take into consideration any evidence relevant to the allegation, such 
as: 
5.1.2.1 notes about sources of plagiarised content (whether identified by text 
matching software or by other searches) 
5.1.2.2 a text-matching software report containing the student’s similarity score and 
highlighted passages of text 
