RELATED DOCUMENTS  
9.1 
The Learning and Teaching Resources Catalogue provides resources to guide staff on how to: 
9.1.1 ensure students understand academic integrity and acquire the skills they need to avoid 
breaching it 
9.1.2 design course assessment to ensure academic integrity 
9.1.3 themselves model ethical behaviour 
9.1.4 identify contract cheating 
9.1.5 interpret Turnitin similarity reports, and 
9.1.6 set up Turnitin so students can submit drafts of assessment work and receive similarity 
reports on them, to help them improve their referencing practice. 
9.2 
The University of Notre Dame Australia Style and Referencing Guide. 
10 DEFINITIONS 
10.1 The definitions of the following terns are stated in the Policy: Academic Integrity. 
10.1.1 Academic integrity 
10.1.2 Academic integrity breach 
10.1.3 Collusion or unauthorised collaboration 
10.1.4 Contract cheating 
10.1.5 Plagiarism 
10.1.6 Unauthorised assignment recycling 
10.2 The following terms have the definition stated the Definitions section of the Policy: Programs 
and Courses 
10.2.1 Assessment task 
10.2.2 Course 
10.2.3 Coursework 
10.2.4 Grade 
10.2.5 Mark 
10.2.6 Program 
10.3 The following additional terms have the meanings stated below 
10.3.1 Academic integrity register has the same meaning as in clause 7.1. 
10.3.2 Hurdle assessment task has the definition stated in the Definitions section of the 
Procedure: Assessment and Examinations. 
10.3.3 Marking rubric has the same meaning as in the Procedure: Program and Course 
Information. 
10.3.4 Online academic integrity module means the online educational resource provided by 
the University to support students and staff to understand the principles, processes 
and importance of academic integrity. 
10.3.5 Text-matching software means software used to compare text-based work submitted 
for assessment with other texts for similarity and check that the work of others is 
correctly referenced. 
 
