APPENDIX 1 CLASSIFYING LEVELS AND SUGGESTED RESPONSES FOR 
ACADEMIC INTEGRITY BREACHES 
 
The following table provides a guidance for classifying the level of severity of an academic integrity 
breach and for determining an appropriate response, including the penalty for a breach within the 
disciplinary powers of the head of school, school disciplinary committee or University disciplinary 
committee (in accordance with section 8 of the General Regulations) 
 
Level of breach 
Example of breach 
Suggested response 
One 
Any of the following affecting assessment work 
submitted by student who is in their first year 
of study: 
• 
Unintentional plagiarism resulting from 
poor academic practice 
• 
For example, copying someone else’s 
words without the use of quotation marks 
and/or poorly paraphrasing without 
appropriate acknowledgement while 
broadly referring to the original work in 
some way (i.e. in the reference list or 
bibliography) 
• 
Unauthorised assignment recycling 
• 
Unauthorised collaboration that results in 
similarity with another student’s 
assignment. 
All of the following: 
• 
Written warning 
• 
Refer the student to Student Success for 
consultation 
• 
Explain the breach and how to avoid it. 
Plus, if the breach is level one and does not 
have the effect that a substantial or central 
part of an assessment task lacks integrity 
• 
deduct marks allocated for paraphrasing, 
correct citation practice and other 
referencing skills. 
Or, if the breach is level one but has the effect 
that a substantial or central part of an 
assessment task lacks integrity 
• 
student to revise and resubmit the work 
for a maximum mark of half the highest 
possible mark for the assessment task. 
Two 
Where the breach: 
• 
is either intentional or cannot be 
explained by inexperience or a lack of 
knowledge 
• 
For example, there is evidence of a prior 
Level One breach that has been 
remediated or the student has progressed 
beyond their first year of study 
• 
But does not have the effect that a 
substantial or central component of an 
assessment task designed to assess a 
program learning outcome/s lacks 
integrity. 
Any of the following: 
• 
Plagiarism or misuse of intellectual 
property 
• 
For example, copying someone else’s 
words, including their citations, and 
representing this as your own research 
and writing 
• 
Unauthorised assignment recycling 
• 
Collusion or unauthorised collaboration 
that results in similarity with another 
student’s assignment 
• 
For example, unauthorised sharing of 
questions or responses to discussion or 
quiz based assessment tasks ahead of or 
All of the following: 
• 
Written reprimand 
• 
Refer the student to Student Success for 
consultation 
• 
Explain the breach and how to avoid it 
• 
Student to revise and resubmit the work 
for a maximum mark of half the highest 
possible mark for the assessment task (for 
example a maximum of 10 if the highest 
possible mark was 20). 
