on the following matters: 
4.2.1 preventative actions to mitigate risks to academic integrity (Standard A5.2.2) 
4.2.2 guidance for students on what constitutes academic misconduct and developing 
practices that uphold academic integrity (Standard A5.2.3) 
4.2.3 monitoring breaches of academic integrity and addressing the underlying causes 
(Standard A6.2.1.j) 
4.2.4 ensuring the University’s academic integrity policy and other information about 
academic integrity requirements is available to students prior to accepting an offer 
of admission (Standard A7.2.2.d) 
4.2.5 maintaining the security and confidentiality of information systems and records to 
prevent unauthorised access that may lead a breach of academic integrity (A7.3.3.b) 
4.2.6 documenting and recording responses to breaches of academic integrity (A7.3.3.c), 
and 
4.2.7 institutional processes for maintaining academic integrity (B1.3.8). 
4.3 
Support for staff and students to uphold academic integrity  
4.3.1 To support staff and students to uphold academic integrity, the University will provide  
4.3.1.1 clear and consistent expectations for maintaining academic integrity and 
responding to potential breaches when they are detected 
4.3.1.2 guidance and training for students and staff to understand the importance of 
academic integrity and how to maintain it, and 
4.3.1.3 professional learning, resources and other support for staff to develop teaching 
and assessment practices to address risks to academic integrity. 
4.4 
Maintaining the integrity of programs and qualifications 
4.4.1 Staff and students have obligations to maintain the integrity of programs, including for 
the following activities that lead to the awarding of qualifications. 
4.4.1.1 Student admission 
4.4.1.2 Teaching  
4.4.1.3 Student assessment  
4.4.1.4 Awarding of grades  
4.4.1.5 Student appeals, and 
4.4.1.6 Approving students to graduate. 
4.4.2 Staff will correctly acknowledge the sources of learning resources they use for teaching 
(including, text, images, video and other resources) if it is not their original work. 
4.4.2.1 The use of teaching resources will also comply with any copyright, licensing and 
intellectual property requirements that may apply, in accordance with the 
Policy: Intellectual Property. 
4.4.3 The University will monitor and block access to websites that are suspected of 
facilitating breaches of academic integrity, including websites offering illegal contract 
cheating services and file-sharing websites.  
4.5 
Academic integrity breaches 
4.5.1 A breach of academic integrity is conduct, intentional or not, that reflects a lack of 
honesty, fairness, respect and responsibility in academic activity and decision-making.  
4.5.2 Breaches of academic integrity may include: 
4.5.2.1 plagiarism – representing as one’s own original work (without appropriate 
acknowledgement) the ideas, interpretations, words or creative works of 
another; this includes works in print and electronic media, published and 
unpublished documents, designs, music, sounds, images, photographs, 
