4.5.2.2 unauthorised assessment recycling – submitting or re-submitting, without 
authorisation, one’s own work that has already been assessed 
4.5.2.3 unauthorised use of generative artificial intelligence – submitting work that has 
been produced or modified using artificial intelligence tools including Large 
Language Models, content generators, Chatbots and other forms of generative 
artificial intelligence (writing assistance tools, including grammar checkers, 
translation and paraphrasing tools, and reference generators) (unless expressly 
allowed). 
4.5.2.4 contract cheating – submission of work for assessment that has been 
completed by a third party; this includes submitting work that has been 
produced by 
 a commercial provider, a current or former student, a family member or a 
friend 
 work that has been produced or modified using software including 
generative artificial intelligence (unless expressly allowed), or  
 organising another person to complete an examination 
4.5.2.5 file-sharing – uploading assessment work or course materials to file-sharing 
websites, or any other form of unauthorised distribution of assessment work 
or course materials 
4.5.2.6 collusion or unauthorised collaboration – collaboration with another person/s 
to prepare assessed work beyond the extent of collaboration permitted by the 
specific instructions for the task  
4.5.2.7 all forms of cheating in examinations, tests or other assessment tasks 
4.5.2.8 falsification, fabrication, deception or intentional misrepresentation of 
information, data, research methods or results  
4.5.2.9 conflict of interest – failure to declare or manage a conflict of interest 
4.5.2.10 impersonation – assuming the character or appearance of, or pretending to be 
another person 
4.5.2.11 concealing or facilitating a breach of academic integrity by others 
4.5.2.12 offering or accepting bribes – such as money, favours or benefits of any kind as 
an inducement for an offer of admission or advanced standing, granting a 
particular assessment mark or final grade, or any other action that would 
provide an unjustified academic advantage. 
4.6 
Responding to suspected breaches 
4.6.1 The University will respond to all suspected breaches of academic integrity in 
accordance with the requirements of the Procedure: Academic Integrity. 
4.6.2 The University recognises that there will be varying degrees of severity for breaches of 
academic integrity. 
4.6.2.1 Breaches of academic integrity by students that are naïve and unintentional 
may not be treated as student misconduct 
4.6.2.2 More serious breaches will be considered as student misconduct. 
4.6.3 The University will formally document and record its responses to all alleged breaches 
of academic integrity. This information will be used to 
4.6.3.1 inform the investigation of future breaches, and to 
4.6.3.2 monitor trends for the occurrence academic integrity breaches and the 
