academic freedom. In the exercise of academic freedom, academic staff will act in a manner 
consistent with the Statement: Academic Freedom and Policy: Protecting Academic Freedom 
and Freedom of Speech, applicable laws and regulations, and the University’s Employee Code of 
Conduct and Ethical Behaviour. 
Appropriate Credentials 
4.6 
Research must be conducted or overseen by persons who have qualifications, research 
experience and skills relevant to the type and scope of research undertaken in their role. 
Ethics Approval 
4.7 
Any research involving human participants in which the University is involved is subject to ethics 
approval prior to commencement of the research in accordance with the Policy: Ethics Approval 
for Research Involving Human Participants. This process includes ensuring human research 
participants have provided their informed consent to participate in research. 
4.8 
Where research involves the collection, use or disclosure of health information without the 
consent of individuals, approval must be granted by a Human Research Ethics Committee in 
accordance with the Guidelines approved under section 95A of the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth). 
Child Safety in Research 
4.9 
Research involving children, or research activities where Researchers may have contact with 
children, must be conducted in a manner consistent with the National Principles for Child Safe 
Organisations and the Commonwealth Child Safe Framework (Second Edition), including the 
identification, mitigation and reporting of risks to children. 
Conflicts of Interest 
4.10 A Researcher must ensure any conflicts of interest are disclosed and managed appropriately, 
including but not limited to: 
4.10.1 
Disclosing any conflict of interest to editors or journals, to the readers of published 
work and to external bodies from which funds are sought; and 
4.10.2 
Disclosing any conflicts of interest when proposing research to be undertaken, for 
example in grant or ethics applications or in collaborative projects. 
4.11 A full disclosure of a conflict of interest must be made by a Researcher as soon as reasonably 
practicable to the National Head of School or Director of a Research Institute, except where the 
Researcher is a National Head of School or Director of a Research Institute, the disclosure must 
be made to an Executive Dean of the faculty or where the researcher is an Executive Dean of a 
faculty, the disclosure must be made to the Pro Vice Chancellor (PVC), Research.  
4.11.1 
The recipient of the disclosure as outlined above must discuss the matter with the staff 
member concerned and determine a procedure for the management or elimination of 
the Conflict of Interest. The procedure must be documented and the Researcher 
advised in writing. 
Peer Review 
4.12 In accordance with the National Health and Medical Research Council’s Peer Review: A guide 
supporting the Australian Code for the Responsible Conduct of Research, the University 
recognises the importance of peer review processes by encouraging and supporting the 
participation of its Researchers. 
4.13 Participation in peer review processes is an important part of the research endeavour, and 
