Collaborative Research 
4.24 Collaborative and sponsored research projects will be assessed with the intention of ensuring 
that the research supports the University’s Objects; that the University remains, and is seen to 
remain, independent and objective in any third party research interactions; and that the rights 
and welfare of students and staff are protected. 
4.25 Where research is managed by Collaborators or external parties, the relevant matters, including 
intellectual property, confidentiality and copyright, commercialization, conflicts of interest, compliance 
with the Foreign Relations (State and Territory Arrangements) Act 2020 (Cth), ethics and/or other 
required approvals, reporting, data management, and dissemination of research findings, must be 
agreed prior to the commencement of the research. 
4.26 A formal agreement must be executed by an authorised officer in accordance with the 
University’s Delegations of Authority prior to the commencement of the research for any 
significant collaborations or where required to manage legal, commercial, or regulatory risk, or 
to protect the University’s interests. 
4.27 Where research involves an external grant or research contract the Chief Investigator is 
responsible for: 
4.27.1 
Ensuring appropriate ethical clearance has been granted prior to the commencement 
of research; 
4.27.2 
Providing oversight of research outcomes according to the Australian Code and this 
Policy; and 
4.27.3 
Management of the research project, in accordance with the Policy: Research Funding 
and Agreements. 
4.28 Research involving Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples must be conducted with respect to 
their legal rights and local laws, customs, protocols and cultural knowledges. Engaging with 
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples from the initial conception of research is 
encouraged as is the establishment of long-term/enduring relationships and conducting 
research with transparency aligned to the principles of free, prior and informed consent. 
Guidance is available on request through the Nulungu Research Institute and the National 
Director of Indigenous Strategy and Planning. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Cultural 
Advisors may also be requested as needed. 
4.29 Researchers must consider Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property (ICIP) Protocols of the 
respective Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander individuals, communities and organisations 
when undertaking collaboration and/or engagement relating to research endeavours. 
4.30 Research that involves, affects or is of particular significance to Aboriginal and Torres Strait 
Islander peoples should take into account principles outlined in the NHMRC’s Ethical Conduct 
in Research with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Peoples and communities: Guidelines for 
Researchers and stakeholders and the Australian Institute of Aboriginal and Torres Strait 
Islander Studies (AIATSIS) Code of Ethics for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Research. 
 
