6.2.1 
Ensuring that risks associated with Research, including those relating to business, 
financial, environmental or health and safety matters, are identified and managed; 
6.2.2 
Assessing supervisory expertise and capacity, workspace, and other facilities for 
research student needs; 
6.2.3 
Assessing quality of proposed Research relating to research contracts or grants; 
6.2.4 
Ensuring the quality of the research environment within which research subject to a 
grant or contract will be undertaken, and experience and expertise of the Chief 
Investigator and other key Researchers involved; and 
6.2.5 
Reviewing budgets for research involving an external grant or research contract. 
6.3 
Research Integrity Advisors are responsible for: 
6.3.1 
Assisting in promoting and fostering responsible research conduct and compliance with 
the principles and responsibilities of this Code of Conduct and with the Australian Code; 
and 
6.3.2 
Providing advice about potential breaches of this Code of Conduct and of the Australian 
Code, including on the University processes to be followed to progress any concerns or 
complaints. 
6.4 
PVC Research is responsible for: 
6.4.1 
Appointing appropriately skilled and experienced persons to investigate potential 
breaches of this Code of Conduct and ensure any investigation is timely, effective and 
in accordance with procedural fairness; 
6.4.2 
Determining actions to be taken based on recommendations and findings arising from 
formal investigations about breaches of this Code of Conduct; 
6.4.3 
Addressing any systemic issues in relation to matters of research integrity and 
overseeing the implementation of corrective actions as necessary; 
6.4.4 
Overseeing data collection and information management systems to allow the 
University to monitor the academic progress and research outputs of its students and 
staff; 
6.4.5 
Overseeing the reporting to relevant external authorities in line with requirements; 
6.4.6 
Making recommendation to the Vice Chancellor about whether a public statement is 
necessary and appropriate to communicate findings of any investigation of serious 
breaches of this Code of Conduct; 
6.4.7 
Ensuring review of effectiveness of this Code of Conduct and related policies, 
procedures and guidelines; and 
6.4.8 
Providing an annual report for Academic Council on the effectiveness of the University’s 
processes for maintaining research integrity and the monitoring of potential risks. 
6.5 
Director, Research Services is responsible for: 
6.5.1 
Overseeing the University’s processes that promote adherence to the Code of 
Conduct, including responding to potential breaches of the code and facilitating 
prevention of potential breaches; 
6.5.2 
Supporting the responsible dissemination of research findings and taking action to 
correct the record in a timely manner; 
6.5.3 
Developing, disseminating and monitoring required training programs for Human 
Research Committee (HREC) members, Research Integrity Advisors, staff, students and 
