, ""L040"": ""clauses 47.3.2 or 47.3.3 of the University’s Enterprise Agreement 2022-2026."", ""L041"": ""PROCEDURE: Managing and Investigating Breaches of the Code of Conduct: Research Page 5 of 14"", ""L042"": ""Effective Date: 10/11/2026""}","PROCEDURE: Managing and Investigating Breaches of the Code of Conduct: Research 
Page 5 of 14 
Effective Date: 10/11/2026 
5.4 
A minor breach is a failure to meet the principles or responsibilities of the Code of Conduct: 
Research that is not intentional, reckless or negligent, and that has limited impact on research 
participants, communities or the integrity of the research, but requires remedial action to 
prevent recurrence. 
5.5 
A major breach (Research Misconduct) is a serious breach of the Code of Conduct: Research that is 
intentional, reckless or negligent, and that has caused, or has the potential to cause, significant harm 
to research participants or communities, substantially undermines the integrity or trustworthiness of 
research, or seriously damages public confidence in research. 
5.6 
The classification of a breach will inform the level of investigation, and any outcomes or actions, 
consistent with the requirement for proportionality under the Australian Code. 
 
6 
MAKING A COMPLAINT  
6.1 
Prior to initiating a complaint, a person who believes they may have a complaint against a 
Researcher about a potential breach of the Code of Conduct: Research should seek advice from 
a Research Integrity Advisor (RIA). 
6.2 
Formal complaints against a Researcher that an alleged breach of the Code of Conduct: Research 
has occurred can be made in writing to Research Integrity. 
6.3 
Complaints should include all information relevant to the alleged breach and any available 
evidence to support the complaint. 
6.4 
Anonymous complaints or complaints lodged by a third party will be accepted and considered, 
although progression of an assessment will depend on the nature of the complaint and the 
evidence presented. It may not be possible to investigate anonymous concerns or complaints.  
6.5 
The University will take all reasonable steps to ensure an employee is not victimized or unfairly 
treated as a result of making a complaint. 
6.6 
On receipt of a complaint the PVC Research will allocate an Investigating Officer to the matter.  
6.7 
The Investigating Officer will immediately notify the relevant Executive Dean or Institute 
Director and, in the case of staff matters only, the Vice Chancellor, consider the information 
provided and will assess whether the complaint, if established, could constitute a breach of the 
Code of Conduct: Research. 
6.8 
The Investigating Officer will provide their assessment in writing to the relevant Executive Dean 
or Institute Director.  
6.9 
Accordingly, the assessment will be deemed to meet the requirements of the local level 
management process for managing misconduct under clause 47.3.2 of the University’s 
Enterprise Agreement 2022-2026. 
6.10 If the Executive Dean or Institute Director forms the view that the complaint does not constitute 
a breach of the Code of Conduct: Research, the Executive Dean or Institute Director will provide 
confirmation of the outcome to the Complainant, the Respondent and, where required, the 
Vice-Chancellor. 
6.11 If the Executive Dean or Institute Director forms the view that the complaint may constitute 
misconduct other than a breach of the Code of Conduct: Research, it may be dealt with under 
clauses 47.3.2 or 47.3.3 of the University’s Enterprise Agreement 2022-2026. 
