Ethical Behaviour . 
6.14 Where the Executive Dean or Institute Director forms the view that the complaint, if proven, 
would constitute a breach of the Code of Conduct: Research it will proceed to preliminary 
assessment. 
6.15  Where the Executive Dean or Institute Director forms the view that the complaint, if proven, 
would constitute a major breach (in reference to Clause 5), of the Code of Conduct: Research 
they will advise the Investigating Officer who will notify the PVC Research to commence the 
Investigation in accordance with Clause 8. 
 
7 
PRELIMINARY ASSESSMENT 
7.1 
The Investigating Officer will appoint and instruct an Assessment Officer to conduct a 
Preliminary Assessment. 
7.2 
The Preliminary Assessment will be determined by the specifics of the particular case and will 
be proportional to the seriousness of the matters involved or extent of the potential breach. 
The Assessment Officer will: 
a) Identify, collect and evaluate relevant facts, documentation and information; 
b) Consider and support the need to consult with the Investigating Officer, other institutional 
stakeholders and any internal or external experts to provide specific and/or independent 
advice to facilitate the preliminary assessment;  
c) Advise the Respondent that the complaint has been made, outline the complaint in writing 
and provide a copy of the complaint, if appropriate; 
d) Advise the Respondent of the process to be followed along with a copy of the Code of 
Conduct: Research, relevant policies, this Procedure and the Australian Code for the 
Responsible Conduct of Research 2018 (the Australian Code) and where the allegation 
relates to human research, the Respondent must also be provided with the National 
Statement on Ethical Conduct in Human Research (2025);  
e) Arrange a meeting with the Respondent and the relevant Executive Dean or Institute 
Director to discuss the complaint, at which the Respondent may bring a support person, 
and allow/advise the Respondent to submit a written response to the complaint; and 
f) 
