8 
FORMAL INVESTIGATION 
Investigating Officer Confirmation and Panel Composition 
8.1 
On receipt of recommendation that the matter proceed to a formal investigation, the PVC 
Research will:  
a) Where the complaint relates to a staff member, jointly with the respective Executive Dean 
or Institute Director, refer the allegation to the Vice Chancellor who will review and confirm 
the appointment of an Investigating Officer for the formal investigation of the allegation in 
accordance with clause 47.3.4 of the University’s Enterprise Agreement 2022-2026; or 
b) Where the complaint relates to a student, ask the appointed Investigating Officer to 
formally investigate the allegation in accordance with clause 8.8 of the University’s General 
Regulations. The investigation process will follow the procedure below but shall be deemed 
to have met the requirements of the University Disciplinary Committee under section 8.8 
of the General Regulations.  
8.2 
In managing the formal investigation the appointed Investigating Officer, in partnership with 
People and Culture (in relation to a staff member) will: 
a) Prepare a clear statement of allegations; 
b) Develop the terms of Reference for the investigation, taking into account the checklist at 
Appendix 2 of the Guide to Managing and Investigating Potential Breaches of the Australian 
Code for the Responsible Conduct of Research; 
c) Determine whether the investigation requires an Investigation Panel; and 
d) Seek legal advice on matters of process where necessary. 
8.3 
The Investigating Officer will appoint members to a Panel if deemed necessary in accordance 
with clause 8.2 c). The Panel will comprise: 
a) A suitably qualified person as Chair; and 
b) Any other person(s) that the Investigating Officer considers to be appropriate, whether a 
staff member or not, and in the case of a complaint relating to a student this may include 
the Chair of the University Discipline Committee or nominee. 
8.4 
In nominating a member of the Panel, the Investigating Officer must consider: 
a) The expertise, skills and appropriate numbers of members required; 
b) Actual or perceived conflict of interest or bias of proposed members; 
c) The gender/diversity of members;  
d) Knowledge and understanding of the Australian Code and of the Code of Conduct: 
Research; and 
