e) Where the allegation concerns Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander research, ensuring the 
Investigation has access to appropriate skills and experience to consider Aboriginal and 
Torres Strait Islander research ethics and governance. 
8.5 
The Investigating Officer must inform the Respondent in writing of the terms of the 
allegation(s), the decision to undertake an investigation, and, if required, details of the Panel 
appointed to undertake the investigation. Where the Respondent is a staff member, the 
Investigating Officer must write to the Employee within five (5) working days of the referral of 
the allegation to the Vice Chancellor in accordance with Clause 47.3.4.2 of the Enterprise 
Agreement Staff 2022-2026. 
8.6 
The Respondent must be given an opportunity to raise any valid concerns with the Panel 
composition in writing within seven (7) working days of notification. 
8.7 
Panel members who are not staff members of the University will be provided with written 
appointments, and any external members must sign a Deed of Indemnity. 
8.8 
Prior to the investigation, the Investigating Officer must give due consideration to any additional 
considerations such as: 
a) Whether the potential breach relates to a collaborative research project and whether it will 
be necessary to involve or advise parties across multiple institutions and jurisdictions; 
b) Whether the allegation relates to allegations of corrupt and/or criminal behaviour, the 
reporting obligations to external bodies and authorities, and the effect of this on the 
internal management of the allegation; 
c) Whether the alleged conduct raises any immediate safety issues for the Complainant, 
fellow researchers, research participants or the environment; 
d) Where the allegation concerns risks to human research participants, whether the research 
should be suspended because risks are no longer justified by potential benefits, and the 
requirement to notify the relevant ethics review body promptly of any suspension and 
subsequent decisions; 
e) Where the allegation involves Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander research, including 
research that involves or affects Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples or 
communities, ensuring that relevant Indigenous rights, interests and potential community 
harms are considered, and that appropriate engagement and accountability to 
communities is undertaken; 
f) 
Whether the allegation involves children or young people, including research participants 
under 18 years of age, and the applicable child safety, complaint handling and mandatory 
reporting requirements and 
g) Where the allegation involves the collection, use or disclosure of health information for 
research without consent, whether consent was impracticable, whether de-identified 
information could reasonably have been used, and whether approval by a Human Research 
Ethics Committee was obtained in accordance with the Guidelines approved under section 
95A of the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth). 
8.9 
Where an allegation relates to allegations of a type that the University is required to report to 
external authorities, such as the police or other regulatory agency, the University will ensure 
that the allegation is dealt with in a manner that does not jeopardise or adversely impact an 
investigation by any external authority. In some circumstances this may include suspending the 
