8.32 A Respondent, who is a student, may lodge an appeal against the findings of the Investigation 
Panel in accordance with Regulation 8.14.3 of the General Regulation. 
Reporting Outcomes 
8.33 Where appropriate, the Investigating Officer may notify the Complainant of the outcomes of 
the investigation. 
8.34 Findings of Research Misconduct will be reported to any relevant parties such as funding 
agencies supporting research where the misconduct has occurred, or journals and other media 
through which the research in question has been reported. 
8.35 Every effort will be taken to correct any public record of the research if a breach has affected 
the accuracy or trustworthiness of research findings and their dissemination. 
8.36 Where no adverse findings are made the PVC Research may take the steps considered 
appropriate to restore the staff member or student’s reputation. 
8.37 The Vice Chancellor, on recommendation of the PVC Research, will determine if a public 
statement is necessary and appropriate to communicate the findings of the Investigation. 
8.38 If a Respondent ceases to be an employee or student of the University, the matter may be 
referred to the Respondent’s new employing institution at the discretion of the Vice Chancellor, 
following appropriate legal advice. 
8.39 An external review of the Investigation process by the Australian Research Integrity Committee 
may be requested in cases where funding from the National Health and Medical Research 
Council (NHMRC) or Australian Research Council (ARC) has been used. 
8.40 Where an allegation relates to child safety, the University will ensure that responses to 
complaints and concerns are child-focused, that mandatory reporting and other legal 
obligations are met, and that cooperation with relevant authorities occurs where required. 
Maintaining records and reporting for potential research integrity breaches 
8.41 Research Services will maintain a central record of the processes followed and the outcomes 
for investigations into potential breaches of the Code of Conduct: Research. 
8.42 Summary information relating to investigations into potential breaches of the Code of Conduct: 
Research will be included in the annual research integrity reports provided by the PVC Research 
to Academic Council, in accordance with clause 6.8.7 of the Code of Conduct: Research. 
 
9 
DEFINITIONS 
9.1 
For the purpose of this Procedure, the definitions outlined in the Code of Conduct: Research 
apply. 
9.2 
In addition, the following definitions apply to this Procedure: 
Assessment Officer means the person appointed by the Investigating Officer to conduct the 
preliminary assessment 
Australian Code means the Australian Government’s Australian Code for the Responsible 
Conduct of Research 2018. 
Breach means a failure to meet the principles and responsibilities outlined in the University’s Code 
