7 
SHARING AND PUBLICATION 
 
7.1 
Plans for end-of-project publication of data outputs or archiving/accession of data to 
appropriate repositories must be documented and be based on the principle of “As open as 
possible, as closed as necessary” (EU Horizon2020). In the absence of justifiable reasons such as 
respect for cultural sensitivity or unmanageable risks regarding sensitive or highly sensitive 
information, researchers should lodge data outputs publicly within a data repository under an 
open license. Datasets that cannot be exposed without mediation should be lodged in a 
repository supporting mediated access with clear instructions describing access conditions. 
Valid reasons must be articulated explicitly for mediating or restricting data access or for more 
restrictive licensing;  
7.2 
Researchers must make research data available for use by other researchers and interested 
persons for further research in accordance with this policy and associated procedures through 
publication of data or metadata to enable the application of FAIR, CARE and data sovereignty 
principles alongside the University’s data classification; and 
7.3 
Ensure appropriate arrangements are made if researchers require and have rights to continued 
access to research data after leaving the University. 
8 
ROLES AND RESPONSIBILITIES 
 
8.1 
The University is responsible for: 
8.1.1 Defining University roles and responsibilities for the management of research data; 
8.1.2 Custodianship of research data, which includes providing the necessary infrastructure, 
services and training to enable researchers to meet their responsibilities for research 
data throughout the research data lifecycle including access to the data and materials 
and any information derived from the data following the completion of a project. 
8.2 
Faculty Executive Deans (or delegate) are responsible for: 
8.2.1 Communicating the requirements of this policy and facilitating its adoption through the 
provision of training and guidance to researchers; 
8.2.2 Facilitating provision of resources for the safe and secure storage of physical Research 
Data within the relevant School. 
8.3 
Supervisors are responsible for: 
8.3.1 Helping researchers under their supervision develop the necessary skills for appropriate 
data management, understand their obligations, and meet data management 
requirements; 
8.3.2 Ensuring that Higher Degree by Research students are aware of their obligations per the 
Policy: Research Data Management and Procedure: Research Data Management. 
8.4 
Researchers are responsible for: 
8.4.1 Ensuring their conduct reflects the principles and responsibilities in the University’s 
Code of Conduct: Research and the practices described in this Policy; 
8.4.2 Implementing the requirements of the Procedure: Research Data Management; 
8.4.3 Reporting potential breaches of the University’s Code for the responsible conduct of 
research or data breaches in a timely manner; and  
8.4.4 Undertaking training and/or seeking advice as required to implement this Policy and FAIR 
