3.1.3 Planning should apply, to the extent possible, the Findable, Accessible, 
Interoperable, and Reusable (FAIR) data principles data principles, and the 
Collective benefits, Authority to control, Responsibility and Ethics (CARE) 
principles for Indigenous data governance where appropriate. 
3.2 
The RDMP must be updated as appropriate, either annually or as changes arise (for 
example when researchers change institutions or withdraw from a collaborative 
project, or when the program of research, funding source or objectives are changed) by 
the principal investigator of the project, a delegated research data steward, or the 
Higher Degree Research (HDR) student, for all projects which are: 
3.2.1 seeking human ethics approval through the University Human Research Ethics 
Committee (HREC); 
3.2.2 successful in gaining funding from the Australian Research Council (ARC) and/or 
the National Health and Medical Research Council (NHMRC); 
3.2.3 involving researchers accessing sensitive or highly sensitive information 
(following the Research Data Classification schedule). This includes information 
which is under regulatory control (such as that involving controlled technology 
per the Defence Trade Controls Act 2012) or information associated with 
projects that are under regulatory control; 
3.2.4 being undertaken by a HDR student at the University; 
3.2.5 successful in gaining Higher Education Research Data Collection (HERDC) 
reportable funding in categories 1-4); or 
3.2.6 successful in gaining internal University funding. 
3.3 
Any proposed variations to the RDMP of a project that has already gained human ethics 
approval must be requested following the standard processes for an amendment. It is 
the responsibility of the chief investigator to ensure that appropriate approval is 
obtained for all amendments prior to implementation. 
3.4 
At the completion of the project, the RDMP tool’s Dataset Record form should be used 
to plan and progress archival storage of research data to ensure institutional 
compliance with the policy/code. 
3.5 
When data publication for sharing and reuse is required by the conditions of the 
research project, researchers must complete a Publication Record form in the RDMP 
tool with required metadata and adequate information about licenses and permissions. 
4 
STORAGE, RETENTION AND DISPOSAL OF RESEARCH DATA 
 
4.1 
The University is responsible for providing facilities that comply with: 
4.1.1 privacy requirements and other relevant laws. 
4.1.2 regulations and guidelines. 
4.1.3 standards related to the safe and secure storage of data and information in 
accordance with the Policy: Information and Information Technology and 
Procedure: Information Management. 
4.2 
Researchers must take all reasonable steps to ensure that research data subject to 
legislative or security requirements are managed appropriately to mitigate any risks to 
