the University, the research team and any human participants involved in the research. 
Researchers must also take all reasonable steps to ensure that such data are in a 
durable, indexed and retrievable form, not vulnerable to unauthorised access, and 
stored securely. 
4.3 
Except in the circumstances outlined in sections 4.4 and 4.5 below, master copies of 
digital research data should be stored on an appropriate University-managed research 
storage service or other trusted storage service approved by the University Chief 
Information Officer. Refer to the Research Data Management Storage and Access and 
Archiving, Retention and Preservation guides for help in assessing the sensitivity of data, 
appropriate security measures, data resilience and retrieval requirements and 
suggested platforms. 
4.4 
Where data must be stored at a location outside the University, such as a public archive 
or for collaborative projects: 
4.4.1 a copy of the data should be stored at the University, if permissible. 
4.4.2 researchers must as a minimum ensure the platforms or facilities conform to the 
data security requirements and protocols as those required by the University. 
4.5 
Data collected off campus must be stored securely and regularly transferred or 
transported to the University where relevant, including: 
4.5.1 for electronic research data by the secure means provided by the University as 
regularly as is practical. 
4.5.2 for non-electronic research data, at the conclusion of the off-campus data 
collection and/or at regular intervals where the data collection period is 
protracted. 
4.6 
Generally, research data must be retained for sufficient time to allow reference by other 
researchers and for as long as interest and discussion persist following publication. This 
is often much longer than the minimal requirement and should be considered from the 
outset of a project. 
4.7 
Retention is required where the data is crucial to the substantiation of the research 
findings, cannot be readily or practically duplicated, and the research is: 
4.7.1 controversial or of high public interest, or has influence in the research domain; 
4.7.2 costly or impossible to reproduce or substitute if the primary data is not available 
(i.e. cannot be substituted with an alternative data set of acceptable quality and 
useability, or if data reproduction would place unnecessary burden on human 
research participants or animals); or 
4.7.3 relates to the use of an innovative technique for the first time. 
4.8 
Longer retention times are encouraged if sharing or secondary data use is expected. For 
sensitive data, intended retention time should be included in the application to HREC. 
At the end of the retention period, data must be destroyed in a manner which 
permanently prevents its reassembly. Relevant metadata items describing the data 
should be updated to indicate that the data has been destroyed. 
4.9 
Should changes to research data storage affect ethical considerations, then revised 
ethics approvals must be sought. 
