Conduct: Research. 
8 
DEFINITIONS 
 
 
For the purpose of this procedure, the definitions outlined in the Policy: Research Data 
Management apply. In addition, the following definitions apply: 
Chief investigator (sometimes also referred to as a Principal investigator), means the person 
who is responsible for the intellectual, administrative and ethical aspects of a research project. 
Data custodian means organisations (and/or persons) with responsibility for managing the 
necessary digital and physical infrastructure to maintain the capability, security, 
confidentiality, accessibility, safety and integrity of stored data. Ensures adequate system 
backups, disaster recovery plans, system response times and system availability. 
Data owner means persons and/or organisations with ultimate legal or moral rights that 
gives them the authority to determine storage, retention, disposal, publication or licensing 
arrangements and recipient of any proceeds from commercialisation. They are accountable 
for ensuring the integrity of data, overseeing management, allocating resources and 
resolving escalated issues and authorise access to and use of data. These responsibilities can 
be delegated to a data steward. 
Data security is the protection against unauthorised access. 
Data steward means persons with day-to-day responsibility for ensuring all aspects of ethical 
research data management are conducted in alignment with all related policies and 
agreements. Monitors data quality, identify data entry errors and correcting data to match 
university standards and handle enquiries about data (standards, definitions and usage). 
Typically the chief investigator of a research project is the data steward unless otherwise 
delegated. 
Data transfer/sharing/use agreement means a formal signed document between two or 
more parties describing the obligations, responsibilities and liabilities around a shared 
dataset. Typically, these detail the data being shared along with its permitted uses, retention 
time and security requirements. 
Data output means data that is being disseminated. 
Destruction means the irreversible physical obliteration of all existing copies of data carried 
out using appropriate methods such as shredding or pulping and in the case of electronic 
