data, rendering them unreadable. 
Higher degree by research (HDR) student means a student enrolled in a higher degree by 
research program at the University. 
Interoperability means the ability to exchange and integrate data between systems, 
databases and devices. This requires use of well-described, common (ideally open) data 
formats and comprehensive metadata. 
Metadata relates to the information about a dataset or other resource (including its size, 
description of contents, creation date, inclusion/exclusion criteria). 
Open data refers to the publicly accessible, analysable and reusable research data. It is most 
commonly used for verification of published results, further research, and interdisciplinary 
studies. Data is considered to be sufficiently open when it is: 
• 
Findable (easily discoverable and indexed in appropriate services) 
• 
Accessible (available in full by download or via another service using an API or similar 
system) 
• 
Interoperable (in a modifiable, non-proprietary, and machine-readable format) 
• 
Reusable (licensed to enable reuse and redistribution) 
Research data means records, files or other evidence, irrespective of their content or form 
(e.g. in print, digital, physical or other forms), that comprise research observations, findings or 
outcomes. This includes primary materials and analysed data. Research data may be 
numerical, descriptive, audio-visual or tactile. It may be raw, cleaned or processed, and may 
be held in any format or media. It includes digital and hardcopy data; materials, specimens, 
and samples; code, scripts and software; research and field notebooks. 
Researcher is a staff member, adjunct or student who conducts research at or on behalf of 
the University. 
RDMP author is the researcher that prepares and maintains the RDMP. The RDMP author may 
be the same person as the data custodian. 
RDMP template is a Research Data Management Plan template that is provided by the 
University for researchers to use in preparing their RDMP. 
Sensitive data relates to data that can be used to identify an individual, species, object, or 
location that introduces a risk of discrimination, harm, or unwanted attention. It can include 
identifiable personal and health/medical data, Indigenous data, ecological data about 
vulnerable species, and commercial-in-confidence data. 
Supervisor means a person appointed by the Pro Vice Chancellor, Research (or delegate) who 
meets the requirements set out in the Procedure: Higher Degree by Research Supervision. 
 
