10 INFORMATION SECURITY BREACHES – ASSESSMENT AND 
ASSIGNMENT 
10.1 An information security breach is the suspected or actual release of information classified as 
internal, sensitive or very sensitive to people who are not authorised to have access to the 
information and/or so that the information is or may be circulating outside the control of the 
University. 
10.2 Where a staff member becomes aware of a possible or actual information security breach, 
they will immediately report the breach to the IT Service Desk, as soon as practicable, report 
the breach to their supervisor. These reports will include the type of data involved and the 
context, cause and extent of the breach. 
10.3 The IT Service Desk will raise a ticket for the incident and immediately inform the Chief 
Information Officer and IT Security Lead and take whatever remedial action the IT Service 
Desk can. The IT Service Desk’s record of such tickets is the University’s register of 
information of security breaches and how they have been managed. 
10.4 The staff member’s supervisor will assess the breach to determine whether an information 
security breach has in fact occurred. If they conclude that a breach has occurred or may have 
occurred, the supervisor must immediately contact the IT Security Lead, by phone and email 
(itsecurity@nd.edu.au), to provide a description of the breach, details of any action they have 
taken to address the breach and its outcome, and advice on whether further action is needed. 
10.5 The IT Security Lead will assess the seriousness of the breach in consultation with the relevant 
directors and manager(s) of the Office of Information Technology. The IT Security Lead may 
also consult the Legal Office and keep the Chief Information Officer informed of the 
assessment process and resulting assessment. 
10.6 As an outcome of this assessment, the Chief Information Officer may decide a breach can be 
handled by the Office of Information Technology, or that it is serious enough to initiate the 
critical incident management process. Where the Chief Information Officer considers that a 
breach needs to be managed by the critical incident management process, they will initiate 
that process. 
11 INFORMATION SECURITY BREACHES – MANAGEMENT 
11.1 Information security breaches are managed by the Office of Information technology who will 
assess the risks of a breach and deciding what actions are needed to mitigate those risks. To 
achieve this, the Office of Information Technology will take the following four steps:  
11.1.1 contain the breach and complete a preliminary assessment 
11.1.2 evaluate the risks of the breach 
11.1.3 notify those who need to be aware of the breach, then 
11.1.4 prevent future breaches. 
11.2 To contain the breach and make a preliminary assessment: 
11.2.1 For a physical breach, Campus Security will be alerted. 
