11.2.2 Where the breach has resulted from a cyber-attack and it is continuing, the IT security 
team will ensure no more information is released. 
11.2.3 Any evidence should be preserved that may help identify the cause of the breach and/or 
inform corrective action. 
11.3 The preliminary assessment should address the following questions: 
11.3.1 What steps have already been taken to contain, assess and remedy the breach? 
11.3.2 What information does the breach involve? 
11.3.3 What caused the breach? 
11.3.4 What is the extent of the breach? 
11.3.5 What harm could arise to persons whose information was released? 
11.3.6 What other harm might the breach cause? 
11.3.7 How can the breach be contained? 
11.4 Where a data breach has involved the release of personal information so it is beyond the 
control of the University, and there is a risk of harm to the persons whose information has 
been released, the Chief Information Officer will ensure the breach is reported to the Office 
of the Australian Information Commissioner. 
11.5 Where a cyber-attack has breached security of an information system, the Chief Information 
Officer will report the breach to the Australian Cyber Security Centre. 
12 ROLES AND RESPONSIBILITIES 
12.1 The Policy: Information and Information Technology states general responsibilities of 
everyone who uses a university information system. 
12.2 Employees must: 
12.2.1 notify the IT Service Desk as soon as they become aware of a breach of information 
security, or that someone has gained access to university information they are not 
authorised to access, and: 
12.2.2 follow any instructions maintained by the Chief Information Officer for secure use of 
software, personal computers, mobile devices and tablets provided by the University. 
12.3 Business owners of information systems will: 
12.3.1 where an Office of Information Technology audit recommends improvements to the 
system’s security, implement the recommendation in the time frame stated in the audit 
report, and 
12.3.2 where they are the owner of an enterprise information system, define processes for 
access to the system to ensure data quality and correct use to minimise security risks, 
and maintain a document of security risks/mitigations for the system. 
12.4 Chief Information Officer will: 
12.4.1 in collaboration with business owners of enterprise information systems, maintain 
detailed instructions for staff to ensure information security of these systems in matters 
(such as system management, software development, passwords and other credentials 
for access, protecting against loss of data, security patches, and use of cloud computing 
services), and: 
12.4.2 maintain instructions for secure use of university-provided software, personal 
computers, mobile devices and tablets, and 
12.4.3 maintain instructions for reliable erasure of destruction of university digital storage 
