1 
PURPOSE 
 
1.1 
The expectations for the formatting and presentation of a research theses vary by discipline, 
and The University of Notre Dame Australia (‘University’) also mandates certain requirements 
and minimum expectations for research theses. It is imperative that theses be presented 
professionally and with careful attention to academic expectations. 
1.2 
The purpose of the Guideline: Presentation and Submission of Research Theses (‘Guideline’) 
is to clarify expectations around thesis requirements and the process for submission for Higher 
Degree by Research (HDR) students and their supervisors at the University. 
 
2 
RELATED DOCUMENTS 
 
This Guideline should be read in conjunction with the following Policies and Procedures: 
2.1 
Procedure: Higher Degree by Research Candidature 
2.2 
Program Regulations for the Research Degree being studied 
2.3 
Code of Conduct: Research 
2.4 
Policy: Academic Integrity 
2.5 
Policy: Ethics Approval for Research Involving Human Participants 
2.6 
Policy: Research Data Management 
2.7 
Procedure: Nomination of Thesis Examiners for a Higher Degree by Research 
2.8 
Australian Code for the Responsible Conduct of Research 2018 
 
3 
THESIS REQUIREMENTS 
 
The requirements for presentation of a thesis are as follows: 
3.1 
Word Limit 
The University does not mandate upper or lower word limits for research theses. However, the 
following may be used as guide. 
3.1.1 PhD or Professional Doctorate: 80,000 – 100,000 words 
3.1.2 Masters by Research or Masters of Philosophy: 40,000 - 50,000 words 
Theses may also be inclusive of both project and dissertation components if a practice-based 
methodology is used. Advice from the Supervisors and Associate Dean Research (ADR) should 
be taken into account when deciding both word length and project scope. 
3.2 
Thesis by Publication 
3.2.1 A thesis by publication is assessed by the same criteria as theses not undertaken by 
publication and must meet the standards outlined in AQF 9 (for Masters by Research) 
and AQF 10 (for Doctorates) as a whole.The thesis should be presented in a logical and 
cohesive way, containing an introduction and review of the literature that 
contextualises the research project and must make a sustained argument as a whole.  
3.2.2  While research outputs may be co-authored, it is expected that the HDR student is the 
primary author on the majority of publications included in the thesis. Where co-
authored outputs are included: 
a) A written statement must be included for each co-authored publication as 
