3.4 
Editing Support 
3.4.1 The University allows editing support to assist the HDR student in the development of 
their writing skills (limited to Standards D and E as outlined in the Australian Standards 
for Editing Practice, available at the Council of Australian Societies of Editors webpage 
at http://iped-editors.org/). You can find an experienced editor of theses in your discipline by 
searching the Society of Editors website. 
3.4.2 All substantive and conceptual guidance must be provided by the supervisor(s) only and 
not by a professional editor. 
3.4.3 When an editor has been used:  
3.4.3.1 the name of the editor and a brief description of the service rendered must be 
printed as part of the list of acknowledgements or other prefatory matter near 
the front of the work when it is presented for examination; and  
3.4.3.2 if the editor’s current or former area of academic specialisation is similar to that 
of the student, this must also be stated. 
3.4.4 Students must consult with supervisors before using artificial intelligence or generative 
artificial intelligence to assist with academic writing. If artificial intelligence or generative 
artificial intelligence is used to assist with writing the thesis, the full nature and scope of 
its use must be acknowledged in the thesis. It is the responsibility of supervisors and 
students to be aware of the proper and improper use of artificial intelligence and 
generative artificial intelligence, and to ensure that its use is not in breach of the 
University’s Academic Integrity Policy and Procedure. 
3.4.5 The HDR student must liaise with the Research Office for approval in advance of 
finalising arrangements with an editor to ensure that the costs are available within the 
HDR student’s project budget for disbursement (see also, Guideline: Support for Higher 
Degree Research Students). Retrospective applications for funding will not be approved. 
3.5 
Referencing 
3.5.1 The referencing system should be decided in consultation with the Supervisor, in line 
with the conventions of the discipline in which the HDR student is enrolled and the 
University-endorsed referencing styles: American Psychological Association (APA), 
Chicago Notes and Bibliography Style (Chicago), Australian Guide to Legal Citation 
(AGLC), and the Vancouver referencing style. Guidance on use of these styles may be 
sought from the University Library. Attachment 4 provides a list of resources. 
3.5.2 All substantive contributions by others to the work presented in the thesis (including 
jointly-authored publications, editorial advice, etc.) must be clearly acknowledged 
generally in a preface or introduction, and specifically in notes, references and 
appendices. 
3.6 
