3.6.1 HDR students must ensure that their thesis submission is compliant with copyright law 
and the University’s policies relating to copyright and intellectual property. 
3.6.2 If work owned by a third party has been used within the thesis, the HDR student must 
seek permission to use this work either at the time they decide to use it, or prior to 
submission for examination. This permission is in addition to the normal academic 
practice of citing resources. 
3.6.3 Any data created in the course of the research degree should be made available for use 
whenever possible, taking into account any constraints which may arise from ethical, 
privacy, confidentiality, cultural and intellectual property requirements. 
3.6.4 In instances where students have published, sold or otherwise formally shared outputs 
of the research in the public domain prior to submission, students should check the 
copyright status of their outputs. For instance, if the copyright is held by the publisher 
of the journal, then the student must obtain permission to re-use the material from the 
publisher in their thesis for examination. If the copyright resides with the authors, or if 
permission has been obtained from a publisher, then the student must (also) obtain 
copyright permission from the co-authors in order to reproduce the material. In the case 
of journal publication, usually the copyright is assigned to the journal, therefore the 
candidate must take steps to avoid copyright infringement. Copyright laws may differ 
from country to country, therefore it is important to check with each publisher. This 
should be done at the time the output is accepted for publication. If permissions cannot 
be obtained in time, the thesis can be put under indefinite embargo if required. 
3.6.5 When permission has been obtained, the HDR student is required to include a copy of 
the permission within an appendix to the thesis. 
3.6.6 If permission has not been obtained at the point of examination, the HDR student is 
required to remove (redact) the material(s) from the public version of the thesis. The 
areas where the redacted work(s) resided within the thesis should alternatively 
include a short statement that stipulates ‘Figure/Chart/Diagram/Image/Text/etc: 
Removed due to copyright restrictions’. Failure to do so may result in the thesis not 
being passed. 
3.6.7 Two copies of the thesis are then submitted for publication to ResearchOnline. The 
original (for examination) copy will be held in a dark repository. The redacted version 
will be loaded to the open-access, publicly available ResearchOnline site. 
3.6.8 Advice on copyright matters and permissions may be sought from the University’s 
Copyright 
Officer, 
who 
can 
be 
contacted 
via 
the 
University 
Library 
(http://library.nd.edu.au/copyright). 
3.7 
Order and Content 
3.7.1  Thesis format varies by discipline. Advice on format and presentation for theses is 
available on the Research Office Website.  
 
