and share the resultant originality reports with their Supervisory Team, seeking their approval 
prior to lodgement.  
4.2 
HDR supervisors must also complete the Examiner Nomination form in accordance with the 
Procedure: Nomination of Thesis Examiners for a Higher Degree by Research. 
4.3 
When the thesis is ready for examination, the HDR student should complete the Student 
Statement Form and Supervisors' Certificate for Thesis Submission Form, which is available via 
the University website. This form must be signed by the relevant parties as indicated on the 
form. 
4.4 
The University is committed to environmental sustainability, and to reduce paper consumption 
an e-preferred policy is in place for thesis submissions. Examiners will be sent PDFs of the 
thesis and related documents via email. 
4.5 
HDR students should submit their thesis or the dissertation component of a project-based 
thesis as a single PDF file attached to an email to their Supervisors. The HDR student should 
also attach their completed Student Statement Form and Supervisors' Certificate for Thesis 
Submission Form and a copy of their originality report. The signed Certificate of Original 
Authorship should be included after the title page of the thesis. 
4.6 
Examiners may request a hard copy of the thesis. If this is the case, the Research Office will 
provide a hard copy of the thesis. 
4.7 
If the PDF file is too large to be sent as an email attachment, the HDR student should use a 
University-approved file-transfer service to transmit the thesis, as per the University Policy: 
Research Data Management. Once the file has been uploaded, the HDR student must email 
the link to the relevant Research Office email address indicated in section 4.4. 
4.8 
In the case of a practice-led thesis where the project is able to be transferred digitally for 
examination, an appropriate durable record of the project component should be made available 
using technologies appropriate to the field of study. If this material is too large to be sent as an 
email attachment, the HDR student should use a University-approved file-transfer service to 
transmit the thesis, as per clause 4.6. 
4.9 
In the case of a practice-based thesis where the project requires in-person access for 
examination (such as but not limited to exhibition or performance), there are two options 
available and will depend on the type of project and the norms of the discipline area: 
4.9.1 Where the submission of the dissertation precedes the project examination, the 
dissertation must be available to examiners no later than 28 days prior to examination 
of the project. 
4.9.2 Where the project examination precedes the submission of the dissertation, the HDR 
student must provide examiners with an extended thesis abstract (1000-3000 words) at 
least 14 days prior to the viewing, and submit the dissertation and an appropriate 
durable record of project no later than six months following the viewing. 
4.10 In the case of thesis by publication, outputs to be included should: 
4.10.1 meet the research output and category-specific criteria set out in the most recent  
Excellence in Research for Australia (ERA) Submission Guidelines. Research output 
categories are: research books, chapters in research books, journal articles, conference 
publications, and non-traditional research outputs (NTROs). If necessary, students may 
