8.4 
Where an employee wishes to install software that is not on the relevant list of permitted 
software, they will only do this after they have requested and received approval from the 
Office of Information Technology via a request to the IT Service Desk. 
8.5 
To develop software to be used on university information technology infrastructure, an 
employee must first have requested and received permission to do this from the Office of 
Information Technology via a request to the IT Service Desk. 
8.6 
An academic unit that needs its employees or students to develop software for research or 
teaching purposes must seek approval from the Office of Information Technology to do this 
and must meet any conditions for the approval specified by the Office of Information 
Technology. 
8.7 
All software development activities must follow approved secure development practices, 
including the use of secure code repositories, least-privilege access controls, and approved 
methods for managing credentials and secrets. 
8.8 
Student or employee data must not be stored, referenced, or used in development, test, or 
non-production environments. Only anonymised or synthetic data may be used for 
development, testing, or demonstration purposes. 
8.9 
Where software is classified as a critical application and intended for production use, 
appropriate security assurance activities, including penetration testing, should be undertaken 
prior to go-live to identify and address security vulnerabilities. 
9 
INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY REGISTER 
9.1 
The Chief Information Officer will ensure that the Office of Information Technology maintains 
a register of the University’s: 
9.1.1 software and information technology licences 
9.1.2 information technology infrastructure (hardware, servers and switches), and 
9.1.3 information systems and their business owners. 
10 BACKUP AND DISASTER RECOVERY 
10.1 The Chief Information Officer will ensure that the Office of Information Technology maintains: 
10.1.1 a backup plan that states how and how often each enterprise information system will 
be backed up, and 
10.1.2 a disaster recovery plan stating processes for restoring enterprise information systems 
and returning them to operation if they have gone down. 
11 RESPONSIBILITIES 
11.1 The Policy: Information and Information Technology states general responsibilities of 
everyone who uses a university information system. 
11.2 Employees will: 
11.2.1 only access information they need carry out the functions of their role 
11.2.2 where the University provides them with a mobile device for their work, meet the 
requirements for its use stated in section 5 
