5.1.8 In exercising any delegated authority, a Delegate may seek appropriate advice in order 
to be properly informed. However, delegated authority must be exercised by the 
Delegate without any undue influence by any other person or body and the Delegate 
remains responsible and accountable for the decision or action taken to exercise the 
delegated authority. 
5.1.9 Where, in the opinion of the Delegate, special or unusual circumstances exist which 
would make it more transparent or ethical for the matter to be dealt with other than by 
the Delegate, the decision should be transferred to a senior staff member who has 
responsibility for the Delegate’s Accountability Area. 
5.1.10 Delegation limits apply to transactions and a transaction may not be separated into 
parts, nor may the total cost of a transaction be set off by deductions or trade-ins in order 
to meet a limit on a Delegate’s authority. Generally, tax and duties are to be included in 
this total cost of the transaction except, where claimable with the exception of Goods 
and Services Tax (GST), which is to be excluded for the purposes of determining the total 
cost of a transaction. A Delegation is required prior to entering into a transaction and to 
authorise the payments under the transaction. 
5.1.11 Authorisation to operate a University corporate credit card does not of itself confer on a 
staff member a delegation to commit or expend funds. Acquittal of purchases made by 
credit card, as for purchases made by any other means, must be approved by the 
Delegate who has authorisation. 
5.1.12 A Delegate may not exercise a delegation that may provide them with any direct or 
indirect benefit or that may present them with a conflict of interest (refer Policy: Conflict 
of Interest). Therefore, Delegates may not approve matters including their own 
appointment, remuneration, payment or reimbursement, promotion, transfer or 
secondment, travel, or absence. In all cases, approval can only be granted by a staff 
member who holds a higher level of delegated authority and responsibility within the 
University. 
5.1.13 A Senior Leader may withdraw or reduce (for any period of time, including permanently 
and without notice) delegation(s) given to a staff member in their Accountability Area if 
the Senior Leader considers it to be in the best interests of the University. 
5.1.14 The Vice Chancellor may withdraw or vary any delegation at any time without notice. 
5.1.15 If a position or committee is abolished or re-named so that there is a re-assignment of 
responsibility for a particular function, the delegation is re-assigned accordingly. Each re-
assignment is approved by the Vice Chancellor. 
5.1.16 Where a delegation is to a committee, the delegation refers to the committee acting as a 
whole, not to individual members of that committee. 
5.1.17 Staff who believe that a Delegation has been improperly exercised should immediately 
refer the matter to the Chief People Officer, or Director, Risk and Assurance, who will 
arrange for the matter to be investigated. 
5.1.18 A breach of a delegation made under these Standing Delegations of Authority may be 
regarded as misconduct and may result in disciplinary action in accordance with the 
relevant policies and procedures. 
 
