1 
OBJECTS OF THE UNIVERSITY 
The University's Objects are defined in Section 5 of its Act of Parliament: 
 
The Objects of the University are: 
 
(a) The provision of university education, within a context of Catholic faith and values; and 
(b) The provision of an excellent standard of - 
i. 
Teaching, scholarship and research; 
ii. 
Training for the professions; and 
iii. 
Pastoral care for its students. 
2 
PURPOSE  
2.1 
The University of Notre Dame Australia (University) is committed to a strategic and structured 
organisational wide approach to risk management that: 
2.1.1 supports consistent and effective identification, management and reporting of risks;  
2.1.2 assists in the achievement of the University’s Objects and the delivery of 
organisational strategy; and 
2.1.3 is aligned to the Risk Appetite as prescribed by the Board of Directors. 
2.2 
This Policy provides a framework for managing risks across the University and clarifies the 
responsibilities and accountabilities of Staff in the University’s Risk Management process. 
3 
SCOPE 
3.1 
This Policy applies to all areas of the University. 
4 
PRINCIPLES 
4.1 
The University’s Risk Management system will be consistent with: 
4.1.1 Requirements of the Higher Education Standards Framework and the VET Quality 
Framework; and 
4.1.2 AS/NZS ISO 31000:2018, Risk management – Guidelines. 
4.2 
Risk Management will be incorporated into strategic and operational planning and delivery 
across the University. 
4.3 
Identified risks will be recorded in divisional Risk Registers and the University Material Risk 
Register. 
4.4 
Risks will be regularly identified, reviewed and monitored at nominated levels within the 
University.  
4.5 
Risks will be assessed according to agreed definitions of likelihood and impact/consequence, 
as described in Appendices A, B and C of the Procedure: Risk Management. 
4.6 
Risk Assessments will be conducted on major ventures prior to commencement to ensure 
alignment with the University’s Risk Appetite and its Objects. These may include projects, 
ventures, major events, agreements and commercial activities. 
