7 
DEFINITIONS  
7.1 
For the purpose of this Policy, the following definitions apply: 
AS/NZS ISO 31000:2018 means the joint Australian/New Zealand Risk Management Standard 
on Risk Management Principles and Guidelines. 
Risk is the effect of uncertainty on the University’s Objects and strategic goals. Risks are 
measured in terms of consequence and likelihood. Risk may have a positive or negative impact.  
University Material Risk means a Risk that has substantial impacts at the whole-of-University 
level and its ability to achieve its Objects and strategic goals. 
Academic Risk means a Risk relating to the integrity and quality of the core higher education 
activities of teaching, student learning, research (including research training) and scholarship. 
Control means a measure, such as a process or practice, that mitigates and/or modifies a Risk. 
Risk Appetite means the amount of risk that the University is prepared to accept or be exposed 
to at any point in time, as prescribed by the Board. 
Risk Assessment means the process of identifying, analysing and evaluating risks. 
Risk Management means the structured processes and activities to identify, assess and 
prioritise Risks, implement appropriate Risk Treatments and Controls, monitor and review 
Risks. 
Risk Management framework means the organisational arrangements and resources for 
designing, implementing, monitoring, reviewing and continually improving Risk Management 
throughout the University. 
Executive Management means the Principal Officers of the University as defined by the 
University Statutes.  
Risk Owner means the person with the accountability and authority to manage a risk. 
Risk Treatment means the process of selecting and implementing appropriate options of 
