1 
OBJECTS OF THE UNIVERSITY 
The University's Objects are defined in Section 5 of its Act of Parliament and are: 
(a) the provision of university education, within a context of Catholic faith and values; and 
(b) the provision of an excellent standard of - 
i. 
teaching, scholarship and research; 
ii. 
training for the professions; and 
iii. 
pastoral care for its students. 
2 
PURPOSE 
2.1 
The purpose of the Policy: Intellectual Property is to create a framework in which research, 
invention and the creation of intellectual property by employees and students is encouraged, 
protected and rewarded for the benefit of employees, students and the University.  
2.2 
This Policy defines: 
2.2.1 the circumstances in which the University will claim ownership of Intellectual Property 
created by employees, students and external participants 
2.2.2 the circumstances in which employees and students are entitled to ownership of 
Intellectual Property created by them in the course of their employment or studies at 
the University 
2.2.3 the extent to which the University is entitled to exploit the rights attaching to any 
Intellectual Property owned by it, and 
2.2.4 the extent to which employees, students and the University are entitled to share in the 
profits in the event that Intellectual Property is commercialised. 
3 
SCOPE 
3.1 
The scope of this Policy applies to all forms of intellectual property rights (Intellectual Property) 
whether arising out of legislation or existing at law as amended or replaced from time to time. 
This includes without limitation, all rights including the right to registration, in relation to: 
3.1.1 copyright protected under the Copyright Act 1968 (Cth), including copyright in literary 
works (including computer programs), dramatic works, musical works, artistic works, 
sound recordings, cinematograph films, television and sound broadcasts and tapes and 
published editions as defined by the Act 
3.1.2 discoveries and inventions including both products and processes which may be 
patentable under the Patents Act 1990 (Cth) (Patents Act) 
3.1.3 patents, including applications for patents (national or international), whether standard, 
provisional or innovation as defined in the Patents Act 
3.1.4 plant varieties qualifying for rights under the Plant Breeders’ Rights Act 1994 (Cth) 
3.1.5 Trade marks whether registered or not as defined by the Trade Marks Act 1995 (Cth) 
3.1.6 eligible layouts as defined by the Circuits Layouts Act 1989 (Cth) 
3.1.7 confidential information meaning any confidential information protected by law either 
contractual or equitable 
3.1.8 designs as defined by the Designs Act 2003 (Cth), and 
3.1.9 any other intellectual property not expressly dealt with in this Policy 
