5 
INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY OWNED BY EMPLOYEES 
5.1 
Unless otherwise stated in this Policy the University owns all Intellectual Property and the rights 
attaching to that Intellectual Property created by an employee in the course of their 
employment with the University or participation in a University Project.  
5.2 
The University does not claim ownership in Intellectual Property that has been created by an 
employee outside of the duties set out in or incidental to their contract of employment with 
the University.  
5.3 
The University owns the Intellectual Property in all Course Materials and computer programs 
created by employees in the course of their employment with the University. 
5.4 
Subject to any obligation of confidentiality, legal agreement and restrictions on the use of third-
party Intellectual Property, the University grants employees who develop Course Materials in 
the course of their employment with the University a personal, non-transferable licence to 
adapt and modify those Course Materials for teaching, research and scholarship purposes at 
other educational institutions. Use of Course Materials under this clause does not include the 
use of those Course Materials to compete directly with the University or use for any commercial 
purpose. 
5.5 
Where the University delivers the Course Material for its educational or administrative 
programs the University will do so with appropriate acknowledgement and with respect to the 
creator’s moral rights in accordance with section 9 below. 
5.6 
Where an employee has created a computer program and the University owns the copyright in 
that computer program, the employee must not disclose that program or any information 
about it other than reasonable disclosure to persons employed by the University, without prior 
approval of the Deputy Vice Chancellor, Research, Partnerships and International.  
5.7 
Where the University does not intend to seek legal protection for University-owned copyright 
in Course Materials or a computer program created by an employee it may: 
5.7.1 enter into an IPA that assigns ownership to the creator of the work 
5.7.2 subject to the agreement of the creator, provide public access to the work via open 
access or publication, or 
5.7.3 retain the copyright in commercial confidence. 
5.8 
To support requirements for academic publication, the University assigns copyright ownership 
for Scholarly Work back to the employee who has created the work, unless: 
5.8.1 it forms part of Course Materials or a computer program created by an employee 
5.8.2 the employee has assigned their ownership rights to the University in an IPA 
5.8.3 the University has specifically commissioned the work 
5.8.4 the work was created as a result of the employee’s participation in a University Project  
5.8.5 the University has contributed other University owned Intellectual Property to the 
development of the work, or  
5.8.6 the University has contributed funding, resources, facilities or equipment (i.e. 
Substantial University Resources) for creating the work beyond that which is ordinarily 
contributed to an employee in the course of their employment.  
5.9 
Assignment of copyright ownership to Scholarly Work in accordance with clause 5.8 is subject 
to the following conditions: 
5.9.1 the University shall retain an automatic, non-exclusive royalty free and irrevocable 
