International or their delegate, who will undertake an initial assessment and decide on the 
ownership of the Intellectual Property, the appropriate form of legal protection, if any, and the 
potential for Commercialisation.  
10.2 Where the University decides to pursue a patent or other legal protection for a discovery or 
invention, the University may do so in consultation with the employee or student, in accordance 
with process for patenting and Commercialisation of inventions and discoveries in Schedule 1 
of this Policy. 
10.3 Where the Deputy Vice Chancellor Research, Partnerships and International decides not to 
assert ownership of the Intellectual Property, the employee or student who created the 
Intellectual Property will be informed of the decision in writing. The employee or student may 
then independently assert ownership and undertake Commercialisation of the Intellectual 
Property. In such circumstances the University will enter an IPA with the employee or student 
to assign its rights to any ownership or Commercialisation in the Intellectual Property in favour 
of the employee or student.  
10.4 Research Services will maintain records of the notification and assessment of Intellectual 
Property. 
11 MORAL RIGHTS 
11.1 The University will, in the case of copyright owned by the University or to which the University 
has a licence in relation to, respect all Moral Rights of the authors as provided for by Pt IX of 
the Copyright Act 1968 including the right to be identified as the creator of a work, the right to 
prevent false attribution of authorship and the right to object to any derogatory treatment of 
the work.  
11.2 The University will attribute the names of creators of works and take reasonable steps to ensure 
that creators can disassociate themselves from any modification or amendment to a work.  
12 OBLIGATIONS RELATING TO THE USE OF INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY 
12.1 Employees and students may use University-owned Intellectual Property they have created in 
the course of their employment or studies with the University provided such use does not 
interfere with the protection or Commercialisation of that Intellectual Property.  
12.2 Employees and Students of the University have a duty to ensure they do not breach the 
Intellectual Property rights of third parties in the course of their employment or studies at the 
University. Employees and students must not use Intellectual Property owned by a third party 
unless they have written permission to do so or are permitted to do so in accordance with 
Copyright Act 1968 or any statutory licence provided for under the Copyright Act 1968. Further 
requirements for ensuring the use of Course Materials by employees and students complies 
with copyright legislation are set out in the Procedure: Program and Course Delivery and 
Management. 
12.3 An employee or student who does not own the Intellectual Property in a particular work must 
not do any act or thing inconsistent with the University’s rights under this Policy, including but 
not limited to applying for any form of protection or Commercialisation of that Intellectual 
Property. This includes: 
