12.3.1 not holding any discussions with any person outside the University concerning the 
Intellectual Property unless those discussions have been approved by the Deputy Vice 
Chancellor Research, Partnerships and International, or 
12.3.2 publishing in any way or form the Intellectual Property until the Deputy Vice Chancellor 
Research, Partnerships and International or their delegate has made an assessment of 
the validity, protection and Commercialisation of the Intellectual Property and approved 
such publication.  
12.4 An employee or student who becomes aware of any unauthorised use or infringement of 
Intellectual Property owned by the University must notify Research Services in writing of all 
relevant details of the Intellectual Property unauthorised use or infringement.  
13 UNIVERSITY NAME AND LOGO 
13.1 The University name and logo are owned by the University. 
13.2 Employees may use the name and logo of the University for legitimate purposes of the 
University. Employees wishing to use the name and logo of the University for external purposes 
must obtain prior approval from the Deputy Vice Chancellor Research, Partnerships and 
International.  
13.3 A Third Party will only be permitted to use the University’s name or logo with the approval of 
the Vice Chancellor. Any approval by a third party to use the University’s name or logo must be 
obtained prior to any use and will be subject to conditions set down by the Vice Chancellor.  
14 DISTRIBUTING REVENUE FROM THE COMMERCIALISATION OF 
INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY 
14.1 Subject to any other agreement between the University and an employee or student who 
creates Intellectual Property over which the University claims ownership, the University will 
share any cumulative Net Revenue received by the University from the Commercialisation of 
Intellectual Property as a reward for the employee or student’s involvement and contribution. 
14.2 For the purpose of this clause the term Net Revenue means the return received by the 
University from the Commercialisation of Intellectual Property after deducting all expenses 
incurred by the University in the creation, protection, and Commercialisation of the Intellectual 
Property.  
14.3 In the absence of any IPA or other written agreement and subject to the determination of the 
Deputy Vice Chancellor Research, Partnerships and International, Net Revenue from the 
Commercialisation of Intellectual Property owned by the University will typically be distributed 
as follows.  
14.3.1 fifty percent to the creator, being the employee or student who created the Intellectual 
Property, and 
14.3.2 fifty percent to the University. 
14.4 The Vice Chancellor has discretion to redirect the University’s share of Net Revenue to faculties 
other areas within the University to ensure the efficient allocation of resources.  
14.5 Where there is more than one creator, the share of Net Revenue will be apportioned equitably 
between the creators and subject to their agreement. If the creators cannot reach agreement, 
