Page 14 of 14 
Effective Date: 13/10/2026 
SCHEDULE 1 PATENT APPLICATION AND COMMERCIALISATION 
1. 
Where an assessment of Intellectual Property has been completed in accordance with clause 
10.1 of this policy and the Deputy Vice Chancellor, Research, Partnerships and International 
believes a patent application will be successful and commercially viable, Research Services will 
notify the Legal Office who will arrange for a patent attorney to make a final evaluation of the 
patentability of the invention and prepare a provisional patent application.  
2. 
The University will pay the costs of the patent attorney and any costs incidental to the 
provisional patent application.  
3. 
Disclosures of the invention must not occur until the patent application process is complete.  
4. 
To ensure that any subsequent or amended patent applications are not compromised any 
disclosures should be on the basis of a confidentiality agreement. Any agreement must be 
executed by the University through the Deputy Vice Chancellor, Research, Partnerships and 
International.  
5. 
During the twelve (12) month period of the provisional patent commercial interest should be 
sought.  
6. 
Not later than three months before the expiry of the provisional patent the Deputy Vice 
Chancellor Research, Partnerships and International or their delegate will reassess the 
commercial potential of the invention and decide whether to proceed to file a complete patent 
specification.  
7. 
Where no commercial interest has been secured the University will generally not proceed with 
a complete patent specification.  
8. 
If the University decides not to proceed with a complete specification it may assign its 
Intellectual Property rights in favour of the employee or student who created the invention. Any 
assignment must be in writing in the form of an Intellectual Property Agreement.  
9. 
Once the University has assigned its Intellectual Property rights to the creator, the creator may 
apply for a complete patent. Where the University has divested its ownership rights the creator 
will be responsible for meeting the costs of any further legal protection sought.  
10. Where the University proceeds with a complete patent specification it will determine whether 
to register the invention in other countries. 
 
