1 
PURPOSE  
1.1 
The purpose of this Policy is to set out a university recordkeeping framework for the effective 
capture and management of records in electronic and physical formats from creation through 
to disposal or permanent retention. 
1.2 
The objectives of the framework are: 
1.2.1 Providing a base for a university wide recordkeeping framework and program of 
works.  
1.2.2 Implementation of appropriate technology to capture, protect and manage University 
records as valuable information assets.   
1.2.3 Design and application of records management practices that are referenced against 
relevant legislative obligations with the aim of achieving best practice in alignment 
with relevant standards.   
2 
SCOPE 
2.1 
This Policy applies to all employees (staff) of the University and contractors. 
3 
PRINCIPLES 
3.1 
University records are key information assets that function as transactional evidence of 
business activities. This information is required to provide an accurate representation of the 
University’s activities and why these activities were undertaken. Some examples of the ways 
that accurate and complete records are important and need to be managed correctly at the 
University include: 
3.1.1 acting as historical records of the University’s governance, structure, operations, and 
activities to assist in sound decision making 
3.1.2 evidence of business transactions and decisions for the purpose of accountability, and 
3.1.3 a record of the academic achievement and qualifications of students both past and 
present. 
3.2 
Records will exist in a range of types and formats (electronic and physical). Examples of 
University records include policies and procedures, staff and student newsletters, media 
releases, legal agreements, research grant applications, student administration records, 
curriculum and timetable documents, photographs, academic results, Board and committee 
papers and Vice Chancellor reports. 
3.3 
All employees must ensure they are aware of their obligations under this Policy and their 
responsibilities within the procedures and related processes within the records management 
framework.  
3.4 
Employees must ensure they capture and manage records in the University’s approved 
business systems, which provide functionality for effective security controls, records 
classification, retention, version control and audit trails. 
