4.3.1.2.3 
Australian/New Zealand Standard AS/NZS ISO 30300:2020 – This 
standard defines the core concepts and vocabulary relevant to the 
records management domain.   
4.4 
University policies, procedures and guidelines 
4.4.1 The Policy: Records Management Framework (this policy) provides a base for the 
university-wide recordkeeping framework and program of work. It outlines why the 
University should create, manage, and dispose of its records to ensure compliance, 
efficiency, and accountability. 
4.4.2 The Policy: Information and Information Technology defines the expected standards of 
University information usage, management, and security for users. 
4.4.3 The Procedure: Information Management states requirements for classifying 
information, managing access to restricted university information, ensuring the quality 
of data on university systems, and assessing and managing breaches of the 
confidentiality and/or security of information held by the University. 
4.4.4 The Process: Vital records, Process: Naming conventions, Process: Archiving records for 
off-site storage, Process: Records disposal under normal administrative practice, and 
Process: Request for authorised records destruction provide the practical steps and 
rules for implementing the framework, ensuring that records are created, maintained, 
accessed, and disposed of in a consistent and compliant manner. 
4.5 
Records/Information Management Tools  
4.5.1 Records/information management tools support the implementation of records 
management policy and provide consistency of recordkeeping practices across the 
University. These tools include but are not limited to:  
4.5.1.1 
Retention and Disposal Schedule (RDS) – this outlines the retention period 
and disposal actions for the various types of University records. It ensures 
that records of continuing value are preserved, and those records no longer 
needed are disposed of in a timely and compliant manner.  
4.5.1.2 
Business Classification Scheme (BCS) – is a structured, hierarchical system 
for categorising University information and records based on their business 
functions, activities, and transactions. The classifications can be linked to 
retention and disposal schedules in the recordkeeping systems and are used 
to implement security measures and access controls to protect sensitive 
information. 
4.5.1.3 
Information Asset Register – this catalogues the University’s information 
assets, including their details and associated risks.  
4.6 
Recordkeeping System  
4.6.1 The University’s records management framework will be enabled by a records system, 
which will support and promote accuracy, accessibility, and the integrity of the 
University’s records.  
4.6.2 Staff training will be provided on the use of the recordkeeping system.  
4.6.3 The system will be monitored on an ongoing basis according to Policy: Information and 
