3.2.1.3 The Reviewer: 
• Builds in reflection opportunities by way of asking questions as part of 
review commentary 
• Respectfully challenges ideas behind rationale for particular strategies 
• Introduces alternative viewpoints 
• Tackles troubling concepts 
• Changes positions if data and evidence suggest it, and 
• Uses the indicators of the review template (if used) to strengthen 
suggestions. 
3.2.2 Adopting an inquiry habit of mind 
3.2.2.1 Recognise the process as an opportunity for growth. 
3.2.2.2 The Reviewee: 
• Recognises the challenges of a professional conversation 
• Seeks deep understanding 
• Is open to difference, and 
• Uses the four-stage active reflective cycle (See Procedure). 
3.2.2.3 The Reviewer: 
• Respects the disciplinary context 
• Provides feedback that is: 
o Aligned to the indicators in the template (if used), and 
o Focused on improvement, and 
• Accepts that not all variables will be known by you as the reviewer. 
3.2.3 Make use of data and evidence 
3.2.3.1 Observation of any aspect of teaching will use and generate data. A recognition 
of this not only grounds the review in evidence, but provides opportunities for 
the scholarship of teaching and learning. 
3.2.3.2 The Reviewee: 
• Selects artefacts and evidence to be used in the review (for example, 
course outline, samples of student work, resources) 
• Determines the review boundaries, and 
• Uses review data to feed into the active reflective cycle. 
3.2.3.3 The Reviewer: 
• Judges if the artefacts provided are relevant to the review 
• Is mindful of student confidentiality 
• Identifies gaps 
• Uses the available templates, if relevant, to guide observation, and 
• Makes notes. 
