Bundle page 387 | N191 | source page 4 | line numbers at left","TOCOL: CONVERSATIONAL APPROACH TO FEEDBACK 
3.1 
Background 
3.1.1 Asking for and giving feedback can be confronting for all parties. Recognising this, a 
conversational approach is recommended. The defining element of a conversational 
approach to feedback is collaborative dialogue between professionals to promote 
continuing professional learning (Wilson, Bedford & Readman, 2019, p.11). 
3.2 
Protocol 
The conversational protocol has three components, illustrated by Figure 1: 
 
 
 
 
3.2.1 Developing relationships of respect and challenge 
3.2.1.1 Begin with an understanding that the peer review relationship provides 
opportunities for learning for all parties to the process. 
3.2.1.2 The Reviewee sets the: 
• Parameters of the review 
• Areas of feedback requested, and 
• Meeting times. 
Figure 1 The Conversational Protocol. Adapted from Earl and Timperley (2009) 
