examination have been developed for use at Notre Dame, and are outlined below in 
accordance with the learning and teaching cycle. Links to these templates are found on 
the University Policy Portal Website. They have been created as living documents in line 
with best practice. 
4.2 
Course Outlines: External Referencing of Standards Report (ERoS) 
4.2.1 The template provides a framework for a report that may be used by internal reviewers 
to formally and thoroughly review a course outline, or by external observers to 
benchmark the course outline against comparable ones in other institutions (Sefcik et 
al., 2018). The report structure contains sections on compliance with AQF descriptors, 
course outcome alignment with program alignment, and all aspects of the assessment 
items (requiring a range of marked student work) and assessment processes. Developed 
by a consortium of Australian universities with an Office of Learning and Teaching grant, 
this process is transparent and open and builds a community of practice among the 
academics involved. Because it is values based rather than compliance focused it 
encourages collaboration and capability building (Sefcik et al., 2018). 
4.3 
Peer Review Portal 
4.3.1 The portal provides opportunities for external referencing across a wider range of 
disciplines and subjects for a fee (see https://peerreviewportal.com/). It connects 
potential reviewers with institutions requiring external course or program review, or 
benchmarking, and can include TEQSA case managers, accreditation boards, students, 
institution academic board members into a review. The platform simplifies and reduces 
the burden of administration of the review process. 
4.4 
Other external sources 
4.4.1 There are a range of external services providing support for those wishing to review 
their practice. Two of note are: 
• The Flipcurric website developed by Emeritus Professor Geoff Scott as part of an 
Office of Learning and Teaching Fellowship provides a framework for designing and 
reviewing learning outcomes in order to improve the quality of achievement 
standards and assessment. 
• Quality Matters is a paid American service whose mission is to promote and 
improve the quality of online education. It has developed a suite of rubrics that may 
be used to assess aspects of an online course, (one may self-assess, use the rubrics 
for internal peer review, or have Quality Matters perform the review) and also 
offers professional development and certification. 
4.5 
Template Peer Review of Template – Course Outline 
4.5.1 This template is adapted from EROS. The template only covers items identified in the 
course outline. Ideally it should be used in its entirety as it enables a reviewer to provide overall 
feedback on the quality of information being provided to students e.g. items such as 
constructive alignment, explanation of assessment tasks. This template would be useful at the 
curriculum renewal/development phase.  
4.6 
Template Peer Review of Template - Teaching  
4.6.1 The synchronous teaching template has indicators in four areas of teaching: 
• Pedagogy 
• Learning environment 
