4.6.2 The standards-based template is synthesised from the University’s Quality Teaching 
Framework (2017), which was in turn based upon the Australian Teaching & Criteria & 
Standards framework. Using a scholarly design-based cycle of user feedback and 
refinement (Ardzejewska, Casey, Steketee, Robertson, Geoghegan & Pizzica, 
Stephenson, under review), the template is organised into logical areas of teaching, 
making it simple to use in situ. It can be used in a review of all types of face-to-face 
teaching  (e.g. seminar, workshop, field, laboratory), or of a synchronous online class. 
4.7 
Synchronous Peer Review of Teaching Observation Aid 
4.7.1 There is also an observation aid, a one-page table of all of the descriptors extracted from 
the template (i.e. exactly the same) for reviewers who prefer a narrative style 
observation, while still making sure standard criteria have been noted. 
4.8 
The Learning Management System Course Site Template 
4.8.1 The Course Sites Review Template is a standards-based template using criteria 
developed by the Australian Society for Computers in learning in Tertiary Education 
(ASCILITE), grouped into Community of Inquiry (Garrison, 2017) themes. The template 
provides for a review of how the course is deployed through Blackboard and the 
indicators provide a guide to continuous improvement. 
4.9 
Exam Review Template 
4.9.1 The Exam Review Template applies a cognitive load lens to the practice wisdom that 
exists for writing excellent examinations. The template guides peer reviewers through 
the general principles into the specifics of question types, and finally into an evaluation 
phase to ensure continuous improvement not only of the examination artefact, but of 
learning and throughout the course. 
