4.2 
The scope, terms of reference and processes for institutional reviews are determined in 
accordance with the following principles. 
4.2.1 Independent - involving scrutiny separate to the staff involved in delivery of the 
program, processes, function or area being reviewed. 
4.2.2 Fit for purpose - tailored to take account of context and need, including strategic and 
operational goals, relevant legislation, standards, codes of practice. 
4.2.3 Recognise areas of excellence – in teaching, student learning, research, research 
training, and scholarship 
4.2.4 Transparent - clear and understood terms of reference and process. 
4.2.5 Evidence-based - using data and information from a range of sources, including 
performance measures against institutional targets, benchmarks and external points 
of reference where available. 
4.2.6 Rigorous - thorough processes designed to achieve maximum positive impact, tracking 
of outcomes, and support for implementation. 
4.2.7 Efficient - timely and cost effective, including in the use of staff time and avoidance of 
duplication of effort where possible. 
4.2.8 Integrated - cohesive with and informed by strategic and operational planning, 
resourcing and risk assessment. 
4.2.9 Risk based - focusing on known or potential areas of risk. 
4.3 
Institutional reviews include, as appropriate, opportunities for internal and external 
consultation with stakeholders and subject matter experts, including from students, staff, 
graduates, academic disciplines, professions, and community groups. 
4.4 
Institutional reviews are informed and supported by regular monitoring of quality and 
performance in the context of implementing strategic and operational plans, and through 
focused quality assurance processes carried out in accordance with relevant policies and 
procedures. 
4.5 
Executive management and governance oversight for institutional monitoring and review 
processes and outcomes is provided in accordance with the Statutes, and as delegated in the 
relevant policy, procedure or committee charter, including by: 
4.5.1 establishing and monitoring institutional benchmarks for quality and performance 
4.5.2 overseeing collection, storage and use of comparative data for review purposes 
4.5.3 approving and monitoring the effectiveness of policies and procedures for institutional 
monitoring, review and improvement  
4.5.4 approving plans for individual reviews, including reviewers, scope, terms of reference 
and review processes 
4.5.5 finalising review reports and approving improvement plans to address review findings, 
and 
4.5.6 monitoring progress for the completion of actions for improvement.   
5 
INSTITUTIONAL MONITORING AND REVIEW PROCESSES 
5.1 
The University’s processes for institutional monitoring, review and improvement are outlined 
below. 
5.2 
University operations and quality of service provision 
5.2.1 Annual planning and delivery of independent internal audits to provide assurance of 
operations and quality of service provision for high-risk areas, key frameworks, core and 
enabling business processes, systems and technology. 
