6 ACADEMIC GOVERNANCE AND SELF-ASSURANCE 
6.1 
Academic governance at the University operates interdependently with, but is distinctly 
separate from, corporate governance.  The University recognises that maintaining links 
between academic governance, corporate governance and University management is 
necessary to achieve whole-of-institution accountability for identifying and responding to 
academic risk and ensuring effective self-assurance.  This is achieved primarily through: 
6.1.1 ensuring the Board of Directors receives relevant and regular information about the 
quality and outcomes of the University’s higher education provision and other academic 
activities, including through: 
6.1.1.1 regular reporting provided by academic leaders on academic quality assurance, 
management of compliance with the Threshold Standards and the ESOS Act, 
and the management of academic risk and risks to the safety and wellbeing of 
students 
6.1.1.2 regular reports provided by the Chair of Academic Council to the Board of 
Directors on matters relating to Academic Council duties, responsibilities, 
authority and decisions, as well as emerging priorities for Academic Council 
6.1.1.3 the Chair of Academic Council attending meetings of the Board of Directors 
6.1.2 ensuring effective participation of Academic Council and relevant Academic Council sub-
committees in strategic and operational planning 
6.1.3 establishing and assigning roles and responsibilities to oversee institutional monitoring, 
review and improvement of academic quality and outcomes as set the Policy: 
Institutional Review Framework. 
6.2 
The capacity for academic governance at the University to operate independently of corporate 
governance and management is achieved by: 
6.2.1 mitigating risks associated with conflicts of interest by requiring members of academic 
governance committees to declare any potential, perceived or actual conflicts of 
interest upon appointment to the committee, and at the commencement of each 
meeting for any matters listed in an agenda 
6.2.2 involving elected academic staff and students in Academic Council and faculty board 
decision making  
6.2.3 including external independent membership on Academic Council, experienced in 
academic governance, to provide expert contributions and advice in academic decision 
making, and 
6.2.4 delegation of academic decisions affecting the quality and integrity of higher education 
provision to Academic Council, which includes but is not limited to: 
6.2.4.1 approving the accreditation and reaccreditation of the University’s higher 
education programs and qualifications  
6.2.4.2 conferral of higher education qualifications awarded by the University 
6.2.4.3 endorsing new academic policies and procedures, major changes to existing 
academic policies and procedures and their recission, and 
6.2.4.4 providing oversight of the University’s Academic Risk register and approval of 
annual Academic Risk Assessments. 
