5 
AGREEING TO AUTHORSHIP  
5.1 
Authorship of a research output should be discussed between researchers, including 
research students, where relevant. Discussion should take place at the earliest possible stage 
in a project and reviewed whenever there are changes in participation. This is particularly 
important for students undertaking a thesis by publication. 
5.2 
Every attempt should be made by the individuals involved to reach agreement on the 
authorship of a publication and the order in which authors are listed. 
5.3 
In attempting to reach agreement on the authorship of a publication and the order in which 
authors are listed: 
5.3.1 
A Research Integrity Advisor may be approached for assistance in facilitating 
agreement.  
5.3.2 
The Research Integrity Advisor may seek support from the Research Office in 
facilitating agreement. 
5.3.3 
The Pro Vice Chancellor, Research, has the responsibility to determine the 
appropriate course of engagement with another institution when facilitation is 
needed between the researcher and an external party. 
5.4 
In accepting authorship, the author is responsible for the integrity and accuracy of their 
contribution to the research and output, and shares responsibility for the entire output with 
all other listed authors. 
5.5 
Where there is more than one author, the order of authorship should be a joint decision of 
the co-authors based on an objective and fair assessment of the proportional contributions 
of each individual. 
5.6 
Where there is more than one author, an authorship agreement should be in place that 
includes the following information: 
5.6.1 
Who will be recognised as the authors of the research output; 
5.6.2 
A description of the contribution that each author has made (or will make) to the 
research output; 
5.6.3 
The order in which the authors appear, consistent with any applicable disciplinary 
norms and publication requirements; 
5.6.4 
At least one corresponding author who is responsible for communication with the 
publisher and managing communication between the authors including maintaining 
records of authorship agreements.   
5.7 
An authorship agreement may be in the form of emails, a transcript of an online discussion 
or other evidence of agreement. Appropriate records must be kept, and it is the 
responsibility of the corresponding author to maintain the records of authorship agreement. 
If the corresponding author is an HDR student, then their supervisors have responsibility for 
ensuring compliance. See Appendix A for an example of an authorship agreement.    
5.8 
When agreeing to authorship, each author will declare any potential or actual conflicts of 
interest, in accordance with the Code of Conduct: Research.  
5.9 
If a conflict of interest arises following the agreement of authorship, the author should 
declare their conflict to the other authors and any other relevant parties, such as funding 
