research outputs.  Other versions of the name must not be used, for example “The University 
of Notre Dame Sydney Australia” or “Notre Dame, Sydney, Australia”.  Researchers are 
responsible for ensuring the University is not affiliated in the dissemination of research in 
low quality or predatory journals, books, or conferences. 
6.5 
As per the University’s Policy: Honorary Degrees, Emeritus Titles and Naming Rights, 
Emeritus title holders must provide appropriate acknowledgement in publications and grant 
applications arising from research involving the University. 
7 
AUTHORSHIP DISPUTES 
7.1 
A breach of the principles and responsibilities relating to authorship outlined in this 
Guideline are breaches of the University’s Code of Conduct: Research. This includes a failure 
to report concerns or suspected breaches. 
7.2 
Complaints of a breach and/or reporting of a concern or suspected breach will be managed 
in accordance with the University’s Procedure: Managing and Investigating Breaches of the 
Code of Conduct: Research. 
7.3 
Where a complaint has been made and is subject to formal investigation, the Pro Vice 
Chancellor, Research may determine that steps should be taken to halt a publication process 
or otherwise suspend the public dissemination of any research related document (including 
research outputs, curriculum vitae, grant or job applications or other public statements). 
8 
