8.1 
All staff and students involved in research must engage with peer review processes in 
accordance with Peer Review: A Guide supporting the Australian Code for the Responsible 
Conduct of Research 2019 (the Guide).  
8.2 
In accordance with the Guide and the Australian Code for the Responsible Conduct of 
Research 2018, Peer Review is “the impartial and independent assessment of research by 
others working in the same or a related field”. Peer review is employed in a range of research 
functions including the selection, assessment and/or review of: 
8.2.1 
research proposals and grant applications 
8.2.2 
material for publication and dissemination 
8.2.3 
the research of Higher Degree Research (HDR) candidates 
8.2.4 
research quality, engagement and impact by government bodies 
8.2.5 
research conducted by individual researchers, teams, academic units and 
institutions 
8.3 
Participation in peer review processes is an essential part of academic work, and researchers 
with the appropriate expertise are encouraged to participate in peer review to contribute to 
their discipline, stay current with the most recent research, and ensure visibility and 
recognition as experts in the field. 
8.4 
Peer reviewers should be fair, rigorous and timely. They should maintain the confidentiality 
of the content under review and also: 
8.4.1 
Ensure that they are informed about, and comply with, the criteria to be applied in 
the peer review process; 
8.4.2 
Review research objectively and respectfully with respect to review criteria; 
8.4.3 
Declare any potential conflicts of interest prior to the review in accordance with the 
Code of Conduct: Research; 
8.4.4 
Apply standards equally to all research under review; 
8.4.5 
Maintain professionalism in the tone of their comments, ensuring that reviews are 
as constructive as possible; 
8.4.6 
Must not seek to unduly influence or gain competitive advantage from the peer 
review process, for example by taking advantage of knowledge obtained during the 
peer review process without permission, reviewing outside of the limits of their 
expertise, allowing personal prejudice or unconscious bias to influence their review, 
or communicating with authors or other reviewers outside of the allowed scope of 
the process. 
8.4.7 
Act in accordance with the rights associated with the Statement: Academic 
Freedom, by giving proper consideration to research that challenges or changes 
accepted ways of thinking, which may include innovative, interdisciplinary or 
collaborative research. 
8.5 
Researchers whose work is undergoing peer review must not seek to influence the process 
or outcomes.  
8.6 
A breach of the principles and responsibilities relating to peer review outlined in this 
Guideline are breaches of the University’s Code of Conduct: Research. This includes a failure 
to report concerns or suspected breaches. 
8.7 
Complaints of a breach and/or reporting of a concern or suspected breach will be managed 
