9 
PUBLICATION AND DISSEMINATION OF RESEARCH  
9.1 
Researchers are encouraged to prioritise research output quality and significant research 
contribution when disseminating research findings, rather than seeking to publish multiple 
outputs with less significant individual contributions. 
9.2 
Researchers are strongly encouraged to disseminate their original research findings through 
high quality, high prestige research outlets and publishers to maximise opportunities for the 
research contribution to be discovered and used by the research discipline. 
9.3 
Research translation for community, industry/sector, and general public audiences through 
outlets aimed at professional or other non-academic audiences should, where appropriate 
and with citation to the relevant research outputs, be disseminated after the dissemination 
of original research findings in high quality research outlets.  Reputable research outlets 
(journals, book publishers, etc) will normally not accept manuscripts reporting research 
findings that have been previously published, including those previously published through 
non-academic or professional audience outlets. 
9.4 
Self-publishing is not an acceptable form of research dissemination. 
9.5 
The University encourages open access publishing. 
9.5.1 
Researchers must comply with all relevant funder policies and requirements for 
open access output and data publishing. 
9.5.2 
Researchers are encouraged to deposit acceptable open access versions, such as 
the Author’s Accepted Manuscript of their research outputs in the institutional 
repository, ResearchOnline@ND, where copyright and publisher constraints allow. 
10 PUBLISHING AND DISSEMINATION RESPONSIBILITIES  
10.1 
A researcher must not intentionally, negligently or recklessly include inaccurate or 
misleading information, or exclude relevant contradictory information, in any research 
related document (including research outputs, curriculum vitae, grant or job applications or 
other public statements). 
10.2 
Where there has been publication or dissemination of any research related document 
(including research outputs, curriculum vitae, grant or job applications or other public 
statements) that includes inaccurate or misleading information, or excludes relevant 
contradictory information, the researcher and/or university employee who is responsible 
must take active, reasonable and timely steps to correct the public record. 
10.3 
In general, research outputs should not be made publicly available before undergoing peer 
review. In disciplines where pre-print publishing is acceptable, works may be made publicly 
available with appropriate disclosure of peer review status and permission from the 
publisher. 
10.4 
Where there is private reporting of research outputs that have not yet undergone peer 
review (for example, to prospective or current financial supporters), researchers should 
explain fully the status of the work and the Peer Review that will be undertaken. 
