10.5 
Researchers are responsible for exercising due diligence to ensure that research is 
disseminated through trusted academic outlets and publishers that meet best practice 
expectations for the discipline. In line with their responsibilities under The Code, researchers 
must actively avoid publishing with, or providing editorial services for, predatory publishers. 
10.6 
The same research findings must not be published or disseminated in multiple outputs 
unless: 
10.6.1 The subsequent output builds on the original output and is significantly different 
and novel in research contribution, scale, and/or outcomes. 
10.6.2 The subsequent output is a reprint with clear and appropriate citation of the original 
output and has permission from the copyright holder, for example foreign language 
translations of existing work or inclusion in an anthology or edited book of key 
works. 
10.6.3 The research findings are disseminated to a non-academic audience and the new 
work appropriately cites the original output and makes clear what the new 
contribution comprises, for example public engagement and media content, 
accessible publications for an industry/sector, community, or general public 
audience.  
10.7 
A researcher must not submit substantially similar work to more than one publisher without 
disclosing this to the publishers at the time of submission, unless this is expressly permitted 
by the publisher. 
10.8 
A breach of the principles and responsibilities relating to publication and dissemination of 
research outlined in this Guideline are breaches of the University’s Code of Conduct: 
Research. This includes a failure to report concerns or suspected breaches. 
10.9 
All complaints of a breach and/or reporting of a concern or suspected breach will be 
managed in accordance with the University’s Procedure: Managing and Investigating 
Breaches of the Code of Conduct: Research. 
10.10 Where a complaint has been made and is subject to formal investigation, the Pro Vice 
Chancellor, Research may determine that steps should be taken to halt a publication process 
or otherwise suspend the public dissemination of any research related document (including 
research outputs, curriculum vitae, grant or job applications or other public statements. 
11 DEFINITIONS 
11.1 
For the purpose of this Guideline, the definitions outlined in the Code of Conduct: Research 
apply.  
11.2 
In addition, the following definitions apply to this Guideline: 
11.2.1 Author means an individual who has made a significant intellectual or scholarly 
contribution to research and its output and who has agreed to be listed as an 
author1. 
11.2.2 Research output or output communicates or makes available the products of 
research and include (but are not limited to) journal articles, book chapters, books, 
conference papers, reports, performances or exhibitions and may be in hardcopy, 
 
