Effective Date: 14 June 2024 
 
electronic or other form.  A research output is one that meets the Excellence in 
Research for Australia output specifications.  An output (or non-research output) is 
one that does not meet the Excellence in Research for Australia output 
specifications but that still communicates or is related to research or scholarship. 
11.2.3 Outlet is the channel of dissemination of an output, for example the outlet of a 
journal article output is the journal, the outlet of a book chapter output is the edited 
book.  
11.2.4 Open access means making scholarly outputs or research related documents freely 
available online with licences that allow for free access and use. See 
https://library.nd.edu.au/open_access. 
11.2.5 Self-publishing refers to the dissemination or public release of outputs or research 
related documents without a publisher or publishing entity taking responsibility for 
editorial scrutiny, robust independent peer review by qualified experts, or other 
method of ensuring scientific rigour and integrity.  Examples of self-publishing can 
include but are not limited to articles on non-curated online platforms such as 
ResearchGate, books printed by print-on-demand services with little or no editorial 
oversight such as Amazon Books, or dissemination through personal or University 
websites. 
11.2.6 Predatory publisher refers to journal, book, or conference publishers with 
intentional or non-intentional practices below acceptable standards for academic 
publishing.  Examples include but are not limited to inadequate peer review 
processes, lack of transparency in article costs, manuscripts are accepted and 
published with little or no review, or information in or about the outlets are 
misleading or false.  Further information can be found at Think Check Submit. 
11.2.7 High quality outlet/publisher refers to journals and publishers that are highly 
regarded by the relevant discipline for publishing scientifically and/or 
methodologically rigorous, nationally or internationally influential research that 
significantly contributes to the research discipline.  Indicators of quality include but 
are not limited to rigorous peer review and/or editorial processes, high standards 
for manuscript acceptance, high citation rates suggesting research is contributing 
to and influencing the discipline, clear requirements for ethics and integrity for both 
research and publishing, and high levels of trust within the discipline. 
11.2.8 Professional audience outlet refers to journals, books, or conferences aimed at 
communication or translating research for a professional, practitioner, clinical, 
industry, or other non-research audience.  These outlets often contain a mix of peer 
reviewed and non-peer reviewed articles which may report research, technical or 
practical discussions, and professional news.  Although original research and 
research translation can be communicated through these outlets, they are often 
not considered to be high quality or high prestige research outlets by other 
researchers due to the intended non-academic audience. 
 
 
 
