Course Administration
Financial and academic penalty dates Students who withdraw from the course must do so by
certain dates to avoid financial and/or academic penalty. Census dates for each semester or
term are located on the University Calendar.
Course feesFor information about your course fees, visit the Fees page if you are a domestic
student, and the International Fees page if you are international student.
Academic integrity
All students have an obligation to uphold the University community’s standards on ethical
scholarship. This means that students produce and present their own work, honestly,
truthfully, fairly and respectfully. This often involves building on the work of others and
acknowledging ‘others’ work appropriately in all forms of assessment. This means that your
work should clearly identify what is your original work, the work of others you have built on,
and the work you have used directly from others. Failure to do so constitutes a breach of
academic integrity under the provisions of the General Regulations [Chapter 8].
Working with others to complete an assessment task is not allowed unless expressly stipulated
in an assessment, or when working with others represents legitimate cooperation*. ‘Others’
includes but is not limited to, other people, contract cheating, whether paid or unpaid, and
artificial intelligence (AI) technologies. It is important to understand the risks involved and the
expectations of the university in relation to contract cheating and AI and how the use of these
may affect your studies.
The following articles from the Australian Government Tertiary Education Quality and
Standards Agency provide some important information.
Identifying avoiding and reporting illegal cheating services l Tertiary Education Quality
Standards Agency (teqsa.gov.au)
Artificial intelligence: advice for students | Tertiary Education Quality and Standards Agency
(teqsa.gov.au)
There are many ways that the University works with students to support, monitor and assess
student learning and its legitimacy. Turnitin (text-matching software), is one way the
University reviews assessment tasks for both gaps in students' understanding and skills and
potential breaches of academic integrity.
All submitted papers may be retained as source documents in the reference database solely
for detection purposes (for further information regarding how the data is used see the Turnitin
Privacy Policy.
The University also provides students with support in understanding and achieving academic
integrity through curriculum design, academic integrity training modules and Studiosity
(available through Blackboard), the University Library, and Study Success.
If you require further explanation or help, contact your Course Coordinator or the Student
Success team.
*Legitimate cooperation (appropriately working with others) is to discuss the ideas and themes
you are learning about in your studies, general approaches to and requirements of assessment
tasks, such as, research strategies or methods, instructions or assessment rubrics.
Learning support
