y, he is committed to supporting students from diverse backgrounds and"", ""L041"": ""recognising the importance of their unique lived and living experiences.A highlight of Mark’s"", ""L042"": ""work is creating a safe and inclusive space for students and providing a familiar and"", ""L043"": ""approachable presence throughout their time studying at Notre Dame.Outside of work, Mark"", ""L044"": ""enjoys spending time with his niece and nephew, discussing theatre and the arts, and"", ""L045"": ""continually expanding his knowledge by learning from the people around him."", ""L046"": ""Liz DruryAccess and Inclusion Advisor – Fremantle Campus"", ""L047"": ""Liz is an Access and Inclusion Advisor with a background in psychology and several years of"", ""L048"": ""experience providing therapeutic support to people with disability. In her current role, she"", ""L049"": ""works with students to identify and implement reasonable adjustments that promote equitable"", ""L050"": ""access to learning and support them in achieving their academic goals. She is passionate"", ""L051"": ""about fostering an inclusive and supportive environment where students feel respected, heard,"", ""L052"": ""and empowered throughout their university journey.""}","If you are a new student, please upload your supporting medical documentation when you
register and make an appointment.
If you are an existing student, you can update your medical documentation via our Student
Portal.
Existing students – Access and Inclusion student portal
If you have previously registered with Access and Inclusion and have a Learning Access Plan
(‘LAP’) in place you may access your current LAP within the Access and Inclusion student
portal.
You can use the student portal to view your LAP, make a further appointment with an Access
and Inclusion Advisor and request additional adjustments. You may be required to provide
additional supporting medical documentation for additional adjustments.
Access Student Portal
Meet the Team
Fiona DarcySenior Access and Inclusion Advisor – Sydney Campus
Fiona has been working as an Access and Inclusion Advisor in higher education since 2002.
During this time, Fiona has supported thousands of students with disability, medical conditions
and mental health conditions across two universities to participate equitably in their studies.
Fiona finds great joy and satisfaction in accompanying students on their journey from
enrolment to graduation and post-university employment.Prior to becoming an Access and
Inclusion Advisor, Fiona worked in workplace planning and equity, learning and development,
and volunteer management. Fiona has a Social Science (Honours) degree in Disability as well
as a Master of Arts by research degree. Fiona’s master’s thesis was focussed on the
motivations for volunteering at Mary Mackillop Place. Fiona completed this thesis while
employed as the Manager of Volunteers at Mary Mackillop Place which she held for a number
of years.One of Fiona’s most notable roles was developing and delivering the disability
awareness training for over 7000 staff and volunteers working in the Paralympic Village during
the Sydney 2000 Olympics and Paralympics as well as acting as a Venue Staffing Manager at
the Village during the Games.Fiona enjoys spending time with family and friends, cooking,
reading, and conducting family history. Fiona has also been known to enjoy jet boating,
zorbing, and leaping off tall buildings like the Sky Tower in Auckland.
Mark AndersonAccess and Inclusion Advisor – Sydney Campus
Mark, a Notre Dame alumnus, greatly values the university experience that shaped him,
particularly its supportive learning environment, strong sense of community, and opportunities
for personal and academic growth. With degrees in Counselling and Acting, he brings a
genuine curiosity about the human experience, combined with humility, empathy, and
sensitivity.Having worked in Access and Inclusion for over a year, Mark is passionate about
empowering students to develop autonomy and confidence as they navigate their university
journey. His strong interpersonal skills and compassionate approach enable him to build
meaningful relationships with students. Balancing professionalism with warmth and
approachability, he is committed to supporting students from diverse backgrounds and
recognising the importance of their unique lived and living experiences.A highlight of Mark’s
work is creating a safe and inclusive space for students and providing a familiar and
approachable presence throughout their time studying at Notre Dame.Outside of work, Mark
enjoys spending time with his niece and nephew, discussing theatre and the arts, and
continually expanding his knowledge by learning from the people around him.
Liz DruryAccess and Inclusion Advisor – Fremantle Campus
Liz is an Access and Inclusion Advisor with a background in psychology and several years of
experience providing therapeutic support to people with disability. In her current role, she
works with students to identify and implement reasonable adjustments that promote equitable
access to learning and support them in achieving their academic goals. She is passionate
about fostering an inclusive and supportive environment where students feel respected, heard,
and empowered throughout their university journey.
