(f) HDR academic misconduct cases must be handled in accordance with the procedures set out in 
Section 2 of Schedule 3.  
 
9 
Part 6 – Procedural Fairness  
9.1 
The University’s Academic Misconduct process ensures a coursework student or HDR student alleged 
to have engaged in academic misconduct is afforded procedural fairness. Procedural fairness 
involves:  
(a) a student’s right to know the details of any allegation against them; 
(b) the opportunity to present their case; 
(c) the right to be treated without bias; and  
(d) a decision made on an objective and unbiased assessment of the evidence.  
9.2 
Coursework students / HDR students are given the opportunity to respond to any concerns raised, to 
be advised of any information or material available to the decision. 
10 
Part 7 – Authority and Delegations 
10.1 The authority of decision-makers for imposing a penalty for academic misconduct is aligned with the 
powers prescribed in the Regulations for Student Conduct and Discipline. 
10.2 A decision-maker may delegate the investigation of an alleged academic misconduct case, and the 
powers of decision-making to a delegated nominee. 
10.3 The decision-makers for imposing a penalty for academic misconduct and for considering an appeal 
are set out in the table below (Table 1). 
10.4 Where the decision-maker has a real or perceived conflict of interest (e.g., decision-maker is a friend, 
supervisor, or family member of the student or the School Academic Integrity Officer is the Unit 
Coordinator for a unit where there is an alleged Level 2 academic misconduct in the first or second 
instance), alternative arrangements for decision-making may occur subject to approval by the Head of 
School or delegate. 
10.5 In relation to an appeal against findings of academic misconduct, and/or the penalty imposed in such 
cases, the existing Regulations for Student Conduct and Discipline,  details a student's right of appeal 
against a decision of a staff member. A written appeal must be submitted within ten University working 
days of notification to the next most senior staff member or body as set out in the table above.  
10.6 An appeal may be referred to an external authority, where available, in accordance with the 
Regulations for Student Conduct and Discipline and any applicable local law or regulatory 
requirement. For students undertaking study through an offshore campus or other approved delivery 
location, applicable external pathways may be identified in a University-approved local procedure or 
schedule. 
 
