1. MINOR ACADEMIC MISCONDUCT – LEVEL 1 
1.1. 
An academic misconduct incident is deemed MINOR where the activity may be reasonably judged to result from careless practices and/or neglect of specific 
guidelines relating to assessment requirements by students, whose outcome compromises the purpose of an assessment to a limited extent only. 
1.2. 
The activity does not include relatively trivial breaches by an entry-level student in their first 24 points of study in a course, which in the opinion of the relevant unit 
coordinator may routinely occur in the course of learning the techniques, methodologies and presentation conventions within an area or discipline. 
1.3. 
Instances of Level 1 minor academic misconduct may arise most often, although not exclusively, in relation to first year undergraduate student assessment items.  
Examples of minor academic misconduct may include but are not limited to: 
(a) minor plagiarism; 
(b) minor copying of material, such as copying one or two sentences including copying where a student utilises a verbatim transcription in their notes and 
presents it as their own words; 
(c) collusion resulting in minor plagiarism 
  
2. MODERATE ACADEMIC MISCONDUCT – LEVEL 2 
2.1. 
An academic misconduct incident is deemed MODERATE where the activity may be reasonably judged to be a moderate breach of ethical scholarship and 
includes (but is not limited to):  
(a)  moderate plagiarism (refer Policy Section (5)(d), in an assessment item other than a thesis or dissertation; 
(b) recycling an item of assessment from one unit and re-submitting it in complete or substantial form for another assessment; 
(c) fabricating or falsifying data, analyses, and/or experimental results or sources of information in an assessment item other than a thesis or dissertation; 
(d) colluding with another student about assessable work and representing that as individual work when such collusion has not been specified as acceptable 
within unit outlines or other assessment requirements; 
(e) receipt of unauthorised editorial assistance. 
 
3. MAJOR ACADEMIC MISCONDUCT – LEVEL 3 
3.1. 
An academic misconduct incident is deemed MAJOR where the activity may be reasonably judged to be a serious and substantial breach of ethical scholarship 
and includes (but is not limited to): 
(a) cheating in examinations 
(b) major plagiarism particularly, in a dissertation; 
(c) fabricating or falsifying data, analyses, and/or experimental results or sources of information  in a thesis or dissertation;  
(d) submitting the work of another person as one’s own. 
 
Forms available via ADVOCATE 
Form 1– Notice of Academic Counselling (NAC)) (issued to offenders only in the first instance and in the first 48 points of study 
Form 2 – Coursework Academic Misconduct Investigation and Recording (Cwk-AMIR) 
 
Note 1: Consequences for academic misconduct in dissertation units undertaken as part of coursework course are dealt in accordance with this Schedule (Schedule 1) for 
academic misconduct in honours / master’s dissertation units undertaken as part of coursework study.  
 
Note 2: Dissertation means the output of research in a course in which the output constitutes at least 25 per cent but less than 66.6 per cent of the requirements of the 
course in question. Whereas a thesis, which is part of HDR, means the output of research in a course in which the output constitutes more than 66.6 per cent per cent of 
the requirements of the course in question. 
