EVA Submission 
Eligibility check 
is a procedure that requires students to conduct a self-check for determining the 
point at which they are not permitted to submit an EVA. See Section B of Policy 
Procedure for more details. 
exceptional 
circumstances 
mean circumstances not ordinarily encountered or anticipated, which are beyond 
the control of the individual concerned and which are of such severity or gravity 
that they may impact adversely on the individual’s capacity to perform in an 
assessment. 
health practitioner 
means a health practitioner registered with an applicable professional registration 
body in the jurisdiction in which the health service is provided, or another health 
professional recognised by the University for the purposes of this policy. 
participation 
involves students engaging actively in non-lecture based interactive classes or 
equivalent. Mere attendance does not constitute active participation and is not 
assessed. 
reasonable adjustments 
as defined in the Disability Discrimination Act 1992, means “an adjustment to be 
made by a person is a reasonable adjustment unless making the adjustment 
would impose an unjustifiable hardship on the person”. For the purpose of this 
policy, reasonable adjustments, which does not include mark adjustment, is 
defined as a process to help students with disability to minimise the impact of 
certain adverse and unforeseen circumstances on their progression and 
performance in units of study. These may include special examinations, 
alternative exam arrangements, and replacement exams. 
summative assessment 
is assessment of learning meaning any assessment that contributes to the final 
grade/mark of a unit to provide a measure of student performance in relation to 
the learning outcomes and assessment criteria. 
University 
Is The University of Western Australia,  
3 
Scope 
3.1 
This policy applies to all coursework students and students undertaking coursework components of a 
Research Training Program (RTP) compliant course. 
3.2 
This policy applies to students undertaking study through an offshore campus, transnational education 
arrangement or other approved delivery location, subject to any applicable local laws, regulatory 
requirements and University-approved local procedures or schedules. 
4 
General Principles 
4.1 
The University is committed to equity and fairness in supporting all aspects of student learning and 
welfare. 
4.2 
Applications for EVA are made and assessed having regard to the Student Code of Conduct and the 
Support for Students Policy (UP23/14). 
4.3 
Applicants and assessors must observe the principles of equity and academic integrity. 
4.4 
A student who seeks to gain an unfair advantage in assessment by submitting an application for EVA 
that is not genuine is subject to appropriate disciplinary action in accordance with the Regulations for 
Student Conduct and Discipline, whether or not the application for EVA is subsequently withdrawn. 
4.5 
Any person with access to an application for EVA or any supporting documentation is responsible for 
respecting confidentiality and the student’s entitlement to privacy protection. 
4.6 
Students with disability who are entitled to reasonable adjustments under applicable disability 
discrimination, disability services or equivalent local legislation must seek approval of their reasonable 
adjustments through UniAccess or another University-approved academic adjustment process. Such 
students are not precluded from applying for EVA under this policy, subject to meeting the eligibility 
requirements under Section 5 of this policy. 
