EVA must submit a complete application as soon as possible but no later than three calendar days 
after the date on which the work for assessment was due or class held, or provide evidence to 
substantiate incapacity to meet the EVA submission deadline.  
8.2 
A student cannot request a review and appeal of a decision to reject an application for EVA if the 
requirement specified in 8.1 is not met.   
8.3 
Where an assessment item is worth 10 per cent or less and comprises a series of weighted 
assessment tasks (e.g. weekly quizzes), an application for EVA can be made only on completion of 
the assessment item rather than the individual assessment task. The completion of the assessment 
item is determined by the due date of the last assessment task.  
8.4 
Before applying for EVA, students must observe the EVA Submission Eligibility Check in accordance 
with the policy procedures set out in Appendix A. 
8.5 
Students must provide clear and appropriate evidentiary support for any EVA application, in English or 
accompanied by an English translation from an appropriately accredited or recognised translator, that 
demonstrates the duration and impact of the exceptional circumstances on their academic 
performance in, or preparation for, an assessment. Evidentiary support must be provided in line with 
Table 1 for any EVA application,or any University-approved local procedure or schedule.  
8.6 
A student is responsible for providing all documentation at the time of submitting an application for 
EVA. Where a student faces delays that are beyond their control in obtaining relevant supporting 
documentation, exceptions may be made by the assessor.   
9 
Evidentiary Support 
9.1 
Evidence supporting an application for EVA must be provided in accordance with Table 1 of this 
policy. 
9.2 
Except in the case of examinations and other timed assessments medical certificates are not required 
for illness lasting one or two days. Where a medical certificate serves as supporting documentation for 
illness lasting three days or more, it must: 
(a) be based on consultation with a registered health practitioner or on the health practitioner’s 
knowledge of the student’s medical history in the case of a temporary exacerbation of an existing 
medical condition; 
(b) include the health practitioner’s professional registration details, provider number or equivalent 
identifier, where applicable, duration of impact, signature and date of consultation; 
(c) in situations where the medical certificate is issued for extended periods of seven days or more, or 
where a student has presented medical certificates totalling seven days or more within a teaching 
period, be accompanied by a UWA Verification Form to be completed by a health practitioner.  
9.3 
Students submitting an EVA must include a declaration that both the application and the supporting 
evidence are true and genuine, and that they are aware that submitting false or misleading information 
is a breach of the University’s Regulations for Student Conduct and Discipline. 
9.4 
Any evidence submitted may be investigated, and false or misleading evidence may result in a penalty 
imposed in accordance with the Regulations for Student Conduct and Discipline. 
9.5 
Falsifying evidence may constitute misconduct and may also constitute an offence under applicable 
law. 
10 
Assessing an EVA 
10.1 Assessors must: 
(a) ensure applicants have met the eligibility and application requirements outlined in sections 5 and 8 
of this policy.  
(b) consider subsequent application(s) where an applicant has lodged further application(s) due to 
worsening of the initial circumstances; and 
 
