University Policy on Exceptional Variation of Assessment (EVA) 
GUIDANCE NOTES FOR STUDENTS 
 
These Guidance Notes, which must be read in conjunction with the EVA policy, are intended to assist 
students’ understanding of the intent of and principles underpinning the EVA Policy. They are not to be 
viewed as a substitute for the policy and are not mandated.     
 
 
What are the improvements to the policy that should help students without the need to apply for 
EVA? 
1. Waiver of late penalties for the first 48 hours after an assessment is due. An assessment submitted 
within 48 hours of the deadline will be marked as late, but no penalty applied. A penalty of 5 per 
cent per day will accrue throughout, being applied as a 15 per cent penalty on day three and a 
further 5 per cent per day until day 7, after which the assessment will not be accepted. (Note that 
where EVA is granted, the late penalty waiver does not apply to the extended due date.) 
2. For any assessment worth 10 per cent or less that is composed of multiple evenly weighted tasks 
across the semester, only the top 80 per cent of marks will be counted (unless otherwise noted in 
the unit outline). If you still need to apply for EVA because you have missed more than 20 per cent 
of the tasks and you are able to demonstrate that your performance was significantly impacted with 
regard to the whole assessment item, then that application can only be submitted on or after the 
due date for the final task in that semester.  (Note: keep your documentation from throughout the 
semester.) 
 
Do you have a disability, mental health or medical condition affecting your studies?  
Whether you have anxiety or depression or a learning difficulty, our UniAccess team supports you to 
stay healthy during your studies and perform at your academic best, no matter whether your condition 
is ongoing, temporary (eg a broken arm), or episodic. UniAccess is a safe place to meet confidentially 
with highly qualified advisers who will listen, provide information, and work with you to come up with 
solutions. We’ll also keep your health documentation private and secure, and it will not be seen by your 
School, home university, or government. UniAccess will work with students to develop a UniAccess 
Academic Adjustment Plan (UAAP). 
 
If you have a UAAP and experience an exacerbation of your registered condition meaning you require 
further accommodation beyond what is listed in your UAAP, you should apply for further reasonable 
adjustments, using your UAAP as your supporting documentation.  
 
If you have a UAAP and require accommodations for reasons not related to your registered condition 
(that is a short-term medical condition or non-medical condition) you should apply for an EVA with 
documentation as set out in Table 1 of the EVA policy. You cannot use your UAAP as supporting 
documentation. 
 
Students experiencing medical emergencies in relation to the above should apply for EVA in the first 
instance.  
 
What is going to be handled differently? 
• 
Attendance of at least 80 per cent at non-lecture-based interactive classes or equivalent will be 
required where these classes are described as compulsory in the unit outline. (Note: check your 
unit outline to see whether your unit has a higher attendance threshold.) Lecture attendance 
remains non-compulsory in most cases. 
• 
Assessment items that are worth 10 per cent or less and comprise a series of evenly weighted tasks 
are not eligible for EVA until the completion of all the tasks. Examples include weekly quizzes and 
laboratory worksheets. This does not include assessed participation, which is assessed holistically 
over the teaching period. Students may not apply for EVA for missing individual classes. 
