University Policy on Exceptional Variation of Assessment (EVA) 
GUIDANCE NOTES FOR UNIT COORDINATORS (UCs) 
 
These Guidance Notes, which must be read in conjunction with the EVA and Assessment policies, address 
two key aims. The first aim is to explain the implications of the EVA policy for UWA Assessment policy principles 
and practice. The second aim is to assist Unit Coordinators and teaching staff to understand the principles 
underpinning the EVA policy for implementation and provision of appropriate advice to students. These 
Guidance Notes are not to be viewed as a substitute for the related policies.     
 
A. Implications of EVA for Assessment Policy Principles 
Section 5.3(2)(e) The following consequences of late or non-submission must be made clear to students: 
(i) A penalty of five per cent of the possible mark allocated for the assessment item is deducted per day.  
     (1) A penalty waiver is granted for the first two 24-hour blocks (including weekends and public holidays) 
          following the due date of an assessment. This excludes tests/laboratories/mid-semester and final 
          exams administered centrally or by schools. 
     (2) Where a student submits after the first two 24-hour blocks, the penalty is applied per day for days 
          1-7 (including weekends and public holidays). For example, a student, who has submitted a piece 
          of work for an assessment item late by three days and was awarded a mark of 45 out of a possible 
          mark of 501, receives after penalty a mark of 37.5 out of 50 (a mark of 2.5 is deducted per day from 
          the mark obtained). An assessment that is submitted after seven days including the two days of 
          penalty waiver is not accepted. Each 24-hour block is recorded from the time the assignment is due.  
     (3) Where there are a number of tasks within an assessment item, the late penalty may be applied  
          holistically to the assessment item after all tasks have been completed. 
 
• 
The rationale for providing a penalty waiver is that it will assist students to manage their workload (e.g. 
multiple assessments due on the same day, work/care commitments). 
• 
An assessment submitted within 48 hours of the deadline will be registered as a late submission, but 
no penalty will be applied. A penalty of 5% per day will accrue throughout, being applied as a 15% 
penalty on day 3 and a further 5% per day until day 7, after which the assessment will not be accepted. 
• 
Students do not need to notify their UC if they intend to make use of the penalty waiver, and UCs do 
not need to monitor uptake. 
• 
Note that where EVA is granted, the two-day penalty waiver does not apply to the extended due date 
– i.e. if the student submits after the extended deadline, the penalty applies to each day after the 
extended due date. 
• 
Note on UniAccess Adjustment Plans (UAAP): 
o 
UAAPs provide students with disabilities or mental or physical health conditions that affect their 
studies with reasonable adjustments. 
o 
Students are required to provide their UCs with their UAAP if they wish to make use of the 
reasonable adjustments. 
o 
Where a student with a UAAP makes use of an extension adjustment (e.g. for a written 
assessment), the two-day penalty waiver does not apply to the extended due date – i.e. if the 
student submits after the extended deadline, the penalty applies to each day after the extended 
due date. 
 
1 If expressed in percentage terms, this example may be illustrated as follows: The original mark of 90 percent is reduced 
to 75 percent after 15 percent penalty applied.   
