sult for the assessment item. This principle applies across all units unless the unit"", ""L036"": ""outline provides otherwise in the case where the unit forms part of a sequence in a professionally"", ""L037"": ""accredited course or unless approval has been granted by the Pro Vice-Chancellor (Academic) to Unit"", ""L038"": ""Coordinators to opt out. Any approval for opting out is captured and maintained in a register which must"", ""L039"": ""be reviewed biennially to monitor trends and patterns."", ""L040"": ""• Examples of such an assessment item are weekly quizzes or laboratory worksheets."", ""L041"": ""• Where participation is assessed holistically on the basis of student activity/contributions over the"", ""L042"": ""course of the semester rather than on a per-session basis, this section does not apply. Students are"", ""L043"": ""allowed to miss up to 20% of non-lecture-based classes unless a higher threshold has been set by the"", ""L044"": ""UC and communicated in the Unit Outline.""}","o 
If a student with a UAAP experiences an exacerbation of their registered condition and requires 
further accommodation beyond what is listed in their UAAP, they are able to apply for further 
reasonable adjustments, using their UAAP as their supporting documentation.  
o 
If a student with a UAAP requires accommodations for reasons not related to their registered 
condition, they should apply for EVA but cannot use their UAAP as supporting documentation. 
Instead, they should provide documentation as set out in Table 1 of the EVA policy. 
 
Section 5.2(3)(c) Attendance of at least 80 per cent at non-lecture based interactive classes or equivalent 
is required where these classes are described as compulsory in the unit outline. Students at risk of not 
meeting this requirement may be referred to their advising Student Office.  
• 
This provision obviates the need for a ‘participation’ mark which is mainly intended to encourage 
students to attend class. 
• 
The classes must be described as compulsory in the LMS Unit Outline, or this provision will not apply.  
• 
A UC who is concerned about a student’s attendance may refer the student to the relevant Student 
Office (but is not required to). It would make sense to refer a student early in the semester when early 
intervention will be possible. The Student Office will counsel the student about their obligation to attend 
but might also identify the need for academic and/or wellbeing supports. 
• 
There is no penalty for non-attendance. This provision is intended, over time, to change the culture at 
UWA by educating students about the learning that occurs in non-lecture based interactive classes. 
• 
UCs may impose a higher or lower attendance threshold if this is clearly stated in the LMS unit outline. 
80% was chosen because it provides a balance between flexibility for students and sufficient 
attendance to meet learning outcomes. 80% corresponds to 8 out of 10 or roughly 10 out of 12 weeks 
of attendance at compulsory classes. 
• 
Attendance at lectures remains non-compulsory as recordings are available for students. This provides 
flexibility for students with work and personal commitments. 
 
Section 5.2(3)(d) Allocation of marks for participation is based on a marking scheme or other documented 
assessment methods (e.g. submitted worksheets for laboratory-based classes). 
• 
In all cases where participation is assessed (i.e. allocated marks) it must be made explicitly clear to 
students how they are being assessed. This requires the UC to publish a marking scheme (e.g. rubric, 
marking criteria) or spell out to students in the LMS unit outline what they need to do to complete this 
assessment item successfully (e.g. preparation, submission of worksheets). 
• 
Marks for participation must not be awarded for mere attendance.  
• 
Ideally, formative feedback on participation will be given by the mid-way point of the semester. 
 
Section 5.2(4)(e) Where an assessment item with a weighting of 10 per cent or less comprises a series 
of evenly weighted assessment tasks, the top 80 per cent of marks of the assessment tasks is used to 
calculate the overall result for the assessment item. This principle applies across all units unless the unit 
outline provides otherwise in the case where the unit forms part of a sequence in a professionally 
accredited course or unless approval has been granted by the Pro Vice-Chancellor (Academic) to Unit 
Coordinators to opt out. Any approval for opting out is captured and maintained in a register which must 
be reviewed biennially to monitor trends and patterns. 
• 
Examples of such an assessment item are weekly quizzes or laboratory worksheets. 
• 
Where participation is assessed holistically on the basis of student activity/contributions over the 
course of the semester rather than on a per-session basis, this section does not apply. Students are 
allowed to miss up to 20% of non-lecture-based classes unless a higher threshold has been set by the 
UC and communicated in the Unit Outline. 
