then the eight marks are used to calculate the final mark for"", ""L019"": ""this assessment item."", ""L020"": ""(iii) If a student completes fewer than eight quizzes, then the remaining quizzes are awarded a mark"", ""L021"": ""of zero and the top eight marks (including any zeros) are used to calculate the final mark for this"", ""L022"": ""assessment item."", ""L023"": ""(iv) A student may apply for EVA at the end of semester if they have grounds and can demonstrate"", ""L024"": ""their performance was significantly impacted over the whole assessment item (i.e. all, or at least"", ""L025"": ""most, quizzes). If EVA is granted, then 5.2(f) of the Policy on EVA may be applied to calculate an"", ""L026"": ""amended mark for this assessment item."", ""L027"": ""B. Understanding Exceptional Variation of Assessment (EVA)"", ""L028"": ""Section 5.2 The University does not provide EVA for conditions or circumstances that result from"", ""L029"": ""planned activities or a student’s actions or inactions. Examples of circumstances that are not eligible"", ""L030"": ""grounds for EVA include:"", ""L031"": ""(a) misreading/misunderstanding unit outline, exam timetable or assessment deadline;"", ""L032"": ""(b) any holiday travel;"", ""L033"": ""(c) attending social events (e.g. birthdays, concerts), or sporting events (excluding participation in"", ""L034"": ""University games / elite competitions);"", ""L035"": ""(d) usual rostered work commitments or professional experience organised independently by the"", ""L036"": ""student;"", ""L037"": ""(e) student workloads and stress associated with assessments;"", ""L038"": ""(f) overloading with additional units in a given teaching period;"", ""L039"": ""(g) computer and/or IT (Information Technology) failure, unless experienced during an online exam"", ""L040"": ""or equivalent (e.g. major folio submission) or an unplanned computer update occurs during an"", ""L041"": ""online assessment;"", ""L042"": ""(h) volunteering commitments; and"", ""L043"": ""(i) commencing a unit late."", ""L044"": ""• Note that the examples of circumstances that are not eligible grounds for EVA only apply when the"", ""L045"": ""circumstance results from a student's action or inaction."", ""L046"": ""Section 6.2 The possible outcomes of an EVA include the following:"", ""L047"": ""(e) exemption of assessment and reassignment of marks to other assessments, provided the exempted"", ""L048"": ""assessment is worth more than ten per cent and the unit learning outcomes are met by other assessment"", ""L049"": ""items. In an event where the reassignment of marks is allocated to a final exam, the weighting of the final"", ""L050"": ""exam must be aligned to the requirements set out in the University Policy on Assessment.""}","• 
Teaching Ops can assist UCs to set up a column in the LMS Grade Centre to automatically calculate 
the result for the assessment item based on the top 80% of marks of the assessment tasks (as close 
to 80% as possible, depending on the number of tasks) as follows: 
o 
Where an assessment item worth 10% or less comprises between 7 and 12 equally weighted 
tasks, the bottom 2 marks (including any 0s) will be excluded from the calculation of the overall 
result for the assessment item. 
o 
Where an assessment item worth 10% or less comprises 6 or fewer equally weighted tasks, the 
bottom 1 mark (including any 0s) will be excluded from the calculation of the overall result for the 
assessment item. 
o 
Where the assessment item includes only a small number of weighted tasks, UCs should consider 
increasing the weighting above 10% or applying to the PVC-A to opt out of this clause. 
• 
If the assessment item is worth more than 10 per cent, the UC determines whether marks for all tasks 
comprising the assessment item will be counted. 
 
• 
Example of implementation:  An assessment item worth 10% comprises ten evenly weighted quizzes 
across the semester. 
(i) 
If a student completes more than eight quizzes, then the top eight marks are used to calculate 
the final mark for this assessment item. 
(ii) If a student completes eight quizzes, then the eight marks are used to calculate the final mark for 
this assessment item. 
(iii) If a student completes fewer than eight quizzes, then the remaining quizzes are awarded a mark 
of zero and the top eight marks (including any zeros) are used to calculate the final mark for this 
assessment item. 
(iv) A student may apply for EVA at the end of semester if they have grounds and can demonstrate 
their performance was significantly impacted over the whole assessment item (i.e. all, or at least 
most, quizzes). If EVA is granted, then 5.2(f) of the Policy on EVA may be applied to calculate an 
amended mark for this assessment item. 
 
B. Understanding Exceptional Variation of Assessment (EVA) 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
• 
Note that the examples of circumstances that are not eligible grounds for EVA only apply when the 
circumstance results from a student's action or inaction. 
Section 6.2 The possible outcomes of an EVA include the following: 
(e) exemption of assessment and reassignment of marks to other assessments, provided the exempted 
assessment is worth more than ten per cent and the unit learning outcomes are met by other assessment 
items. In an event where the reassignment of marks is allocated to a final exam, the weighting of the final 
exam must be aligned to the requirements set out in the University Policy on Assessment. 
Section 5.2 The University does not provide EVA for conditions or circumstances that result from 
planned activities or a student’s actions or inactions. Examples of circumstances that are not eligible 
grounds for EVA include: 
(a) 
misreading/misunderstanding unit outline, exam timetable or assessment deadline; 
(b) 
any holiday travel;  
(c) 
attending social events (e.g. birthdays, concerts), or sporting events (excluding participation in 
University games / elite competitions); 
(d) 
usual rostered work commitments or professional experience organised independently by the 
student;  
(e) 
student workloads and stress associated with assessments; 
(f) 
overloading with additional units in a given teaching period; 
(g) 
computer and/or IT (Information Technology) failure, unless experienced during an online exam 
or equivalent (e.g. major folio submission) or an unplanned computer update occurs during an 
online assessment;  
(h) 
volunteering commitments; and 
(i) 
commencing a unit late.  
