Example: a student misses a 15% mid-semester test, and a deferred test is not available.  The final 
exam is worth 45%.  If all learning outcomes are met by the other assessments, then the 15% from 
the missed test could be reassigned to the final exam, which is a similar assessment task. Note that 
the final exam would be worth 60% for this student, which complies with the University Policy on 
Assessment. 
Section 6.2 The possible outcomes of an EVA application include the following:  
(f) aggregation or averaging of marks obtained for completed assessment items to achieve a final 
percentage mark for the unit, provided the student has met all the learning outcomes in the unit unless 
specifically stated otherwise in the LMS (Learning Management System) Unit Outline where the unit forms 
part of an accredited sequence. 
• 
Example 1: an assessment item comprises three labs, each worth 10% of the final unit mark.  A 
student receives EVA for one of the labs and there is no make-up lab.  If all learning outcomes are 
met by the other assessments, then the marks attained in the two labs could be averaged and 
moderated to become a mark out of 30, e.g. Lab 1 = 7/10, Lab 2 = EVA, Lab 3 = 8/10.  Average lab 
mark out of 30 = [(7+8)/20]*30 = 22.5/30 
• 
Example 2: a student receives EVA for an assessment worth 15% of the unit.  If all learning outcomes 
are met by the other assessments, then the student’s final mark out of 100 could be determined as 
[(mark out of 85)/85]*100. 
Section 6.6 In cases where a student who is part of a group assessment receives approval for an EVA 
application, the outcome must be considered in consultation with the Unit Coordinator. The possible 
outcomes include: 
(a) extension to an assessment deadline for the whole group; 
(b) provision of alternative assessment for the individual student; and 
i. 
redistribution of the assigned work to the rest of the group members with the possibility of an 
extension to the assessment deadline; and/or 
ii. 
a reduced assessment task for the group. 
(c) Reassignment of group mark to other assessments if assessment task/item is weighted 10 per cent 
or less. This may be relevant for in-class group activities such as group presentations 
(d) Where a group member has a UAAP in place, any approved adjustment may be extended to the whole 
group within reason at the discretion of the UC. 
 
• 
UCs should consider which outcome would be most appropriate in the context of their unit. 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
• 
The phrase 'timed assessments' includes any assessment that has to be taken by the student at a 
specific time. 
 
Section 9.2 Except in the case of examinations and other timed assessments medical certificates are 
not required for illness lasting one or two days. Where a medical certificate serves as supporting 
documentation for illness lasting three days or more, it must: 
(a) 
be based on consultation with a registered health practitioner or on the health practitioner’s 
knowledge of the student’s medical history in the case of a temporary exacerbation of an existing 
medical condition; 
(b) 
include the Health Practitioner’s Provider Number, duration of impact, signature and date of 
consultation; 
(c) 
in situations where the medical certificate is issued for extended periods of seven days or more, 
or where a student has presented medical certificates totalling seven days or more within a 
teaching period, be accompanied by a UWA Verification Form to be completed by a health 
practitioner.  
