": ""(iii) teacher-assessment;"", ""L043"": ""(iv) collaborative-assessment; and"", ""L044"": ""(b) summative assessment which must also have a formative function."", ""L045"": ""5.2 Minimum unit assessment requirements"", ""L046"": ""(1) All formal assessments contributing to the University’s award must be:"", ""L047"": ""(a) conducted in English, except in the case of units teaching languages other than English; and"", ""L048"": ""(b) designed to measure or evaluate the student’s knowledge and capabilities."", ""L049"": ""(2) Each unit must have an assessment plan expressed through a written Assessment Mechanism"", ""L050"": ""Statement, which sets out clearly the way in which the final result for the unit is calculated, and a"", ""L051"": ""unit outline, published via the Learning Management System."", ""L052"": ""(3) Assessment items and methods:"", ""L053"": ""3""}","(6)
monitor and compare grade distributions across units, majors and courses by consulting and 
negotiating with other relevant schools, where relevant, and reporting annually on this 
comparison to the Pro Vice-Chancellor (Academic);
(7)
monitor school processes for safe, secure assessment submission and return, and ensure the 
quality and security of exam questions, and exam papers;
(8)
consider students' unit results and ensure they are reported to Student Services and 
Engagement for recording and publication, and carry out any other task relating to students' 
results that may be delegated to them by the school concerned; 
(9)
determine prize and award recipients, and report its decisions to the Prizes Unit within Student 
Services and Engagement; and
(10)
where necessary adjust marks in a unit in line with the policy principles that assessment is fair, 
valid, and integral to the learning process.
4.5
A school board of examiners is normally chaired by the Head of School concerned, or nominee.
4.6
A school board of examiners may delegate one or more of its functions to the Head of School or 
another member or members of the board of examiners.
4.7
A school governance document must refer to section 4.4 of this policy for identifying the role of its 
board of examiners together with any school-specific delegations.
4.8
By delegation of the Curriculum Committee, the role of the Board of Examiners of the Curriculum 
Committee is delegated to the Chair of the Curriculum Committee who deals executively with all 
related academic matters including, but not limited to, the following:
(1) the award of honours classifications, where these are not routine in nature, or relate to joint-
honours classifications for new undergraduate degree courses;
(2) academic progress in exceptional cases that have been escalated for further deliberation; and
(3) school recommendations on applications for readmission from students who have been previously 
excluded for a period of at least 12 months from an undergraduate degree course or a diploma 
course.
4.9
By delegation of the School Board or equivalent, the School Learning and Teaching Committee or 
equivalent is responsible for quality assurance in the conduct of assessment by:
(1) identifying emerging learning, assessment and teaching issues; 
(2) developing processes in relation to learning, teaching and assessment; and
(3) articulating, implementing and monitoring academic standards for learning and teaching at the 
University.
4.10
The relevant board has the authority to consider and approve exemptions as provided for in the 
following clauses of this policy: 5.2(3)(b); 5.2(4)(a); 5.2(4)(b); 5.3(2)(a); 5.4(3); 5.5(4); and 5.7(4), and 
in accordance with the procedures set out in Appendix 3.
5
Part 3: Academic Provisions For Coursework units 
5.1
Aims of Assessment:
(1) Each unit taught at the University aims to balance and integrate the provision of:
(a) formative assessment which may include:
(i)
self-assessment; 
(ii) peer-assessment;
(iii) teacher-assessment; 
(iv) collaborative-assessment; and
(b) summative assessment which must also have a formative function.
5.2
Minimum unit assessment requirements 
(1) All formal assessments contributing to the University’s award must be:
(a) conducted in English, except in the case of units teaching  languages other than English; and
(b) designed to measure or evaluate the student’s knowledge and capabilities.
(2) Each unit must have an assessment plan expressed through a written Assessment Mechanism 
Statement, which sets out clearly the way in which the final result for the unit is calculated, and a 
unit outline, published via the Learning Management System.
(3) Assessment items and methods:
