": ""(d) The assessment of a group item that is prepared and presented as a single entity within which"", ""L042"": ""the contributions of individual students are identified, is not limited to the weighting restrictions"", ""L043"": ""referred to in 5.2(4)(c)(i)."", ""L044"": ""(e) Where an assessment item with a weighting of 10 per cent or less comprises a series of"", ""L045"": ""evenly weighted assessment tasks, the top 80 per cent of marks of the assessment tasks is"", ""L046"": ""used to calculate the overall result for the assessment item. This principle applies across all"", ""L047"": ""units unless the unit outline provides otherwise in the case where the unit forms part of a"", ""L048"": ""sequence in a professionally accredited course or unless approval has been granted by the"", ""L049"": ""Pro Vice-Chancellor (Academic Excellence) to Unit Coordinators to opt out. Any approval for"", ""L050"": ""opting out is captured and maintained in a register which must be reviewed biennially to"", ""L051"": ""monitor trends and patterns*."", ""L052"": ""(5) Assessment criteria and marking schemes:"", ""L053"": ""(a) In accordance with the Higher Education Standards, each unit adopts a criterion-referenced"", ""L054"": ""method of assessment."", ""L055"": ""(b) Each assessment item has explicit assessment criteria expressed in the marking scheme and"", ""L056"": ""aligned with the learning outcomes that provide the basis for evaluating and differentiating the"", ""L057"": ""quality of a student’s work."", ""L058"": ""(c) Assessment criteria are aligned with the University grades and grade descriptors (see Part 5"", ""L059"": ""(Grading System) which provides the University grades, grade codes and corresponding"", ""L060"": ""marks and descriptors)."", ""L061"": ""4""}","(a) Assessment items must be tailored to meet unit learning outcomes that include higher order 
learning and capabilities.  
(b) Unless an exception has been granted in accordance with Part 2 (section 4.10) of this policy, 
and with the exemption of dissertation units, each six-credit point unit must employ at least 
two summative assessment items and up to three summative assessment items, unless an 
exception has been granted (or proportional number of assessment items for units with 
multiples of six credit points (e.g. four to six assessment items for twelve- credit point unit)) of 
different methods including, but not limited to, the following:
(i)
examinations and tests: selected and/or constructed response, practical and oral 
examinations;
(ii) assignments: written, oral, planning, problem-solving, reports, research-based, practice-
based, reviews, research (e.g. dissertations)
(iii) assessment based on:

observation or record of practice;

performance, creation or discussion; and

participation, which does not include attendance alone.
(c) Attendance of at least 80 per cent at non-lecture based interactive classes or equivalent is 
required where these classes are described as compulsory in the unit outline. Students at risk 
of not meeting this requirement may be referred to their advising student office*.
(d) Allocation of marks for participation is based on a marking scheme or other documented 
assessment methods (e.g. submitted worksheets for laboratory-based classes)*. 
(e) An assessment item may comprise a number of tasks completed at different times in a unit.
(f)
Assessments for a unit must be designed to assess student learning outcomes in the most 
efficient manner and must be proportionate to the 150 hours of student workload represented 
by the credit weighting of the unit (6 credit points). 
(g) All coursework students must be provided with formative feedback in the first half of a 
teaching period in each unit. 
(4) Assessment item weighting:
(a) An assessment item comprises at least 10 per cent and up to 70 per cent of a unit’s final 
mark. Exemption may be considered in accordance with Part 2 (section 4.10) of this policy for 
folio and project units. Assessment item weighting for dissertation units are set in accordance 
with the University Policy on Courses: Coursework Dissertation (UP15/6).
(b) Where a unit comprises a formal University examination component, the component 
constitutes at least 40 per cent and no more than 60 per cent of the unit’s final mark, unless 
an exception has been granted in accordance with Part 2 (section 4.10) of this policy. 
Dissertation units are exempted from this requirement.
(c) Where a unit comprises a group assessment task which is presented as a single entity and 
where the contributions of individual students cannot be identified:
(i)
the assessment item is limited to no more than 30 per cent of the unit’s final mark; and
(ii) all members of the group are awarded the same mark.
(d) The assessment of a group item that is prepared and presented as a single entity within which 
the contributions of individual students are identified, is not limited to the weighting restrictions 
referred to in 5.2(4)(c)(i). 
(e) Where an assessment item with a weighting of 10 per cent or less comprises a series of 
evenly weighted assessment tasks, the top 80 per cent of marks of the assessment tasks is 
used to calculate the overall result for the assessment item. This principle applies across all 
units unless the unit outline provides otherwise in the case where the unit forms part of a 
sequence in a professionally accredited course or unless approval has been granted by the 
Pro Vice-Chancellor (Academic Excellence) to Unit Coordinators to opt out. Any approval for 
opting out is captured and maintained in a register which must be reviewed biennially to 
monitor trends and patterns*.
(5) Assessment criteria and marking schemes: 
(a) In accordance with the Higher Education Standards, each unit adopts a criterion-referenced 
method of assessment. 
(b) Each assessment item has explicit assessment criteria expressed in the marking scheme and 
aligned with the learning outcomes that provide the basis for evaluating and differentiating the 
quality of a student’s work.
(c) Assessment criteria are aligned with the University grades and grade descriptors (see Part 5 
(Grading System) which provides the University grades, grade codes and corresponding 
marks and descriptors).
