(7) Where appropriate, feedback on assessment items may take the form of generic feedback to a 
student group, e.g. in the form of an ‘examiner’s report’ on each exam question or similar.
(8) Students are entitled to view their marked examination script within five University working days 
from receipt of release of results or during the first week of the next semester or trimester; 
however, this examination script remains the property of the University.
5.9
Student mobility (Study Abroad) and assessment
(1) The treatment of assessment during a period of study abroad must be clear and transparent.
(2) Results obtained overseas during approved study abroad/exchange programs are normally 
recorded on a pass/fail basis only, and normally excluded from any calculations for postgraduate 
or honours applications.
(3) Where host universities are well known, and especially where exchange agreements are in place, 
the University may recognise the grades awarded (where appropriate) and create conversion 
tables so that grades achieved abroad may be used within a WAM calculation at Level 3. If such 
tables are produced or updated, they must be presented by the Admissions Centre to the Pro 
Vice-Chancellor (Academic) for verification on an annual basis.
(4) In advance of choosing a study abroad unit, students must be informed how their marks, grades 
or credits are treated when they return.
5.10
Ownership and archiving of students’ assessed work
(1) Students hold the intellectual property inherent in all work produced for assessments.
(2) Material produced by students for assessment (essays, projects, dissertations, artworks, digital 
artefacts, etc.) may be retained pending confirmation of marks awarded by the assessor/board of 
examiners, possible reviews and appeals and quality audits. 
(3) With the exception of examination scripts, the University endeavours to return to students all 
assessed work, where applicable.
(4) With the exception of examination scripts non-digital assessment tasks that have not been 
collected by the student are retained by the University for one month after the publication of 
results after which time they may be disposed of. Arrangements for the disposal of such work are 
the responsibility of the relevant School. 
