(ii) Academic Threshold Standards: obtained a WAM (Weighted Average Mark) of at least 55; 
and
(iii) Academic Integrity Standards: has no academic integrity misconduct finding* which results 
in loss of marks in the units undertaken in a progression period in accordance with the 
Penalties Matrix (Schedule 1) of the University Policy on Academic Integrity.
OR
(b) Concession Ground (by offer): students must meet – 
(i)
Eligibility Requirement: obtained a mark range of 40 – 49 in the unit overall and/or a mark 
of at least 40 overall and a mark in the range of 40 – 49 in the failed component(s) within the 
unit; 
(ii) Academic Integrity Standards: has no finding* of academic integrity misconduct against 
them in the units undertaken in the progression period; and
(iii) Milestone Requirement: offered in the last remaining unit required for course completion 
undertaken in the final teaching period of study or where a required unit is being taught out 
and will not be offered again.
(2) Unit Coordinators seeking to opt out from providing supplementary assessment on academic 
grounds must seek approval from the Pro Vice-Chancellor (Academic Excellence).
(3) Units approved with exemption from providing supplementary assessment must be clearly 
identified and published as such in the Handbook and Unit Outlines.
(4) Any failed final assessment is checked to ensure that there are no errors in marking process and 
procedures prior to the release of results.
(5) The granting of supplementary assessment is overseen by the course advising student offices led 
by the Managers of Student Experience within Student Life or Program Directors for units taught 
during non-standard teaching period.
*Where a student is under investigation for academic misconduct but has met other requirements for a supp, the 
student should be awarded the Supp. The result of the Supp however be withheld until it is proven that there is no 
finding of academic misconduct against the student.
6.4
Formal University Exam Timetable Deviation
(1) The University offers an exam timetable deviation in recognition of religious needs and to ensure 
ongoing compliance with the Disability Discrimination Act 1992, Disability Standards for Education 
2005, and the Elite Athlete Friendly University Network Agreement. 
(2) Exam timetable deviation arrangements must not result in the lowering of academic standards or 
affect the integrity of the examination process.
(3) Exam timetable deviation must include provision for students who have technical issues during 
their online exams to apply for special consideration to sit a deferred exam. The special 
consideration is applied in accordance with the procedures set out in Appendix 3.
6.5
School Examinations
(1) Examinations held outside the official period referred to in 6.1(1)(c) include, but are not limited to, 
the following:
Other examination types
Examination period
Mid-term examinations
Mid / during semester or mid / during trimester 
examinations.
Deferred summer session examination
Following the initial summer session exam
