"", ""L043"": ""(4) Rounding marks for tasks that form part of an assessment item:"", ""L044"": ""(a) Where an assessment item is one single piece of work, a single mark is awarded."", ""L045"": ""(b) Where marks of assessment tasks are combined, the overall mark for the assessment item is"", ""L046"": ""rounded to one decimal place."", ""L047"": ""(c) Where the calculation in 33.8 above results in a decimal number whose second decimal place"", ""L048"": ""is 5 or above, the mark is rounded up to one decimal place."", ""L049"": ""(d) Example: If an assessment item has three assessment tasks of equal weighting which are"", ""L050"": ""given marks of 52.9, 61.8 and 72.7 then the final unit mark is calculated to be 62.46 and"", ""L051"": ""rounded up to 62.5."", ""L052"": ""(5) Rounding final unit mark:"", ""L053"": ""(a) A unit’s final mark is expressed as an integer."", ""L054"": ""(b) A unit’s final mark is calculated by way of combining assessment items to produce a weighted"", ""L055"": ""average mark. Such a mark is rounded to the nearest integer, such that a unit mark which has"", ""L056"": ""17""}","(b) Category 2: A unit involving group activity in which individual contributions of students cannot 
be distinguished from those of the group (for example, participation in the University 
Chorale/Orchestra and compulsory field tours);
(c) Category 3: A unit involving skill acquisition and where attendance and participation is 
sufficient (for example, use of medical equipment, legal skills such as negotiation and 
mediation);
(d) Category 4: A unit in which an acceptable case for exemption has been made for an individual 
student or an assessment item.
(2) The ungraded pass and ungraded fail grading scheme, for the categories of units referred to in 
7.2(1), must be approved by the relevant board. 
(3) Proposals for new units or changes to existing units must indicate whether the proposed grading 
scheme is ungraded pass and ungraded fail, and if so, into which of the exemption categories, 
referred to in 7.2(1), the unit falls.
(4) Ungraded fails have the value of zero in calculations of the Grade Point Average. 
7.3
Amending results and other academic decisions  
(1) The University must formally amend its records and notify students irrespective of outcome, where 
errors have occurred in the process of:
(a) calculating and reporting final mark or grade; 
(b) making academic decisions for admission to courses;
(c) assigning or removing progress status;
(d) awarding scholarships; or 
(e) similar situations where errors have been made.
(2) Where possible, the University strives to ensure that an error found to be made after formal 
notification of an error referred to in 7.3(1), does not result in any student at the University losing 
an opportunity already granted, or prevented from accessing an opportunity that may have 
otherwise been made available.
7.4
Mark calculation and rounding principles
(1) The rounding of marks is determined by the Unit Coordinator in accordance with this policy.
(2) The rounding of marks occurs when calculating the overall mark for:
(a) a number of tasks and/or sub-tasks that form part of an assessment item within a unit; and/or
(b) a number of assessment items contributing to the calculation of a final unit mark.
(3) Rounding marks for sub-tasks that form part of an assessment task:
(a) Where an assessment task is one single piece of work, a single mark is awarded. 
(b) Where marks of sub-tasks are combined, the overall assessment mark for the task is rounded 
to one decimal place. 
(c) Where marks of multiple tasks that form part of an assessment item are combined, the overall 
assessment mark for that item is rounded to one decimal place. 
(d) Where the calculation in 7.4(3)(b) and 7.4(3)(c) above results in a decimal number whose 
second decimal place is 5 or above, the mark is rounded up to one decimal place. Example: If 
an assessment task has three sub-tasks of equal weighting which are given marks of 54, 61 
and 70 then the overall assessment mark for the task is 61.67, which is rounded up to one 
decimal place of 61.7. 
(4) Rounding marks for tasks that form part of an assessment item:
(a) Where an assessment item is one single piece of work, a single mark is awarded. 
(b) Where marks of assessment tasks are combined, the overall mark for the assessment item is 
rounded to one decimal place. 
(c) Where the calculation in 33.8 above results in a decimal number whose second decimal place 
is 5 or above, the mark is rounded up to one decimal place.
(d) Example: If an assessment item has three assessment tasks of equal weighting which are 
given marks of 52.9, 61.8 and 72.7 then the final unit mark is calculated to be 62.46 and 
rounded up to 62.5. 
(5) Rounding final unit mark:
(a) A unit’s final mark is expressed as an integer.
(b) A unit’s final mark is calculated by way of combining assessment items to produce a weighted 
average mark. Such a mark is rounded to the nearest integer, such that a unit mark which has 
