Introduction 
The purpose of this Model Guide is to provide an innovative collection of assessment concepts, 
from and for academics who are actively teaching at the tertiary level. As assessment ideas 
were raised and discussed amongst academics throughout 2023, many of the models 
presented in this guide performed similarly or better than expected as generative artificial 
intelligence (generative AI) tools reached ubiquity. Through this Guide, the Culture of 
Assessment Working Party has attempted to generalise the structure, tasks, and technology 
considerations – including those emerging from generative AI – of successful examples raised by 
teaching academics into models, with the hope that these will enable efficient adaptation 
across different disciplines and levels. Each model is designed to be self-contained, where 
multiple Tables of Contents are provided to help quickly identify the most useful models for a 
given discipline or activity. All discussion within this Guide assumes that the institution places 
neither restrictions nor endorsements on AI tools.  
After interviewing academics from various disciplines, the working party observed that many 
successful assessment models employed dialogic methods in multiple tasks. These features 
meant that students had the opportunity to reflect on, and discuss with instructors and/or each 
other, their progress early in the semester: where students could choose assessment attributes 
(e.g., topics or themes) of personal interest, their ability to receive and act on highly specialised 
(i.e., instructor) feedback early in semester may further motivate them to attempt a larger 
and/or more complex task in later weeks. Generative AI tools may be particularly useful in 
helping students contextualise their interests within a given assessment task, with additional value 
to be found in students discussing additional/deeper nuances with peers or instructors. 
The design of each model assumes that a central/institutional feedback mechanism will be used 
to gather students’ perspectives on assessment and unit outcomes at the end of semester. 
Further, the generalised models are not presented with discussion around the impact of class size 
or structure, noting the importance of institutional, technological, and disciplinary standards and 
expectations in deploying assessments requiring a high level of engagement (as many of these 
models do) in large classes. Of emerging technologies, generative AI tools may enable more 
efficient use of high-engagement assessments with larger class sizes, by both (i) providing an 
approximate means by which to connect students’ individual interests with the assessment’s 
context; and (ii) improving the efficiency of providing feedback on, or repeating feedback 
across, student submissions.  
 
 
 
