Background 
This guide is an outcome of the Culture of Assessment Working Party at The University of Western 
Australia (UWA) and aims to share some of the most innovative assessment practices from 
academics across the university. The rapid evolution of the educational and technological 
landscapes requires an iterative, responsive approach to assessment. To that end, the intention 
of this guide is that it should be updated every few years, incorporating feedback and new 
success stories of academics across the disciplines. Our working party, comprising experienced 
educators across our fields of education, embarked on this journey with the goal of learning 
about the very best of what we do at UWA to produce this guide. We believe that assessment, 
at its best, is not merely a means to measure understanding, but a tool to enhance learning, 
stimulate curiosity, and empower students. This guide encapsulates that belief, offering insights 
into creating assessment models that are rigorous, fair, engaging, and above all, conducive to 
learning.  
This guide is not intended to be prescriptive: instead, it seeks to present a range of proven 
formative and summative assessment models that have been raised by our colleagues as 
exemplars. We have attempted to present the detail of each model with minimal reference to 
the originating discipline, in the hope this will enhance the useability across fields of education. 
These models have been developed with a keen eye for detail, ensuring that they are 
sufficiently comprehensive to enable easy replication, yet flexible enough to accommodate 
individual teaching styles and unit requirements. Each Exemplar Model included in this guide is 
detailed in several sections. We begin with an overview, providing the context and the rationale 
behind the model. The design of the assessment task is elaborated, with specific focus on 
student prompts, aims, and tasks. We delve into the feedback mechanism, elucidating how it 
fosters skill development.  
At the outset of this project, the Working Party identified three core values that we wanted to 
flow through each of the exemplar models: academic integrity; student-centred learning 
design; and the equity and accessibility of our assessments. These values are reflected and 
discussed with each exemplar model, where a “gauge” indicates the degree to which each 
generalised model aligns with these core values: 
 
Minimal  
Alignment 
Moderate 
Alignment 
Substantial 
Alignment 
Strong  
Alignment 
Complete 
Alignment 
 
 
 
 
 
 
A detailed discussion around each core value is included in the description of each model, to 
help establish how pedagogical and deployment choices may affect the value itself (i.e., while 
the intrinsic academic integrity in a model may be high, variations can affect this). Alongside 
these values, practical considerations such as best practices for successful implementation, 
technology considerations, and a week-by-week breakdown of student and instructor activities 
have also been included to provide a well-rounded understanding of each model. Our hope is 
that this guide will serve as a valuable resource for educators at UWA and beyond, encouraging 
