innovative, student-centred assessment practices that are rooted in academic integrity and 
committed to fostering a culture of continuous learning and improvement. 
Student-Centred Assessments 
Student-centred learning has emerged as a pivotal component in contemporary education, 
shifting the focus from passive absorption of knowledge to active engagement in the learning 
process. While maintaining the highest standards of scholarship in the academy, this paradigm 
shift has been instrumental in creating an educational environment that encourages students to 
take ownership of their learning journey. The working party recognises the transformative 
potential of student-centred learning in fostering active engagement and personal 
development. Student-centred learning encourages students to take an active role in their 
education, fostering a deeper understanding and promoting the development of critical 
thinking skills. Our discussions highlighted a shared belief in the importance of ensuring our 
assessments are not just tools for measuring understanding, but instruments for enhancing 
learning, stimulating curiosity, and empowering students.  
Throughout this exploration, our discussions have underscored the crucial role of learning design 
in implementing a student-centred approach. A well-thought-out learning design can provide a 
structure that supports student-centred learning, helping students navigate their learning 
journey. For instance, Bloom’s taxonomy, which provides a comprehensive model with cognitive 
and knowledge dimensions, can be a useful tool for those first exploring the design of student-
centred learning experiences: beyond this foundation, alternative taxonomies can prove useful 
when adapting a curriculum for hybrid or online-first delivery, and can stimulate different 
perspectives on assessment and learning. This flexibility in learning design is a strength, as it allows 
for the adaptation of the learning experience to suit individual students’ learning styles, interests, 
and career aspirations. 
The assessment models are designed with this philosophy in mind, focusing not only on 
academic integrity but also on creating an environment that encourages students to take 
ownership of their learning process: when successful, this approach allows the embedding of 
equity and accessibility considerations within assessments. A student-centred approach to 
learning should ensure an inclusive learning environment, where diverse learners can succeed. 
By placing students at the centre of the learning process, we aim to prepare them for a future 
where they can apply their knowledge and skills in diverse contexts, thereby contributing 
meaningfully to their communities and their disciplines of choice. 
UWA participates in the Advance HE, and our approach here aligns with the Professional 
Standards Framework for Teaching and Supporting Learning in Higher Education 2023, including 
Area of Activity A3 (“assess and give feedback for learning”), Professional Value V1 (“respect 
individual learners and diverse groups of learners”), and Professional Value V2 (“promote 
engagement in learning and equity of opportunity for all to reach their potential”). The university 
also has legal obligations to provide reasonable adjustments for students with disabilities, which 
is supported by the UniAccess office and further complemented by the StudySmarter programs 
at UWA.  
Universal Design for Learning 
We firmly believe in creating an inclusive educational experience that respects and values the 
diversity of our students and facilitates their academic growth. Key to achieving this is 
understanding and implementing the principles of Universal Design for Learning (UDL), a 
concept that while not familiar to all, holds immense potential for our educational practice. The 
