UDL framework considers the needs of diverse learners, eliminates barriers those learners might 
experience through careful design and planning, and helps educators to develop flexible 
pedagogies to improve the learning process and outcomes for all students. Although UDL may 
be a new term for some, its principles align with our commitment to student-focused learning, 
equity, and accessibility: the principles call for multiple means of engagement to tap into 
learners' interests and motivations, diverse methods of presenting information to cater to 
learners' varied ways of acquiring information, and different modes of expression to provide 
learners with various ways of demonstrating their understanding. Implementing UDL means 
understanding that learners come with a wide variety of prior experiences, abilities, preferences 
and needs and we can often eliminate barriers to learning and offer a variety of learning and 
assessment options to meet their need. For example, this could mean replacing a single mode 
of assignment submission (e.g., 1000-word essay) with multiple modes that allows the student 
choice while maintaining alignment with the unit learning outcomes (e.g., written essay, 
recorded presentation, photo essay, or audio lecture). UDL encourages us to think about the 
accessibility of assessments for all students and the diversity of options available to cater to 
different learning styles and requirements, each of which can meet the learning outcomes of 
the course or unit. 
Authentic Assessment 
Authentic assessments are those in which students are asked to perform real-world tasks that 
demonstrate meaningful application of essential knowledge and skills. As academics routinely 
engage with the professionals and practitioners of their discipline(s) – for example, through 
fieldwork, industry research, or technology development projects – there exists a natural 
opportunity for academics to deploy their understanding in the classroom environment. This 
‘authentic’ approach aligns with our commitment to providing an education that is not only 
academically rigorous, but practically relevant to the goal of preparing our students for future 
professional practice. Authentic assessment emphasises the practical application of knowledge 
and skills, rather than just theoretical understanding. It involves tasks that mirror the challenges 
and problem-solving situations faced in the workplaces that students will meet upon graduation. 
The working party places significant value in this approach, as it allows students to relate what 
they are learning in the classroom to real-world contexts, thus enhancing their engagement and 
motivation.  
The implementation of authentic assessment can vary widely across different disciplines. In the 
Business School, for example, students may be asked to develop a business plan, marketing 
strategy, or financial analysis for a hypothetical company: these tasks require students to apply 
theoretical concepts to practical scenarios, mirroring the kinds of tasks they might undertake in 
a business environment. In engineering, authentic assessment could involve designing and 
testing a prototype, conducting a feasibility study, or solving a complex, open-ended problem. 
These tasks require students to apply their technical knowledge, problem-solving skills, and 
creativity, closely resembling the kind of work they will undertake as professional engineers. 
Authentic assessment tasks can provide students with tangible evidence of their skills and 
abilities, which they can then use to demonstrate their competence to potential employers.  
Part 1 of this guide contains models of formative assessment, which UWA’s Assessment Policy 
defines as “assessment for learning meaning an assessment item or activity that has a 
developmental purpose with feedback for students about their learning and may carry a mark.”  
Although not all formative assessments may carry a mark, those provided in this guide do contain 
indicative weighting in unit assessments.  Part 2 of this guide contains summative assessment 
models, defined as an “assessment that contributes to the final grade/mark of a unit to provide a 
