• Feedback: Feedback is provided in two parts: for the photo essay and for the oral 
presentation. The marking rubric assesses the relevance, creativity, and originality of the 
photos, as well as the structure, content, understanding, and delivery of the presentation. This 
feedback is intended to help students refine their photography, research, presentation, and 
critical thinking skills.  
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
The requirement for students to take their own original photographs and to explain these 
through an oral presentation ensures the authenticity of their work, mitigates the risk of 
plagiarism, and fosters academic integrity. In particular, the opportunity for students to present 
their work (either to peers and/or instructors) and to discuss and openly reflect on the 
connection with class content. The flexibility for students to choose their specific topic also 
minimises the possibility of reusing previous works. Importantly, the incorporation of student 
presentations as a final step provides the opportunity for dialogic engagement between 
students and the lecturer, as a vehicle to establish the integrity of each submission.  
Varying Assessment Elements to Maintain Academic Integrity 
While the broad general topic can remain the same, the specific topics chosen by students will 
naturally vary each year or each semester, maintaining academic integrity. The requirement for 
original photographs and an oral presentation also naturally varies the output each year, as 
each student will have a unique interpretation and response to the task. 
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
The photo essay allows students to choose a topic of personal interest within the broader theme 
of the unit. The freedom to select their own topic encourages students to engage more deeply 
with the subject matter and allows them to bring their personal interests and career aspirations 
into the assessment model. This personalised learning design promotes motivation, engagement, 
and deeper learning.  
Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
The requirement for original photos and the flexibility in topic selection allows for a wide range of 
interpretations and responses to the task, accommodating diverse perspectives and 
approaches.  However, some students may have barriers to effective participation in the 
assessment task (for example, those who are visually impaired) and may require the use of 
assistive technology to enable participation, or other reasonable adjustments, or alternative 
assessment options.  The assignment does not require any specialised equipment, ensuring 
broad accessibility. Ethical considerations in photography ensure respect for diversity and 
equity. Students who experience anxiety in delivering oral presentations before a group may 
have specified alternatives in their UniAccess Adjustment Plan (at UWA). 
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
Successful implementation of this assignment requires clear communication of expectations to 
the students, including the requirements for the photographs and the oral presentation, as well 
as ethical considerations. Providing students with the marking expectations in advance can help 
guide their work and ensure they understand what is expected of them. Prompt and detailed 
