Generative AI and Other Technology Considerations 
The assignment requires a device capable of capturing images (such as a digital camera or a 
smartphone), access to the internet, a designated platform for submission of the photographs, 
and PowerPoint or similar software for preparing the submission. Critically, generative AI could 
be deployed in a variety of capacities to support and enhance this assessment activity: students 
could use chat-based systems in the initial ideation stages of the assessment (e.g., to identify or 
refine potential photo sequences/perspectives), while image-based systems could alternatively 
be deployed directly for the creation of synthetic image sequences or to augment existing 
student photographs (e.g., as a further task after students capture initial photographs).  
More advanced generative AI systems, capable of producing static images or animated movies 
based on text-based prompts, may enable alternative approaches that don’t necessitate 
access to a natural or built environment. While ensuring equity of access (e.g., particularly across 
tools with differential paywalls), the open-ended nature of these tools may also provide an 
opportunity to visualise future or alternative environments: while the standard model may ask 
students to identify an engineering technology that serves the natural environment (i.e., using a 
camera), deploying generative AI might instead involve asking students to visualise how that 
same technology might serve the natural environment of an alternative reality (e.g., utopian or 
dystopian). 
