Overview of Assessment Model 
This assessment model is designed to immerse students in a real-world scenario where they apply 
their theoretical knowledge to address a practical problem in their discipline area, where the 
student focusses on deploying critical thinking and problem-solving skills within the class context. 
The model's crux is a letter-writing task in which students role-play as professionals providing 
service to a client. This letter integrates data analysis, interpretation, and problem-solving, along 
with effective communication skills. The assignment is designed to mimic an actual professional 
engagement, requiring students to utilise data (from a source suitable to the discipline, such as a 
laboratory) to analyse, interpret, and present their findings in a digestible format for a non-
specialist audience. The assignment also encourages students to make recommendations 
based on their findings, thus pushing them to think critically and analytically about real-world 
applications of their theoretical knowledge.  
Assessment Design 
The structure of the assignment provides a comprehensive exploration of a topic, inclusive of 
practicing the communication of technical content to a non-technical audience. The 
assignment culminates in a professional recommendation activity (as an exemplar of authentic, 
real-world experience), requiring students to apply their findings to a practical problem that 
simulates what they are likely to encounter in the field.  
• Student prompt: For the assignment, you will assume the role of a consultant who has been 
hired by a client to analyse, interpret, and report on a particular topic. In the case of the 
motivating assessment in soil science, an example proposed task may involve characterizing 
soil chemistry to identify contaminants and propose remediation strategies. The client is not a 
specialist in the student’s field, so the letter must be clear, concise, and devoid of jargon 
where possible: it should be understandable to someone without the student’s emerging 
specialist knowledge. Students will use data collected either directly (e.g., 
through corresponding laboratory exercises/access) or indirectly (e.g., government or 
institutional data repositories), to support their analysis of the problem. The student’s letter 
should not only present relevant data, but to further analyse and/or contextualise it within the 
problem (as appropriate for the level of data literacy expected in the discipline) to draw 
supported conclusions for the client. Critically, the letter is typically expected to propose a 
clear and unambiguous recommendation, which is supported by evidence presented in the 
letter (again, to a level expected by the discipline). The students’ recommendations should be 
justified and based on the evidence they have presented, which provides an opportunity to 
identify the key knowledge gaps (including assumptions) that could be resolved with further 
engagement and consultation. The letter should follow a professional format and should be 
free of spelling, grammatical, and typographical errors.  
• Aims: The primary aim of this assessment task is to strengthen students' understanding and 
application of the theoretical knowledge gained in class. Where relevant to the discipline, the 
analysis and interpretation of data (relevant to the motivating ‘client project’), students 
engage with the unit content at a deeper level, enabling them to comprehend how 
theoretical concepts apply to practical scenarios. The assignment also aims to develop 
students' problem-solving skills. By requiring students to draw conclusions from their data and 
make recommendations, the task challenges students to think critically and apply their 
knowledge and understanding in a problem-solving context. Additionally, this task is designed 
to foster students' communication skills, particularly around complex, technical information for 
a non-specialist audience. Critical to those professions where specialists must communicate 
their findings and recommendations to clients or colleagues who may not have the same level 
