of expertise in the field, this task provides an ‘authentic’ experience to prepare students for 
future career activities. By role-playing as a professional providing a service to a client, students 
gain a sense of the responsibilities and challenges involved in such work. While students may 
work in groups to collect data, perform analysis, or interpret results (if and where appropriate 
to the discipline), the generation of their response letters should be done individually. 
• Tasks: There are no component tasks involved in this assessment model, where the single task 
can be broken down into four phases (depending on the discipline and cohort level): 
1. Data Collection: Students collect data independently or during their practical classes. 
In the example of the motivating soil science assignment, this involves measuring 
various soil chemical properties for each soil horizon. Alternatively, this stage may be 
supported by public-facing data repositories (e.g., from national laboratories) or 
instructor-prepared datasets. 
2. Data Analysis: Students analyse the data they have collected or received, including 
an appropriate level of discussion around uncertainty (appropriate to the level and 
discipline). More complex calculations may be done as a group activity to foster 
collaboration and mathematical skill development, particularly when students are 
working across different datasets.  
3. Data Interpretation: Students begin drawing and refining recommendations based on 
what the data means in the context of the assignment topic. Students should ideally 
work alone on their interpretations (particularly for shared data sources), to foster 
complex reasoning skills. For higher class levels, students should contextualise their 
recommendations within the uncertainty of the data collection and analysis activities.  
4. Letter Draft: Students write a professional letter to a non-specialist client, presenting 
their data and explaining their conclusions in a clear and understandable way. The 
letter should also include a recommendation based on the data (as evidence). 
Ideally, students should receive interim feedback on these recommendations from 
peers and/or instructors, providing them an opportunity to further probe and refine 
their recommendations. 
• Feedback: Students receive feedback on their data collection during practical classes or 
consultations, depending on the structure of the unit. This helps ensure that the data they 
collect is accurate and reliable. After the letter is submitted, students receive written feedback 
on their work. This feedback is comprehensive, covering not only the content of the letter, but 
also its structure and style: it provides constructive criticism, highlighting areas of strength and 
identifying areas for improvement.  
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
The model requires students to submit individual work. Even though students might work in 
groups to collect data, each student must independently analyse and interpret the data and 
write their own letter. This ensures that each student can understand and applying the unit 
content. The real-world nature of the task requires students to apply their knowledge in unique 
ways, discouraging rote learning and promoting original thinking. The data collection process 
requires students to accurately report their results, discouraging data fabrication or 
manipulation. As an extension concept, asking students to independently identify and collect 
data to be used in the letter is a student-centred approach that simultaneously promotes 
integrity. Similarly, the inclusion of an interim update presentation (either live to the class or 
submitted asynchronously to the lecturer) provides an extension pathway to further establish 
integrity on a per-student basis.  
