Varying Assessment Elements to Maintain Academic Integrity 
The data collection element can be varied by changing the nature of the data to be collected 
or the methods by which it is collected. This can be done from year to year, or between different 
sections of the same unit. The interpretation and analysis elements can be varied by changing 
the specific questions or problems students are asked to address in their letters. These questions 
can be designed to focus on different aspects of the unit content, ensuring that students gain a 
comprehensive understanding of the material. The presentation element can be varied by 
changing the format or medium of the final product (for example, a 15-minute professional 
summary presentation versus a 3-minute ‘pitch’ to the client for further consultation activities). 
While a letter is used in this model, other formats such as reports, presentations, or multimedia 
projects could also be used.  
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
Students are not simply absorbing information; they are actively engaging with the material, 
collecting/analysing data, and applying their knowledge to a real-world scenario. The model 
promotes critical thinking and problem-solving skills, as the tasks challenge students to interpret 
their data, draw conclusions, and make recommendations. These are key skills that are valuable 
not only in academic contexts, but also in professional and personal ones. The requirement to 
write a letter to a non-specialist client pushes students to present their findings in a clear and 
understandable way. This helps them develop the ability to communicate complex ideas to 
diverse audiences. While a specific topic and format are given, the tasks can be adapted to fit 
different unit content and learning outcomes. This allows for a personalised learning experience 
that caters to the needs and interests of the students.   
Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
Students from diverse backgrounds bring unique perspectives and experiences to their work, 
and this model allows them to incorporate these into their assignments. This model is adaptable 
to accommodate students with different backgrounds and learning styles, where the tasks can 
be modified to fit different learning needs, and the flexible format of the assignment allows for 
the use of assistive technologies or alternative submission formats. The model promotes inclusivity 
by requiring respectful and professional communication: this not only prepares students for the 
professional world but creates an inclusive learning environment where students must be aware 
of, and appreciate, the perspectives of each participant/role within the project. The model 
supports equity by assessing students based on their understanding and application of the unit 
content, rather than solely on their ability to regurgitate information.  
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
For successful implementation of this model, instructors should ensure that students have a clear 
understanding of the assignment requirements and the grading rubric. This can be achieved 
through detailed assignment instructions and in-class discussions. Lecturers should also provide 
support and resources to help students succeed. This might include resources on data analysis 
and interpretation, examples of professional letters, and opportunities for feedback and revision. 
Importantly, the degree to which students can nominate their own data sources, questions, and 
clients (as the extension concepts described above) correlates directly with the student-centred 
engagement achievable through the model.  
