Overview of Assessment Model 
The proposed assessment model is a quiz-based approach that leverages the capabilities of 
Learning Management Systems (LMS), such as Blackboard. This approach employs quizzes that 
are built from a large bank of questions, stratified by topic and difficulty level, to promote 
continuous engagement and active learning. These quizzes are deployed sequentially 
throughout the semester to reinforce foundational knowledge and provide formative feedback. 
The approach seeks to optimise the balance between the number of questions included in 
each quiz and the overall size of the question bank. While challenges related to accessibility and 
academic integrity may arise, the model incorporates a range of strategies to mitigate these 
concerns, such as random question selection, varied assessment components, and student-
centric learning designs.  
Assessment Design 
 Student prompt: Students will be instructed to undertake a series of quizzes throughout the 
term, hosted on a learning management system (including Blackboard). Each quiz should 
ideally consist of 20 questions or more, randomly selected from a substantially larger question 
bank (nominally with around 20 times the volume of questions deployed in any student’s quiz). 
The questions can cover a broad range of topics and vary in difficulty, to ensure a 
comprehensive assessment of students' understanding and application of unit content. 
Students will be advised to attempt each quiz only after they have thoroughly reviewed the 
relevant unit material: by making both the class materials and quizzes available at the 
beginning of semester, students are able to manage and set their own study and work 
schedules. This approach to successive quizzes is designed to provide instant feedback, 
allowing students to immediately identify and address knowledge gaps, while adapting to the 
multi-faceted demands on each student’s time. 
 Aims: The quizzes aim to foster an environment of active learning, continuous engagement 
with unit content, and self-directed study habits. Through regular and timely feedback, 
students can monitor their progress and adjust their study strategies accordingly. The quizzes 
are also intended to promote academic integrity by using a large, diversified question bank 
and randomising question selection, which makes it more challenging for students to engage 
in dishonest practices.  
 Tasks: Students will be required to complete quizzes at regular intervals throughout the 
semester, where each successive quiz can incorporate topics covered during the preceding 
weeks as a cumulative activity. Employing a variety of question types – including multiple-
choice, fill-in-the-blank, calculated formula, topic matching, and short answer questions – 
provides the opportunity to target different levels of taxonomy (e.g., a calculated formula may 
be deployed, where each student is presented with a different set of input values).  
 Feedback: Immediate feedback will be provided upon completion of each quiz, where 
instructors can provide general feedback to incorrect question responses during the design 
and setup of the question pools. This feedback should include explanations where necessary, 
and references to relevant unit materials for further reading (as appropriate).  
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
This model promotes a moderate degree of academic integrity by utilising a large question 
bank and randomising question selection for each quiz. By doing so, the model makes it more 
difficult for students to share answers or engage in other dishonest behaviours. The use of timed 
quizzes, while introducing a further barrier to dishonest behaviour, also carries concerns around 
