the limitations to equity and accessibility of the approach: in preference to introducing time 
limits, the expansion of question bank size is a recommended strategy to enhance academic 
integrity. 
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
This model partially fosters student-centred learning by allowing students to engage with unit 
material and receive feedback at their own pace. Particularly in student cohorts with extra-
curricular commitments (e.g., postgraduate students who are working full time), releasing all 
class materials and quizzes at the start of semester allows students to plan their study, work, and 
personal schedules more effectively.  
Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
The model allows students to take quizzes at a time and location that suits them, subject to the 
constraints of the quiz window (if employed). Accommodations can be made for students with 
disabilities (e.g., submission timelines via UniAccess Adjustment Plans at UWA), although these 
are limited when compared with alternative assessment vehicles presented in this Guide. 
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
• To implement this model successfully, it is crucial to maintain a large and diverse question 
bank, provide immediate and constructive feedback, and regularly update the quizzes to 
maintain academic integrity and relevance. It is also crucial to ensure the reliability of the 
technology being used and provide technical support to students. 
• Each quiz should ideally contain at least 20 questions, with natural variation by the distribution 
of question types and discipline. This number allows for a comprehensive assessment of 
students' understanding without causing cognitive overload. As multiple quizzes are deployed 
in sequence (four, in this example), the composite assessment item would ultimately require 
students to answer at least 80 questions by the end of semester. 
• Importantly, the number of questions in each quiz should represent about 5% of the total 
number of questions in the bank. This ratio ensures a wide coverage of unit content while 
maintaining the integrity of the assessment vehicle. This approach does require a significant 
up-front time investment from academics, with a more moderate workload required to 
expand/revise question banks each year thereafter. Varying the type and difficulty of 
questions can help maintain student engagement and fairness in assessment. It is also crucial 
to provide immediate, constructive feedback to guide students' learning. Lastly, consider the 
accessibility of the quizzes and make necessary accommodations for students with UniAccess 
Adjustment Plans (at UWA). 
Generative AI and Other Technology Considerations 
The Learning Management System (e.g., Blackboard) is integral to this model, as it provides the 
necessary features for quiz construction, administration, and grading. The academic team’s time 
investment required to generate these models is substantial, largely the first instance in which the 
model is deployed (as mentioned, this time commitment decreases in continuing years for the 
model). As the model is neither dialogic nor collaborative, generative AI largely poses only risks 
to the integrity of the assessment model (e.g., as a vehicle for students to quickly search for an 
answer, as opposed to using AI as a collaborative learning tool). For this reason, the selection of 
quiz question types is important. For example, the use of calculated formula questions – where 
