Overview of Assessment Model 
The formative model focusses on the development of creative and analytical skills, and enables 
students to explore creative composition, articulate their creative process, analyse a given work, 
and participate in a content-relevant quiz. The model engages students in a dynamic process of 
creation, self-reflection, analysis, and evaluation. It is designed to cater to a wide range of 
creative disciplines, including but not limited to music, literature, visual arts, and performing arts. 
Feedback forms an integral part of the model, providing students with constructive critique and 
guidance for improvement.  
Assessment Design 
The assessment is based on a sequential nature of the tasks, which ensures a holistic learning 
experience where students are not only creators but also critics of their own work and that of 
others. The tasks are strategically designed to foster creativity, critical thinking, analytical skills, 
and effective communication.  
• Student prompt: Students are presented with a prompt that requires them to create two short 
creative pieces (works) within their discipline. The prompt is intentionally broad, encouraging 
students to delve into their creativity and personal interests. It offers the opportunity to explore 
themes, techniques, or concepts that resonate with them: in this way, students can integrate 
one or more topics of personal interest within the assessment, as an exemplar of student-
centred learning. The second part of the prompt involves analysing a given work relevant to 
their field of study. The purpose of the analysis is to enhance their understanding and 
appreciation of existing works, improving their own creative processes. The prompt also guides 
students to articulate their creative process and choices, fostering reflection and critical 
thinking. This reflection is not merely a description of the process, but a deep exploration of the 
decisions made, the inspirations drawn upon, and the challenges encountered. It encourages 
students to understand their own creative identity and evolution.  
• Aims: At its core, this model seeks to help students further develop their creative skills, by 
encouraging them to express themselves through their creative pieces while showcasing their 
originality and personal style. Depending on the degree of technical scaffolding relevant and 
available (i.e., to the discipline), the students’ subsequent analyses of their creative effort 
provide an opportunity to think both analytically and critically about their early draft(s). As the 
first two tasks involve students providing a draft of their creative works, the feedback they 
receive (from either peers and/or instructors) is critical in determining the learning potential of 
the analysis task (detailed below).   
• Tasks: The tasks under this model, designed to be sequential and progressive, including: 
• Task 1: Creation of First Short Creative Piece (30% weighting): Students create their first 
piece, experimenting with themes, techniques, or concepts that interest them. The piece 
should be accompanied by a written explanation of their creative process and choices. 
• Task 2: Creation of Second Short Creative Piece (30% weighting): Students create their 
second piece, building on the feedback and learning from the first task. Again, a written 
explanation of their creative process and choices should accompany the piece. 
• Task 3: Analysis of a Given Work (30% weighting): Students critically analyse a given work 
relevant to their discipline, discussing its structure, technique, impact, and other relevant 
aspects. 
