• Task 4: Participation in a Relevant Quiz (10% weighting): Students participate in a quiz 
focused on relevant unit content, encouraging active engagement with the unit 
material.  
• Feedback: For each creative piece, discipline-specific feedback should focus on creativity, 
technique, communication of ideas, and reflection on the creative process. The layered 
feedback for each submission task also highlights areas for improvement and development, 
such that students can use this feedback in the formulation and submission of the next task. For 
the analysis task, feedback revolves around the depth of understanding, clarity of expression, 
and quality of insights. The quiz offers instant feedback, reinforcing understanding of the unit 
content. 
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
The creative tasks are designed to be personal and authentic, reflecting each student's unique 
style and ideas, reducing chances of plagiarism. The written reflections require students to delve 
into their thought process, further ensuring originality. The analysis task, while based on an 
existing work, requires personal insights and understanding, discouraging mere replication of 
existing analyses. The quiz, being time-bound and individual, ensures personal understanding of 
the content.  
Varying Assessment Elements to Maintain Academic Integrity 
To maintain freshness and academic integrity, various elements can be adjusted in each 
deployment of the unit (such as the type/nature of the creative piece to be developed, or the 
existing piece to be analysed). The prompts for the creative pieces can be changed to reflect 
evolving themes or concepts. The content of the quiz should reflect the most current unit 
material, based largely on revisions to the first two tasks in the model. 
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
This model allows students to explore themes or concepts that resonate with them, making the 
learning experience personally relevant and engaging. They can bring in their career 
aspirations, experimenting with styles or techniques relevant to their professional goals. The 
model also enables students to understand their creative identity and process, enhancing self-
awareness and personal growth. As one extension concept, allowing students to choose the 
style to be used for their own creative piece, and/or nominate the existing work that they wish to 
analyse, provides a pathway to maximise the student-centred focus of the assessment.  
Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
The broad nature of the prompts allows students from diverse backgrounds and interests to find 
relevance and self-expression within the remit of the model. Reasonable adjustments can be 
made to accommodate students with UniAccess Plans (at UWA), ensuring they can fully 
participate in the tasks. For most creative disciplines, the model can be implemented in a hybrid 
mode without directly inhibiting the learning opportunities, making it accessible regardless of 
geographical or campus access constraints.  
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
Key factors for successful implementation include: 
