• Clarity in Communication: Clear explanation of tasks, expectations, and evaluation 
criteria. 
• Timely and Constructive Feedback: Regular feedback to help students improve their 
work. 
• Flexibility: Ability to adjust tasks to accommodate diverse student needs. 
• Regular Monitoring and Evaluation: Ensuring the effectiveness and relevance of the 
tasks. 
Generative AI and Other Technology Considerations 
Dependent on the discipline, creative composition software and digital platforms for submission 
and feedback may be required. As creative, professional software tools often require substantial 
training, the consideration of previous knowledge/experience and deployment of asynchronous 
guides is critical to ensuring students can focus on the assessment itself (i.e., in preference to 
spending time learning software functions). In some cases, the accessibility of these tools is 
another consideration, both in the case of on-device use (e.g., the provision of computers 
capable of running high-end design software) and cloud-based applications (e.g., a sufficient 
internet speed to access cloud platforms).  
The suite of current and emerging generative AI tools is worth careful consideration by the 
academic, as these may be a very useful first step (depending on the discipline of deployment) 
in helping students rapidly iterate through, and refine, different ideas for their creative 
compositions. The multimodal nature of generative AI is also helpful in helping students consider 
expressing their creativity in a mode that comes most naturally with guided transformation to 
another mode of expression: for example, a student be best able to express their creativity in 
written words, and to then use generative AI to translate this written text to sound and/or visual 
media.  
