Overview of Assessment Model 
The Creative Writing Workshop uses Liz Lerman’s Critical Response Process (CRP) to help students 
work towards their final assessment while giving them the opportunity to improve feedback 
literacy and apply craft-based and theoretical concepts discussed in unit materials. 
Workshopping functions alongside other unit activities, in the second half of two-hour tutorials, 
and while students are working in small groups. It complements students’ skill development and 
assessment tasks, helping them to achieve all learning outcomes. It also helps to build 
camaraderie within the cohort.  
Assessment Design – Writing Workshop  
• Aims: The aim of the Creative Writing Workshop is to give students the opportunity to develop 
and employ the disciplinary skills learned over the course of the unit. This applies both to 
practical assessment tasks and to the ways in which feedback might be provided safely and 
with tact. Improved feedback literacy is one result of this. It allows students to experiment with 
work that uses unit concepts. Writing and editing, alongside attendant career paths requiring 
high-level communication skills, require the kind of dexterity initiated in the workshop. This is 
applied, again, both in the work students do, and in the ways they are required to interact and 
provide feedback.  
• Student prompt: Students are provided with workshopping guidelines and assessment rubrics 
(these are included below) for the work they are required to workshop; they are guided 
through the workshopping steps in the first two weeks of semester, both with readings and 
explicit instruction, alongside practice efforts. While the workshopping is only assessed as part 
of the students’ participation requirements, it is made clear through the assessment structure 
that its outcomes are taken seriously (students are required to reflect on the process, and the 
practice directly informs their creative work). Emphasis is also placed on the value of 
community for creative writing; two articles shared with students on this theme are referenced 
below. Feedback on workshopping practice is ongoing through the unit, and informal; 
opportunity for explicit feedback comes through the assessment of the workshopping 
reflection. Academic integrity is discussed in terms of the requirements to produce original 
work, and emphasis is also placed on the ways students should work with each other in the 
classroom.  
• Tasks: Across the semester, students share their work via the LMS. This should be a work-in-
progress towards their final assessment (the Creative Folio). This writing is then discussed using 
Lerman’s Critical Response Process. Students also guide this process, under supervision from 
their tutor. After class, students must complete a ‘statement of meaning’ for each piece of 
writing workshopped. This is a response to the work which captures what made it meaningful 
for them, and it should be posted to the LMS as a record of the workshop. At other times 
through the semester students are required to reflect on workshopping and participate 
actively in the process.  
• Feedback: The process is one of feedback: students work with each other to develop 
feedback skills (both in the offering and in the receiving). They receive guidance from their 
tutors in this process. Alongside this, they receive written feedback on the work they are 
proposing (and which they workshop), their workshopping reflections, and their final piece of 
creative work. In the assessment tasks, feedback is directed towards the rubrics, which is 
directed in turn at the unit learning outcomes.  
 
