Extension Assessment Design – Content Folio 
Students workshop towards a Creative Folio, which is a piece or pieces of creative writing (in 
prose, poetry, or script form), alongside a statement of intent, which aims to introduce 
exegetical writing. Exegetical writing helps to frame a creative work’s contribution as research. 
The workshopping is designed both to provide students with ongoing feedback and support in 
working towards their Creative Folio, and to equip students with skills in deciphering and 
providing feedback. Their other written assessments aim to work alongside the workshopping 
process too, in that they are expected to consider the concepts underpinning their Creative 
Folio, and the progress within workshops. Here are brief descriptions of the assessments tasks:  
Folio Outline: Conceptual Underpinnings: this assignment presents the form and 
subject/concept of creative work to follow in the final folio. The outline will develop a 
framework for the creative practice, contextualising it within the themes and ideas of the 
unit. It is submitted early in the semester, with the aim of giving the student’s creative 
work a firm conceptual context.  
Participation: Workshopping Reflection: this assessment comes in two parts: a 
workshopping reflection designed to continue the conceptual thinking behind the 
student’s Creative Folio, and general participation in workshop discussions and class 
activities, including the submission and presentation of creative writing for group 
workshops, and a record of responses to the workshop material of others collected via 
the LMS. The reflection component requires students to consider the first phase of 
workshopping alongside their peers. They are expected to consider both their skills within 
the workshop and the ways their perceptions/conceptions of their creative work have 
developed through workshopping.  
Creative Folio: one extended piece or a suite of related short pieces, demonstrating the 
creative practice outlined in the previous assessments, and accompanied by a 
statement of intent. Word length: Creative - 2,000 words prose; OR three poems (max. 50 
lines each) for poetry; OR maximum eight pages script; OR equivalent lengths for 
different forms of work to be discussed with your tutor. Statement of Intent - 200 words. 
(Total word length, 2,200 words maximum in any form.) 
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
The unfolding nature of the Creative Writing Workshop means that students invest in each 
other’s work. This allows a degree of academic integrity assurance: excitement and enthusiasm 
for a project help to build the writer’s investment in their own work too. By emphasising the duty 
they have to each other through the community focus of their workshopping groups (that two-
way investment), and by giving space to write and space to consider software like AI, the 
workshop method attempts to tackle academic integrity within the fabric of each workshop. 
Where students are expected to present their work to both class peers and the academic, the 
ensuing nature of the discussion provides one critical vehicle through which to establish 
academic integrity in the model. 
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
(i) 
Disciplinary skills are developed actively. The Creative Writing Workshop functions 
akin to a lab environment, where students can test and try new writing, and receive 
real-time feedback from their peers and tutor. This builds towards final assessment of 
the creative work.  
