(ii) 
Industry/professional competencies within the creative arts and fields of 
communication more broadly require high-level interpersonal, written, and verbal 
dexterity. The Creative Writing Workshop focuses on ways to offer formative 
feedback across that spectrum. At the same time, students develop their own 
creative practice in more direct alignment with the goals of a creative writing/English 
unit.  
(iii) 
Tutorials should be places where students get to test a skillset and put that skillset into 
practice. The practical elements of the Creative Writing Workshop simulate a variety 
of workplace environments, and encourage students to feel confident in both 
delivering and receiving feedback. They also gain confidence in producing writing 
for readers they will interact with.  
Students reflect actively (rubric below) on the Creative Writing Workshop at the midpoint of 
semester. This is designed to allow them to develop their abilities in providing and receiving 
feedback. This is important in their personal writing practice and will offer soft skills in 
communication which are applicable in the workplace and within creative disciplines. The 
growth experienced by students in this unit is significant in both disciplinary skills (to be measured 
in the assessment) and personal development through the dialogic nature of the assessment 
activity.  
Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
This task only requires the LMS to function (and could proceed in other ways without it, as the 
basic needs are writing, and readers). The assessment task has, in the past, accommodated a 
range of different access requirements: for example, a student with vision impairment was able 
to read submissions on their screen, to increase text size. The task is impacted by access 
requirements in the same way an in-person tutorial might be. As the task requires active 
participation, students with communication difficulties, speak a first language other than English, 
or have difficulty with group settings may find it less accessible. The small groups – and the 
curation of those groups -- is one best practice for mitigating these risks, while stimulating cohort 
bonding experiences.  
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
 Provide clear instruction regarding the Critical Response Process, with modelling provided 
across the workshops; 
 Institute firm structure throughout the Creative Writing Workshop activities; 
 Take care to monitor small-group dynamics and student work-in-progress (in creative writing 
classrooms students are sharing vulnerable offerings, and this space can be delicate to 
manage) 
Generative AI and Other Technology Considerations 
Student work is uploaded weekly via the File Exchange feature on the LMS, which is filtered for 
students using tutorial groups on LMS: students provide statements of meaning to each other 
through the Discussion Board on the LMS. In the originating discipline, students participate in a 
discussion about generative AI tools, and even attempt to use them to complete similar creative 
tasks to those the students are asked to complete. This transparency can be useful, where 
students are generally surprised at the poor quality of writing produced by AI (this is especially 
the case for poetry at present). Importantly, the deployment of this model across different 
creative disciplines does present the opportunity to leverage the multi-modal nature of 
