In the originating discipline, students are provided with workshopping guidelines on the LMS, in 
lectures, and in tutorials:  
Workshopping 
Our aim is to create a workshopping space which is safe, structured, and supports the 
artist. We should always come back to the essential principle of respect.  
Three roles: 
• 
Artist/Author (offers the work in progress) 
• 
Responder/Reader (engages in dialogue) 
• 
Facilitator (guides process) 
As an author in workshopping: 
• 
Listen considerately to the ideas and opinions expressed by others, even if you 
feel they have misread or misconstrued your purpose in writing. 
• 
Remember this may signal that your piece is failing to communicate your desired 
effect to its reader. 
• 
Acknowledge the attention that your work has been paid by responding with 
respect. 
As a reader in workshopping: 
• 
Ensure your comments come with reflection and represent sincere engagement 
rather than a superficial response. 
• 
Strive to be honest and candid in your comments.  
• 
Do not withhold your opinion but speak with regard paid to the writer’s effort and 
feelings—demonstrate sincerity, frankness, and tact! 
Workshopping structure: each week of workshopping will see one or two people present, 
and one or two people facilitate. The order of presentations will be decided in the Week 
One tutorials, and your tutor will assign a week for you to facilitate.  
Step One  
• 
Statements of meaning. 
• 
Writers need to understand how the work they have produced has meaning to 
other people. 
• 
Facilitators will ask: ‘What has meaning for you in this piece? What was 
stimulating, surprising, provocative, evocative, memorable, touching, unique…?’ 
• 
All Responders answer according to their individual engagement. 
Step Two 
• 
Artist as Questioner. 
