Overview of Assessment Model 
This assessment model is centred around the creation of a comprehensive field or lab book that 
can be useful across a variety of disciplines. Initially developed for undergraduates studying the 
biological sciences, the model integrates both theory and practice captured in an experiential 
journal, drawing value from both critical observation and reflection. Excellent for use with a 
flipped classroom, this approach requires students to first prepare and revise background 
materials (e.g., online learning materials) prior to participating in the flipped environment: as 
they then progress to capturing experiential activities in their physical or digital notebooks, the 
opportunity to socialise observations with peers and/or instructor(s) provides a key opportunity 
for students to reflect critically on emerging hypotheses. As students progress through the 
assessment, they may also receive feedback to improve their preparation of discipline-specific 
materials (e.g., a laboratory notebook) that will be required in their practice.  
Assessment Design 
The model adopts continuous assessment and feedback throughout the semester, ensuring 
regular engagement and constant feedback on students' performance and understanding. It 
also promotes the development of general skills such as data analysis, interpretation, and 
communication of results, as well as interpersonal and teamwork skills. 
• Student prompt: The approach to creating a comprehensive field or lab book can be 
effectively applied across a wide range of disciplines, with some example implementations 
shown below. In each case, the primary assessment task involves students applying theoretical 
knowledge to a practical context, whether that's an environmental site, a community setting, 
geological sites, a classroom, a public health context, or another ‘authentic’ setting (i.e., a 
practice or expectation that may be derived from the professional practice of the discipline). 
The process of documenting their observations and analyses in a field or lab book encourages 
students to deploy unit material in a practical context, to develop practical skills, and to reflect 
on their learning in an iterative format. 
• Environmental Science: students could create a field book documenting their 
observations and analysis of a local ecosystem. They might examine water and soil 
quality, biodiversity, or the impact of human activities on the environment. Through these 
activities, students gain hands-on experience in environmental sampling techniques and 
data collection, and they learn to interpret their findings in the context of ecological 
theory and environmental policy. 
• Anthropology: students could create a field book based on participant observation in a 
community. They would document social behaviours, cultural practices, and community 
dynamics, combining descriptive detail with theoretical analysis. This encourages 
students to apply anthropological theories to real-world contexts and to develop 
research skills. 
• Geology: students could create a field book from a series of field trips to different 
geological sites. They could document observations on rock formations, stratigraphy, or 
fossil evidence, linking these observations to theories of geological processes like 
tectonics, erosion, or sedimentation. This hands-on experience helps students develop 
skills in field observation, sample collection, and the interpretation of geological data. 
• Education: students could compile a field book based on observations in a classroom 
setting. They could document teaching strategies, student interactions, classroom 
management techniques, and educational outcomes. By linking their observations to 
