educational theory, students can develop a deeper understanding of teaching and 
learning processes, classroom dynamics, and the practical application of educational 
theories. 
• Public Health: students could create a lab book documenting a community health 
assessment. They might collect and analyse data on health indicators, health service 
utilisation, and social determinants of health in a specific community. This allows students 
to apply epidemiological methods and health theory to real-world public health 
problems. 
• Aims: This model seeks to evaluate the student’s understanding of the concepts and methods 
used in their chosen academic discipline (e.g., analytical chemistry): they should be able to 
explain specific concepts, including those related to the practical experiment or analysis you 
will be conducting, while also documenting their use of, and experience with, the practical 
and/or professional skills relevant to the discipline. Critically, the assessment model fosters the 
students’ skills in, and awareness of, the level of professional documentation required by the 
discipline (beyond the basic note-taking exercises they may have previously used). The 
iterative, dialogic nature of the model design means that students are able to share their 
interim results/book with instructors, receive feedback, and immediately implement these 
learnings as they continue their documentation exercises. In this way, the progressive and 
sequential nature of the lab or field book serves as an exemplar of intrinsic academic integrity, 
while also allowing the student to reflect on their personal growth (e.g., by comparing their 
documentation/reporting/analysis methods and outcomes in an early portion of the book 
against those captured toward the end). In some disciplines, the deployment of a reflective 
activity (e.g., reflective essay or recording) may provide further opportunity to establish 
integrity and foster further learning opportunity.   
• Tasks: the sole task within this assessment is the preparation of the field/lab book, noting the 
academic may wish to introduce complementary tasks, for example the presentation of key 
results or a reflective statement. For the field/lab book, the student is to document their 
understanding of specific concepts and the planning, preparation, and execution of a 
specific practical experiment or analysis. This task will be evaluated based on the student’s 
ability to explain theoretical concepts, your documentation of experimental procedures and 
results, and your critical analysis and troubleshooting skills.  
• Feedback: Feedback will be provided continuously throughout the semester to ensure that 
students are progressing in their understanding and application of specific concepts and 
methods. This feedback will focus on both specialised and general skills. Specialised feedback 
should address students' understanding of specific concepts, their ability to plan, prepare, and 
execute a specific practical experiment or analysis, their critical analysis and troubleshooting 
skills, and their documentation and reporting of outcomes. General feedback should focus on 
students' data analysis and interpretation skills and their written communication skills.  
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
The model requires students to demonstrate understanding and application of specific 
concepts and methods, and to use their own organisation, diagramming/illustration, and writing 
to connect foundational concepts (e.g., from the textbook) with practical 
observation/experience (e.g., in the laboratory/field). The practical nature of the task requires 
students to demonstrate their own skills and abilities, making it difficult for students to submit 
others' work as their own and particularly difficult to do so during discussion with instructor(s). 
Moreover, continuous assessment throughout the semester allows for regular monitoring of 
