Varying Assessment Elements to Maintain Academic Integrity 
To maximise academic integrity, the specific experiment or analysis that students must conduct 
as part of their field or lab book can be varied each year. This ensures that students cannot 
simply replicate past students' experiments. The effort required to change this element will 
depend on the complexity of the experiment or analysis involved. However, it is expected that 
updating this element will be a part of the regular unit preparation and should not significantly 
increase the workload for academics. 
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
The model provides students with the opportunity to apply their theoretical knowledge to 
practical tasks, promoting a deep understanding of specific concepts and methods. It also 
encourages students to develop generic skills such as data analysis, interpretation, and 
communication, as valuable technical practice across many disciplines. Students can engage 
with the online learning materials in their own time, ensuring that they are prepared for the 
laboratory or field activities. The continuous assessment throughout the semester provides 
regular feedback on students' performance and understanding, helping them to identify areas 
for improvement and develop effective learning strategies. It also ensures that students are 
regularly engaged with the unit material, promoting ongoing learning and understanding.   
Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
The use of a flipped classroom approach when deploying this model allows for flexible learning, 
better accommodating students with different learning styles and needs. Students can engage 
with the online learning materials at their own pace and in their own time, making the unit more 
accessible for students with work or family commitments and those who benefit from revisiting 
recorded content. Students can tailor their field or lab book to their interests if prompts or pre-
reading questions are designed with a mild degree of flexibility (e.g., asking students to reflect 
on when, in their life experience, they may have observed a particular phenomena occurring 
naturally), ensuring that the unit is relevant and engaging for as many students as possible.  
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
 Preparation: Prior to the start of the unit, prepare the online learning materials and plan the 
laboratory or field activities. This will ensure a smooth implementation of the flipped classroom 
approach, wherein students can use the planned class time to observe/explore/experience a 
concept following their asynchronous preparation questions. 
 Communication: Communicate the assessment requirements and expectations clearly to 
students at the start of the unit. This includes the task description, deadlines, and marking 
criteria. 
 Support: Provide ongoing support to students throughout the unit. This could include regular 
feedback on their field or lab book, additional resources to aid their learning, and 
opportunities for consultation or discussion. 
 Review: At the end of the unit, review the effectiveness of the assessment model, particularly 
around prompting (pre-class) questions and whether these effectively prepared most students 
to extract value in the observation/field/laboratory experience. This could involve collecting 
