Generative AI and Other Technology Considerations 
The high degree of flexibility in this model provides for an opening for a number of types of tools 
to improve both student experience and efficiency:  
 Online Learning Platform: An online learning platform will be needed to deliver the online 
learning materials. This platform should allow for the uploading of videos, readings, and other 
learning resources, and should be easy for students to access and navigate. 
 Laboratory or Field Equipment: The field or lab book task will require access to a laboratory or 
field location and the necessary equipment for conducting the chosen practical experiment 
or analysis. 
 A smartphone camera (2D or 3D) can be used to visually document observations in the lab or 
field. This could include photographs of specific sites or objects, video recordings of processes 
or behaviours, or time-lapse images capturing changes over time. Audio recording features 
can be used to document verbal observations, interviews, or ambient sounds. Many 
smartphones are equipped with a variety of sensors that can collect data relevant to certain 
disciplines. For example, GPS can track locations for field studies, while a phone’s 
accelerometers can measure motion or vibration: there are also numerous apps available that 
can measure environmental variables like light or sound. 
 Cloud platforms such as Google Drive or Dropbox can be used to store and organise all this 
data. These platforms also enable real-time collaboration, making it easier for students to work 
together on group projects, share their findings, or receive feedback from their instructors. 
 Digital note-taking apps, such as Evernote or OneNote, can be used to create the lab or field 
book itself. These apps allow students to combine text, images, audio, and other media in a 
single document, which can be easily edited, organised, and shared. 
 Generative AI could play a meaningful role in enhancing this lab/field book assignment in 
several ways. AI could assist in the analysis of data collected in the field or lab. For example, 
machine learning algorithms could help identify patterns or correlations in large datasets, while 
natural language processing could analyse textual data or transcribe audio recordings. AI 
could also be used to generate feedback on students' work. For example, AI could provide 
instant feedback on students' reflections as drafted, highlighting areas of strength and 
suggesting areas for improvement: this could help students refine their work before submitting it 
for formal assessment. Similarly, AI tools could also be used to create interactive, adaptive 
learning resources to support students as they work on their lab or field book. For example, AI 
could generate personalised quizzes based on the content of the online learning materials, 
helping students test their understanding and identify areas for further study. 
