aspects of a complex system (e.g., its sensitivity to different parameters or inputs) and then bring 
this understanding together in a final, comprehensive assessment task (e.g., a technical 
engineering report). Whether deployed as a physical or digital laboratory activity (as 
appropriate to the discipline), this experiential learning model is designed to be hands-on, 
interactive, and research-driven where possible, providing students with the opportunity to 
directly apply theoretical knowledge to practical tasks. With its emphasis on real-world 
experimentation, this model focusses on developing students’ ability to interpret and analyse 
results from components of a complex system (e.g., a pilot-scale process equipment).  
As the semester progresses, component tasks build on each other so that students progressively 
deepen their understanding of the technical subject matter. The emphasis on sequential 
learning facilitates the development of a scaffolded understanding of complex topics, thereby 
promoting long-term retention of knowledge. The tasks should ideally be self-contained, with 
clear objectives, procedures, and expected outcomes: in the motivating discipline of 
engineering, the use of tasks that standardize the collection, analysis, and interpretation of data, 
such that students can receive formative feedback on more general data skills while exploring 
practice on different aspects of a technical system (e.g., steady-state vs. transient behaviour). 
The assessment model also incorporates a continuous feedback mechanism through sequential 
submissions: where the formatting of these submissions can be standardized (e.g., a technical 
engineering memo), students may also gain experience producing documents similar to those 
of a practicing professional/expert. This formative feedback approach facilitates ongoing 
learning and improvement, enabling students to understand their strengths and areas for 
development, and to adjust their approaches accordingly: as such, the approach provides an 
opportunity to simultaneously grow technical and non-technical skill sets. 
Assessment Design 
The assessment model is structured as a series of sequential tasks that sit atop the course 
scaffold, where students can practice and receive feedback on less complex technical skills 
(i.e., in an early task) before then applying these alongside more complex technical skills in a 
subsequent task: in this way, the model provides an opportunity to both revise and perfect 
foundational, technical skills through both repetition and extension. Ideally, tasks should involve 
a combination of practical experimentation and theoretical analysis. For instance, a task might 
involve adjusting the parameters of a hardware module to generate specific signals, collecting 
data on these signals, and then analysing this data to draw conclusions. The tasks are designed 
to be completed in sequence, where each task can build on the previous (technical) content: 
this scaffolding approach facilitates the progressive development of understanding and skills 
and promotes a deeper and more integrated understanding of the subject matter. 
• Student prompt (generalised example): In this lab-based assignment, you will explore key 
principles and methods of a technical task relevant to your chosen discipline. You will conduct 
a series of experiments, each designed to deepen your understanding of a specific topic. You 
will work with a real-world (‘authentic’) piece of hardware or experiential site, and manipulate 
various parameters to observe and interpret the outcomes. Your results will provide empirical 
evidence to verify theoretical principles and understand practical applications. Throughout this 
assignment, you will be required to think critically, apply your knowledge, and interpret your 
results. You will be expected to draw upon your theoretical understanding of the subject 
matter, use appropriate methodologies, and analyse your results in depth. You will also need 
to discuss your findings in relation to the wider context of real-world systems and implications. 
You should demonstrate your ability to investigate a problem, use appropriate methods to 
address it, and both contextualise and quantify uncertainty in your outcomes. You should also 
