• Aims: The assignment aims to deepen students' technical understanding of key principles and 
methods through iteration of practice (experiment) and reflection (reporting). By conducting 
actual experiments, students can see these principles in action and understand their real-world 
applications. Critical thinking and problem-solving skills are cultivated in students, in both the 
initial effort (conducting experiments or engaging in authentic/professional technical 
experiences) and through the interpretation of their results, formulation of conclusions, and 
relation of their findings to foundational and technical concepts in the discipline. This dual 
approach fosters a deeper understanding of the subject matter and the ability to apply 
knowledge in novel contexts (e.g., engineering scenarios similar in kind, but with distinct 
features). The sequential nature of the submissions further aims to develop students' 
independence and time-management skills, particularly where portions of tasks overlap (e.g., 
experiments are conducted for Task 2 while receiving/reflecting on feedback from Task 1). 
• Tasks: While this model is designed with four equally-weighted tasks (ideally following similar 
writeup/presentation formats similar to professional practice), the approach could equivalently 
be deployed with an alternative number of tasks that were re-balanced to maintain the 
expected intensity (in hours) for students. In these example tasks, the use of “laboratory” 
denotes a physical, in-person experience within engineering and applied science, although 
the context may readily be transferred to any experiential learning opportunity appropriate to 
the discipline (e.g., in earth science curricula, sequential field excursions could be deployed in 
place of physical laboratory exercises).  
• Task 1: First Technical Laboratory Report (Weight: 25%) 
In the first technical laboratory assignment, students should ideally be presented with a 
technical application or experiential scenario that they have encountered – at least in 
part – through a previous learning experience in their course of study. This approach can 
provide some degree of comfort around the specific application of interest, while 
enabling an increase in the complexity and rigour of the analysis and laboratory 
reporting expectations (e.g., closer to that required of the professional practitioner). 
Ideally, the structure and expectations of the analysis and reporting should be 
maintained throughout each sequential laboratory report, such that students can 
directly apply the feedback they receive in this first laboratory report as they approach 
their second laboratory activity. 
• Task 2: Second Technical Laboratory Report (Weight: 25%) 
Building on the feedback received in Task 1, students undertake a second technical 
laboratory assignment around a new technical skill or application (e.g., a new piece of 
laboratory or pilot-scale equipment). As they have established a degree of comfort with 
the analysis and reporting expectations – and can immediately implement feedback 
from the first task – they can focus more of their attention toward the comprehensive 
understanding of the second laboratory task. The feedback they receive should ideally 
be focussed on the context of the second technical laboratory, with minimal necessary 
criticism applied to analysis or reporting expectations  
• Task 3: Third Technical Laboratory Report (Weight: 25%) 
Mimicking the approach of the second task, the third laboratory report provides another 
opportunity to expand or change technical context, while maintaining the analysis and 
reporting expectations of both submitted laboratory reports to date. 
