• Task 4: Integrated Analysis and Report (Weight: 25%) 
The final task involves the integration of the findings from Tasks 1 to 3. Students will write a 
comprehensive report discussing their overall findings, the relationships between the 
different tasks, and the implications relative to their chosen discipline. This report should 
demonstrate a deep understanding of the subject matter, as well as the ability to 
synthesise information and identify commonalities between the assignments (e.g., the 
propagation of uncertainty in different technical scenarios). 
• Feedback: Feedback is designed to be formative, meaning that it is provided throughout the 
process. This enables students to learn from their mistakes, improve their understanding, and 
enhance their skills on an ongoing basis. Feedback is provided in relation to both the specific 
tasks and the overall objectives of the assignment. This includes feedback on students' 
understanding of the subject matter, their ability to apply knowledge in practical contexts, and 
their data analysis and interpretation skills. Feedback is intended to be constructive, providing 
clear guidance on areas for improvement, as well as recognition of areas of strength. It 
encourages students to reflect on their learning, identify areas for development, and take 
responsibility for their own improvement. After the final task, overall feedback will be provided 
on the students' performance throughout the assignment. This feedback will highlight the 
students' strengths and areas for improvement, and provide guidance for future learning. In 
addition to instructor feedback, students may also be encouraged to provide feedback to 
each other. This can help students develop their ability to critically evaluate work and provide 
constructive feedback, and can also provide additional perspectives and insights. 
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
In this model, tasks are designed to be completed independently, requiring students to 
demonstrate their own understanding and skills. The requirement for in-depth analysis and 
interpretation encourages one mechanism of integrity, as students are expected to provide their 
own insights and conclusions, rather than simply reporting results. Critically, the sequential nature 
of the laboratory assignments, when combined with individualised feedback along each vector 
of criterion reference, ensures that each student can demonstrate their development in latter 
submissions. As one extension concept, the instructor may also use a flipped classroom 
environment to discuss feedback and expectations with each student following their individual 
submissions, as a vehicle through which to establish academic integrity on a per-student basis. 
Varying Assessment Elements to Maintain Academic Integrity 
To maintain academic integrity and keep the assessment model fresh and challenging, it is 
recommended to vary certain elements of the tasks each year. This could involve changing the 
parameters of the physical or digital laboratory experiences/tasks, the data collection 
requirements, or the analysis and interpretation tasks. It should be noted that varying the tasks in 
this way requires a moderate amount of effort from the academic, particularly in the limit that 
physical laboratory exercises will be affected. However, the sequential variation of each aspect 
in the assessment (e.g., over a three-year period) may function to balance the overall workload 
expectation in preparing each class. 
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
The use of open-ended technical questions is a best practice to provide students with the 
freedom to approach the tasks in their own way and to explore areas of particular interest. For 
example, asking a student to “recommend a technical option” of those presented in the 
