laboratory prompt/experience requires them to develop and reflect on their criteria for 
recommendation, combining both their developing technical skills and personal outlook. While 
the recommendation of this kind must be supported by evidence, it is a vehicle through which 
they can demonstrate their creative approach to problem-solving and, most importantly, learn 
how to insert their personal areas of interest as an emerging technical expert. Similarly, open-
ended laboratory prompts (e.g. in Task 3) could encourage them to think critically and 
creatively, to ask their own questions, and to seek their own answers. This not only enhances their 
learning, but also makes the assessment more engaging and relevant.  
Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
In developing the laboratory prompts and, depending on disciplines, target questions, the 
model should deploy clear and inclusive language, to make the task selection and execution 
accessible to all students. The requirements should provide flexibility in terms of task completion, 
allowing students to work at their own pace and in their own way within the time limits of 
providing feedback prior to the next task submission. The model also recommends flexibility for 
necessary accommodations, such as alternative submission formats (e.g., a recorded oral 
presentation of results) or additional time, to ensure that all students can fully participate or 
choose the communication format (written, oral recorded, oral presentation) that would 
provide the greatest growth opportunity. Where open-ended technical questions can be 
deployed, the model encourages students to bring their unique perspectives and experiences 
to the tasks, fostering an inclusive learning environment where all students feel valued and 
respected.  
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
For successful implementation of the assessment model, instructors should: 
 Provide clear instructions and expectations for each task, including explicit instructions to 
guide experiential laboratory/field exercises.  
 Provide ongoing, constructive feedback to help students improve, which is particularly 
valuable as students are completing their experiential learning components. 
 Foster an inclusive and respectful learning environment, including considerations around 
access and workload. 
Generative AI and Other Technology Considerations 
The use of laboratory, pilot-scale, or experiential field activities can each carry significant 
technology considerations, particularly in the limit that digital laboratory methods are to be 
deployed. As importantly, the use of complex or discipline-specific analysis methods or software 
should be considered from the perspective of previous training/experience and accessibility: 
where necessary, asynchronous training modules should be provided to ensure students are 
able to equally address the data analysis methods.  
Particularly for complex, technical assignments, chat-based generative AI tools can be useful for 
students to workshop and bound the nature of questions and assessment methods they plan to 
deploy. For example, if they are approaching a new piece of equipment for the first time, 
generative AI tools could be used to help them identify the aspects of the equipment that are 
new to their experience, enabling them to create a personalised study plan using class 
resources (e.g., textbook or online materials) to ensure they are prepared for the laboratory 
exercises. In analysing laboratory or field data, chat-based AI tools may also provide 
recommendations for common techniques, as a way to help students get started with the 
