Overview of Assessment Model 
This two-part assessment aims to provide students with a comprehensive understanding of 
organisational wellbeing issues and the development of evidence-based action plans. The 
assignment is versatile and applicable across various disciplines. Part 1 of the assignment 
requires students to conduct an in-depth literature review guided by a challenge faced by a 
real organisation. Students will identify an organisational problem related to wellbeing by 
interviewing a manager or supervisor from a real organisation. They will then ""diagnose"" the 
context in which wellbeing is being analysed and conduct a targeted literature review on a 
relevant wellbeing issue. The word limit for this task is 1,500 words, excluding references and 
cover page. Part 2 focuses on generating an evidence-based action plan to address the issue 
identified in Part 1. The plan should include a problem diagnosis plan, recommendations for 
strategic interventions, and an evaluation plan for the interventions. The word limit for this part is 
also 1,500 words, excluding references and cover page. 
Assessment Design 
Throughout both parts of the assignment, students are expected to adhere to the guidelines 
and marking criteria provided, which includes using appropriate headings, conducting 
comprehensive literature reviews, and ensuring their work aligns with the evidence-based 
practitioner model. The assignment will be graded using a rubric, and students are encouraged 
to review it to understand the expectations. 
• Student prompt: The model requires students to conduct an in-depth literature review to 
analyse a real-world organisational issue related to the discipline. This project consists of two 
parts. In Part 1, students interview a practitioner from a real organisation to identify an issue 
relevant to the discipline area (e.g., a manger to identify an employee issue). Students then 
research the organisation and conduct a targeted literature review on the relevant issue. In 
Part 2, students develop an evidence-based action plan to address the identified issue, 
including a problem diagnosis, recommended strategic interventions, and an evaluation plan. 
Students are expected to support their analyses and recommendations with relevant 
academic literature and empirical evidence.  
• Aims: The primary aim of this assessment is to develop students' understanding of a real-world 
organisational challenge, and to deploy their disciplinary knowledge to recommend a 
solution. Through this assignment, students will learn to: 
• Apply theoretical and empirical knowledge to diagnose and address real-world 
organisational problems. 
• Conduct in-depth literature reviews to explore the causes and potential solutions for 
specific wellbeing issues. 
• Develop evidence-based action plans that align with disciplinary principles and best 
practices. 
• Evaluate the effectiveness of proposed interventions using appropriate methodologies. 
• Enhance critical thinking, problem-solving, and communication skills. 
• Tasks: There are two component tasks within this assessment structure, enabling students to 
receive interim feedback on their choice of organisation/issue and to fine-tune their research 
methods and sources before progressing to the second submission. There is a third (optional, 
but recommended) task on student self-reflection.  
