• Strategic Case Analysis (Part 1) 
• Conduct an interview with a practitioner from a real organisation to identify an 
issue related to the discipline. 
• Research the organisation using secondary sources to provide contextual 
information. 
• Conduct a targeted literature review on the identified issue, focusing on factors 
relevant to the organisation's context. 
• Strategic Case Analysis (Part 2) 
• Develop a problem diagnosis plan based on the literature review findings. 
• Propose two strategic interventions to address the identified issue, supported by 
academic literature and empirical evidence. 
• Develop an evaluation plan to measure the effectiveness of the proposed 
interventions. 
• Reflection (optional): Following the receipt of feedback from their second submission, 
students are asked to reflect on the learning experience and integrate newly acquired 
knowledge and skills into their professional development. 
• Feedback: The feedback aims to help students identify their strengths and areas for 
improvement, providing guidance on how to enhance their understanding of disciplinary 
concepts, critical thinking skills, and professional development. Across all three above 
submissions (including the optional reflection), students will receive feedback in the following 
areas, aligned with a detailed set of criterion references (see below for an example): 
• Quality and relevance of the literature review: How well did the student synthesise and 
apply academic literature to the organisational issue? 
• Problem diagnosis and intervention proposals: How effectively did the student identify 
the root causes of the issue and propose evidence-based solutions? 
• Evaluation plan: Was the proposed evaluation plan appropriate and well-designed to 
assess the effectiveness of the interventions? 
• Communication and presentation: How well did the student organise, structure, and 
present their ideas, using appropriate language, citations, and formatting? 
• Reflection and professional development: How effectively did the student reflect on 
their learning experience and identify opportunities for personal growth? 
As one feedback extension concept, students may optionally have the opportunity to present 
their outcomes to the original interviewee (or company/partner), and to receive and 
document feedback for the above-described reflection activity. This would enable both an 
exemplar of Work-Integrated Learning (wherein the student’s contributions may provide 
meaningful value to the participating staff/organisation), while helping the student develop an 
enhanced sense of purpose within their chosen discipline.  
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
In this assessment model, academic integrity is promoted by incorporating real-world context, 
fostering transparency in data collection, requiring a rigorous literature review, providing 
