structured guidelines and marking criteria, emphasising proper attribution and citation norms, 
and ensuring continuity and integration across the assignment: 
• Continuity and integration: Part 2 of the assignment requires students to include a full 
copy of Part 1, ensuring continuity and coherence across the various components of the 
assessment. This requirement promotes academic integrity by encouraging students to 
maintain consistency and build upon their previous work. 
• Real-world context: Students are required to interview a practitioner from a real 
organisation, providing an authentic basis for their analysis. This fosters an appreciation 
for applied research and discourages plagiarism or fabrication of data. 
• Audio recording of interviews: To maintain transparency and ensure the authenticity of 
the interview, students must audio record their conversations and upload them to the 
Learning Management System (LMS). This requirement helps verify the legitimacy of the 
primary data collected. Critically, any recording must be explicitly discussed and agreed 
to between all parties, as instructors and students must first be aware of appropriate 
governing regulation (e.g., whether single- or multi-party recording consent is required in 
any jurisdictions involved).  
• Structured guidelines and marking criteria: The assignment provides clear guidelines and 
marking criteria, which support students in understanding the expectations and 
maintaining academic integrity: by including preliminary review and discussion 
opportunities with students, the assessment provides an opportunity for instructors to 
familiarise themselves with each student’s intended workplan (before it commences).  
• Attribution and citation norms: The assignment emphasises the importance of proper 
citation and referencing, which ensures that students acknowledge the intellectual 
contributions of others and avoid plagiarism.  
Varying Assessment Elements to Maintain Academic Integrity 
As students are able to nominate their preferred organisation and case study perspective for 
analysis, the model naturally introduces variability to minimise the use or possibility of misconduct 
while encouraging a student-centred approach to learning. It is important for the lecturer to be 
aware and supportive of the student’s choices, alongside previous case study perspectives and 
participating organisations. 
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
This assessment task fosters student-centred learning by engaging students in real-world 
problem-solving, promoting research and information literacy, encouraging collaboration and 
reflection (although optional in the assessment structure), and integrating theory with practice. 
By providing a flexible and adaptable framework, this task prepares students for success in a 
diverse range of professional environments. 
• Active engagement with a real organisation: Students are required to conduct 
interviews with practitioners from a real organisation, allowing them to interact with 
professionals and gain insights into organisational challenges. This experience helps 
students develop communication and analytical skills while encouraging them to take 
responsibility for their learning process. 
• Research and information literacy: Students are expected to perform in-depth literature 
reviews and gather secondary data to diagnose and analyse the selected organisation. 
