By encouraging students to access, evaluate, and use information from various sources, 
this assessment enhances their research and information literacy skills. 
• Problem-solving and critical thinking: The task requires students to diagnose the context 
in which well-being is being analysed, identify potential causes of the problem, and 
propose evidence-based strategic interventions on behalf of the discipline area. This 
process fosters problem-solving skills and critical thinking as students evaluate different 
solutions and make informed decisions. 
• Collaboration and peer learning: Students are expected to upload and share their 
interviews with their tutor and peers, creating opportunities for collaboration, feedback, 
and peer learning. This process encourages students to learn from each other's 
experiences, develop their communication skills, and work together in a supportive 
environment. 
• Reflection and personal development: The assessment task includes a reflection 
component, which requires students to evaluate their learning experiences and identify 
areas for improvement. This encourages self-awareness, personal growth, and a 
commitment to continuous improvement. 
• Integration of theory and practice: The task demands that students apply theoretical 
concepts and empirical research findings to a real-life organisational problem. This 
integration helps students understand the relevance of their academic learning to 
practical situations and fosters their ability to transfer knowledge across contexts. 
• Flexibility and adaptability: The assignment is designed to be applicable across various 
disciplines, promoting transferable skills and the ability to adapt to different contexts. This 
adaptability prepares students for diverse professional environments and supports their 
long-term career development. 
Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
This assessment task design allows students to engage with real-world organisations and conduct 
interviews with practitioners in their discipline area. This approach enables students from various 
socioeconomic backgrounds to access resources and gain valuable insights into organisational 
challenges, as it does not require any costly materials or resources to complete. Critically, the 
thoughtful implementation of this assessment model ensures that all graduates have some 
exposure to industrial practice (relevant to the discipline). It includes the option of online 
interviews with the organisation in lieu of face-to-face, if required or preferable, reducing costs, 
and providing accessibility to those with mobility challenges or caring responsibilities. 
Generative AI and Other Technology Considerations 
To ensure equity in student interviews and engagement, access to a digital meeting platform 
(such as Zoom or Microsoft Teams) and appropriate equipment is necessary. Increasingly, these 
platforms are equipped with generative AI tools for meeting minutes/summarisation, which 
could be critical in allowing the student to focus on the subject of the interview as opposed to 
both engaging with the subject while manually taking notes.  
When first approaching a case study, the use of chat-based generative AI tools may also 
provide the opportunity for students to explore different dimensions to the problem. If the chosen 
organisation and perspective represent a large potential workload, chat-based tools could 
allow the student to quickly bound the key aspects of the problem, and to refine this bounding 
in to a work scope that is suitable for delivery within the assessment time frame. Where there 
