Overview of Assessment Model 
The assessment model revolves around a field trip report and is an excellent method of 
engaging students with real-world applications of their academic knowledge. In the motivating 
example, the student is required to visit a location (a farm) and then analyse and reflect on the 
practices and techniques being used there in a technical, discipline-specific report. This 
submission must then be linked back to unit concepts and theories, providing an opportunity for 
students to apply their understanding in a practical context. This model is beneficial because it 
encourages critical thinking, observational skills, and the ability to analyse and interpret real-
world scenarios. Furthermore, it fosters independent learning as students must conduct their own 
research and form conclusions based on their observations and findings. 
Assessment Design 
• Student prompt: For this assessment, students are required to participate in a field trip to a 
location relevant to their unit of study. They are then to write a report that critically analyses the 
practices and techniques being used at this location, prompted by questions or considerations 
relevant to their class level and discipline area (e.g., from the perspective of soil science, the 
students may be prompted with an abstract, high-level question such as whether a given 
operation may result in site water contamination and how this could be prevented). The report 
should demonstrate the student's understanding of the unit theories and concepts, and their 
application to real-world scenarios. Students should provide an analysis of whether the 
practices observed are sustainable, economically viable, and yield-enhancing. The report 
must also assess how the practices align with the principles of sustainable intensification. 
Students are encouraged to support their analysis with relevant scientific literature and to 
discuss the broader implications of the practices observed.  
• Aims: The primary aim of this assessment is to facilitate student understanding of the practical 
application of unit concepts and theories. The task encourages students to think critically 
about the information they have learned and apply it in a real-world context. They are 
required to evaluate the practices and techniques observed during the field trip, thereby 
enhancing their observational and analytical skills. Another aim is to promote independent 
research skills. Students are expected to supplement their findings from the field trip with 
scientific literature and data. This not only broadens their knowledge base but also provides 
them with a well-rounded perspective on the topic. The report also aims to foster written 
communication skills. Students must present their findings and analysis in a clear, concise, and 
structured manner. The ability to communicate complex ideas effectively is an essential skill 
that will be beneficial in their academic journey and beyond. Finally, the assessment aims to 
instil an appreciation for sustainable practices. Students are encouraged to critically assess 
whether the practices observed align with the principles of sustainable intensification, and to 
discuss the broader implications of these practices on the environment and society.  
• Tasks: Students are first required to participate in a field trip to a location relevant to their unit 
of study: while the motivating assessment deployed in-person activities, the use of advanced 
(video-conferencing and/or metaverse) technologies may enable alternative means of 
accessing off-campus resources. Students should take this opportunity to observe and gather 
data on the practices and techniques being used at the location. It would be beneficial for 
students to take notes or photos to aid in their analysis. Following the field trip, students should 
conduct independent research to supplement their findings. This could involve accessing 
scientific literature, data, or other relevant resources. Students should then analyse their 
findings and form conclusions about the practices observed. The final task is to write a report 
presenting their analysis. The report should include an introduction, discussion of the practices 
