observed, analysis of these practices in the context of unit theories and concepts, and a 
conclusion.  
• Feedback: Feedback on the student's report will be provided in a comprehensive and 
detailed manner, focusing on the content, structure, and quality of analysis. Feedback will 
highlight the strengths of the report, such as well-reasoned arguments, effective use of 
evidence, and clear communication of ideas. Areas for improvement, such as gaps in analysis, 
lack of depth in the discussion, or issues with the structure, will also be pointed out. In addition 
to written feedback, there may be opportunities for one-on-one feedback sessions. These 
sessions will allow students to ask questions and gain further understanding of how to improve 
their work. Feedback is intended to be constructive, aimed at helping students improve their 
future work: as such, maximising the alignment between these task submission(s) and 
expected disciplinary practice is essential to realising the model’s value.  
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
The unique nature of the task(s), which requires students to attend a field trip and base their 
report on their personal observations, provides an intrinsic method to establish integrity of each 
student’s learning and assessment submission. The requirement for students to integrate unit 
concepts and theories into their report further ensures that they demonstrate their understanding 
and do not simply copy from sources. While students may attend the field trip together, each 
student is expected to write their own unique report. This promotes a sense of responsibility and 
ownership of one's work. As one extension concept, lecturers may wish to meet with students to 
review their preliminary findings, where the continued nature of the dialogic interaction further 
promotes the establishment of academic integrity for each student throughout the experience 
(i.e., as opposed to detecting violations of integrity only at the end of the process). 
Varying Assessment Elements to Maintain Academic Integrity 
Variety in assessment elements is critical to maintain academic integrity and to ensure that the 
learning needs of all students are met. As the design of this model focusses on a singular field trip 
experience, changing the location of, or parameters within, this experience presents the simplest 
vehicle to fundamentally alter the assessment each year for the entire cohort. However, such an 
approach may be costly or require significant time investment from the academic: if repeated 
experiences are to be used, altering the perspective of analysis (e.g., choosing a different 
technical thread on which to focus within the same field trip) between years may be a useful 
secondary point of alteration.  
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
The model encourages active learning and critical thinking, as students must observe, analyse, 
and interpret real-world practices and relate them back to unit concepts and theories. The field 
trip component of the assessment provides a unique learning experience for students. It provides 
students with the opportunity to observe and engage with the real-world applications of what 
they have learned in class. This experiential learning enhances their understanding and makes 
the learning experience more meaningful and engaging. The research component of the 
assessment promotes independent learning. It also encourages self-directed learning, as 
students must take responsibility for their own learning and develop their own conclusions. The 
report writing component of the assessment enhances students' written communication skills: it 
requires them to present their findings and analysis in a clear, structured, and coherent manner. 
This is an essential skill that will be beneficial in their future academic and professional careers.  
