example, the use of video conferencing technology may be appropriate if the experience 
primarily relies on engagement with others in the field. Alternatively, the emerging capabilities of 
virtual/augmented reality technology may provide a vehicle for students to explore 
environments without the need for travel. The research component of the assessment allows for 
flexibility and caters to diverse learning styles. Students can choose from a variety of resources to 
conduct their research, allowing them to learn in a way that suits them best. The report writing 
component of the assessment is equally accessible to all students, as it only requires access to a 
computer and the internet. Students who require additional support, such as those with learning 
differences, can be provided with extra resources or accommodations to ensure they can 
complete the assessment effectively (e.g., through their UniAccess Adjustment Plan). 
Importantly, the assessment encourages diverse perspectives and interpretations, valuing the 
unique insights that each student brings. Feedback is provided in a constructive and respectful 
manner, ensuring that all students feel valued and supported in their learning.  
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
To successfully implement this assessment model, there are several best practices to follow. 
Clear and detailed instructions for the assessment should be provided to all students. This should 
include the objectives of the assessment, the tasks required, the grading criteria, and the due 
dates. Regular reminders and updates can also be helpful to keep students on track.  Students 
should also be given the opportunity at an early stage to raise any concerns about their ability 
to participate in the field trip, for example due to ill-health, disability, or caring responsibilities so 
that suitable accommodations can be made. Adequate support should be provided for the 
field trip component of the assessment: this could include arranging transportation, providing 
safety instructions, and ensuring that the location is accessible for all students. Critically, 
feedback should be provided in a timely and constructive manner, particularly when students 
are actively collecting and reflecting on their observations: this feedback should highlight the 
strengths of the student's work (particularly if they have nominated one or more dimensions of 
personal interest in the work), while discussing areas for improvement.  
Generative AI and Other Technology Considerations 
In this assessment model, technology plays a crucial role in enhancing learning and ensuring 
accessibility. For the field trip component, technology can be used to enhance the learning 
experience. For example, students can use their phones or digital cameras to take photos or 
videos, which can be included in their report. For the research component, students will require 
access to online databases and resources, which may take the form of standard internet 
searches (i.e., from trustworthy resources: generative AI tools connected with search databases 
may also provide students with the opportunity to quickly scaffold and integrate areas of 
personal interest within the project. For example, in a farm visit within a soil science class, the 
student may also wish to also comment on the economic productivity and viability of the farm 
itself: while this content may be beyond the remit of the class curriculum, the use of AI-powered 
literature search tools may aid students in extending disciplinary context. 
