Overview of Assessment Model 
The assessment model is a hands-on, project-based assignment designed to simulate a real-
world application of accounting principles and financial management. The assignment tasks 
students to manage the financial transactions of a fictional business using MYOB Essentials (in the 
motivating assessment), a cloud-based accounting software. In place of MYOB Essentials, other 
disciplines may consider a corollary professional software that is deployed within their respective 
industries (e.g., industry-standard process engineering software). This model integrates 
theoretical knowledge with practical skills, moving beyond traditional lecture-based assessments 
to provide students with a controlled exposure to professional practice. It encourages active 
learning, problem-solving, critical thinking, and the practical application of concepts learned in 
class. The model also emphasises accountability, as students are responsible for managing the 
company's financial health (as a non-negotiable requirement of the profession), making it an 
effective tool for teaching personal responsibility and professionalism. 
Assessment Design 
• Student prompt: Students are presented with the scenario of stepping into the role of a 
bookkeeper for a fictional business. They are provided with a series of transactions and 
financial data related to the business, which they must input and manage using MYOB 
Essentials software. Throughout the assignment, students are expected to create and maintain 
the company's accounting ledgers, handle bank reconciliation, manage sales and purchases, 
and deal with inventory. They are also required to generate and interpret various financial 
reports such as profit-and-loss statements, balance sheets, and cash flow statements.  
• Aims: The primary aim of this assessment is to enable students to apply theoretical accounting 
knowledge in a practical, real-world context. The students are expected to demonstrate their 
understanding and proficiency in handling various financial transactions, managing financial 
data, and using accounting software. Specifically, this assessment aims to help students: 
• Develop a comprehensive understanding of the role and responsibilities of a 
bookkeeper (or similar required, professional task) in a professional setting. 
• Gain hands-on experience in managing and interpreting data sources required for 
professional practice (financial data, in this example). 
• Familiarise themselves with the use of standardized professional software, preparing 
them for similar tools they may encounter in their future careers while underscoring the 
importance of these skill set(s) to professional practice. 
• Develop problem-solving skills and the ability to think critically about professional 
practice (e.g., financial management), alongside ethical considerations that emerge 
with confidential data management and utilisation. 
• Tasks: The assessment tasks are designed to cover a sequenced range of professional activities 
(that is, a gradually expanding scope of professional exposure, allowing students to check and 
confirm foundational steps in software before analysis and the drafting of recommendations). 
