• Task #2 - Software Deployment: This task requires students to generate a series of mock 
professional entries/uses within the software. In the specific case of an accounting 
student, this involves recording business transactions (students must accurately record 
various financial transactions, including sales, purchases, and payments), managing 
bank feeds (students must correctly link bank transactions with the corresponding entries 
in the software), and reconciling bank accounts (students are tasked with ensuring the 
company's financial records match the bank statements). Students are provided with 
self-directed instructions and have two weeks to complete this task. 
• Task #3 - Professional Deliverable: After having successfully setup and deployed the 
professional software, students are asked to complete a formal deliverable that would 
be expected of a practicing industry professional. In the example of an accounting 
student, this would involve the generation of financial reports: at various stages of the 
assignment, students are required to generate and analyse financial reports, including 
profit-and-loss statements, balance sheets, and cash flow statements. Students should 
be given a larger amount of time (than in previous tasks) to complete the professional 
deliverable, and should ideally be able to demonstrate that they have acted on 
previous feedback in the submission of this task. 
• Feedback: Feedback is provided at various stages of the assignment, allowing students to 
correct errors and improve their performance in subsequent tasks. Feedback mechanisms 
include: (i) Instructor feedback, which comes in the form of written comments on submitted 
work; (ii) peer feedback, where students are encouraged to review each other's work and 
provide feedback through the class Learning Management System; and/or (iii) self-assessment, 
where each student is encouraged to reflect on their prior submissions relative to criterion 
references, identifying areas where they did well and areas where they can improve.  
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
The provision of a unique set of financial transactions for each student represents a best practice 
to encourage original work, wherein students are unable to copy solutions or approaches from 
peers or previous years. The authentic (professional) nature of the tasks within the students’ 
chosen discipline requires active engagement and understanding of the material, discouraging 
short-cut methods where students appreciate they will be expected to perform the same task as 
a professional. The use of an accounting software also allows for the tracking of each student's 
actions, providing a record of their work that can be checked for authenticity (identical to what 
is expected in the field).  
Varying Assessment Elements to Maintain Academic Integrity 
 Varying financial transactions: Each student can be given a unique set of financial 
transactions to manage. This not only discourages plagiarism but also provides a more diverse 
learning experience.  
 Different business scenarios: Different fictional business scenarios can be used, each with its 
own unique challenges and considerations. This adds an extra layer of complexity and ensures 
that students cannot simply copy answers from previous assignments. 
 Varying assessment formats: While the main task is hands-on and project-based, other 
assessment formats such as quizzes, exams, presentations, or written reports can also be 
incorporated. This ensures a comprehensive evaluation of the students' understanding and 
skills. 
