 Peer review: Incorporating a peer review element not only promotes a collaborative learning 
environment but also adds another layer of scrutiny, discouraging academic misconduct. 
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
This assessment model is heavily student-focused, promoting active learning, engagement, and 
personal responsibility. The hands-on nature of the tasks encourages students to take ownership 
of their learning, fostering a deep understanding of the material. The model is designed to be 
flexible, allowing students to work at their own pace and approach the tasks in a way that suits 
their learning style. The use of real-world software also provides a relevant and engaging 
learning experience, preparing students for future careers. Furthermore, the model promotes a 
collaborative learning environment. Students are encouraged to work together, share ideas, 
and help each other understand complex concepts, in the limit they have received sufficiently 
diverse inputs/conditions such that direct copying is not practical. This not only enhances their 
learning experience but also fosters teamwork and communication skills.   
Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
This model is designed with a focus on inclusivity, considering the diverse needs and 
backgrounds of students. By promoting an accessible learning environment, the model ensures 
that all students, regardless of their backgrounds or circumstances, have an equal opportunity 
to succeed. The use of a cloud-based accounting software is a significant aspect of this. Since 
the software is accessible online, students can access it from anywhere, at any time. This is 
particularly beneficial for students who may not have regular access to campus facilities, 
including remote learners and those with caring responsibilities. The professional-level software 
platform (MYOB, in this example) is designed user-friendly and intuitive (being an industry 
standard), accommodating students with varying levels of technical proficiency. Tutorials and 
support resources are provided to help students familiarise themselves with the software, 
ensuring that all students can effectively engage with the tasks. The model also considers the 
diverse learning styles and needs of students.  
The hands-on, practical nature of the tasks caters to hands-on learners (e.g., those who may 
wish to print out and analyse accounting data by hand), while the inclusion of financial charts 
and data presentations can cater to visual learners. Auditory learners can benefit from 
discussions and feedback/consultation sessions. For students with disabilities, the model can be 
adapted to meet their specific needs. For instance, students with visual impairments can use 
screen readers or other assistive technologies to interact with the software. Students with 
disabilities can be provided with additional support and accommodations, such as extended 
deadlines or one-on-one tutoring (per their UniAccess Accommodation Plan at UWA).  
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
 Clear Communication: Clearly communicate the objectives, tasks, and expectations of the 
assignment to the students. Ensure that they understand what they are required to do and how 
they will be assessed, including discussion across criterion references. 
 Technical Support: Provide adequate technical support to help students familiarise themselves 
with the software. This can include tutorials, FAQ sections, or dedicated technical support staff. 
Critically, this requires instructors to explicitly inform students of those problems that require 
support from the university’s IT department (or similar), and the instructors or tutors/facilitators to 
whom the remaining inquiries should be directed.  
