Overview of Assessment Model 
The dialogue-based assessment model motivated from UWA’s School of Design is an innovative 
approach to learning, evaluation, and feedback. This model places a significant emphasis on 
process-oriented learning and iterative design, employing a conversation-based framework that 
encourages students to think critically, communicate effectively, and develop their own unique 
design voice. The model revolves around the use of dialogue and discussion, encouraging 
students to delve into the intricacies of design. It allows for a dynamic exploration of concepts, 
principles, and methodologies, with specialised, disciplinary tasks designed to stimulate the 
students' thinking process. Instead of a one-sided submission and evaluation approach, the 
model incorporates continuous interaction between students and instructors throughout the 
duration of the assessment (e.g., similar to how a junior and senior professional may work 
together, for the purpose of both completing the project and mentoring the junior professional). 
The model uses a variety of strategies to ensure that the work submitted by students is original 
and authentic, and incorporates elements that can be varied each year to maintain academic 
integrity and keep the assessment fresh and relevant. The approach encourages students to 
bring their some of their technical interests into the tasks, helping students foster a sense of 
emerging, professional individualism. 
Assessment Design 
This assessment model consists of clearly defined tasks that students must complete, each with its 
own unique set of expectations, objectives, and deliverables. These tasks are designed to be 
sequential and progressive, with each one building on the skills and knowledge gained in the 
previous task. A key feature of this assessment model is the emphasis on feedback, which serves 
as a critical tool for learning and improvement. Feedback is provided on each task, allowing 
students to refine their work and hone their design skills. The feedback process is designed to be 
comprehensive and constructive, focusing not just on the end product but also on the process 
and methodology used by the students.  
• Student prompt: The prompt for the assessment tasks in this model is deliberately designed to 
be open-ended, allowing students to explore various design ideas and concepts. It provides a 
general theme or problem statement related to design, which students are then expected to 
interpret and explore in their own unique way. It’s important to emphasise that the open-
ended nature of the task prompt is intended to challenge students, pushing them to think 
critically and creatively about the given theme or problem statement (in other words, to 
translate a “general” expectation in to a specific set of discipline-specific, specialised tasks). It 
encourages students to delve into the intricacies of design, considering various factors such as 
functionality, aesthetics, user experience, and cultural and social implications (in the 
motivating area of design).  
 
The prompt is usually presented as a question or a challenge, asking students to design a 
solution for a specific problem or to explore a particular theme in depth. For example, the 
prompt might ask students to design a sustainable living space for a specific user group, or to 
explore some cultural implications of a particular design trend. The prompt is typically 
accompanied by a set of guidelines or expectations, outlining the objectives of the task, the 
deliverables, and the evaluation criteria. However, these guidelines are usually flexible, 
allowing students to interpret the prompt in their own unique way and to develop their own 
design methodologies.  
• Aims: The dialogue-based assessment model has several key aims, each of which is designed 
to contribute to the overall learning experience of the students. 
