unique design solutions. 
3. Develop Professional Communication: The dialogue-based nature of the model 
encourages students to develop their communication skills, both in terms of 
articulating their design ideas and in terms of receiving and responding to feedback. 
4. Enhance Discipline-Specific Skills: The tasks aim to enhance the students' technical 
skill sets (i.e., those specific to the discipline), providing them with practical experience 
in various aspects of the design process, from initial ideation and concept 
development to final execution and presentation. 
5. Foster a Professional Mindset: The model aims to help students foster a professional 
mindset (or to help migrate toward the expectation of the professional practitioner), 
which is critically developed through continuous engagement and feedback 
between students and instructors. 
• Tasks: The assessment model involves multiple tasks, each designed to help students develop 
and demonstrate their design skills. The tasks are sequential, with each one building on the skills 
and knowledge gained in the previous task. 
6. Task 1: Concept Development (30% weighting): Students are asked to develop a 
concept in response to the prompt. This involves initial research, ideation, and 
concept development. Students are expected to present their initial concept in the 
form of a concept board, along with a written explanation of their idea and the 
rationale behind it. 
7. Task 2: Design Development (40% weighting): Based on feedback from Task 1, 
students refine their concept and start developing their design. This involves further 
research, sketching, prototyping, and testing. Students are expected to present their 
design development process in the form of a design development board, along with 
a written explanation of the changes they made and the rationale behind them. 
8. Task 3: Final Design Presentation (30% weighting): Students present their final design, 
along with a comprehensive explanation of their design process, the decisions they 
made, and the rationale behind them. This can be in the form of a physical model, a 
digital rendering, a video presentation, or any other format that effectively 
communicates the design.  
• Feedback serves as a tool for learning and improvement, helping students to refine their work 
and hone their design skills. In each stage of development, students receive feedback through 
dialogue with lecturer(s) and/or expert(s) in the discipline, focusing on both the process and 
the product. The feedback process is designed to be comprehensive and constructive, 
providing students with clear guidance on how to improve their work. The feedback focuses 
on a variety of areas, including conceptual understanding, creativity, critical thinking, 
communication skills, and design skills. It encourages students to reflect on their work, to 
consider the feedback received, and to apply this feedback in future tasks. The feedback 
process also encourages students to develop their own self-assessment skills, helping them to 
become more self-aware and reflective in their design practice. This can be achieved through 
the use of self-assessment forms, reflection journals, or other self-assessment tools accessible 
within the discipline. 
