Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
The dialogue-based assessment model uses a variety of strategies to ensure academic integrity. 
These strategies include: 
 Sequential Tasks and Continuous Interaction: The tasks are designed to be sequential, with 
each one building on the skills and knowledge gained in the previous task. As students 
dialogue their progress and intermediate outcomes with instructors(s) and/or practicing 
expert(s) at each stage of the process, the approach makes it difficult for students to plagiarise 
or submit work that is not their own. 
 Detailed Feedback: The detailed feedback provided on each task – through live dialogue 
and/or written means – helps to ensure that the work submitted by students is their own, as it 
requires them to reflect on their work and to make improvements based on the feedback 
received (not just asynchronously, but in real time). Importantly, they are expected to have 
addressed this feedback (in one form or another) before their next engagement with 
instructor(s) or practicing expert(s), providing an intrinsic vehicle for accountability in the 
process. 
Varying Assessment Elements to Maintain Academic Integrity 
To maintain academic integrity and keep the assessment fresh and relevant, the model 
incorporates elements that can be varied each year, including the prompt, the tasks, the 
evaluation criteria, and the feedback process. The dialogic feedback process can also be 
varied by changing the methods used to provide feedback (e.g., written, recorded audio, or 
live interaction), the timing of the feedback, or the specific focus or foci of the feedback. 
Importantly, variations in feedback can be facilitated by a team of instructors working together, 
where practical. 
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
The model encourages students to take ownership of their learning, allowing them to explore 
their interests and passions within the framework of the assessment. The open-ended nature of 
the tasks allows students to choose their own direction and focus, making the learning 
experience more personal and meaningful. The model also allows students to develop skills and 
knowledge that are relevant to their career aspirations: tasks are designed to simulate real-world 
design challenges, providing students with practical experience and preparing them for the 
realities of the design profession. The continuous feedback process requires students to reflect 
on their work and to make improvements based on the feedback received, underscored 
through dialogic interaction with instructor(s)/expert(s). This encourages students to be proactive 
in their learning, and helps them to develop a growth mindset.   
Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
The model is flexible, allowing for adjustments to be made to accommodate the needs of 
different students, through direct dialogue with students. The incorporation of UniAccess 
Adjustment Plans (at UWA) within the assessment framework ensures students with disabilities can 
be provided with additional support or accommodations to ensure that they can fully 
participate in the assessment. The model also supports diversity by encouraging students to bring 
their unique perspectives and experiences into their work. The open-ended nature of the tasks 
allows students to explore diverse ideas and concepts, and to express their unique design voice. 
The model is accessible, using straightforward language and clear instructions to ensure that all 
students can understand and complete the tasks. The feedback process is also designed to be 
