accessible (with written, recorded audio/video, and/or live feedback sessions as preferred by 
the student), providing clear and constructive feedback that students can use to improve their 
work.  
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
The successful implementation of the dialogue-based assessment model depends on a few key 
factors: 
• Clear Communication: It's important to communicate the expectations and objectives 
of the assessment clearly to students. This includes explaining the tasks, the evaluation 
criteria, and the feedback process in detail. 
• Continuous Feedback: Regular interaction between students and instructors is crucial. 
This can be achieved through regular check-ins, discussions, and feedback sessions. The 
feedback provided should be constructive, focusing not just on the end product but 
also on the process and methodology used by the students. 
• Flexibility: The model should be flexible, allowing for adjustments to be made to 
accommodate the needs of different students. Regular monitoring and evaluation of 
the assessment process is important to ensure that it remains effective and relevant, 
particularly within the individual interests and growth opportunities for different students. 
Generative AI and Other Technology Considerations 
The dialogue-based assessment model can be implemented using a variety of digital tools and 
platforms. These can include online discussion forums, digital submission platforms, virtual 
meeting tools, and digital design software. Where the use of industry- or discipline-specific 
software is required, consideration should be given to the previous scaffolding/learning 
experiences within the curriculum. If students have had limited exposure to necessary software, 
the use of asynchronous training modules may provide additional assurance that each student 
can approach the design tasks on equitable footing.  
The authentic open-ended nature of this assessment model may provide the opportunity to 
combine the use of chat-based generative AI models as a complement to the dialogic 
interaction with instructors and experts. Particularly for students with limited exposure to open-
ended design problems, the use of chat-based AI may provide support in identifying boundaries 
to the problem, nominating common solution approaches, and together identifying a unique 
solution strategy to the student. However, the value of deploying generative AI to open-ended 
problems varies heavily across technical and creative disciplines, where clear instructions and 
examples should be provided early in the experience to ensure students are cognisant of the 
expectations of professional deployment.   
 
Example Criterion References 
In this example, the unsatisfactory, borderline (benchmark), satisfactory, and excellent 
adjudication bands could similarly correspond with 30, 60, 80, and 100% achievement thresholds 
as with above criterion references. The weighting of each row and column (in the example 
below) should scale with the level and discipline of the unit involved, considering higher level 
units may have greater technical and professional performance expectations. 
