Overview of Assessment Model 
The assessment is designed to test students' research, analytical, and writing skills in their 
respective discipline. Students will choose one of three research topics and write a well-
researched, well-structured paper of 2250-2500 words. This assessment will contribute to 30% of 
the final grade for the unit. Depending on the academic’s intention, the assessment can be 
deployed as a “deep dive” within discipline or as an exploration of interdisciplinary or 
transdisciplinary content, fostering a broader understanding and critical thinking skills. The 
expected word count and assessment weighting is adaptable to reflect unit level and discipline 
approach.  
Assessment Design 
 Student Prompt: The student prompt should clearly outline the objectives of the assignment, 
providing a clear and concise description of the task at hand. It should include relevant 
context, instructions on the format and structure of the assignment, and any specific topics or 
questions that students must address. Additionally, the student prompt should set expectations 
regarding the depth and breadth of research required and any particular focus areas to be 
considered. For example, in the motivating Law School assignment, the student prompt 
presents three research topics, each with its own context and task. This allows students to 
choose a topic that best suits their interests while ensuring they understand the expectations 
and requirements for the assignment. The student prompt can be broken up and explored in 
class as per the scaffolding approach, outlined in the timeline model above, or released in a 
combined way early in the teaching period.  
 Aims: The primary aim of the assessment is to evaluate students' understanding of the subject 
matter and their ability to apply disciplinary principles and concepts to specific scenarios or 
issues. Within this, the assessment task aims to support student development in the following 
domains:  
• Conduct independent research within the discipline, and engage with diverse sources 
to develop a comprehensive understanding of a chosen topic;  
• Analyse and critically evaluate principles and arguments within the discipline, while 
demonstrating an understanding of the subject matter;  
• Formulate and present persuasive, well-structured, and coherent arguments within the 
context of the given research topic;  
• Develop original arguments and engage in original analysis, demonstrating their ability 
to think critically and creatively about the discipline area. 
 Tasks: There is only one submission task within this assessment (the Research Paper itself). To 
complete this, students are responsible for completing a sequential set of cognitive sub-tasks 
(although these are neither submitted nor assessed individually):  
• Selecting one of the three research topics provided in the prompt (the instructor(s) 
nominate the three), providing students with a partial ability to infuse their personal and 
emerging professional interests within the assessment;  
• Conducting independent research on the chosen topic, engaging with a range of 
appropriate sources, including legislation, case law, and scholarly articles;  
• Developing a clear and persuasive argument or analysis, supported by evidence from 
the research, and presenting this argument in a logical, coherent manner; and  
