• Ensuring compliance with the nominated referencing style, including accurate footnotes 
and bibliography, alongside any stylistic or other submission requirement expected by 
disciplinary experts. 
 Feedback: Feedback should ideally focus on the student's ability to apply discipline-specific 
principles and engage in critical analysis. Additionally, feedback should highlight the 
effectiveness of the student's research and use of sources, as well as their compliance with 
citation and referencing guidelines. Feedback can be provided through written comments on 
the submitted work, face-to-face or virtual meetings, or in-class discussions, noting the 
importance of ensuring there is a record of feedback on which the student can reflect 
afterward.  
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
The guidelines for research tasks provide detailed instructions on how to approach the 
assignment, including how to choose a research topic, how to narrow down the focus of the 
paper, and how to properly use sources and references. By providing clear and detailed 
instructions, the assignment ensures that students have a solid understanding of what is 
expected of them and how to achieve it. By encouraging students to select the topical paper 
that most interests them, the assessment fosters a degree of intrinsic integrity by ensuring the 
student’s personal interests (and associated personal/professional growth prospects) are aligned 
with the assessment of their research paper. The addition of an interim submission or review 
stage – facilitated by dialogic interaction between instructors and students – may further 
enhance the intrinsic integrity of the assignment.  
Varying Assessment Elements to Maintain Academic Integrity 
As the academic may wish to nominate specific target questions or topics for each year’s 
assessment(s), the topics/questions should be varied each year to ensure students cannot simply 
copy from previous years’ assignments. Choosing topics that are trending in the news can 
provide students with a sense of timeliness, wherein their work authentically reflects how the 
discipline/profession is considering or approaching a topic of immediate relevance. 
Alternatively, the assessment structure can be varied to allow students to propose an alternative 
topic of their own choosing (against criteria set within the unit), to create a more flexible and 
student-centred approach: in this extension concept, the academic should keep a record of 
topics/perspectives each year for reference when providing feedback on student proposals.  
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
By offering students the opportunity to choose from a range of research topics, it promotes 
autonomy and engagement, catering to diverse interests and learning preferences. This 
flexibility encourages students to take responsibility for their own learning, fostering a deeper 
understanding of the subject matter and cultivating critical thinking skills. The assignment’s 
emphasis on research promotes the development of essential skills such as information literacy, 
time management, and organisation. Students must independently seek out high-quality 
sources to support their arguments, learning to differentiate between reliable and unreliable 
information. By requiring students to work within a set word limit, the assignment encourages 
concise and clear writing, promoting effective communication skills. 
The structure of the assessment also supports the development of analytical and problem-
solving abilities. Tasks are designed to be open-ended, prompting students to critically analyse 
issues relevant to the discipline, evaluate the effectiveness of discipline-specific resources, and 
