propose well-justified recommendations or conclusions. This approach encourages students to 
think creatively and engage with the subject matter at a deeper level, fostering the 
development of critical thinking skills. By requiring students to examine arguments and 
counterarguments and come to well-supported conclusions, the criteria encourage a holistic 
understanding of the subject matter, rather than merely memorising or reciting facts.  
Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
By providing multiple topics or submission methods/modes to select from, the assignment can 
cater to the diverse interests and learning styles of students: this flexibility allows students to 
engage with the material from a perspective that most closely resonates with them, 
accommodating different backgrounds and personal experiences. Noting the extension 
concepts discussed below, allowing students to propose their own topics (against set criteria) 
can further increase this flexibility and student-centred approach, particularly where they may 
be able to submit the assessment in a format of their preference (e.g., accepting written essays 
or oral/recorded presentations). 
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
 As much as possible, socialise with students in the first week (or two) of class to determine any 
current interest topics with which they may wish to engage.  
 As an extension concept, consider allowing students to nominate a topic/perspective of their 
own preference (against established criteria): providing feedback on both the proposal and 
final submission further allows the academic to establish academic integrity on the per-student 
level.  
Generative AI and Other Technology Considerations 
There are no unique technology considerations included in this exemplar model, beyond the use 
of Turnitin (or similar) for plagiarism detection. Academics may want to consider requiring 
students to use alternative tools (such as Cadmus) that promote intrinsic integrity in written 
assignments, while highlighting the importance of dialogic instructor-student interaction as a 
best practice in establishing academic integrity with each student.  
In this assessment model, chat-based generative AI tools may be useful to help students scope  
and bound a broad, open-ended problem, before subsequently honing in on the specific 
perspective they wish to pursue with their assignments. This opportunity may be particularly 
valuable for students from diverse disciplinary backgrounds (or extents of preparation), wherein 
generative AI tools can provide a more customised revision experience as the student prepares 
to undertake the current unit’s assessment. If the assessment is deployed to target inter- or trans-
disciplinary topics, the use of chat-based AI becomes even more relevant, as the assessment 
may be more accommodating to students from diverse disciplinary backgrounds: such an 
approach may be complemented by flipped-classroom or workshop-type discussions, allowing 
the instructor(s) to socialise with students on the individual or group level, to ensure their AI-
based bounding activities are appropriate for the assignment.  
