Overview of Assessment Model 
Participants will learn and practice skills in the pedagogical methodology of community of 
inquiry (CoI), which combines critical and creative thinking skills with collaborative dialogue. 
Designed to promote transferable, lifelong learning in children by cultivating curiosity, 
collaboration, and self-correction, CoI is now used with all ages in numerous contexts, from 
preschools to retirement homes, hospitals, prisons, science centres and art galleries.  
The CoI methodology can be incorporated into teaching practices by academics and 
employed as a pedagogical and discussion tool in workshops and tutorials. It can also be taught 
to students at any level, in any degree, as a communication tool that is applicable in a range of 
industries, such as, but not limited to education, social/youth work and elderly services. Weekly 
experience of CoI praxis will support participants to develop the knowledge and skills for 
designing and facilitating similar processes. It can be generally applied to all learning outcomes 
in a unit, as it is a methodology of communication, involving a broad range of skills.  
Assessment Design 
Student prompt: Marking rubrics are provided for both parts of the assessment. This enables 
clarification of expectations and cohesion between unit materials, information, and practical 
skill building. Academic integrity is essential and highlighted. Links to relevant library and 
university resources are provided. Students are provided with the rubric and are aware that 
detailed feedback will be provided within two weeks of submission.  
The prompt for the analysis, due week 6:  
This assignment is an individual writing exercise (1,000 words), that offers reflective feedback on 
one of the Community of Inquiries from the first five weeks of the unit. Consideration of the 
following questions may assist your discussion: 
What are your reflections on the effectiveness of the stimulus material? 
Was there progress? Describe and assess.  
Was the CoI useful?  
Write up your analysis of the CoI. Some inclusion of ideas from relevant articles, provided in this 
unit, is needed. Please use the Chicago Referencing Style (link provided in LMS). A reference 
list, not included in the word count, is essential.  
The prompt for the CoI: 
For this task, you will design a stimulus and question for a community of inquiry (CoI) to be run in 
class during one of the workshops in Weeks 10, 11, & 12. The stimulus material must focus on an 
[insert topic here] challenge explored in the unit. 
Upload your CoI question, and stimulus material here by the end of Week 8. Do this by 
providing a link or copy and paste of a referenced article onto a Word Doc. In week 9, you will 
be assigned a workshop to run your CoI in.   
Aims: the aims of the assessment tasks are to:  
1) Demonstrate knowledge of [insert theory or theme here] and its applications in 
popular culture, chosen professions, and/or institutional settings. 
2) Demonstrate an understanding of community of inquiry processes and a capacity to 
apply basic principles of philosophy, including questioning, reasoning, and critical 
thinking. 
