3) Facilitate community of inquiry processes. 
The assessment is an opportunity to learn and apply a methodology, so it could be designed 
to any specific area of interest, like a particular sub-theme in bioethics, such as euthanasia, or 
questions on the newgenics of prenatal testing. Philosophical theories on mind, could be 
applied to questions about artificial intelligence, and the ethics of ChatGPT. Or feminist 
theories could be applied to questions about cultural bias and the position of Indigenous 
Australians in contemporary society. Any big question is up for consideration and can be 
applied as theme for the assessment.   
Tasks: As above, outlined in the Student Prompt. This assessment accounts for 40% of a 
complete unit of study via the two tasks: an analysis (15%), and the designing of a CoI prompt 
and discussion (25%).  
Feedback: Feedback is provided via Turnitin on LMS. The digital rubric is weighted to 
automatically calculate the grade. Detailed individual comments and in-text feedback is 
provided for the analysis and final CoI. The assessment does provide formative feedback, at 
weeks 8, 9, and 14.  
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
Academic integrity is essential to the analysis, as theory is used to analyse the practice. Chicago 
style referencing required. There is no room for cheating as it is based upon the application of a 
methodology learnt through workshop participation. In terms of the final prompt and material, 
there is very little room for the help of generative models. Having to conduct a CoI themselves, 
leaves no room for such tools. These benefits are generalizable to any field of practice, thematic 
focus, as it is practice based.  
Student-Focused Learning Design within the Model    
i. 
Discipline-specific skills: the CoI methodology requires the inculcation of philosophical 
thinking skills. The motto, ‘teaching people how to think, not what to think’, drives the 
approach. Focus is on development of the ‘5 C’s’ – critical, creative, collaborative, 
careful and caring thinking skills. Essential to these is learning how to develop questions, 
give and ask for reasons, and negotiate around different perspectives, opinions, and 
belief systems. The method also contributes to participant tool kits required to navigate 
and disarm disagreement. 
ii. 
Industry/professional competencies: problem solving, negotiation, skills to perform 
different tasks required, acquisition of thinking skills that enhance capacity to respond to 
problems, the unexpected and non-routine events, and skills needed to deal with 
conflict in all aspects of the workplace, the organisation, and colleagues. 
iii. 
Professional maturation: the potential skills development of individuals is high, and as 
stated previously, can be applied to a broad range of topic areas. This integrates the 
growth of the student, as an individual, and in relation to the unit content more broadly. 
Reflection on their own learning through the process of practicing the methodology is 
built into the model. These skills can then be converted into workplace skills. Further, 
critical and creative thinking are highly sought after skills in employment currently. 
Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
The approach can accommodate all people, at any level of education, age, knowledge, etc.  
CoI methods have been completed with kids as young as 5, for example. Similar examples have 
