been completed by UWA staff members alongside young people living in Mission Australia’s 
Drug and Alcohol Youth Services residence, with immigrants from various African nations, with 
elderly people, and am in the process of developing a program with the Constable Care 
Foundation, to work with detainees at Banksia Hill Youth Detention Centre. The success of these 
programs led by UWA staff, here and overseas, are testimony to the effectiveness of the 
method: participants have had various neuro and physical diversities, such as Foetal Alcohol 
Spectrum Disorder, Autism, ADHD, addiction, religious fundamentalism, and so on. Particularly 
where a CoI may be serving diverse learners, academics should liaise with the relevant support 
offices (UniAccess at UWA) to ensure they are able to facilitate the CoI effectively.   
Generative AI and Other Technology Considerations 
The intimate nature of topics raised and discussed within a CoI necessitates a much greater 
degree of care when approaching generative AI tools. The appropriate use of these tools 
should be introduced by the academic, with emphasis on the level of nuance they are capable 
of providing: this discussion can help students understand the point at which they need to turn to 
professional literature to dive deeper within a topic. For each foundational AI model, choices in 
training structure, materials and feedback can significantly affect the sensitivity and nuance 
within a model: this behaviour can vary wildly between closely related topics, such that the 
response provided on one sensitive topic may have little bearing on an output of a closely-
related sensitive topic. While it is not possible to test every incarnation of AI output on a student’s 
behalf, any introduction of generative AI tools should be preceded by a detailed discussion on 
how to avoid, identify, or handle inappropriate outputs (e.g., discussing with instructor(s) and/or 
submitting feedback to the AI provider to support future model revisions). 
With this background, chat-based generative AI tools may be an effective port-of-call for 
students from different disciplinary backgrounds (particularly if they are approaching the CoI in 
a cross- or trans-disciplinary way), to quickly contextualise and identify boundaries within a 
complex, trans-disciplinary problem. For their final submissions, the use of Turnitin and a LMS is a 
best practice, noting the dialogic nature of the model provides the ability for academics to 
proactively establish academic integrity on the per-student level.  
Further Reading 
 Baumfield, V. M., (2016) “CHANGING MINDS: The professional learning of teachers in a 
classroom community of inquiry” in Gregory, M. R., Haynes, J., & Murris, K. (Eds.) The Routledge 
International Handbook of Philosophy for Children, New York: Routledge. pp. 119-126. 
 Burgh, G., & Thornton, S., (2016) “Philosophy goes to school in Australia: A history 1982-2016”. 
Journal of Philosophy in Schools Vol. 3, No. 1, pp. 59–83 
 Cam, P., (2018), “The Generic Argument for teaching philosophy”. Journal of Philosophy in 
Schools, Vol. 5 No. 1, pp. 59-75. 
 Chetty, D., & Suissa, J., (2016) “‘NO GO AREAS’ Racism and discomfort in the community of 
inquiry” in Gregory, M. R., Haynes, J., & Murris, K. (Eds.) The Routledge International Handbook 
of Philosophy for Children, New York: Routledge. pp. 11-18. 
 Costa-Carvalho, M. & Mendonça, D., (2016) “Thinking as a Community: Reasonableness and 
emotions” in Gregory, M. R., Haynes, J., & Murris, K. (Eds.) The Routledge International 
Handbook of Philosophy for Children, New York: Routledge. pp. 127-134. 
 Dewey, J., (1938), ‘Nature & Freedom’ & ‘Experience – the Means & Goals of Education’ in 
Experience & Education, New York: Simon & Schuster. 
