• Tasks are sequenced to ensure students receive and can act on feedback from instructors at 
multiple (five, in this design) instances before submitting their final professional task. 
 
Field Observations and Measurements (20% of assessment mark): Students should 
document all observations and measurements in a field notebook, noting any initial 
interpretations of the observed geological features. 
Draft Map Creation (15% of assessment mark): Every night during the field trip, students 
should update their draft map with the day's observations and interpretations. 
Final Map Creation (20% of assessment mark): After returning from the field trip, students 
should refine their draft map into a final map, ensuring it accurately reflects their field 
observations and interpretations. 
Cross-Section Construction (15% of assessment mark): Using their final map as a 
reference, students should construct a cross-section that represents a vertical slice of 
the area's geological structure. 
Key Formulation (10% of assessment mark): Students should create a key that 
accurately represents the symbols and colours used in their final map and cross-
section.  
Geological History Writing (20% of assessment mark): Students should write a 
comprehensive geological history that explains the geological processes and events 
that have shaped the area, drawing upon their field observations, final map, cross-
section, and key. 
• Feedback: The feedback process in this model is designed to be constructive, comprehensive, 
and tailored to the individual needs of the students, focusing on both their specialised skills and 
general competencies. The feedback process begins with the review of the students' field 
observations and measurements. The instructor provides students with insights on their data 
collection techniques, the accuracy and detail of their observations, and the quality of their 
initial interpretations. This feedback aims to bolster students' field observation skills and their 
ability to make accurate and meaningful interpretations of geological features. When students 
submit their draft and final maps, cross-section, key, and geological history, the instructor 
provides feedback on these elements individually and collectively. The instructor evaluates the 
accuracy of the maps and cross-section, the clarity of the key, and (in the final submission) the 
depth and coherence of the geological history. The feedback emphasises the importance of 
accurate data representation and effective communication of geological concepts and 
findings.  
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
The model requires students to conduct hands-on fieldwork, gather their own data, and develop 
their own interpretations that are relevant to professional disciplinary practice. This process 
minimises the opportunity for academic misconduct as it relies heavily on individual effort and 
personal engagement with the task. The assignment's structure ensures that each student's work 
is unique, reflecting their individual observations and interpretations. This inherently promotes 
academic integrity as it discourages plagiarism and encourages original thought. This 
personalised feedback system discourages academic misconduct, as students actively discuss 
and reflect on their performance with instructors throughout the assessment journey.  
