Varying Assessment Elements to Maintain Academic Integrity 
The nature of the model inherently fosters academic integrity due to its focus on first-hand 
observations, field data collection, and individual interpretations: as this is completed with 
academics and students working side-by-side, the model provides the ability for academics to 
establish academic integrity on the per-student level, in preference to detecting ‘violations’ 
when final assessment items are submitted. At the highest level, the location of the field trip 
could change from year to year, where less time-intensive variations (for instructors) may involve 
selecting a site that can be used three or more times to target different technical perspectives.  
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
The model recognises that learning is most effective when students are actively involved in the 
process and can connect their learning to real-world contexts. The assignment allows students to 
bring their personal interests and career aspirations into their work, enhancing motivation and 
engagement. The field trip aspect of the assignment allows students to experience geology in its 
natural context, fostering a deeper understanding of the subject. The hands-on nature of the 
tasks enables students to engage with the material in a meaningful way. The autonomy for 
students are provided (i.e., to engage with the built/natural environment) to shape their learning 
experience provides an outstanding opportunity to integrate each student’s personal and 
emerging professional interests. This can be accentuated by the field trip experience, where 
students can discuss their observations and interpretations with their peers, fostering 
collaborative learning: such a peer interaction can enrich their understanding of the subject 
and enhance their communication and teamwork skills.  
Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
Given the nature of field trips, there are inherent challenges in accommodating all students, 
particularly those with physical disabilities. To address this issue, the originating discipline has 
been adapting field activities to accommodate students with a variety of physical or medical 
needs. In cases where students are unable to participate in the field trip, alternative assignments 
have been arranged, such as working on activities through online or university laboratory 
environments. In the coming years, this may extend to virtual field trips as a potential solution, 
allowing students who cannot physically attend the field trip to participate in the assignment. 
The discipline is committed to ensuring that all students, regardless of their physical abilities or 
health conditions, have an equal opportunity to learn and succeed in their studies.  
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
For the successful implementation of the Model, several best practices are recommended: 
1. Thorough planning: A successful field trip requires careful planning. The location should be 
chosen based on its geological significance and accessibility. It's also important to prepare a 
detailed itinerary, outlining the activities for each day of the trip. 
2. Clear communication: The expectations for the assignment should be clearly communicated 
to students beforehand. This includes details about the tasks to be completed on site 
(particularly to ensure each student meets the minimum threshold for data collection and/or 
observational notes), the grading criteria, and the submission process. 
3. Support during the field trip: Instructors should be available to provide guidance and support 
during the field trip. This can help ensure that students are making accurate observations and 
interpretations. 
