4. Constructive feedback: Feedback on the assignment should be timely and constructive, 
providing students with specific areas for improvement and attempting, as much as possible, 
to ensure students have early feedback in the assessment process as they build toward their 
final submission. 
Generative AI and Other Technology Considerations 
While the Model is primarily hands-on and does not rely heavily on technology, there are a few 
technology considerations that could enhance the learning experience: 
 Digital tools for field data collection: Students could use digital tools or apps to help with data 
collection in the field, such as digital compasses or GPS applications that may be built-in to 
their phones. 
 Virtual field trips: Virtual field trips could be used to supplement or replace physical field trips for 
students who cannot participate due to physical limitations. These could include 3D models or 
panoramic photos of geological sites. 
 Digital submission and feedback: While the current model requires handwritten submissions, 
digital submissions could be considered to streamline the submission and feedback process. 
Chat-based generative AI tools may be valuable as students first receive the description of the 
field trip location, in providing rapid access to background discussion around the environment. 
Such tools may also allow students to better define (and determine how best to integrate) their 
personal/professional interests within the context of the field trip: for example, AI tools may help 
identify appropriate disciplinary interfaces, if students wish to pursue an extension concept. If the 
model is adapted to target other dimensions of the environment – including people and culture 
– with more open-ended problems, the deployment of chat-based tools in a flipped classroom 
environment enables targeted discussion with each student (or small groups) to help quickly 
establish the bounds of each student’s area of focus within the topic.   
