Overview of Assessment Model 
This assessment model serves as a comprehensive, task-based exercise that integrates 
theoretical knowledge, practical skills, and critical thinking. It is an all-encompassing learning 
model that emphasises problem-solving abilities, thereby promoting deep learning. This is 
achieved by applying theoretical principles to real-world scenarios. The model requires students 
to use specific tools to design and optimise a process, conduct a basic economic analysis of the 
chosen process, and apply their knowledge to solve complex problems. The multi-faceted 
nature of a technical (real-world) design task ensures that students not only acquire knowledge 
but also develop a variety of skills, including critical thinking, problem-solving, and practical 
application of theoretical principles. In essence, this model serves as a holistic, immersive 
learning experience that is designed to promote a comprehensive understanding of the subject 
and emulate design work in industry.  
Assessment Design 
• Student prompt: In the assignment, students will be provided with a multi-faceted task that 
necessitates the application of knowledge and tools learned in their respective disciplines. The 
objective will be to simulate a specific process and subsequently conduct an economic 
analysis of the resulting process design (i.e., reflecting that which the students are expected to 
tackle in their professions). They will be given limited parameters and conditions necessary to 
satisfy the task, and they will be expected to make informed (referenced) assumptions for any 
missing parameters and ultimately integrate their theoretical knowledge to design, optimise, 
and critically analyse the outcome of their technical design. The project will be designed to be 
challenging yet achievable, prompting students to engage with the material on a deeper 
level. The project will also encourage and prompt creativity and innovative thinking, as 
students will be tasked with finding the most effective and efficient process design.  
• Aims: The aims of the assessment model are three-fold. First, to provide students with an 
opportunity to apply theoretical technical knowledge in a practical context. Second, to 
develop their problem-solving skills by presenting them with complex, real-world challenges. 
Third, to enhance their understanding of the broader economic implications of their work, 
thereby preparing them for the multi-faceted challenges they will face in their professional 
careers. Students integrate and enhance their understanding of discipline subject matter by 
bridging the gap between abstract theoretical principles and tangible, real-world tasks. They 
develop advanced problem-solving skills by applying theoretical technical knowledge to 
achieve ‘optimal’ solutions to real-world scenarios. Additionally, the model enhances the 
students' understanding of the broader context in which technical design is undertaken by 
requiring students to consider technical viability, safety, and economic feasibility. The 
integration of an economic analysis into the task further enhances the multidimensionality of 
the learning experience, giving students a more comprehensive perspective on the challenges 
and considerations in process design.  
• Tasks: The component tasks embedded in this assessment model are designed to be 
comprehensive and multidimensional, providing students with a complete learning 
experience. Students will first be required to use a simulation tool to design and optimise a 
process based on the given parameters and conditions. This task will test their understanding of 
the theoretical principles they have learned and their ability to apply these principles in a 
practical context. It will also develop their skills in using the simulation tool, which is a valuable 
skill in many disciplines. Once the design is completed, students will then be required to 
conduct a basic economic analysis of their chosen process design (as relevant and 
appropriate to the discipline). This final component task will require them to consider various 
