economic factors that might impact the feasibility and profitability of the design, and enhance 
their understanding of the broader economic implications of their work and develop their skills 
in economic analysis.  
• Feedback: Feedback in this assessment model is carefully structured and designed to be 
constructive, providing students with clear guidance on how they can improve. It addresses 
both the technical aspects of the process design and the economic analysis, providing 
students with a comprehensive review of their performance. The feedback through in-semester 
consultation allows students to apply the suggestions and improvements in their subsequent 
tasks, while providing a vehicle for instructor(s) to establish academic integrity on a per-student 
level.  
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
The complexity and uniqueness of the task can reduce the possibility of plagiarism or copying 
from other sources, particularly where the task has not been studied or built previously. By 
requiring students to explain their work and reasoning, the model enhances the integrity of the 
task: this is underscored by in-semester preliminary consultation between instructor(s) and 
students. Each student's work is expected to be unique, reflecting their individual understanding 
and application of the theoretical principles. The requirement for an economic analysis (as 
appropriate) adds another layer of academic integrity assurance, as such recommendations 
are subjective (to a degree) and requires critical thinking. 
Varying Assessment Elements to Maintain Academic Integrity 
To ensure academic integrity, the assessment elements should be varied for different iterations 
of the task. For example, the process design parameters, the nature of the process to be 
simulated, or the economic factors to be considered can be changed for each student or 
group, and from year to year. This not only helps with academic integrity but also enhances the 
learning experience by exposing students to a variety of scenarios and challenges. Varying the 
assessment elements also prevents students from simply copying answers from previous iterations 
of the task. Each iteration presents a unique challenge, requiring students to think critically and 
apply their knowledge in new ways. This ensures that each student's work reflects their individual 
understanding and application of the theoretical principles. Moreover, the model also 
incorporates measures to detect similarities in student responses, further ensuring academic 
integrity.  
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
By requiring students to apply theoretical principles to practical tasks, the model encourages 
active engagement with the subject matter. The problem-solving nature of the tasks fosters 
critical thinking skills, while the economic analysis component enhances understanding of real-
world implications. The model also emphasises self-directed learning, encouraging students to 
take ownership of their learning. By presenting students with complex, real-world problems to 
solve, the model motivates students to engage deeply with the material and seek out additional 
resources as needed. This promotes a sense of autonomy and self-efficacy, essential skills for 
lifelong learning. Moreover, feedback/consultation during the semester provides the opportunity 
for students to emphasise their personal interests and career aspirations, allowing the academic 
to help identify how students can integrate these within the design task itself. In doing so, the 
