Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
As the design task is open-ended, there is no single ""correct"" answer, and students are 
encouraged to think creatively and develop their unique solutions. It is important to ensure that 
both interim and final submissions are as flexible as possible, both in timeline and expectations: 
allowing students to present their design task in alternative formats is an important consideration 
(e.g., as a written report or an oral/recorded presentation). 
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
Instructors should provide clear instructions and parameters for the task, ensuring that students 
understand what is expected of them. Instructors should also provide sufficient training on the 
simulation tool to be used (including background or refresher modules, as necessary), giving 
students the necessary skills to complete the task. Regular feedback and support during the task 
are also crucial for successful implementation. Instructors should provide timely and constructive 
feedback, helping students understand their mistakes and guiding them towards better 
strategies and approaches. Instructors should also be available to answer any questions or 
concerns students may have, providing support and guidance throughout the task.  
Generative AI and Other Technology Considerations 
Where required by the discipline, the use of unique computational tools and online resources 
should be considered within the reference frame of location (i.e., students able to access 
campus computing resources independent of whether they attend campus or not). The model 
may necessarily assume previous scaffolding knowledge with such resources, where the 
deployment of asynchronous training or refresher modules (e.g., basic methods with a 
computational tool) is important to ensuring each student is able to tackle the design task.  
Chat-based generative AI may be useful in helping students frame the initial scope of the open-
ended design task, noting the accuracy and appropriateness of current-generation tools 
requires ongoing assessment by the instructor(s). Where students may be considering inter- or 
trans-disciplinary design tasks, chat-based AI may allow them to quickly identify scope 
boundaries and the relevance of their previous education (and workplace, where relevant) 
experience.  
