Overview of Assessment Model 
This assessment model considers a comprehensive, problem-oriented project assignment that 
requires the application of scientific principles, technical judgment, and relevant literature to 
address a complex, real-world environmental problem (with the motivating assessment focussed 
on geotechnics). This model is designed to mimic the conditions and challenges professionals 
face in the field, encouraging students to employ critical thinking, technical skills, and sound 
judgment. This model is based on the principle of 'authentic assessment', whereby students are 
assessed on their ability to apply the skills and knowledge they have acquired to realistic 
scenarios, rather than being tested purely on theoretical knowledge. The assignment is designed 
to enable the development and assessment of a range of skills, including problem-solving, data 
interpretation, report writing, and decision-making.  
Assessment Design 
The assignment is designed to enable the development and assessment of a range of skills, 
including problem-solving, data interpretation, report writing, and decision-making.  
• Student prompt: The prompt for the assignment is a detailed scenario that describes a specific 
environmental issue - in this case, a potential contaminant leak in a manufacturing plant. The 
scenario provides a context for the problem, relevant data, and clear objectives. Students are 
tasked with analysing the provided data, making justified assumptions, and using technical 
(e.g., geotechnical engineering) principles to predict the contaminant transport, estimate the 
extent of contamination, and suggest possible remediation methods. Ideally, it encourages 
students to think critically about the problem, requiring them to navigate uncertainties, 
interpret incomplete data, and make decisions based on sound reasoning.  
• Aims: The primary aim of this assignment is to assess students' ability to apply their knowledge 
and skills to a practical disciplinary problem (in the motivating example, environmental 
geotechnics). It targets their understanding of underlying theory and foundational processes 
(e.g., contaminant transport mechanisms), ability to interpret and analyse data, and capacity 
to make reasoned assumptions and judgments. As students must communicate their findings 
through a professional vehicle (e.g., a technical report), the task requires them to clearly and 
succinctly present specialised information and justify any recommendations or conclusions (as 
appropriate). The assignment also aims to develop students' awareness of the real-world 
implications of their work, particularly when they are specifically asked to consider the 
potential human, societal, or environmental consequences of their decisions. 
• Tasks: Students work progressively through the semester on four components, listed in the 
recommended chronological order (for the example problem conveyed in the student 
prompt above). Through revolving consultation with instructor(s), students are able to explore 
and challenge their thinking as their composite understanding of the data, theory and 
relevant interpretation (or prediction) methods evolves. They ultimately will submit a single final 
report (or similar output appropriate to the discipline) containing these four key assessment 
requirements/sections, noting the student and academic have discussed the data 
interpretation, assumption justification, and methodology explanation at least twice prior to 
submission for marking.  
• Data Interpretation (20%): In the first task, students review and perform basic 
interpretation (appropriate to the discipline) on either a provided dataset or one that 
has been collected through a standardised method (i.e., in a “teaching laboratory” 
environment). Within the interpretation task, students should demonstrate their 
understanding of the data and how they intend to use it in their analysis, submitting their 
