results as either a written report or presentation (varying with expectation of the 
disciplinary practitioner).  
• Assumption Justification (20%): Students must clearly outline and justify the assumptions 
they have made relevant to the data interpretation: in the motivating example, this may 
include selecting dimensions, properties, or inputs for the contaminant transport analysis. 
Critically, a detailed exploration of assumptions necessitates a deep understanding of 
relevant theory and its connection to the target application/plant (e.g., to discuss the 
assumption of “steady” fluid flow, the student must be prepared to discuss both fluid 
mechanics and thermodynamics).  
• Methodology Explanation (30%): Students should provide a clear description of the 
methodology they have used, including the type of solution they have adopted, and its 
limitations. 
• Executive Summary and Recommendations (30%): Students are required to summarise 
their findings, respond to the key prompting questions, and provide their 
recommendations in a concise executive summary (as appropriate for the discipline). 
• Feedback: Feedback on the assignment should focus on both technical and general skills. On 
the technical side, feedback should address students' understanding and application of 
environmental geotechnics principles, their data interpretation skills, their choice of 
methodology, and their justification of assumptions. Feedback should also address their ability 
to communicate effectively through their report, including their clarity, coherence, and use of 
appropriate terminology.  
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
This model promotes academic integrity due to its authentic, problem-based nature, and 
critically through the periodic consultation with each student as their tasks evolve. The complex, 
real-world scenarios provide an opportunity for each student to integrate personal or evolving 
professional interests. The incorporation an oral presentation/defence of their final submission to 
assess targeted understanding and encourage reflection may further promote integrity, 
particularly where students’ interests are integrated in their final submission. The consultative and 
dialogic nature of the assessment provides the strongest vehicle through which to establish 
integrity on a per-student basis. 
Varying Assessment Elements to Maintain Academic Integrity 
To maintain academic integrity and prevent students from copying solutions from previous years, 
the scenario used in the assignment should be changed each year. This could involve changing 
the contaminant, the environment, the data provided, or the client's objectives (in the remit of 
the motivating assessment). The fundamental structure and requirements of the assignment can 
remain the same, minimising the effort required for the academic. Further, the academic may 
wish to change the specific tasks or the weightings of different components, to ensure students 
don’t rely too heavily on past solutions, but rather use them inspiration in the development of 
their own, unique solutions. 
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
This model allows students to bring their personal interests and career aspirations into the 
assessment. The complex, open-ended nature of the problem allows students to approach the 
task from different angles and to focus on aspects that interest them. For example, a student 
