interested in environmental policy might choose to focus more on the legal and policy 
implications of the contaminant leak, while a student interested in remediation technologies 
might choose to focus on designing an effective remediation strategy.  
Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
The flexible nature of the assignment, in both how students choose to organise their research 
and their choice over submission strategy, encourages them to approach the tasks in a way 
that bests suits their learning style and needs. Incorporating flexibility into the review and 
consultation timeline may also be critical for students who are new to open-ended problem 
solving (e.g., students might determine their own preferred work plan and request consultations 
on their preferred weeks as they achieve milestone drafts).  
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
Successful implementation of this model requires clear communication of expectations, 
provision of adequate resources and support, and periodic, constructive feedback. The 
assignment prompt should clearly articulate a simple, open-ended problem to be addressed, 
and should avoid over-prescribing constraints on submission (i.e., beyond a page limit and 
presentation/style expectations). Regular check-ins outside of formal consultation sessions can 
help to identify and address issues early, and constructive feedback can help students to 
improve their performance. 
Generative AI and Other Technology Considerations 
The assignment may require the use of specific software or technology, such as for contaminant 
transport modelling in the motivating assessment. Students should be provided with access to 
this software and given adequate training and support to use it effectively. Where students are 
expected to access university computing resources, the academic should interrogate whether 
these can be equitably accessed remotely (online) as well as in-person, to ensure students are 
not disadvantaged by location.  
The open-ended nature of the project, underscored by its ability to accommodate students with 
diverse personal interests and career aspirations, can be supported through the use of chat-
based generative AI tools for initial problem scoping. As students are initially presented with the 
project scope, these tools can help them identify the boundaries between their existing 
knowledge/experience and the problem statement itself, and to identify the most appropriate 
use and deployment of official class resources (textbooks and/or online resources) toward the 
problem.  
