Overview of Assessment Model 
A Multi-Station Practical Assessment (MSPA) is an innovative and rigorous approach designed to 
gauge students' practical skills and theoretical knowledge in various disciplines, especially in 
areas where practical application is paramount. The MSPA offers a well-rounded evaluation of a 
student's ability to apply theoretical knowledge to practical tasks, to think critically and 
analytically, and to demonstrate competency in a controlled, standardised, simulated 
environment. The MSPA is comprised of a set number of stations (six, in this example), each 
evaluating a specific skill or area of knowledge. Students move from one station to the next, 
demonstrating their competency at each one. Each station presents a unique scenario or task 
that students must complete, and they are evaluated based on a detailed rubric. To pass the 
assessment, students must successfully complete a set number of the stations (determined by 
the School), e.g., at least five of the six stations. 
The MSPA has been modelled off a tried and tested assessment tool that is used in many health 
programs of study such as Medicine and is known as the Objective Structured Clinical 
Examination (OSCE). The OSCE is used regularly as an assessment tool to measure students’ 
ability to display their competence in clinical skills in a timed and structured manner. Whilst the 
core clinical skills are measured, additional soft skills such as communication and empathy may 
also be included in such an exam.  
Assessment Design 
The MSPA promotes a holistic learning experience, encouraging students to engage with the 
subject matter in a practical, hands-on manner. The assessment's structure enables students to 
receive immediate feedback, promoting continuous improvement and refining of skills. By 
simulating real-world scenarios, the MSPA prepares students for their future professional roles, 
making it a highly effective tool for practical disciplines. This assessment model is also adaptable 
to various learning environments, from face-to-face to online learning. With the right technology 
and resources, the MSPA can be implemented in any setting, making it a versatile tool for 
different learning contexts.  
• Student prompt: Students will participate in a Multi-Station Practical Assessment (MSPA), 
designed to evaluate their practical skills and theoretical knowledge in the given discipline. 
This assessment comprises six stations, each presenting a unique task or scenario. Students are 
expected to apply their knowledge and skills to these tasks, demonstrating their ability to solve 
problems, think critically, and work under controlled conditions. Students will be given a 
specified time at each station, after which they will move to the next one. For each task, 
students are required to follow the provided instructions, adhere to the set criteria, and 
complete the task within the allocated time. The tasks will test a variety of skills and knowledge 
areas pertinent to the unit content. To pass the assessment, students must successfully 
complete at least five of the six stations (in this example), as evaluated by the designated 
rubric. This rubric will be provided to students in advance, allowing them to understand the 
expectations and criteria for each station. 
• Aims: The MSPA aims to evaluate students' ability to apply theoretical knowledge to practical 
tasks, think critically and analytically, and demonstrate competency in a controlled, simulated 
environment. It aims to promote a well-rounded learning experience, encouraging students to 
engage with the subject matter practically and hands-on. The MSPA also seeks to provide an 
authentic assessment of students' skills and knowledge, simulating real-world scenarios and 
problems that students might encounter in their professional roles. The assessment is designed 
to prepare students for the professional world by testing their ability to perform under pressure, 
adhere to standards, and solve problems efficiently and effectively. The MSPA also aims to 
