foster continuous improvement and learning. Through immediate feedback, students are 
encouraged to reflect on their performance, identify areas for improvement, and refine their 
skills. This process promotes a growth mindset, fostering a culture of continuous learning and 
improvement.  
• Tasks: The MSPA comprises six stations, each presenting a unique task or scenario. The tasks are 
designed to evaluate various skills and knowledge areas relevant to the unit content. Here are 
some example (generalised) station focusses: 
• Station 1: Practical Application (20% weight) Students are required to demonstrate a 
specific skill or process, adhering to established protocols and standards. For example, in 
a medical context, this could involve demonstrating a specific medical procedure. 
• Station 2: Theoretical Application (20% weighting) Students are presented with a 
theoretical problem or scenario and are required to apply their knowledge to solve it. 
This could involve discussing the pathophysiology of a disease or explaining a specific 
biochemical reaction. 
• Station 3: Problem Solving (20% weighting) Students are given a complex problem or 
scenario and are required to identify the problem, propose a solution, and justify their 
approach. 
• Station 4: Data Analysis (20% weighting) Students are given a dataset and are required 
to analyse the data, draw conclusions, and present their findings. 
• Station 5: Communication (10% weighting) Students are evaluated on their ability to 
communicate complex information effectively and concisely, either in writing or 
verbally. 
• Station 6: Ethics and Professionalism (10% weighting) Students are presented with an 
ethical dilemma or a professional scenario and are required to discuss their approach, 
demonstrating their understanding of ethical principles and professional standards. 
• Feedback: Feedback in the MSPA is immediate, specific, and constructive. It targets both the 
specialised and general skills that students should develop. Feedback is given at each station, 
allowing students to understand their performance, identify areas for improvement, and apply 
this feedback in subsequent tasks. Feedback focuses on the task performance, the process 
and strategies used, and the student's understanding and application of knowledge. It is 
designed to promote a growth mindset, fostering a culture of continuous learning and 
improvement. By receiving immediate feedback, students can continuously improve their skills 
and refine their understanding, enhancing their learning experience. Feedback is an integral 
part of the MSPA, promoting self-reflection and continuous improvement.  
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
The practical nature of the tasks makes it difficult for students to plagiarise or cheat, as they must 
demonstrate their skills and knowledge in real time. The varied tasks and scenarios also reduce 
the likelihood of academic misconduct. Each station presents a unique challenge, reducing the 
chances of students sharing answers or colluding. Furthermore, the tasks are designed in a way 
