ensuring academic integrity in various learning environments, although it is noted that the 
motivating assessments at UWA derive from in-person experiences. 
Varying Assessment Elements to Maintain Academic Integrity 
To maintain academic integrity, it is crucial to vary the assessment elements each year. This 
could involve changing the tasks or scenarios at each station, modifying the rubric, or adjusting 
the weightage of each task (noting the alignment of these rubrics or weightings may carry 
further considerations for professional accreditation, thus being less flexible than changing 
station scenarios). The effort required is moderate, involving the development of new tasks or 
scenarios, the revision of the rubric, and the adjustment of station weights. While these changes 
require effort, they are crucial for maintaining academic integrity. However, this effort can be 
minimised by creating a bank of tasks and scenarios that can be rotated each year (i.e., 
deploying a large time-commitment up front for a five-year cycle). 
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
The model promotes a deep understanding of the unit content, fostering meaningful learning 
experiences. The MSPA also allows students to bring their personal interests and career 
aspirations into the assessment. The tasks and scenarios can be designed to reflect real-world 
situations that students might encounter in their chosen careers, making the assessment relevant 
and engaging. Furthermore, the MSPA promotes self-regulated learning. Students are given the 
autonomy to navigate the tasks and scenarios, fostering critical thinking and problem-solving 
skills. This autonomy can be tailored to students' individual learning needs and preferences, 
promoting personalised learning experiences. The MSPA also fosters a growth mindset, 
promoting continuous learning and improvement. Through immediate feedback, students are 
encouraged to reflect on their performance, identify areas for improvement, and refine their 
skills. This promotes a culture of continuous learning, fostering a growth mindset.   
Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
The model is adaptable to various learning environments and resources, but the rigidity of the 
deployment must be considered alongside the learning needs of each student (i.e., the use of 
timed assessment stations may present challenges for some students and should be considered 
within UWA’s UniAccess Adjustment Plan for each student). At a general level, the MSPA delivers 
a highly consistent environment for assessment, where the use of a detailed and professionally-
informed rubric ensures that all students are evaluated on the same criteria. The MSPA can also 
promote a deeper appreciation of cultural diversity, if tasks and scenarios are designed to 
reflect diverse cultural contexts.  
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
For successful implementation of the MSPA, it is important to provide clear instructions and 
expectations to students. This includes providing the rubric in advance, explaining the structure 
of the MSPA, and providing examples of tasks or scenarios. It is also important to provide timely 
and constructive feedback. Feedback should be specific, targeting both the task performance 
and the process or strategies used. This promotes continuous improvement and learning. It is 
crucial to ensure that the MSPA is fair and consistent. This involves using a rigorous rubric for 
grading, ensuring that all students are evaluated on the same criteria. 
