Overview of Assessment Model 
The proposed assessment model is an experiential learning structure designed to equip students 
with the knowledge and skills needed to solve real-world problems within their respective fields 
of study. This model is rooted in task analysis — a method that decomposes a task into 
component sub-tasks to achieve one or more goals. The model pivots around an extended 
assignment, together accounting for a significant portion of the final unit mark. The assignment is 
designed to mimic the kind of task students may encounter in their future careers, thereby 
bridging the gap between academic learning and practical application. This model is not only 
effective in fostering critical thinking, problem-solving, and analytical skills, but also promotes 
academic integrity as students receive feedback – and ideally discuss the feedback with – 
instructor(s) across the semester. 
Assessment Design 
The assessment model is flexible and adaptable, allowing it to be implemented across different 
disciplines. The specifics of the assignment can be tailored to fit the learning outcomes and 
content of a particular unit, and the model can accommodate both in-person and online 
learning environments.   
• Student prompt: For the assignment, students are presented with a problem or scenario 
relevant to their field of study. They are tasked with conducting a comprehensive task analysis 
to identify, assess, and resolve the problem at hand. This involves decomposing the problem 
into its component parts, examining each part in detail, and developing a strategic plan to 
address the problem. This prompt is designed to be open-ended, allowing students the 
flexibility to approach the problem in a way that aligns with their personal interests and career 
aspirations. The problem or scenario can be either hypothetical or derived from real-world 
contexts and should be complex enough to require a detailed analysis.  
• Aims: The primary aim of this assignment is to cultivate students' problem-solving and analytical 
skills. By conducting a task analysis, students will learn to break down complex problems into 
manageable parts, examine each part in detail, and devise strategic plans to address these 
problems. The assignment also aims to foster critical thinking skills. Students will need to critically 
evaluate the problem, considering various perspectives and potential solutions. This process will 
encourage them to question assumptions, identify biases, and develop well-reasoned 
arguments. The assignment further aims to bridge the gap between theory and practice. By 
applying the principles and concepts they've learned in class to a real-world problem, students 
will gain a deeper understanding of these concepts and see how they can be applied in 
practical contexts.  
• Tasks: The assignment is broken down into several sequential tasks, each with a specific goal 
and submission requirement. The tasks are designed to guide students through the process of 
conducting a task analysis, from initial problem identification to final solution proposal. 
• Task 1: Problem Identification (Weight: 20%) Students are required to identify and 
describe the problem they will be addressing in their task analysis. This should include a 
detailed description of the problem and its context, as well as an explanation of why the 
problem is important to address (i.e., within the context of the discipline). 
• Task 2: Component Analysis (Weight: 30%) Students are asked to decompose the 
problem into its component parts. This should involve a detailed examination of each 
part and an explanation of how it contributes to the overall problem. 
