• Task 3: Solution Proposal (Weight: 30%) Students are expected to propose a solution to 
the problem. This should involve a strategic plan detailing how each component of the 
problem will be addressed, as well as a justification for the proposed solution based on 
evidence and reasoning. 
• Task 4: Reflection (Weight: 20%) As a critical final step, students are asked to reflect on 
the task analysis process, including the feedback that they received on their proposed 
solution (Task 3). This should include a discussion of what they learned, any difficulties 
they encountered, and how they might approach similar problems in the future (e.g., 
providing some “best practices” for future students). 
• Feedback: After each task submission, instructors provide detailed feedback to help students 
understand what they did well and where they can improve. This feedback focuses not only 
on the content of the students' work, but also on the development of important skills. 
Particularly through the reflection process, students may consider mapping their preferred 
strategy (for future reference) in approaching complex problems that must be discretised into 
component parts for critical examination and solution development. 
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
The student-directed nature of the solution approach, particularly where periodic feedback is 
provided with consultation, makes it more difficult for students to plagiarise or engage in 
academic misconduct. The requirement for students to submit sequential tasks, each building 
upon the previous, makes it possible for instructors to monitor progress and detect any sudden 
shifts in the quality or style of work, and to ensure students can fully explain and defend 
methodological decisions prior to the final task submission. The use of a reflection task at the end 
of the assignment, particularly with a moderate weighting in the overall assessment, provides 
further incentive for students to act with integrity. 
Varying Assessment Elements to Maintain Academic Integrity 
To maintain academic integrity, it is recommended that the specific problem or scenario 
presented in the student prompt be changed each year. This ensures that students cannot reuse 
previous students' work and are encouraged to think critically and independently about the 
problem at hand. Changing the problem or scenario annually will require a moderate amount 
of effort from the instructor, as they will need to ensure that the new problem or scenario is 
relevant to the unit content, sufficiently complex to require a detailed task analysis, and 
meaningful to the students.  
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
The open-ended nature of the assignment prompt gives students the flexibility to approach the 
problem in a way that aligns with their personal interests and career aspirations. Moreover, by 
conducting a task analysis on a problem relevant to their field of study, students are able to see 
the practical applications of what they are learning in class. This helps them to understand the 
relevance and value of their education, which can increase their motivation and engagement. 
Additionally, the model's focus on experiential learning—learning by doing—aligns with best 
practice where students learn best when they are actively involved. By applying the principles 
and concepts they've learned in class to a real-world problem, students are able to deepen 
their understanding and improve their retention of these concepts.   
