Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
For example, the open-ended nature of the assignment prompt means that students can 
approach the problem in a way that aligns with their strengths and interests. Moreover, the 
model can be implemented in both in-person and online learning environments, making it 
accessible to students regardless of their geographical location or personal circumstances. For 
students who may have difficulties with certain aspects of the assignment, accommodations 
can be made to ensure that they are not disadvantaged (i.e., through their UniAccess 
Adjustment Plans at UWA). By increasing the amount of flexibility in the prompting task – 
including allowing students to choose from pre-determined prompts or to propose their own – 
can lead to a variety of solutions, each reflecting the diverse backgrounds and experiences of 
the students.  
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
To successfully implement this model, it is essential for instructors to provide clear and detailed 
instructions for each task, alongside criterion references (see below) to ensure students are fully 
aware of how submissions will be marked. Instructors should also provide timely and constructive 
feedback to guide students' learning and progress to ensure students continue progressing their 
work. Regular check-ins (outside of planned submissions/consultations) can help ensure that 
students understand the assignment and are making progress, while also serving to establish 
academic integrity on the level of every student. These check-ins can be done through 
individual consultations, group discussions, or online forums, depending on the learning 
environment. 
Generative AI and Other Technology Considerations 
Where the model deploys an open-ended task or problem for consideration, the use of 
generative AI tools can be helpful in supporting students with different educational or 
experiential backgrounds. In particular, the use of chat-based AI tools to help students quickly 
identify and map out the scope of the problem, its relevance to their chosen discipline (if 
relevant), and potential avenues of enquiry on a per-student level. Where such an approach 
can be combined with consultation – either via one-on-one meetings or in a flipped classroom, 
where instructor(s) can socialise with each student/group – the partial employment of 
generative AI can help students understand its present-day limits (relative to the discipline) and 
understanding as to where official classroom resources are best-placed for deeper enquiry.  
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
