format and length of the responses, and the criteria for grading. In the motivating assessment, 
the tasks are divided into two parts: 
• ""Diagnosis and Formulation"" (40% weight) – Students are asked to diagnose the 
hypothetical client using two different diagnostic frameworks and present a case 
formulation as might be presented in an assessment report. This task requires students to 
use their knowledge of the diagnostic frameworks and their ability to interpret and 
synthesise information from the case. 
• ""Intervention and Reflection"" (60% weight) – Based on their diagnosis and formulation, 
students are asked to propose three intervention goals and reflect on how these goals 
can bring about relevant and meaningful change for the client. This task requires 
students to apply their knowledge of intervention strategies and their ability to anticipate 
and reflect on potential outcomes. 
• Feedback: Content feedback should evaluate the correctness and appropriateness of the 
students' responses, their understanding of the subject matter, and their ability to apply this 
knowledge to the case. Process feedback should assess the students' analytical and problem-
solving skills, their ability to interpret and synthesise information from the case, and their 
communication and reflection skills. Feedback should be constructive, highlighting the 
strengths of the students' responses, identifying areas for improvement, and offering 
suggestions for how to improve. It should also encourage students to reflect on their learning 
and their decision-making process, fostering a deeper understanding of the subject matter 
and enhancing their analytical and problem-solving skills.  
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
The unique and complex nature of the case scenarios makes it challenging for students to find 
ready-made solutions, discouraging plagiarism and encouraging original thought. The 
requirement to justify their decisions using the case information and their theoretical knowledge 
further enhances academic integrity. The approach ensures that students have a deep 
understanding of the subject matter and can apply this knowledge in a meaningful way. As one 
extension concept, consultations (either in a flipped classroom environment, or directly with 
each student/group) after the first task submission may provide the opportunity to better 
understand each student’s personal interests and career aspirations, and provide an 
opportunity to help identify ways to embed this within their approach to the second submission. 
While this extension approach does require a significant, mid-semester time investment, the 
approach exemplifies a strategy of establishing academic integrity on a per-student level, as 
opposed to relying on the detection of violations after submissions are received.  
Varying Assessment Elements to Maintain Academic Integrity 
To maintain academic integrity, it is essential to regularly update the case scenarios and tasks. 
This prevents students from using previous students' work and ensures that the assignment 
continues to be a meaningful learning experience. Creating a new case scenario requires a 
moderate effort. The scenario should be complex and detailed enough to allow for multiple 
interpretations and solutions, and relevant to the unit material. The tasks can be varied by 
changing the focus or the expected outcomes. For example, one year the task could focus on 
diagnosing and formulating a case, the next year it could focus on proposing and evaluating 
interventions (as appropriate within the given discipline). By emphasising different aspects of the 
students' responses, such as their analytical skills, their ability to apply theoretical knowledge, or 
