their reflection skills, the grading criteria can encourage students to approach the case in 
different ways. 
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
The model provides students with the opportunity to engage deeply with the subject matter, 
apply their knowledge to a practical scenario, and reflect on their learning: this approach results 
in a moderate degree of flexibility, where students can bring their personal interests and career 
aspirations into the assessment by choosing the aspects of the case that they find most 
interesting or relevant. Students can also tailor their responses to reflect their personal 
perspectives and experiences. By working on a more complex case, students can gain insights 
into the practical applications of the unit material, enhancing their understanding and interest in 
the subject.  
Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
By providing a detailed case scenario, the model ensures that all students have the same 
information and the same opportunity to demonstrate their understanding and skills. The model 
also allows for a high degree of flexibility in responses, accommodating diverse perspectives 
and experiences. Students can choose the aspects of the case that they find most interesting or 
relevant, and tailor their responses to reflect their personal perspectives. The model can be 
adapted to different modes of delivery (online, in-person, or hybrid) making it accessible to a 
wide range of students. It can also be adapted to different formats, such as written reports, 
presentations, or discussions, accommodating different learning styles and preferences. Where 
possible, instructor(s) may wish to discuss and explore the use of multimodal submission formats 
(e.g., a written essay, oral statement, or recorded/live presentation of results) to ensure students 
are able to follow their preferred working strategy to deliver the assessment outcomes. 
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
It's crucial to clearly communicate the expectations to students as early as possible, including 
the tasks, the expected format and length of submissions, and the grading criteria. The case 
scenario should be complex and detailed enough to allow for multiple interpretations and 
solutions, and should also be updated regularly to maintain academic integrity and ensure that 
the assignment continues to be a meaningful learning experience. Feedback should be timely, 
constructive, and supportive, helping students to improve their understanding and skills, and 
should encourage students to reflect on their learning and their decision-making process. 
Generative AI and Other Technology Considerations 
With specific reference to the motivating assessment, the use of generative AI tools in the 
context of medicine and public health requires a detailed consideration of the professional 
expectations: if the use of some AI tools is banned in the profession, this reality should be openly 
discussed and their use in the classroom should be discouraged. If the assessment model is 
adapted to disciplines where generative AI tools are used, the open-ended and highly complex 
nature of the case study may be suitable for the use of chat-based AI tools to help students 
rapidly identify boundaries to the problem, and learn about historical solution strategies to 
address the criteria: such an approach is best-deployed in a flipped classroom environment, 
where instructor(s) are able to socialise these results with students/groups in an effort to help 
demonstrate their strengths, limitations, and the role of professional references in supporting 
latter stages of the assignment.  
