Overview of Assessment Model and Assessment Design 
This involves two linked assessments: 1) an annotated bibliography, and 2) a final research essay. 
They are intended for level 2 or level 3 history or other humanities/social sciences students. The 
research topic is developed by the student in consultation with the unit coordinator/tutor.  
Assessment Design 
Annotated Bibliography: The first part is an annotated bibliography worth 50% of the student’s 
final grade.  This has a few components.  Firstly, the student must come up with their own 
research question that links in some way to the themes of the unit.  Where the student is 
passionate about a topic, the instructor(s) can dialogue with them to find a pathway and 
perspective through which it can be folded into the theme of the unit. If their nominated topic 
isn’t directly related, a corollary topic may be discussed that remains within their interest and 
with appropriate connectivity to the unit learning outcomes. Some students can find choosing 
their own research topic difficult, if they have rarely had the opportunity to reflect on what they 
think is most important in the world, or the opportunity to research it for credit. The continued 
availability of instructors for consultation to discuss this at length is critical to achieving a vibrant 
set of final assessment outcomes.  
The annotated bibliography requires students to develop a title; research question;  paragraph 
explaining their major research question; a paragraph explaining their working answer to that 
question (this will become their thesis statement); a list of 5-10 primary sources that speak to the 
topic; and a list of 5-10 secondary/scholarly sources that speak to their topic.  The primary and 
secondary sources will also be annotated – that is, students will briefly explain how and why the 
sources will be useful.  Students may use readings that were assigned in the unit (if they are the 
best sources), but these are not considered a best practice to demonstrate the most creative or 
impressive research effort. The independent research process deployed in this assessment hones 
their research skills, as well as their capacity for critical analysis of primary and secondary 
sources: in the motivating assessment, the critical analysis of primary and scholarly sources is 
workshopped throughout the semester.  
Students share their bibliographies with each other in Week 6, give and receive feedback, and 
then submit the bibliographies to the LMS at the end of that week. Instructors provide feedback 
on how they can improve their fundamental questions, and make recommendations for useful 
scholarly readings and primary source collections.  Providing this feedback is exceptionally time 
consuming and labour intensive, but is very helpful to supporting each student within their 
research tasks.  This consultation process further functions to establish academic integrity, as 
students are expected to take this feedback and recommended resources on board before  
submitting their final assessments.  
Research Essay: In applying feedback from the first task, students next work on essays due at the 
end of the semester (2500-word essay, worth 50% of the final mark). The assignment requires 
high-level research, writing, and analytical skill, and addresses learning outcomes across both 
professional and personal skill development.  Students should be prepared for their ‘working 
answer’ to evolve over the course of their research, as new dimensions of an interesting question 
emerge during the research process.   
Aims: This assessment model seeks to develop students’ self-directed research skills, and to help 
them understand that developing a good research question is more challenging (and more 
