important) than having a good answer. The model further seeks to help students learn to identify 
and assess the best scholarly and primary sources to answer their questions, as critical to their 
future careers as professionals. In doing so, this approach helps students identify key issues in a 
body of scholarly literature, and develop their own original analysis methods for primary source 
materials to ultimately formulate an argument of their own design  
Feedback: Through submission of the first assessment task, qualitative, written feedback is 
provided highlighting strengths and identifying areas for improvement in annotated 
bibliographies: the same approach is followed for the final essays, to ensure students are able to 
reflect upon and learn from their submissions. In aligning feedback against each rubric 
dimension, and ensuring the rubric is made available alongside assignment materials, instructors 
are able to focus on establish academic integrity for each student (i.e., as they take on board 
recommendations for further references and perspectives), and to dialogue feedback with 
instructors as early as possible in the process.  
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
The self-directed nature of the project, and being able to research a topic they are passionate 
about, encourages students’ diligence helps them understand that this is an opportunity for 
them to learn and grow in their chosen discipline(s) and profession. While plagiarism detection 
tools may be a useful step in detecting violations of academic integrity, the use of a dialogic 
feedback model (i.e., written feedback combined with consultation at one or more points 
throughout the semester) delivers an intrinsic approach to establishing academic integrity.  
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
This assessment was designed to be student-focussed, as it allows students to identify the topics 
and personal interests that they find interesting, and to then explore those topics in significant 
detail.  This approach heightens disciplinary skills and professional competencies and shows 
professional maturation, while also ensuring students feel valued. The assessment also allows 
students to demonstrate improvement and growth between the annotated bibliography and 
the final essay.  Students can reflect on their growth in the unit, as an optional extension activity 
after the submission and receipt of feedback on the final essay.   
Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
The assignments require students to have access to a computer and the University’s research 
library, where consideration should be given to the equivalency of digital and in-person access 
opportunities. Importantly, UniAccess Adjustment Plans (at UWA) provide accommodations for 
submission time frame, where instructors may wish to consider the use of diverse assessment 
modes (e.g., written, spoken, or recorded presentations) to accommodate the learning 
preferences of each student. Students can research the topics that most interest them, and thus 
shape their learning to their interests. These decisions often reflect multiple dimensions of 
students’ backgrounds and personalities, which may require a nuanced, dialogic approach 
with each student. 
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
• 
Routine student consultation opportunities are critical – these conversations both ensure the 
final assessment submission is maximally student-centred while functioning to establish the 
integrity of each submission (as superior to checking for violations at the end). For this reason, 
ensuring consultations are an open dialogue and not one-way is crucial to student success. 
