Anticipated Outcomes: What are the expected outcomes of your project? How will these 
outcomes contribute to the field of [relevant discipline] and the broader community? 
 
Budget: Provide a detailed budget for your project. This should include all necessary costs and 
a justification for each expenditure. 
 
Project Timeline: Develop a comprehensive timeline for your project, detailing key milestones 
and deadlines. 
 
Funding Body Criteria Justification: Explain how your project meets the fictitious funding body’s 
criteria and why it should be funded. This justification should demonstrate a thorough 
understanding of the grant's objectives and a clear vision for your project's contribution to the 
field. 
 
As you develop your proposal, consider the real-world implications of your project. How will it 
impact your discipline? How will it benefit the broader community? Your proposal should 
demonstrate a clear understanding of the subject matter, a well-thought-out plan for project 
execution, and a strong justification for funding."" 
In order to optimally guide students through this process, academics can share examples from 
their own experiences in writing grant applications within the relevant discipline. By showcasing 
these examples, they can effectively highlight the essential components of a compelling 
proposal, including the clear articulation of project objectives, the justification of budget 
expenditures, and the creation of a realistic and achievable timeline. These examples can also 
serve to illustrate some of the challenges often encountered during the grant application 
process, how these obstacles were overcome, and how these experiences have informed the 
academic's research practice. It's beneficial for students to critically engage with these 
examples and use them as a guide when developing their own proposals. This assignment offers 
students an opportunity to take the knowledge they've gained throughout the unit and use it to 
design a project with the potential to make a real difference in their field. As such, it's important 
to encourage students to take this task seriously, to think creatively, and to apply their 
understanding of the relevant discipline to develop a proposal about which they are genuinely 
passionate. 
• Aims: The primary aims of this assessment model are as follows: 
• To encourage students to apply theoretical knowledge to a practical, real-world 
scenario. 
• To foster critical thinking, problem-solving, and creative skills as students develop a 
comprehensive project proposal. 
• To develop students' understanding of the grant application process, including how to 
articulate project objectives, methods, outcomes, and budgeting. 
• To emphasise the importance of community engagement in scientific research. 
• To help students understand the criteria and processes involved in assessing grant 
applications. 
• To provide an opportunity for students to showcase their understanding of unit content 
by applying it to a relevant project.  
• Tasks: The assignment under this model can be divided into several tasks, each with its own 
submission and assessment: 
