Project Proposal (40% weight): The project proposal should outline the project's 
objectives, methods, anticipated outcomes, and relevance to the discipline. Students 
should explain how their project meets the funding body's strategic goals. 
Budget Proposal (20% weight): Students are required to provide a detailed budget for 
their project, including all necessary costs and a justification for each expenditure. 
Project Timeline (15% weight): Students should create a comprehensive timeline for their 
project, detailing key milestones and deadlines. 
Funding Body Criteria Justification (15% weight): Students need to submit a written 
explanation of how their project meets the fictitious funding body’s criteria and why it 
should be funded. 
Reflection (10% weight): After all submissions, students are asked to submit a reflection 
on the assignment, discussing what they learned, challenges faced, and how they 
would approach the task differently next time.  
• Feedback: Feedback in the model is integral to student learning and skill development. 
Feedback should be provided at each task stage, allowing students to learn from their 
mistakes and enhance their understanding of the unit content. Specific skills that students 
should develop through this assignment include: 
• Project planning and management: This includes setting objectives, devising a 
methodology, planning a timeline, and budgeting. 
• Critical thinking and problem-solving: Students should demonstrate the ability to apply 
theoretical knowledge to a real-world scenario and devise innovative solutions to 
potential challenges. 
• Communication skills: Students should demonstrate clear and effective written 
communication, particularly in the context of professional grant applications. 
• Disciplinary understanding (established vs frontier knowledge): Students should 
demonstrate an understanding of the importance of community engagement in 
scientific research. 
• Reflective practice: Students should demonstrate the ability to reflect on their learning 
experience, understand their strengths and weaknesses, and identify areas for 
improvement.  
• Extension (optional): The Student Grant Application Model may be further extended to 
include peer review.  This could involve students reading deidentified submissions from 
other students, preparing a written reviewer report addressing the funding criteria and/or 
participating in a peer review panel discussion and project ranking exercise. Peer review 
reports could be returned to the student applicant to further inform the development of 
their final application. 
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
Each assignment under this model is individualised, as students choose their project topics and 
approach the grant application process in their own way. The model's sequential nature and 
multiple submission points also discourage academic misconduct. It is challenging to plagiarise 
or contract cheat a task that requires multiple, connected submissions over time, particularly 
where interim stages contain dialogic feedback between students and instructors. Such an 
iterative consultation strategy allows instructors to establish academic integrity on a per-student 
