Generative AI and Other Technology Considerations 
Technology can play a crucial role in the successful implementation of the model, particularly in 
catering to both in-person and online students, and considering multiple modes of submission 
(e.g., written submissions, oral submissions, and/or recorded presentations). As students are 
developing novel ideas for further research, the use of generative AI tools should be considered 
with extreme caution, as the license and access terms may affect students’ intellectual 
property: these considerations should be revisited in every incarnation of the class, as the license 
and access terms may vary over this same time scale. In the early stages of the proposal 
process, the use of chat-based generative AI tools may be relevant to help students bound the 
problem or understand current frontiers of knowledge within the field, prior to the point at which 
they begin iterating on their own research proposals within that frontier. This approach is best 
facilitated through a flipped-classroom environment, allowing instructor(s) so socialise with each 
student/group to confirm the accuracy of technical/research boundaries and provide 
recommendations for further professional reading once student’s concepts begin to emerge.  
