Overview of Assessment Model 
The Simulated Journal Paper model is a student-centred, authentic, and rigorous approach to 
assessment that fosters deep learning and professional skill development. This model is 
underpinned by the idea that students learn best when they engage in tasks that mirror those 
carried out by professionals in their field: it involves students individually or collectively 
researching, writing, and submitting a mock academic paper on a specific topic. The paper's 
structure mirrors that of a professional academic paper, including sections such as an abstract, 
introduction, methodology, results, discussion, and conclusion. The model emphasises critical 
thinking, research skills, academic writing, and the application of theoretical knowledge to real-
world problems or phenomena. The specific research question or topic can be set by the 
instructor or (preferably) chosen by the students within broad expectations, enabling a degree 
of student autonomy and allowing for the incorporation of students' personal interests and 
career aspirations. 
Assessment Design 
• Student prompt: The Simulated Journal Paper assignment presents an exciting opportunity to 
explore a topic of your choice within the realm of our unit content. Your task is to craft an 
academic-style paper, mirroring the structure and depth of professional scholarly work. This 
endeavour will allow you to explore a subject that captivates you, while also enhancing your 
research and academic writing skills. Remember, this assignment is a journey of exploration 
and learning. While the final paper is important, the process of researching and writing the 
paper is equally valuable. Use the resources provided, engage deeply with the task, and most 
importantly, enjoy the process of learning and discovery. In preparing for this assignment, you 
will be equipped with a variety of resources and materials to guide your journey. These 
resources are designed to support you in developing the knowledge and skills required to 
successfully complete the assignment. 
• Aims: The model aims to develop a range of academic and professional skills. It seeks to 
develop students' understanding of the structure and conventions of academic writing, as 
they are required to write a paper that mirrors the structure of a professional academic paper. 
This includes understanding the purpose and content of each section of an academic paper, 
and how to appropriately cite and reference appropriate sources (noting these are largely 
drawn from the frontier of the discipline’s research). The assessment further seeks to establish 
students' research skills, including the identification, critical evaluation, and synthesis from 
multiple resources to form a coherent argument. The assessment is an exemplar in further 
developing students' critical thinking skills, as they are required to apply their theoretical 
knowledge to a specific research question or problem (noting the required understanding of 
the discipline’s research frontiers). This involves analysing the problem, forming a hypothesis or 
argument, and evaluating evidence to support this argument.  
• Tasks: The model typically involves a series of tasks leading up to the final submission of the 
paper. These tasks can be structured as separate, sequential submissions, allowing students to 
receive feedback and make improvements at each stage. 
• Task 1: Research Proposal (10% weight) - Students submit a research proposal outlining 
their chosen topic, research question, and planned methodology. 
• Task 2: Annotated Bibliography (10% weight) - Students submit an annotated 
bibliography of the key sources they plan to use in their paper. 
• Task 3: Draft Paper (20% weight) - Students submit a draft of their paper for feedback. 
