• Task 4: Final Paper (60% weight) - Students submit their final paper. 
• Feedback: Feedback in the model focuses on both the product (the paper) and the process 
(the research and writing skills). Feedback on the product focuses on the content and 
structure of the paper, including the clarity and coherence of the argument, the 
appropriateness and rigour of the methodology, the interpretation and presentation of the 
results, the depth and relevance of the discussion, and the quality of the writing. Feedback on 
the process focuses on the skills that students have developed through the task. This includes 
feedback on students' research skills (e.g., their ability to find, evaluate, and synthesise 
information from sources), their project management skills (e.g., their ability to plan and 
manage their research project), and their collaborative skills (if the paper was written in a 
group). Feedback should be provided at each stage of the process (e.g., after the submission 
of the research proposal, annotated bibliography, and draft paper), allowing students to 
make improvements before submitting their final paper.  
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
The requirement for students to choose their own research question (or to select from a new list 
provided each year) makes it difficult for students to reuse previous students' work. This is further 
reinforced by the requirement for students to conduct their own research and to reference their 
sources appropriately. The sequential submission of tasks (research proposal, annotated 
bibliography, draft paper, final paper) allows instructors to monitor students' progress and to 
detect any inconsistencies that may suggest academic misconduct. As an extension concept, 
consultation between students and instructor(s) throughout the process provides the opportunity 
to establish academic integrity on a per-student level, as opposed to relying on an automated 
approach to detecting integrity violations during submission: the best practice of incorporating 
consultation is critical to maximising engagement from the cohort, and ensuring each student’s 
learning is maximised through the assessment process.  
Varying Assessment Elements to Maintain Academic Integrity 
To maintain academic integrity, it is recommended that the specific research question or topic 
for the paper is changed each year. This can be achieved by providing a new list of potential 
research questions each year, or by allowing students to choose their own research question 
within a broad area that aligns with the current unit content (noting the instructor should 
maintain a ledger of student-chosen concepts for reference before “approving” upcoming 
student topic proposals). The effort required to vary these elements should be relatively minimal, 
particularly if instructors have a bank of potential research questions and resources from which 
they can draw. 
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
Students can choose their own research question, allowing them to engage with a topic that is 
of personal interest or relevance to their career aspirations. They can also choose their own 
research methods and sources, allowing them to engage with the task in a way that suits their 
learning style and preferences. The model also fosters deep learning, as students are required to 
engage with the content at a deep level in order to write the paper. To ensure that the model is 
maximally student-focused, it is important to provide adequate support for students throughout 
the process (ideally in the form of multiple consultations throughout semester, either directly or 
through a flipped-classroom environment).  
