Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
The flexibility of the model allows it to be adapted to accommodate a range of student needs 
and circumstances. Students with UniAccess Adjustment Plans (at UWA) can be provided with 
additional time to complete the tasks or may require assistive technology to access the 
resources and materials. Further, flexibility in the mode of early submission tasks (e.g., the 
research proposal) such as oral presentations and/or recorded video presentations, can be 
used to ensure students are able to work in their preferred domain. Instructors can further 
support equity, diversity, and accessibility by providing a range of resources and materials to 
support students in completing the task (e.g., guides to academic writing, research methods, 
citation and referencing), and by providing opportunities for students to ask questions and seek 
help. The provision of appropriate resources and examples is a critical feature of the assessment 
model, where students will likely have had a wide range of exposure to, and previous 
background with, academic journal articles. 
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
Best practices for implementing the assessment model include: 
 Providing clear instructions and expectations: Ensure that students understand what is 
expected of them, including the structure and content of the paper, the specific requirements 
for each task, and the criteria that will be used to assess their work. 
 Providing timely and constructive feedback: Constructive and specific feedback should be 
provided at each stage of the process, allowing students to make improvements before 
submitting their final paper. Feedback should focus on both the product and the process. 
 Providing adequate support: Provide a range of resources and materials to support students in 
completing the task, and provide opportunities for students to ask questions and seek help. The 
use of multiple consultation opportunities throughout semester is a critical support mechanism. 
Generative AI and Other Technology Considerations 
The model can be implemented in an online, in-person, or hybrid teaching and learning 
environment, where technology can also be used to support students in conducting their 
research (e.g., online databases, search engines), writing their paper (e.g., word processing 
software, citation management tools), and collaborating with their peers (if the paper is written 
in a group). Generative AI can significantly enhance the learning experience in the assessment 
model, assisting students at various technical levels with the research and writing processes, 
enabling a more personalised learning experience that can be tailored to the 
educational/experiential background of each student. With research, generative AI can help 
students explore a topic: for generative AI tools capable of working across multiple languages, 
sematic-based search or explanatory conversation may help students explore and contextualise 
disciplinary nuance.  
