to the profession. Your report should mirror the structure of a professional case report and 
should include the following sections: introduction, patient information, clinical findings, 
timeline, diagnostic assessment, therapeutic intervention, follow-up and outcomes, and 
discussion. Your report should be based on rigorous research and should demonstrate a deep 
understanding of the case.""  
• Aims: The assessment model aims to develop a range of academic and professional skills. 
Primary among these is the development of students' understanding of the structure and 
conventions of case report writing. This includes understanding the purpose and content of 
each section of a case report, and how to appropriately cite and reference sources. The 
model also aims to develop students' diagnostic (or technical disciplinary) skills, as they are 
required to analyse a clinical case and come up with diagnostic strategies. This involves 
analysing the case, forming a diagnosis, and evaluating evidence to support this diagnosis. 
Additionally, the model aims to develop students' therapeutic (or practical disciplinary) skills, as 
they are required to suggest therapeutic interventions based on their diagnosis. This requires 
students to apply their theoretical knowledge to a real-world case. Across all disciplines, the 
model further seeks to develop students' research skills, as they are required to conduct 
research to inform their case report, including the identification, critical appraisal, and synthesis 
of relevant source content. 
• Tasks: The Professional Case Report model typically involves the following tasks leading up to 
the final submission of the case report, noting the context that the embedded example is 
drawn from Allied Health:  
• Task 1: Case Selection (10% weight) Students will identify a suitable disciplinary case for 
their report. This should be a real-world example, either from a published case report, a 
clinical experience, or a case study from a textbook or educational resource. The case 
should be relevant to the unit content and should be complex enough to allow for a 
thorough analysis. Students will submit a brief description of the case (approximately 200 
words), outlining the patient information, the presenting problem or condition, and the 
reason for selecting this case. They should also justify their choice, explaining why the 
case is of interest to them and how it relates to the unit content. Students will be 
assessed on the appropriateness of their case selection and the quality of their 
justification. 
• Task 2: Preliminary Findings (20% weight) Students will conduct initial research on their 
case and submit their preliminary findings. This should include a detailed description of 
the patient information, clinical findings, and timeline. Students should use a range of 
sources to inform their findings, including textbooks, journal articles, and other relevant 
resources. Students will be assessed on the depth and accuracy of their findings, the 
quality of their research, and the correct use of citation and referencing conventions. 
• Task 3: Draft Report (20% weight) Students will use their preliminary findings to develop a 
draft of their case report. The draft report should include the following sections: 
introduction, patient information, clinical findings, timeline, diagnostic assessment, and 
therapeutic intervention. The draft report should be structured like a professional case 
report and should demonstrate a deep understanding of the case. Students should use 
their research to inform their diagnostic assessment and therapeutic intervention. 
Students will be assessed on the structure and content of their report, the depth of their 
analysis, and the quality of their writing. 
• Task 4: Final Report (50% weight) In the final task, students will revise and refine their draft 
report, incorporating the feedback received from their instructor. They will also add the 
