final sections to their report: follow-up and outcomes, and discussion. The final report 
should be a polished, professional-quality case report that demonstrates a 
comprehensive understanding of the case and a thorough analysis of the diagnostic 
and therapeutic strategies. Students will be assessed on the overall quality of their report, 
including the content, structure, depth of analysis, quality of writing, and correct use of 
citation and referencing conventions. 
• Feedback: Feedback in the Professional Case Report model focuses on both the product (the 
case report) and the process (e.g., the diagnostic and therapeutic strategies in Allied Health). 
Feedback on the product focuses on the content and structure of the report, including the 
clarity and coherence of the narrative, the appropriateness and rigour of the diagnostic and 
therapeutic strategies, and the quality of the writing. Feedback on the process focuses on the 
skills that students have developed through the task, including the students’ technical and 
practical skills within the discipline, their academic research skills, and their ability to apply 
theoretical knowledge to a real-world scenario. Feedback should be provided at each stage 
of the process, allowing students to make improvements before submitting their final report.  
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
The requirement for students to analyse a specific case and reference their sources 
appropriately makes it difficult for students to reuse previous students' work. This is further 
reinforced by the sequential submission of tasks, which allows instructors to monitor students' 
progress and detect any inconsistencies that may suggest academic misconduct. Ideally, the 
placement of multiple consultation sessions throughout the semester, wherein instructor(s) are 
able to discuss interim outcomes with each student/group (e.g., in a class workshop approach), 
such that academic integrity can be established on a per-student level (i.e., as opposed to 
relying on automated detection of violations). 
Varying Assessment Elements to Maintain Academic Integrity 
To maintain academic integrity, it is recommended that the specific case(s) for the report be 
changed each year. This prevents students from reusing previous students' work and ensures that 
each student has a unique case to analyse. Instructors can also vary the specific requirements 
for each task (e.g., the depth of analysis required, the specific sections required for the draft 
report) to prevent students from reusing work from previous years. The effort required to vary 
these elements should be relatively minimal, particularly if instructors have a bank of potential 
cases from which they can draw. 
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
The model allows for a high degree of student autonomy and personalisation. Ideally, students 
should be able to choose their own case alongside a few standard case options each semester, 
allowing them to engage with a case that is of personal interest or relevance to their career 
aspirations. They could also choose their own professional perspective (e.g., diagnostic and 
therapeutic strategies in Allied Health), allowing them to engage with the task in a way that suits 
their learning style and preferences. This process requires students to engage in higher-order 
thinking skills, which are key to deep learning. To ensure that the model is truly student-focused, it 
is important to provide adequate support for students throughout the process.  
