Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
The flexibility of the model allows it to be adapted to accommodate a range of student needs 
and circumstances. UniAccess Adjustment Plans (at UWA) provide accommodations for 
additional time to complete the tasks, and can help identify where assistive technology is 
required to access the resources and materials. The model also supports students by allowing 
them to engage with a case that is of personal interest or relevance to them: this approach can 
help to make the tasks more engaging and meaningful for students, and can help to foster a 
sense of belonging and inclusion within the cohort.  
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
Best practices for implementing the Professional Case Report model include: 
• Providing clear instructions and expectations: Ensure that students understand what is 
expected of them, including the structure and content of the report, the specific 
requirements for each task, and the criteria that will be used to assess their work. 
• Providing timely and constructive feedback: Feedback should be provided at each 
stage of the process, allowing students to make improvements before submitting their 
final report. Feedback should focus on both the product and the process and should be 
constructive and specific. 
• Providing adequate support: Provide a range of resources and materials to support 
students in completing the task and provide opportunities for students to ask questions 
and seek help. 
Generative AI and Other Technology Considerations 
Beyond digital submission methods within the LMS, technology can also be used to support 
students in conducting their research (e.g., online databases, search engines), writing their 
report (e.g., word processing software, citation management tools), and collaborating with their 
peers (if the report is written in a group). Critically, the use of generative AI tools requires 
discipline-level consideration for appropriateness: for example, health-related professions may 
categorically exclude some AI tools for patient confidentiality, wherein the classroom 
environment should align with this professional expectation. If the model is deployed in other 
disciplines that are less restrictive about generative AI, the use of chat-based generative AI may 
be useful in helping students explore and quickly bound the dimensions of their given/chosen 
case study. In such an approach, it is important to ensure that students have adequate 
opportunity to check this understanding with instructor(s), particularly in identifying where the 
use of generative AI tools should cease and the application of professional/disciplinary 
resources should take over (i.e., to dive more deeply for a given technical aspect).  
