Overview of Assessment Model 
The assessment model is a group-based, student-centred learning experience designed to 
cultivate the competencies required to design, develop, and deliver a training program 
relevant to the discipline and associated profession(s). The model is premised on the necessity 
for students to apply theoretical knowledge to practical applications, fostering critical thinking, 
collaborative work, and presentation skills. The assessment encourages a sense of responsibility, 
as each group is required to prepare and deliver a training program on a given topic. The 
model also incorporates a feedback mechanism, whereby peers evaluate each group's 
presentation, creating a dynamic learning environment that values diverse perspectives and 
encourages continuous improvement.  
Assessment Design 
• Student prompt: Students are tasked with designing and delivering a training program in 
collaboration with a small group of peers. This training program will be delivered during class 
time, in one of the final four seminars. The group will prepare a trainer’s manual and any 
supporting trainee materials. These materials should be submitted to the Learning 
Management System (LMS) on the same day as the scheduled training program presentation. 
The assessment mark contains components for both individual and group performance. 
• Aims: The assessment aims to help students develop the skills required to design and deliver a 
training program and prepare a training manual relevant to their chosen discipline of study. 
The training program should incorporate cognitive principles of learning, motivate trainees, 
address contextual influences on the training process, and incorporate an effective procedure 
for evaluation. To achieve these aims, the students will need to carry out a training needs 
analysis, derive a set of learning objectives, develop a plan of instruction, incorporate post-unit 
activities to enhance retention and transfer, develop a procedure for evaluating the training 
program, document the training process in a trainer’s manual, and develop appropriate 
materials for the trainees.  
• Tasks: The assessment model is divided into several tasks, the granularity of which may be 
varied relative to the level of the student cohort involved: 
• Task 1: ""Training Needs Analysis"" (10% weight): Identify the requirements and goals of the 
training program. This may involve students profiling the audience, performing 
background research into the relevant sector and/or community, and considering how 
progress can be measured (and quantitatively tracked). 
• Task 2: ""Learning Objectives and Plan of Instruction"" (15% weight): Develop the 
educational goals and instructional plan for the program, with a similar approach to 
scrutiny that may be taken for curriculum proposals or revisions at the university level.  
• Task 3: ""Training Program Development"" (30% weight): Create the training program 
incorporating post-unit activities: the specific mode or structure might vary substantially 
based on standard (or best) practice(s) within the discipline(s) involved.  
• Task 4: ""Trainer's Manual Development"" (20% weight): Prepare the trainer's manual that 
documents the training process, which may be combined with Task 3 if necessary to 
represent one “training material” submission. 
• Task 5: ""Trainee Materials Development"" (15% weight): Generate any necessary 
materials for the trainees, which may similarly be combined with Tasks 3 and 4 as 
needed.  
