• Task 6: ""Training Program Presentation"" (10% weight): Deliver the training program in 
class, providing the opportunity for both peer evaluation/feedback and formal 
assessment.   
• Feedback: Feedback is delivered through multiple consultation sessions between 
individuals/groups and instructors during the semester. When combined with multiple 
scaffolded submission tasks (as above), the dialogic nature of feedback in this assessment 
model provides an opportunity for instructors to establish academic integrity on a per-student 
level, as opposed to relying on automated methods to detect integrity violations. Students will 
receive feedback from their peers at the end of each training program, which aims to 
highlight the strengths and areas for improvement in training delivery and presentation style 
(ensuring that all students learn from the presentation experiences, even outside of their own 
groups/topics). By engaging with constructive criticism and recognising the positive aspects of 
their performance, students can refine their skills in public speaking, collaboration, critical 
thinking, and training delivery.  
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
In the example of conducting a training needs analysis (the motivating assessment), the student 
must not only understand the theory behind training needs analysis but also apply this 
understanding to a specific, practical context. They must identify the learning needs, 
preferences, and circumstances of potential trainees, which requires original thought and 
research. This process, by its very nature, discourages academic dishonesty as it is not feasible to 
plagiarise such personalised, contextualised analysis. The multiple submission tasks, when paired 
with continuous and iterative feedback and consultation with instructors, provides an intrinsic 
assurance of academic integrity. The presentation component of the task further discourages 
plagiarism, as students must be able to speak confidently and knowledgeably about their topic 
while addressing questions from their peers and instructors. The role of the peer evaluation 
process further encourages students to deliver high-quality, original work, knowing that their 
peers will critique their performance. The task of designing and delivering a training program 
requires students to apply their knowledge and skills to a real-world scenario, fostering deeper 
understanding and reducing opportunities for academic dishonesty as they prepare to enter 
the profession.  
Varying Assessment Elements to Maintain Academic Integrity 
To maintain academic integrity, it is important to vary the elements of the assessment each year. 
In the context of the unit, this could be achieved by altering the themes or contexts for the 
training programs. For example, one year students might be tasked with creating a training 
program for a corporate context, the next year for educational settings, and the following year 
in a community environment. This approach not only deters academic dishonesty by preventing 
the recycling of previous year’s work, but also enriches the learning experience by exposing 
students to a variety of professional contexts (e.g., they may wish to review a previous year’s 
work in order to better inform their approach to the new setting/environment). 
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
