focus for the training program that is relevant to their career goals, or designing the program to 
address a particular issue or challenge (or community) about which they are passionate. This 
aspect of the model encourages students to take an active role in their learning, fostering 
greater engagement and intrinsic motivation.   
Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
The group-based nature of the assessment ensures that all students have equal opportunities to 
contribute and learn, fostering a strong cohort experience. Students with different skills and 
strengths can each contribute in a way of their choosing and learn from the diverse 
perspectives within their group. The assessment model is also designed to be inclusive, 
encouraging students to consider the mode and format of training materials that best suits their 
preferred teaching (and learning) style. For instance, students who may not be comfortable with 
public speaking could focus on planning and development phases of the training program, 
while recording their presentation component asynchronously.  
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
Educators should clearly communicate the assessment requirements, deliverables, and marking 
criteria to the students (see below). Regular check-ins with groups can help identify and address 
any issues early, particularly with the opportunity to share check-ins across students and groups 
(i.e., a workshop-type dialogue, so students can better perceive the cohort position). The 
iterative feedback and consultation in this model is a best delivered in a supportive 
environment, where students feel comfortable giving and receiving feedback with instructor(s) 
and across the group. 
Generative AI and Other Technology Considerations 
The assessment primarily requires in-person presentations but could be adapted for remote 
learning using video conferencing platforms like Zoom. Documents and manuals could be 
shared and submitted through an LMS or cloud-based platforms like Google Drive to better 
foster collaboration. For remote delivery, educators should consider how to facilitate group 
collaboration and provide technical support as needed. 
Chat-based generative AI tools may be particularly useful as students first commence their work 
on the training program, as a vehicle to quickly explore established training styles and methods, 
and to iterate across various incarnations of their proposed training solution. If deployed, 
instructor(s) should spend some time illustrating the discipline-specific limits of, and appropriate 
use cases for, generative AI in this context, particularly if the profession (e.g., health-related) 
institutes strict confidentiality measures that are not compatible with most tools. In the limit chat-
based AI tools are deployed, instructors should socialise early with each student/group to discuss 
and confirm the correct disciplinary boundaries have been identified, and that students 
understand the point at which they should seek to draw in professional reference sources for 
further reading. 
Further, the use of image-generation AI systems may be useful in developing customised 
illustrations for training materials. As such systems may require additional training or 
experimentation to use, the inclusion of example prompts for each recommended tool should 
be presented and discussed with students. Importantly, the paywall differentiation in accessing 
generative AI systems is an important consideration within this assessment model, where some 
students may have existing (paid) accounts that are not accessible to others: where possible, 
