Overview of Assessment Model 
The Integrated Technical Term Paper is a versatile and comprehensive academic exercise that is 
suitable for a wide range of technical disciplines. This model allows students to delve deep into a 
specific topic that is directly related to their unit content (including scientific disciplines from the 
motivating assessment). The process requires an extensive exploration of the topic, including 
research, data collection and analysis, critical thinking, and the synthesis of findings into a 
coherent, well-structured long-form report submitted at the end of semester (“term” paper). The 
model combines multiple skills that are crucial in and beyond technical disciplines, including the 
acumen of selecting appropriate research sources, analytical skills, problem-solving, and 
effective written communication.  
Assessment Design 
• Student prompt: In this assessment model, students are expected to write a comprehensive 
term paper on a topic that has practical and academic relevance to their unit of study. This 
topic should be chosen with care (ideally nominated by the students themselves within the 
guidelines and expectations of the instructor). The topic should be specific enough to allow for 
an in-depth exploration, yet broad enough to incorporate a range of concepts and theories 
from the unit content. The term paper should be a scholarly document that contains an 
abstract, introduction, literature review, methodology, findings, discussion, conclusion, and 
references. The quality of the term paper will be evaluated based on the depth of research, 
the coherence of arguments, the quality of data analysis, the clarity of writing, and the proper 
citation of sources. 
• Aims: The term paper assignment is designed with several specific aims in mind. At the most 
basic level, the primary aim is to deepen students' understanding of a specific aspect of the 
unit material. By focusing on a single topic and exploring it in detail, students are able to gain a 
thorough understanding of the topic and its relation to the broader field of study. The 
assessment also further develops research skills, including the ability to conduct a literature 
review, to design and implement a research methodology, to collect and analyse data (as 
applicable within the discipline), and to synthesise findings in a coherent and logical manner. 
Through the process of research and analysis, students are encouraged to question existing 
knowledge, to identify gaps or inconsistencies, and to propose new ideas or theories. In 
delivering their final product, students are able to practice and develop their ability to 
communicate complex ideas in a clear and concise manner, and in a tone that is consistent 
with the expectations of the profession. 
• Tasks: The assessment model consists of four primary tasks, each of which correspond to four 
outputs to be submitted by students: 
• Task 1: Proposal Submission (15% weight) - The student submits a proposal that outlines 
the chosen topic, the proposed research question or hypothesis, the planned 
methodology, and a preliminary literature review. This proposal serves as a roadmap for 
the term paper and allows the instructor to provide initial feedback and guidance. 
• Task 2: Literature Review Submission (15% weight) - The student conducts a 
comprehensive literature review on the chosen topic and submits a written summary. 
The literature review should not only summarise existing knowledge on the topic but also 
identify gaps in the literature that the term paper will aim to address. 
• Task 3: Draft Term Paper Submission (no marks; qualitative feedback) - Based on the 
proposal and the literature review, the student conducts research, collects and analyses 
