data, and synthesises findings into a coherent argument. The student submits a draft of 
the term paper that includes all these elements. 
• Task 4: Final Term Paper Submission (70% weight) - After receiving feedback on the draft, 
the student revises the term paper and submits the final version. The final term paper 
should be a polished and refined document that presents the student's research findings 
in a clear, logical, and persuasive manner.  
• Feedback: After each task, the instructor provides written feedback to the student. This 
feedback focuses on various aspects of the term paper, including the clarity of the research 
question or hypothesis, the thoroughness and relevance of the literature review, the 
appropriateness of the methodology, the validity of the data analysis, the coherence of the 
arguments, and the quality of the writing. The feedback aims to guide the student in improving 
their work, to clarify any misunderstandings or misconceptions, and to encourage deeper and 
more critical thinking. Feedback also serves to motivate students and to reinforce positive 
learning behaviours and, when paired with in-class or external consultation opportunities, 
provides the ability for the instructor(s) to establish academic integrity on a per-student basis.  
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
The sequential and progressive nature of the tasks ensures that each student produces original 
work: this is critically underpinned by the feedback mechanism and, ideally, inclusion of 
successive consultation opportunities (e.g., with students/groups in a flipped-classroom 
environment). The requirement for each student to choose a unique topic minimises the chance 
of copying or plagiarism, particularly where the academic maintains a ledger of previous topics 
to provide feedback and recommendations in the lead-up to the first task submission. The 
process of conducting a literature review and citing sources instils in students the importance of 
recognising and respecting the work of others.  
Varying Assessment Elements to Maintain Academic Integrity 
The overall theme or specific focus of the term paper can be changed each year (e.g., varying 
the focus between 3 and 5 key topics within a given unit/curriculum), requiring students to 
explore new topics and ensuring they are not able to simply copy material from pervious year(s). 
Beyond this, the specific requirements for each task can be adjusted, including the depth 
and/or focus of the literature review, the type or extent of data to be collected (as 
appropriate), or the methods of data analysis can be varied (where possible within the 
discipline). It is important to acknowledge that the inclusion of additional consultation points 
throughout semester, within which integrity can be established for each student as they work 
through the process, reduces the need to vary above listed assessment parameters each year.  
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
By allowing students to choose their own topics, the model encourages students to take 
ownership of their learning and to engage deeply with the unit material. The process of 
preparing the term paper fosters independent learning, critical thinking, and problem-solving 
skills. Through the additional use of consultation sessions following each submission, instructors 
are able to dialogue individually with students in order to draw out their personal interests and 
associated career aspirations: the instructors can then discuss how these might integrate with 
the student’s chosen research paper/topic, to maximise the personal and professional 
relevance of the assessment to their future in the profession. 
