Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
By allowing students to choose their own topics, the model ensures that students from diverse 
disciplinary and personal backgrounds can engage with the assignment in a meaningful way, 
and focus their work toward personal and/or emerging professional interests. The structure of the 
assignment, with its sequential tasks and multiple points of feedback, ensures that students with 
different learning styles and abilities can be successful. Accommodations through UniAccess 
Adjustment Plans (at UWA) can be made for students with who require additional time for tasks 
and/or the use accessible technology. Importantly, early submission tasks that do not require a 
written format (i.e., as expected within the profession) could be broadened to multi-modal 
submissions, where students are able to nominate the method they most prefer to communicate 
their findings (e.g., recorded oral presentations). 
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
Clear communication of expectations and requirements is essential. Providing examples of high-
quality term papers can help students understand what is expected of them. Regular check-ins 
and opportunities for consultation can support students throughout the process. Encouraging 
peer interaction and collaboration can foster a supportive learning environment. The use of 
technology, including a learning management system and plagiarism detection software, can 
facilitate the administration of the assignment. 
Generative AI and Other Technology Considerations 
Where appropriate within the corresponding profession, the use of chat-based generative AI 
tools can be very useful in helping students first approach the assessment. In nominating one or 
more prospective areas for research, chat-based AI can be useful to identify the bounding 
requirements of the associated research tasks, and identify common data acquisition and 
analysis skills that are relevant to the discipline. Such tools could further be useful in helping 
students identify permutations to their primary or constituent research questions, so they are best 
prepared to dialogue this strategy (e.g., in a flipped classroom environment) with academics to 
arrive at their preferred outcome (i.e., that which is centred on their personal interests and 
career aspirations). Instructors should demonstrate the optimal use of these tools (including 
appropriate prompts) to help students frame the problem, and to further illustrate the likely 
accuracy bounds between generative AI and the professional, discipline-specific references 
that will be required to facilitate the assessment. Specific discussion should be given to the 
importance of professional and appropriate references, and the requirement to interrogate and 
verify the references before deployment in their assessment tasks.  
