Overview of Assessment Model 
This model assessment is motivated by the final assessment in an advanced applied science 
discipline (specifically, remote sensing in the context of environmental science). This project-
based assessment model emphasises the synthesis of skills, concepts, and knowledge acquired 
throughout the unit. The project requires students to identify, analyse, and address a remote 
sensing problem from start to finish, providing a comprehensive exploration of a selected aspect 
of remote sensing science. The assessment is largely student-driven, allowing them to choose a 
geographic location and define a relevant research problem. They then create a methodology, 
collect and process data, apply their methodology within a Geographic Information System 
(GIS), and present their findings using a digital storytelling tool, ArcGIS StoryMap. The use of 
ArcGIS StoryMap in the description below can be substituted by any professional software 
appropriate to another discipline outside environmental science. The final deliverable is a 
comprehensive report, presented as a StoryMap, encapsulating the research problem, 
methodology, results, discussion, and conclusions. This assessment model is weighted heavily, 
accounting for 70% of the unit grade in this example, emphasising its role in evaluating the 
student's mastery of the unit (and course) content.  
Assessment Design 
By offering students the autonomy to guide their research and present their work (including the 
mode of delivery, where possible), the assessment model seeks to foster deep learning, critical 
thinking, and effective communication. The use of digital tools also encourages students to 
develop digital literacy through professional practice. 
• Student prompt: Students are prompted to conceive and analyse a remote sensing problem 
from start to finish that is directly associated with their environmental science specialisation. To 
do this, they must select a geographic location and define a research problem related to 
remote sensing in this location. Next, they are asked to develop a research methodology to 
address their problem. This includes selecting appropriate data, determining how to process it, 
and deciding how to apply their methodology within a GIS. They are also encouraged to 
consider the quality of the data during the sourcing process. The final component of the 
project is a critical discussion of their data, methods, and outputs, as well as the broader 
implications of their research findings. They are expected to present their project in a digital 
storytelling format using ArcGIS StoryMap.  
• Aims:  
o 
Define a research problem and develop a research question: Students must identify a 
significant problem related to their chosen geographic location and craft a research 
question to guide their investigation. 
o 
Develop a methodology: Students should demonstrate their ability to design a 
comprehensive methodology that can adequately address their research question. This 
includes the selection and processing of appropriate data, the application of relevant 
methods and techniques, and the use of GIS (in the motivating discipline). 
o 
Implement the methodology and analyse the results: The assessment tests students' skills 
in implementing their methodology, interpreting the results, and critically evaluating their 
approach. They are expected to process the data, apply their methodology within a 
GIS, and discuss the outputs critically. 
