o 
Understand and apply remote sensing terminology: The project requires students to use 
appropriate and correct remote sensing terminology, demonstrating their understanding 
of the field's language. 
o 
Communicate their findings effectively: Finally, the assessment measures students' ability 
to present their work in a clear, organised, and engaging manner using ArcGIS StoryMap. 
Students should effectively use the features of the digital storytelling tool to enhance their 
narrative, including custom maps, text, photos, and videos.  
• Tasks: The assessment involves several tasks that build on each other to form a comprehensive 
research project. Initially, students select a geographic location and a corresponding remote 
sensing research problem (alongside the motivating discipline of environmental science, this 
may further extend to any consideration of the natural or built environment). They then 
proceed to develop a methodology for addressing their research question; identifying the 
data they need, retrieving/measuring it, and determining how to process it within the 
expectations of the discipline. Students then provide a critical discussion of their data, 
methods, and outputs, assessing the strengths and weaknesses of their approach and 
highlighting any interesting or surprising findings, concluding with discussion on the broader 
implications of their research findings in relation to their chosen topic. Students should reflect 
on the significance of their work at the end of the activity, including the potential impact on 
the discipline and any future research directions that could stem from their work on this project.  
• Feedback: As the project is complex and extensive, the instructor should provide ongoing 
feedback at various stages of the project (noting the single ‘optional’ feedback 
recommended in the assessment timeline can be expanded to account for the needs of the 
cohort and diversity of projects/sub-disciplines involved). Beyond formal settings, students 
should be encouraged to seek feedback on their project outline, research question, 
methodology, and preliminary results. This allows them to refine their work continuously, 
ensuring that they are on the right track and that their project aligns with the assessment's aims 
and objectives. Feedback should be specific, actionable, and constructive, highlighting areas 
of strength and offering suggestions for improvement. It should guide students in developing a 
critical understanding of their work, enabling them to identify potential issues and devise 
solutions prior to submitting their final work.  
Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
The independent nature of the project requires each student to choose a unique geographic 
location and research problem, where the dialogic nature of the assessment model (i.e., 
through both written feedback and ongoing consultation) provides the intrinsic ability to 
establish integrity on a per-student basis. The requirement to develop an individualised 
methodology further enhances academic integrity, as it demands independent thought and 
application of knowledge and form the centrepiece of the final task submission (including 
feedback from instructors established early in the semester).  
Varying Assessment Elements to Maintain Academic Integrity 
Maintaining academic integrity requires the thoughtful design of assessment tasks. In this model, 
the elements of the assessment are varied to minimise opportunities for dishonest behaviour and 
to ensure the accurate measurement of individual student learning. The project is highly 
individualised, with students selecting their own geographic location and research problem. In 
the motivating discipline, the use of GIS for data analysis and the creation of a StoryMap 
(software platform) for project presentation also adds variability to the assessment.  
