Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
Students choose their own geographic location and research problem, offering them the 
opportunity to engage with topics that are personally interesting and relevant to their 
specialisation. By working on a complex task over a longer period, students have the opportunity 
to explore concepts in depth, apply their knowledge in a practical context, and reflect on their 
learning. Central to this process is the combination of written and dialogic feedback with 
instructors, wherein the students’ personal interests and professional motivations can be 
discussed, with recommendations how best to align and (re)direct their project work toward 
incorporating these ends. In helping shape each project toward student-centred design, and in 
doing so with consultation and feedback, instructors are also able to better establish academic 
integrity on a per-student level. Further to this, the motivating discipline deploys a unique 
combination of software tools (ArcGIS alongside StoryMap) as a presentation mechanism that 
weaves together text, images, videos, and custom maps, and providing a platform to 
demonstrate their understanding in a visually engaging way.  
Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
The flexibility in choosing a geographic location and research problem allows students from 
diverse backgrounds to engage with topics that reflect their experiences, interests, and future 
career aspirations. This inclusive design supports engagement and motivation among a diverse 
student population. Digital storytelling tools are often designed with accessibility in mind, 
including features such as alt text for images and closed captions for videos. Alongside the use 
of UniAccess Adjustment Plans (at UWA) to ensure submission deadlines and assignment 
parameters are aligned with each student’s needs, instructors may broaden the modes of 
allowable submission to include written, oral, and visual/narrated, such that each student can 
deliver their assessment by a means that is most accessible to them.  
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
A key best practice for implementing this assessment model is to provide clear, detailed 
instructions. Given the complex and independent nature of the project, students need to 
understand exactly what is expected of them. The project guidelines should clearly outline the 
tasks, aims, and assessment criteria. Critically, ongoing consultation opportunities and regular 
check-ins can help to ensure that students are on track and provide opportunities for instructors 
to sign off on the integrity of each students’ evolving project 
Generative AI and Other Technology Considerations 
In the motivating assessment, students to use ArcGIS and StoryMap, a digital storytelling tool 
deployed in a number of technical disciplines. It's important to ensure that all students have 
access to these tools, and have sufficient support (e.g., asynchronous training materials for those 
who may require revision on tool functions and capabilities). For students who are not located 
on campus, ensuring virtual access to university computing resources is critical to maintaining a 
high degree of accessibility in the assessments.  
The use of chat-based generative AI tools can provide students with the ability to rapidly iterate 
through their research design methods, and to identify any unique constraints or opportunities 
surrounding their nominated project choices. Critically, these should be approached with 
demonstration by instructors to help students understand the discipline-specific technical 
bounds between generative AI and professional resources (e.g., those that are required to 
complete the assessment). Image-generation AI tools may further be helpful to students who 
