Overview of Assessment Model 
The assessment model provided is a comprehensive, multi-part project, designed to simulate a 
real-world technical task. The motivating (i.e., Engineering-specific) project requires students to 
design, analyse, and evaluate a chemical process plant, thereby integrating and applying a 
wide range of concepts and skills acquired throughout their unit. The model is designed in such 
a way that it encourages collaborative learning, critical thinking, and practical problem-solving. 
It also includes components that focus on enhancing the presentation and communication skills 
of the students. 
Assessment Design 
The model is broken down into several distinct tasks that are spread over several weeks, making 
it manageable and encouraging consistent effort and engagement over time. Each task is 
clearly defined with specific objectives, a detailed description, and clear marking criteria. This 
ensures that students understand what is expected of them and how their work will be 
evaluated. The tasks are a mix of individual and group work, promoting teamwork and 
collaboration, while also ensuring individual accountability and learning. The model also 
includes a feedback mechanism, enabling students to learn from their mistakes and improve 
their work over time. The model is flexible and adaptable and can be modified to suit different 
disciplines or specific learning outcomes.  
• Student prompt: Students are presented with a realistic scenario: they are part of an 
engineering team tasked with designing, analysing, and evaluating a chemical process plant. 
The plant's specifics, including its size, function, and constraints, are described in detail. The 
project is divided into several tasks, each focusing on a different aspect of the project. For 
example, students might start by developing equipment schedules and specifications, then 
move on to designing specific equipment and control systems. Each task comes with specific 
instructions, objectives, and marking criteria. Students are required to work both individually 
and in teams, where some tasks (detailed below) are assessed on the individual level and 
some are assessed as a group (within the remit of the University’s assessment policy). Each task 
must be submitted by a specified deadline, and the final project is a culmination of all these 
tasks. The project is comprehensive and requires the application of a wide range of 
engineering concepts and skills. It is designed to be challenging but achievable, with ample 
support and feedback provided throughout the process.  
• Aims: The primary aim of this assessment model is to provide students with a hands-on, realistic 
experience of engineering design and analysis. The model aims to simulate the challenges and 
considerations encountered in a professional engineering environment, enabling students to 
apply the theoretical knowledge they have gained throughout their unit in a practical context. 
Specifically, the tasks aim to enhance students' understanding and skills in areas such as 
equipment design, process control, safety considerations, risk assessment, and economic 
analysis. By breaking down the project into distinct tasks, students are encouraged to focus on 
each of these areas in detail, ensuring a comprehensive understanding and application of all 
aspects involved in the design and operation of a process plant. The assessment model also 
aims to promote key transferable skills. Through the group work components, students are 
encouraged to develop their teamwork and collaboration skills. The requirement for regular 
submissions promotes time management and organisation, while the presentation and 
communication aspects of the tasks aim to enhance students' ability to communicate 
technical information clearly and effectively. Finally, the model aims to promote continuous 
learning and improvement. Through the provision of regular feedback, students are 
