encouraged to reflect on their work, learn from their mistakes, and continuously improve their 
understanding and skills.  
• Tasks: The tasks in this assessment model are carefully designed to cover all key aspects of the 
design, analysis, and operation of a chemical process plant. They are also structured in a way 
that mirrors the workflow in a professional engineering project (where component tasks below 
are aligned with the example of the motivating assessment in Chemical Engineering): 
• Task 1: Equipment and Resource Schedule: In this task, students are expected to identify 
and list all the resources needed for the project. For non-engineering disciplines, this 
could be identifying the relevant literature, theories, or case studies needed for a 
research paper, or the materials and tools needed for an art project. The goal is to 
encourage students to plan ahead and consider all the resources they will need. 
• Task 2: Preliminary Design: This task involves creating a preliminary or 'shortcut' design of 
the project. In a non-engineering context, this could involve drafting an outline of a 
research paper, creating a storyboard for a video project, or sketching a preliminary 
design for an artwork. The aim is to encourage students to start thinking about the 
structure and flow of their project early on. 
• Task 3: Detailed Specifications: After the preliminary design, students are expected to 
provide detailed specifications. In a non-engineering context, this could mean 
specifying the methods to be used in a research paper, the script and shot list for a 
video project, or the colours, materials, and techniques to be used in an art project. This 
task encourages students to think in detail about how they will execute their project. 
• Task 4: Control Summary and Instrumentation Scheme: In the context of non-engineering 
disciplines, this task could involve planning the steps and timeline for completing the 
project, and identifying potential obstacles and how to address them. The goal is to 
encourage students to anticipate challenges and plan their work effectively. 
• Task 5: Presentation and Communication: In this task, students are expected to present 
their work in a clear, organised, and professional manner. This could involve writing a 
report, giving a presentation, or showcasing a piece of artwork. The aim is to develop 
students' communication skills and their ability to present their work effectively. 
• Task 6: Feedback and Revision: The final task involves revising and improving the work 
based on feedback. This encourages students to reflect on their work, learn from their 
mistakes, and continuously improve. This is a critical skill in any discipline, as it promotes a 
growth mindset and continuous learning. 
• Feedback: Consultation opportunities are provided regularly and systematically, ensuring that 
students have the opportunity to discuss and learn from written feedback in order to 
continuously improve their work. This allows students to understand how well they have met the 
task objectives and marking criteria, while providing instructors with the ability to discuss 
submissions with each student in order to establish academic integrity on a per-student level. 
The feedback covers both the technical aspects of the work (e.g., the accuracy and 
appropriateness of calculations and designs) and the presentation aspects (e.g., clarity, 
organisation, referencing). Feedback is also provided on group dynamics and collaboration, 
where additional consultation and dialogue may be necessary for groups demonstrating early 
signs of unprofessional conduct. The comprehensive feedback strategy in this assessment helps 
students understand their strengths and weaknesses in the context of teamwork, and provides 
guidance on how to improve their collaboration skills.  
