Academic Integrity Assurance through the Model 
The model incorporates a mix of individual and group work. This ensures individual accountability 
and discourages “free-riding,” as each student's contribution to the group work can be 
monitored and evaluated. The use of individual task submissions reenforces this point, such that 
each student develops and demonstrates their understanding and skills in the context of the 
assessment and cannot rely solely on the work of others. The model also requires regular task 
submissions, which discourages procrastination and last-minute work that can often lead to 
academic misconduct. The regular submissions also allow the instructor to monitor each 
student's progress and understanding, and to establish the academic integrity of each student’s 
contribution without the use of plagiarism detection tools. While each task requires the 
application of specific knowledge and skills, and the solutions are unique to the specifics of the 
plant design provided in the assignment, the breadth of the composite assessment activity 
makes it very difficult to plagiarise or use contract cheating.  
Varying Assessment Elements to Maintain Academic Integrity 
To further maintain academic integrity, the assessment model incorporates varying elements in 
the assignment each year. The project description, requirements, and specifics can be modified 
for each iteration of the unit, ensuring that each cohort of students has a unique project to work 
on. This not only discourages plagiarism from previous cohorts, but also ensures that the 
assignment remains fresh and challenging for each new group of students. The model can also 
incorporate random elements, such as different process parameters or design constraints, for 
different groups or students. This ensures that each group or student has a unique task, making it 
impossible to simply copy another's work. Furthermore, the model includes a mix of quantitative 
and qualitative tasks. The quantitative tasks, such as calculations and designs, have clear 
correct answers, making it easy to identify copying. The qualitative tasks, such as discussions and 
justifications, require personal input and critical thinking that is intimately connected to, and 
supported by, the quantitative aspects of the assessment delivery. The model also includes oral 
presentations or interviews as part of the assessment, as a capstone to establishing academic 
integrity on a per-student level (i.e., where the instructor is able to observe and note each 
student’s evolution across the semester, having had multiple consultations and feedback 
sessions). This provides an opportunity for the instructor to directly assess each student's 
understanding and contribution, and identify any inconsistencies between the submitted work 
and the student's knowledge. 
Student-Focussed Learning Design within the Model    
The model incorporates active learning strategies, such as problem-solving, application of 
theory to practice, and critical thinking. While the tasks are designed to be challenging but 
achievable, the nature of the assessment’s group work components encourage students to 
work together, share ideas, and focus on a domain of their preference (i.e., based on their self-
identified strengths or preferred career trajectory). Where necessary, tasks can be modified or 
supplemented during the semester to ensure that all students in the group are both challenged 
and supported in their learning. 
Equity, Diversity and Accessibility Support by the Model  
The mixture of individual and group provides opportunities for students to both demonstrate their 
expected professional competencies while also focussing on domains of their personal interest 
and strengths. Accessibility in this model is enhanced through the clarity of instructions and 
requirements, where modifications to timeline and work scope provide the ability to support 
