UniAccess Adjustment Plans (at UWA) on an individual level. Critically, the iterative consultation 
process at the core of the project ensures that students receive and can discuss regular 
feedback. 
Best Practices for Successful Implementation 
For successful implementation of this model, it is crucial to establish clear communication with 
students. This includes explaining the project's objectives, the tasks' requirements, and the 
marking criteria in detail. Regular updates and reminders can also be useful in keeping students 
on track. Feedback is a key component of this model, and should be provided promptly and 
constructively (in written form prior to consultation sessions, and through the dialogue in each 
session). This feedback helps to motivate and guide students in their learning, and is an 
important step to monitor group dynamics.  
Generative AI and Other Technology Considerations 
Technology plays a crucial role in this model. Students will require access to appropriate 
software for simulations and calculations, as well as for preparing their reports and presentations. 
It is important to ensure that all students have access to the necessary technology and are 
proficient in its use. Training or support should be provided as needed. As discipline-specific 
specialty software is likely to be required for successful delivery, the consideration of in-person vs. 
online access paths is critical to ensuring students are not disadvantaged by location. 
Chat-based generative AI tools may provide limited support during the very early stages of the 
project, where student groups are first bounding the problem to identify key areas of concern 
and focus. Where deployed, instructors should provide consultation with students (either directly 
or in a flipped classroom environment) to ensure these scoping boundaries are appropriate and 
comprehensive, and to help identify the point at which professional/detailed references are 
required. The limitations of generative AI in the professional discipline should be discussed with 
students (where deployed), to best understand any professional risks of using untrustworthy 
resources in their disciplines.  
Example Rubric – Submission 1 
This submission should contain:  
(i) 
A literature review on known processes that will perform the desired task with a focus on low 
carbon technologies.  
(ii) 
Selection of a process route based on technological maturity, economics, capacity limitations, 
environmental and safety issues (and others if you deem them relevant). 
(iii) 
Production of Draft 1 of mass and energy balances, a Process Flow Diagram (PFD) and 
stream tables for selected process route, and brief explanation of how you produced the 
mass and energy balances, and identifying key process parameters. 
(iv) 
construction of an INPUT/OUTPUT diagram listing all feed streams, product streams and 
waste streams. 
(v) 
Team planning – to include Gantt chart for rest of project and division of labour for selected 
process route.  
 
Parts (i) to (iii) MUST be less than 6000 words combined (not including diagrams, references, ToC, 
executive summary etc). (iv) to (vi) MUST not exceed 16 pages. This is a Team report and it is worth 15% 
of the total marks for the course.  
 
This is a group submission. 
 
