tly involve human"", ""L022"": ""participants, ethical review is required for research involving Aboriginal and Torres Strait"", ""L023"": ""Islander individuals, communities or groups, concerning their cultural connections to land,"", ""L024"": ""waters, natural resources, ecologies, cultural places, sites and landscapes."", ""L025"": ""3.5 Professional Responsibility in Research"", ""L026"": ""(A). Research must be designed to respect the welfare, safety, privacy and dignity of human"", ""L027"": ""subjects and to avoid unnecessary pain and suffering to animal subjects."", ""L028"": ""The University of Western uwa.edu.au/policy 4"", ""L029"": ""Australia"", ""L030"": ""Code of Conduct Integrity Academic""}","Appointments.
(G).
Visiting Appointment is defined in the University Policy on Visiting Appointments.
3.2
Intellectual Freedom in Research
(A).
Researchers will be free to select their own topics for Research, to seek support from any 
source for their work, and to formulate their own findings and conclusions.
3.3
Acknowledgement
(A).
Researchers engaged in fieldwork must behave in a manner that upholds and enhances the 
good reputation of the University, both in relation to the land in which they are conducting 
fieldwork and in their interactions with external parties.
(B).
Researchers must acknowledge the Indigenous owners of the land on which they work and 
ensure an appropriate level of Indigenous consultation and engagement when engaged in —
(1).
fieldwork; and/or
(2).
Research that impacts on, or is of particular significance to, Aboriginal and Torres Strait 
Islander peoples.
3.4
AIATSIS Code
(A).
Researchers must apply the Australian Institute of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander 
Studies Code of Ethics for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Research (AIATSIS Code) to all 
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People Research. This includes the planning, collection, 
analysis and dissemination of information or knowledge, in any format or medium, which is 
about and may affect Indigenous peoples both collectively and individually. According to the 
Research Code and regardless of whether the research intends to directly involve human 
participants, ethical review is required for research involving Aboriginal and Torres Strait 
Islander individuals, communities or groups, concerning their cultural connections to land, 
waters, natural resources, ecologies, cultural places, sites and landscapes.
3.5
Professional Responsibility in Research
(A).
Research must be designed to respect the welfare, safety, privacy and dignity of human 
subjects and to avoid unnecessary pain and suffering to animal subjects.
