5
Collaborative Research Across Institutions
(A).
Where the University is involved in a joint research project, including overseas, an 
agreement is to be reached in writing with the collaborating organisations detailing issues to 
do with intellectual property, confidentiality and copyright, sharing commercial returns, 
responsibility for ethics and safety clearances, and reporting to appropriate agencies.
(B).
Effective research data management ensures the responsible conduct of research. Best 
practices in research data management enhance research integrity and promote 
collaborative opportunities, and should be aligned to the FAIR Principles as expressed in 
section 8 of the Research Integrity Policy.
(C).
A Research Data Management Plan —
https://guides.library.uwa.edu.au/RDMtoolkit/planning 
ensures data security, accessibility and validation of results. For further assistance with 
research data management, please refer to the Research Data Management Toolkit —
https://guides.library.uwa.edu.au/RDMtoolkit
(D).
The collaborating parties are to identify the individual to be involved in the management of 
research data, primary materials and other items to be retained at the end of the project.
(E).
When a collaboration is international and involves military or dual use technology, even 
when for non-military aims, it may be necessary to obtain a Defence Trade Controls permit 
from the Commonwealth Department of Defence. Questions and support for this is available 
from the Office of Research.
