2
Topics for RIAs when advising (with resources)
(A).
Some of the following links will help RIAs in giving advice —
(1).
Research Development and Innovation (RDI)
http://rdi.uwa.edu.au/
(2).
RDI works with researchers to develop research opportunities, create impact from 
research, protect and commercialise intellectual property created at the University 
and also works with industry to facilitate collaborative research projects and access 
to new technologies.
(3).
Research Development Advisors (RDAs)
http://research.uwa.edu.au/staff/rdo 
(4).
RDAs understand the dynamics of the academic research environment and provides 
expert and professional support for researchers progressing their careers through 
an integrated framework of professional and career development opportunities and 
resources.
(5).
Research data and primary materials - Institutional Research Data Store (IRDS)
http://library.uwa.edu.au/research/irds 
(6).
The University’s IRDS provides researchers with a centralised, secure and UWA-
University of Western Australia | N057 | Source page 2
Bundle page 331 | N057 | source page 2 | line numbers at left","cts of interest, and management of research data and primary materials. 
(C).
Research Integrity Advisors can also advise about questionable research practices and the 
process of making an allegation of a breach of the codes of ethics and conduct. 
(D).
In essence, the RIAs are to —
(1).
provide advice to those with concerns around research conduct and potential 
complainants about the appropriate way forward;
(2).
provide guidance on relevant University processes and procedures; 
(3).
advise on issues relating to activities external to the University e.g. authorship on 
research papers.
(4).
promote good research practice rather than react to concerns; and
(5).
understand that the role of RIA is advisory, not investigatory or compliance oriented.
