The UWA Privacy office fulfils the role of Privacy Officer, under the Australian Privacy 
Principles, and Data Protection Officer under GDPR, it is responsible for —
(1).
University’s information Privacy policy and policy guideline
(2).
Guidance and advice on privacy obligations and responsibilities
(3).
Training and awareness for University staff of the management of personal 
information
(4).
managing enquiries, concerns or complaints about the University’s collection and 
management of personal information
(5).
managing requests for access to, or correction of personal information, and rights 
afforded under the GDPR
(6).
investigating suspected data breaches (in collaboration with UWA’s Cyber security 
team); and 
(7).
compliance reporting to the University’s Audit and Risk Committee. 
10
Breach of Policy
(A).
The University accepts that breaches of this policy may occur, and individuals whether 
employees or members of the University community should be able to report these, so they 
may be investigated.  Breaches of the policy may include —
(1).
failure to collect, use, or manage an individual’s or individuals’ Personal Information in 
accordance with the policy; or
(2).
where Personal Information has been disclosed in an eligible data breach.
10.2 Definition of an eligible data breach
(A).
An eligible breach is —
(1).
when there is an unauthorised access or disclosure of Personal Information and a 
reasonable person would conclude that the disclosure or access is likely to result in 
serious harm to those individuals affected; or
(2).
when information is lost in circumstances where unauthorised access or disclosure is 
likely to occur and assuming that unauthorised access or disclosure were to occur, a 
reasonable person would conclude that the disclosure or access is likely to result in 
serious harm to the affected individuals; and 
(3).
would qualify as a statutory eligible data breach under the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth).
(B).
An eligible data breach may also be as prescribed by other laws such as the General Data 
Protection Regulation, where it has applicability to the University and the Personal 
