Review and Appeal of HDR Decisions Policy 
Policy UP18/4 (F18/3011) 
 
Approver 
Academic Board  
Sponsor 
Chair of the Academic Board 
Owner 
Pro Vice-Chancellor (Research Training) 
Secretary 
Academic Secretary  
Policy Type 
Academic 
Policy Category 
Academic Governance 
 
1 
Purpose 
1.1 
This policy outlines the principles for review and appeal of HDR (Higher Degree by Research) 
decisions managed by the Graduate Research School.  
 
2 
Definitions 
In this policy and any associated procedures,  
Head of School includes any person acting in the capacity of head of school or authorised by the head of school 
to deal with a particular matter. 
HDR academic decision means a decision made with respect to management or administration of the 
candidate’s HDR course, normally under the University’s statutes, regulations, rules or policy. 
HDRCC means the Higher Degree by Research Candidature Committee, a standing committee of the Board of 
the Graduate Research School with delegated authority to make decisions in regards to candidature 
HDR candidate means a person enrolled in a HDR course of the University, that is managed by the Graduate 
Research School, including candidates who are undertaking a PhD jointly with another institution or in conjunction 
with a course for another award. 
the Board refers to the Board of the Graduate Research School. 
the University refers to The University of Western Australia, and any and all subsidiary or associated 
entities 
University working day means a weekday other than one that is specified as a University holiday. 
 
3 
Scope 
3.1 
The scope of this policy applies to all HDR candidates and units managing reviews and appeals.  
3.2 
This policy applies where a candidate is seeking a review of an academic decision unless another 
review process as set out in the Regulations for Student Conduct takes precedence. 
 
4 
General principles  
4.1 
The University is committed to conducting reviews and appeals of decisions in a fair, transparent and 
respectful manner that remains consistent with University rules, policies and procedures. 
4.2 
Candidates remain bound by the effect of the original decision while a review or appeal is being 
undertaken. 
4.3 
A request for a review of any decision specified in this policy must be initiated by the candidate in 
accordance with the time limits and requirements outlined in Schedule A.  
4.4 
The demonstrable grounds underpinning a review or an appeal of a decision is evidence-based. 
4.5 
A candidate has the right to be accompanied by a support person to any meetings requested as part 
of the review/appeal process. 
