3 
 
7.3 
The review and appeal process referred to in this policy do not apply to scholarships that are not 
controlled by the University. 
 
8 
Right of Appeal 
8.1 
A review of an academic decision precedes a candidate’s right to appeal. 
8.2 
Subject to 8.1, an appeal on any academic decision specified in this policy is considered by the 
University Appeals Committee of the Academic Board and is administered in accordance with 
Schedule B.  
8.3 
The Chair of the Appeals Committee has delegated authority to: 
(a) progress an appeal to the University Appeals Committee for a hearing at which the appellant, who 
may be accompanied by a support person, is asked to present their case; 
(b) summarily dismiss an appeal if the: 
i. 
appeal is made on grounds other than those specified in Schedule B;  
ii. 
review process specified in this policy and in the schedule has not taken place; or 
iii. 
appeal is considered to be frivolous or vexatious; 
(c) explore other means of resolution in cases where an agreeable negotiated solution is regarded as 
likely and/or a hearing by the Committee is regarded as disproportionate in time and cost. 
8.4 
The decision of the University Appeals Committee is final within the University, noting that: 
(a) the candidate does not have any further recourse relating to the outcome of the appeal to any 
authority within the University; and 
(b) the University does not engage in any further communication with the candidate or supporters on 
matters germane to the appeal unless required by any subsequent external process. 
8.5 
A candidate who is dissatisfied with the outcome of their appeal may refer their case for consideration 
by an external body (such as the Ombudsman Western Australia). 
 
 
