Student Complaint Resolution Policy 
Policy UP 07/98 (F45568-02) 
 
Approver 
Academic Board 
Sponsor 
Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Education and Student Experience) 
Owner 
Director, Student Life and Wellbeing 
Secretary 
Academic Secretary  
Policy Type 
Academic 
Policy Category 
Academic Governance 
1 
Purpose 
1.1 
This policy provides for students to make a complaint to the University and to appeal a complaint 
outcome.  It sets out the possible stages of the complaint process and explains a student’s right to 
appeal the outcome of a complaint.  
1.2 
This policy applies where a student makes a complaint about any matter in relation to their student 
experience, unless another process for resolution is prescribed in a specific Statute, regulation, rule or 
policy pertaining to the matter in question.   
2 
Definitions 
Term 
Definition 
complainant 
means a person, group or body who makes a complaint under this policy. 
complaint 
means an expression of dissatisfaction where a response is explicitly or 
implicitly expected. 
conflict of interest 
means a situation that has the potential to undermine the impartiality of a 
person due to a possible clash between their personal, professional or 
public interests. 
dismiss 
means to deny any further consideration of a complaint within the 
University, including the right to appeal. 
frivolous complaint 
means one that is trivial, superficial or senseless. 
good faith 
means a sincere intention to deal fairly with others and participate 
appropriately with the process. 
local procedure or schedule 
means a University-approved procedure, schedule or other instrument that 
supports the application of this policy to an offshore campus, transnational 
education arrangement or other approved delivery location, including where 
required to comply with applicable local laws or regulatory requirements. 
respondent 
means a person, group or body who is the subject of a complaint made 
under this policy. 
Responsible Officer 
is a role undertaken by an officer of the University, either by virtue of their 
position or by appointment by the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Education and 
Student Experience) or delegate, that involves considering and responding 
to a complaint. 
Senior Management 
Representative 
means the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Education and Student Experience), 
Chair of Academic Board, Deputy Vice-Chancellors, Pro Vice-Chancellors, 
Heads of Schools or members of the senior management group as 
determined by the Vice-Chancellor. 
serious matter 
is one which impacts, or has the potential to impact, on the health and 
safety of any person, the University’s reputation or University operations. 
student 
means a person who is enrolled at the University in a higher education 
course or an enabling course, or undertaking units through University 
Extension, other than on an audit-only basis. 
SCRC 
means the Student Complaints and Resolutions Centre. 
student representative 
means a person, group or body that is nominated by a student(s) or former 
student(s) to act as their representative or that makes a complaint under 
this policy in their capacity as a representative body. 
