2 
support person 
means a person who provides practical or emotional assistance to a person 
involved in a complaint process. 
The University 
Is The University of Western Australia,  
vexatious complaint 
means one that is made or pursued without reasonable grounds or made to 
harass or annoy, to cause delay or detriment, or for any other wrongful 
purpose. 
3 
Scope 
3.1 
The scope of this policy applies to all students.  
3.2 
This policy applies to students undertaking study through an offshore campus, transnational education 
arrangement or other approved delivery location, subject to any applicable local laws, regulatory 
requirements and University-approved local procedures or schedules. 
3.3 
This policy operates in conjunction with other governing instruments including, but not limited to, 
legislation, by-laws; and University Statutes, regulations, rules and policies. As such, the University 
may be required to: 
(a) alter its management of a complaint; 
(b) constrain the outcomes provided for a complaint; or 
(c) refer a complaint to another process for resolution. 
3.4 
 The University accepts complaints under this policy: 
(a) from a: 
i. 
student(s) or former student(s); or 
ii. 
student representative; and 
(b) in relation to the student experience which may extend to incidents, situations or activities that are 
not conducted on University premises or sites.  
3.5 
The Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Education and Student Experience) or their delegate may accept a 
complaint more than twelve (12) months following the incident, situation or activity that led to the 
complaint, if:  
(a) the complaint can, despite the delay, be reasonably considered; and 
(b) one of the following applies: 
i. 
the complainant can provide reasonable grounds for the delay; or 
ii. 
the complaint raises a serious matter. 
3.6 
A complaint made more than twelve (12) months following the incident, situation or activity that led to 
the complaint must be submitted to the SCRC. 
3.7 
The Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Education and Student Experience) or delegate may consider an 
anonymous complaint if: 
(a) it raises a serious matter; and 
(b) it can be reasonably considered. 
3.8 
Prior to submitting a complaint, potential complainants should consider the University’s guidance on 
anonymous complaints. 
3.9 
Complaints are to be made in writing and submitted through the complaint lodgement mechanism 
approved by the University. Where a complaint relates to an offshore campus or other approved 
delivery location, the University may specify additional or alternative lodgement channels in an 
approved local procedure or schedule. 
3.10 For UWA India, complaints may be referred to, or considered by, a local student grievance body 
where required by applicable law, regulation or an approved UWA India procedure. The University 
may approve local procedures or schedules to support the application of this policy at offshore 
campuses, transnational education locations or other approved delivery locations. Any local procedure 
or schedule must be consistent with this policy, unless a variation is required to comply with applicable 
local law or regulatory requirements. 
