3 
4 
General principles  
4.1 
The University is committed to conducting complaint resolution that is: 
(a) visible – information about the process is easy to access; 
(b) simple – the process is easy to understand and use; 
(c) timely – complaints are managed efficiently with consideration of urgency; 
(d) objective –complaints are considered and managed without bias; 
(e) fair – procedural fairness is afforded to all; 
(f) confidential – information is kept confidential, as appropriate; 
(g) effective – appropriate complaint outcomes are reached; and 
(h) informative – complaint information is used for the continuous improvement of University 
operations. 
4.2 
The University encourages the informal and early resolution of complaints. 
4.3 
The University determines the complaint process. 
4.4 
Parties to a complaint process must: 
(a) cooperate in good faith; 
(b) act in accordance with the UWA Code of Conduct; 
(c) respect the confidentiality of the complaint process; and 
(d) immediately disclose any actual, perceived or potential conflict of interest. 
4.5 
Parties to a complaint process must not be subjected to reprisals, discrimination or victimisation due to 
their involvement in the process. Any concerns regarding process or the conduct of a person(s) 
involved in a complaint process should be directed to the SCRC as soon as possible. In addition, staff 
must comply with their responsibilities under the Conflicts of Interest Policy. 
4.6 
Parties to a complaint process are advised of the support and representation options available to 
them. Students may seek support and representation from relevant University student support 
services or other approved student representative or support services. Staff who are the subject of a 
complaint, or who are involved in the handling of a complaint, may seek advice and support from 
relevant University staff support services, their Human Resources Business Partner, wellbeing 
support, or other applicable representative bodies. For offshore campuses or other approved delivery 
locations, local procedures may identify additional support services available to students and staff. 
5 
Complaint resolution stages 
5.1 
A person(s) should attempt, where possible and appropriate, to resolve their concerns directly with the 
respondent prior to making a complaint to a Responsible Officer under this policy.  
5.2 
A complaint process may comprise the following two stages, which must be conducted in accordance 
with the requirements in Appendix A. 
(a) Stage 1 – Informal Resolution; and 
(b) Stage 2 – Formal Resolution. 
5.3 
Stage 1 must precede Stage 2 unless the Stage 2 Responsible Officer is satisfied that: 
(a) the complaint raises a serious matter; or 
(b) there are valid reasons for not attempting Stage 1.  
5.4 
A Responsible Officer at Stage 1 and/or 2 may make or initiate inquiries, collect relevant information 
and exercise their own judgement, as appropriate, in an attempt to resolve a complaint. 
5.5 
All parties involved in a complaint process must comply with the procedural requirements in Appendix 
A, as applicable.  
6 
Right of Appeal 
6.1 
Formal complaint resolution under Stage 2 precedes a complainant’s right to appeal. 
