Introduction and Purpose 
The University of Western Australia (UWA) is a community that aspires to excellence in learning, discovery 
and service. We act with integrity, we value inclusion and equity, and we work together with care, kindness 
and respect. This Framework sets the foundation for conduct at UWA and provides a clear, consistent standard 
for how we learn, work and engage as one community. It affirms that leadership is expressed not only through 
formal roles, but through everyday behaviour, personal accountability and the way we treat one another. 
 
Values 
The UWA Values guide the character of our community and inform expectations of behaviour across all 
settings. They provide a common language for decisions, interactions and culture. 
• 
Excellence: we consistently pursue the highest levels of achievement, creating the best outcomes 
possible. 
• 
Integrity: we are honest and ethical, and we show respect for, and appreciate, one another and our 
communities, valuing our differences. 
• 
Innovation: we are constantly, and creatively, improving and adapting. 
• 
Collaboration: we share our collective intelligence to achieve more. 
• 
Equity: we are committed to providing everyone in our community equality of opportunity, experience 
and outcome. 
 
How the Code is Structured 
This Framework sets the overarching principles for conduct at UWA and is complemented by three annexures 
that apply in different contexts: 
• 
Annexure A: Staff Code of Conduct, behavioural standards for all employees and office holders 
acting for the University. 
• 
Annexure B: Student Code of Conduct, behavioural standards for all enrolled students. 
• 
Annexure C: Code for the Protection of Freedom of Speech and Academic Freedom, University 
wide principles and processes supporting lawful speech and academic freedom. 
 
Together, these instruments provide a coherent, fair and consistent framework for conduct across UWA. 
 
Scope 
This Framework applies to all members of the UWA community, including staff, students, contractors, office 
holders, affiliates, adjuncts, emeriti, volunteers, visiting scholars and any person engaged in University activity 
or using University resources. It applies on campus, online and off site, and wherever conduct has a relevant 
connection to the University. Where an individual holds more than one role, the applicable obligations depend 
on the role being performed at the time, and in some circumstances more than one annexure may apply. 
 
The following coverage table refers: 
 
