Principles of Conduct 
Three principles anchor everyday behaviour. They are the lens through which we lead, collaborate, resolve 
issues fairly and steward the University’s reputation. 
 
1. How we work side by side 
We treat one another with dignity, kindness and fairness. We foster a safe, inclusive community where people 
can participate with confidence, contribute openly and feel a sense of belonging. We welcome different 
perspectives and experiences and we uphold the right to rigorous academic inquiry conducted with care for 
others. 
  
We reject discrimination in all its forms. Behaviour that demeans, marginalises or harms others, including 
racism, antisemitism, Islamophobia, sexism, homophobia, transphobia, ableism, religious vilification or any 
other prejudicial conduct, has no place at UWA. We embrace diversity, protect the dignity of every individual 
and set clear expectations against behaviour that is hateful, hostile or exclusionary, ensuring our community 
remains safe, respectful and inclusive for all. 
 
2. How we communicate 
We communicate respectfully and constructively. We listen with care, speak with clarity and courtesy, 
acknowledge contributions, and address concerns early. We engage in discussion and debate in ways that 
strengthen trust and learning, including in digital spaces. 
 
3. How we conduct University business 
We meet our responsibilities with integrity and accountability. We manage risks, use resources prudently, 
follow laws and University policies, make decisions transparently, declare and manage conflicts, and act with 
good judgement in the best interests of the University and its people. 
 
Leadership and Community 
Every member of UWA contributes to the culture we experience. We expect leaders at all levels to model 
values, invite constructive challenge, protect dignity, encourage voice, and create conditions where students 
and staff can thrive. We expect all community members to show everyday leadership by acting with kindness, 
supporting others and calling attention to concerns in safe and appropriate ways. 
 
