3 
Scope 
3.1 
The scope of this Code applies to: 
(a) all students of the University including — 
(i) Undergraduates and postgraduates enrolled in a course undertaking coursework study; 
(ii) Students enrolled in non-award courses, including micro-credentials and extension courses, 
and other entities of the University; and 
(iii) Higher Degree by Research students. 
3.2 
Students who are also employed by the University must adhere to the Staff Code of Conduct when 
acting in their capacity as a member of staff.  
 
 
4 
General and Fundamental Student Rights and Responsibilities 
4.1 
Every student enrolled at UWA has the right: 
(a) to be recognised as an individual member of the University community, with all associated rights of 
access to University services and facilities;  
(b) to be treated fairly and ethically, with respect and dignity; 
(c) to study in a supportive and encouraging environment;  
(d) to pursue resolution when the individual student's rights referred to in this Code are in tension with 
the rights of others, under the rights of representation and grievance specified within the Code; 
(e) to assemble in a peaceful manner in the general open spaces of the campus;  
(f) to fair and transparent process, including procedural fairness, independent advocacy and support, 
proportionate and equitable outcomes, and expect clear explanations for decisions that affect them; 
(g) to access relevant evidence and an opportunity to respond, and to have the University’s records 
concerning them managed securely, with access limited to authorised persons and content 
restricted to information deemed relevant under University policy or legislation; 
(h) to protection against retrospective amendment of any statute, by-law, regulation, rule, policy or 
administrative guideline; 
(i) to have access to the statutes, regulations, rules and policies of the University which apply to them; 
(j) to be informed of the existence of this Code and to have adequate access to it; and 
(k) to expect the University to discharge its duty of care to the wellbeing of all students on campus or 
studying remotely. 
 
4.2 
Every student has the responsibility: 
(a) to conduct themselves in a manner conducive to the proper functioning of the University, recognising 
that a primary function of the University is the pursuit of academic excellence. 
(b) to recognise the rights of others, including the rights of every other student covered by this Code as 
well as the rights of staff and visitors;  
(c) to obey the laws of the land and the University’s land; 
(d) to respect and uphold principles of scholarly integrity; 
(e) to make themselves aware of and comply with statutes, regulations, rules and policies of the 
University which apply to them; 
(f) to ensure that their student record is accurate at all times; 
(g) to uphold the reputation of the University while engaged in University activities;  
(h) to use the University’s name, reputation, or crest responsibly and only with prior written permission;  
(i) to use and care for all University resources in a responsible and appropriate manner, mindful of the 
need for resources to be shared by all members of the University community. This includes 
buildings, equipment and grounds, the University libraries, information and communication 
technology resources, both on-campus and off-campus venues, including online and off-campus 
activities;  
(j) to keep their University student ID and passwords secure and must not disclose their password to 
anyone else;  
(k) not to purport to speak or act on behalf of the University unless explicitly authorised to do so;  
(l) to uphold the principle that antisemitism has no place at the University and ensure all students can 
learn and participate free from hate, intimidation, or vilification. While students may advocate 
lawfully, they must not target or harass Jewish individuals or groups, and must not engage in any 
form of discriminatory, hateful, or prejudicial behaviour, including antisemitism, racism, sexism, 
homophobia, Islamophobia, transphobia, or religious vilification; 
(m)  to contribute to a safe, respectful, and inclusive University community by treating all staff and 
students with courtesy and fairness; avoiding discrimination, harassment, bullying, hostility, or any 
form of prejudicial behaviour; refraining from sexual harassment or inappropriate conduct in any 
medium; and ensuring their actions support the health, safety, and wellbeing of themselves and 
other members of the University Community. They are expected to be fit for study, follow relevant 
