nd monitor their"", ""L019"": ""own progress within the teaching-learning environment;"", ""L020"": ""(e) to understand that despite all efforts to promote successful teaching and learning outcomes, student"", ""L021"": ""work may still not reach the standard required to pass a unit;"", ""L022"": ""(f) to recognise the fundamental principle of academic freedom in accordance with the UWA Code for"", ""L023"": ""the protection of academic freedom and freedom of speech, and to seek to safeguard this principle"", ""L024"": ""and freedom;"", ""L025"": ""(g) to adhere to the principles of ethical scholarship and academic integrity during the course of their"", ""L026"": ""studies at UWA;"", ""L027"": ""(h) to contribute fair and honest feedback on the teaching and supervision they experience when"", ""L028"": ""requested to do so; and"", ""L029"": ""(i) to display respect by behaving in a manner that will not unreasonably impair the freedom of others"", ""L030"": ""to pursue their studies or work and to participate in life at the University."", ""L031"": ""9 Student Representation"", ""L032"": ""9.1 Every student has the right:"", ""L033"": ""(a) to have their opinion represented through the Student Guild on all matters affecting students."", ""L034"": ""(b) to representation, where possible, on major decision-making bodies of the University either through"", ""L035"": ""direct election or by nomination through a recognised student body; and"", ""L036"": ""(c) to have the right, notwithstanding the existence of formal representation, to convey personal or"", ""L037"": ""collective opinion to the Vice-Chancellor."", ""L038"": ""10 Student Grievances, Appeals and Complaints"", ""L039"": ""10.1 Every student has the right:"", ""L040"": ""(a) to be able to appeal or register a complaint or grievance against any academic assessment,"", ""L041"": ""academic procedure, administrative procedure, perceived discrimination or harassment, or the"", ""L042"": ""abrogation of any right stated in this Code;"", ""L043"": ""(b) to a clearly-documented process for dealing with a review and an appeal, complaint or grievance"", ""L044"": ""which is confidential, non-discriminatory, unbiased, and accountable; offers the opportunity for them"", ""L045"": ""to be accompanied to hearings by a person of their choice; and leads to resolution within a"", ""L046"": ""reasonable time;"", ""L047"": ""(c) in respect to an appeal, complaint or grievance, to receive reasons for resulting decisions; to be"", ""L048"": ""advised of opportunity for further appeal; and to experience no repercussions as a result of"", ""L049"": ""participation in the process; and"", ""L050"": ""(d) to seek guidance from a Student Assist Officer, school staff, and/or an Equity and Diversity Adviser"", ""L051"": ""as appropriate when submitting an appeal, complaint or grievance."", ""L052"": ""10.2 Every student has the responsibility to familiarise themselves and comply with the review, appeal,"", ""L053"": ""complaint or grievance procedures."", ""L054"": ""11 Breaches of the Student Code of Conduct"", ""L055"": ""11.1 Students who breach the Student Code of Conduct will be subject to disciplinary action according to the"", ""L056"": ""Regulations for Student Conduct and Discipline."", ""L057"": ""4""}","4 
 
(e) to have their intellectual property and copyright recognised;  
(f) to expect the University to provide a safe and accessible environment, including facilities, equipment 
and grounds; 
(g) to transparency regarding agreements and funding arrangements, between the University and any 
other entity, which might inappropriately impact on a unit or course in which they are enrolled, or 
research project on which they are engaged; and 
(h) to have the opportunity to contribute feedback on the teaching and supervision they experience and 
to make suggestions for future course planning and to do so under full protection of academic 
freedom. 
 
8.2 
Every student has the responsibility: 
(a) to bring an open and enquiring mind and enthusiasm to their studies;  
(b) to participate actively in the teaching and learning and research environment, in particular by 
attending classes as required, complying with workload expectations, and submitting required work 
on time;  
(c) to be aware of all related policies, unit or subject information made available to them and to raise 
any questions or concerns with the appropriate staff member in a timely manner, also to make their 
learning needs known to appropriate staff members in a timely manner; 
(d) to take an active part in the management of their enrolment with the University and monitor their 
own progress within the teaching-learning environment; 
(e) to understand that despite all efforts to promote successful teaching and learning outcomes, student 
work may still not reach the standard required to pass a unit; 
(f) to recognise the fundamental principle of academic freedom in accordance with the UWA Code for 
the protection of academic freedom and freedom of speech, and to seek to safeguard this principle 
and freedom;  
(g) to adhere to the principles of ethical scholarship and academic integrity during the course of their 
studies at UWA;  
(h) to contribute fair and honest feedback on the teaching and supervision they experience when 
requested to do so; and 
(i) to display respect by behaving in a manner that will not unreasonably impair the freedom of others 
to pursue their studies or work and to participate in life at the University. 
 
9 
Student Representation 
9.1 
Every student has the right: 
(a) to have their opinion represented through the Student Guild on all matters affecting students. 
(b) to representation, where possible, on major decision-making bodies of the University either through 
direct election or by nomination through a recognised student body; and 
(c) to have the right, notwithstanding the existence of formal representation, to convey personal or 
collective opinion to the Vice-Chancellor. 
 
10 
Student Grievances, Appeals and Complaints  
10.1 Every student has the right: 
(a) to be able to appeal or register a complaint or grievance against any academic assessment, 
academic procedure, administrative procedure, perceived discrimination or harassment, or the 
abrogation of any right stated in this Code; 
(b) to a clearly-documented process for dealing with a review and an appeal, complaint or grievance 
which is confidential, non-discriminatory, unbiased, and accountable; offers the opportunity for them 
to be accompanied to hearings by a person of their choice; and leads to resolution within a 
reasonable time; 
(c) in respect to an appeal, complaint or grievance, to receive reasons for resulting decisions; to be 
advised of opportunity for further appeal; and to experience no repercussions as a result of 
participation in the process; and 
(d) to seek guidance from a Student Assist Officer, school staff, and/or an Equity and Diversity Adviser 
as appropriate when submitting an appeal, complaint or grievance. 
 
10.2 Every student has the responsibility to familiarise themselves and comply with the review, appeal, 
complaint or grievance procedures. 
 
11 
Breaches of the Student Code of Conduct  
11.1 Students who breach the Student Code of Conduct will be subject to disciplinary action according to the 
Regulations for Student Conduct and Discipline.  
