This is a UWA Official document not authorised for public access. 
4 
Delegations 
4.1 
The Senate is the ultimate authority for all University duties, powers and functions. It may delegate its 
duties, powers and functions (except its powers in relation to the making of statutes, regulations and 
by-laws) to — 
(a) any Senate Member; or 
(b) a committee, council or other body of the University; or 
(c) any officer who is an employee of the University, unless otherwise authorised by the Senate. 
4.2 
The Vice-Chancellor, as the executive officer of the University, has the powers and duties as may be 
prescribed by or under the UWA Act.  
4.3 
The Senate may authorise a Delegate to further delegate (sub-delegate) the power, authority, duty or 
function to a person or body. 
4.4 
The Vice-Chancellor may sub-delegate their delegated power, authority, duty or function either 
unilaterally or when expressly permitted by the Senate. 
5 
Exercising Delegations 
5.1 
Delegates must at all times- 
(a) act in the best interests of the University; 
(b) act within the scope of their responsibilities; 
(c) comply with University policies and procedures; 
(d) not exercise authority where a conflict exists; 
(e) exercise authority within available budgets and resources. 
5.2 
Delegations must be formally recorded, and be transparent and accessible to Senate, University 
Committees, and relevant members of the University Community 
5.3 
Delegations must — 
(a) reflect the organisational and hierarchical structure of the University; and 
(b) be in writing in accordance with the relevant Delegations Procedure. 
5.4 
Persons and entities engaged by the University who are not Employees of the University cannot 
exercise delegated authority on behalf of the University unless authorised in a document such as a 
Power of Attorney. 
5.5 
Delegations apply — 
(a) to a committee, not the individual Committee Members; and 
(b) to a position, not the person occupying the position. 
5.6 
The person or body who made a Delegation may, at any time — 
(a) exercise the Delegation; or 
(b) in writing, revoke or vary the Delegation. 
5.7 
The Senate may exercise all of the powers, authorities, duties, functions or commitments of the 
University, regardless of all Delegations. 
5.8 
The principles applying to Delegations also apply to sub-delegations. 
6 
Delegations Accountability 
6.1 
Delegates will be responsible and accountable for the decisions they make while exercising their 
Delegations. Delegates will be liable to account, to the person or body who made the Delegation, for 
how that Delegation is carried out.  
6.2 
The person or body who made a Delegation remains accountable for the decision or action of the 
Delegate or sub-delegate (as relevant). 
