Step 1.1 Define Scope 
(A).
As the risk management process may be applied at different levels (e.g. strategic, 
operational, programme, project, or other activities), it is important to be clear about 
the scope under consideration, the relevant objectives to be considered and their 
alignment with organisational objectives. 
(B).
When planning the approach, considerations include —
(1).
objectives and decisions that need to be made; 
(2).
outcomes expected from the steps to be taken in the process; 
(3).
time, location, specific inclusions and exclusions; 
(4).
appropriate risk assessment tools and techniques; 
(5).
resources required, responsibilities and records to be kept; and 
(6).
relationships with other projects, processes and activities. 
Step 1.2 Establish internal and external context 
(A).
The context of the risk management process should be established from the 
understanding of the external and internal environment in which the organisation 
operates and should reflect the specific environment of the activity to which the risk 
management process is to be applied. Consideration should be given to the internal 
and external factors that may impact the achievement of the activity or objective. 
(B).
The following questions may assist when establishing the context —
(1).
what are the objectives and strategic priorities of the Business Unit? 
(2).
what are the key activities for the year? 
