ime) —"", ""L022"": ""(1). the UWA Act;"", ""L023"": ""(2). other relevant legislation including, but not limited, to the University of Western"", ""L024"": ""Australia Statute No 1, University regulations and by-laws;"", ""L025"": ""(3). Instruments of Delegation; and"", ""L026"": ""(4). other formal instruments including enterprise agreements and employment"", ""L027"": ""contracts."", ""L028"": ""(C). Instrument of Delegation means the approved document (as it may be amended from time"", ""L029"": ""to time) used to express the powers, authorities, duties, functions or commitments an"", ""L030"": ""individual (or entity) is authorised to carry out on behalf of the Senate or Vice-Chancellor and"", ""L031"": ""includes —"", ""L032"": ""(1). University Committee constitutions;"", ""L033"": ""(2). Matters Retained by the Senate;"", ""L034"": ""University of Western Australia"", ""L035"": ""uwa.edu.au/policy"", ""L036"": ""3""}","(C).
The Senate, which has “the entire control and management of the affairs and concerns of 
the University”,  is the ultimate authority for all University duties, powers and functions.  (s13 
of the University of Western Australia Act 1911 (‘UWA Act’)).
(D).
The Senate may delegate its duties, powers and functions (except its powers in relation to 
the making of statutes, regulations and by-laws) to —
(1).
any Senate Member; or
(2).
a committee, council or other body of the University; or
(3).
any officer of the University (for example, the Vice-Chancellor),
(s16G(1) UWA Act)
(E).
The Senate may authorise a Delegate to further delegate (sub-delegate) the delegated 
power, authority, duty or function to a person or body (s16G(3) UWA Act).
(F).
The Vice-Chancellor as the executive officer of the University, has the powers and duties, as 
may be prescribed by or under the UWA Act (s27(2)). 
(G).
The Vice-Chancellor may sub-delegate their delegated power, authority, duty or function in 
accordance with s27(3) UWA Act or, when expressly permitted by Senate, under s16(G)(3) 
UWA Act.
4
Delegations Framework
(A).
The Delegations Framework outlined in this Policy supports the University's governance 
arrangements, objectives and principles for decision-making that align responsibility, 
accountability and capability in relation to Delegations.
(B).
The Delegations Framework means (as they may be amended from time to time) —
(1).
the UWA Act;
(2).
other relevant legislation including, but not limited, to the University of Western 
Australia Statute No 1, University regulations and by-laws;
(3).
Instruments of Delegation; and 
(4).
other formal instruments including enterprise agreements and employment 
contracts.
(C).
Instrument of Delegation means the approved document (as it may be amended from time 
to time) used to express the powers, authorities, duties, functions or commitments an 
individual (or entity) is authorised to carry out on behalf of the Senate or Vice-Chancellor and 
includes —
(1).
University Committee constitutions;
(2).
Matters Retained by the Senate;
