external legislative requirements.
(C).
The exercise of any Delegation must be with due consideration for the best interests of the 
University.
(D).
Delegations must not be exercised if to do so would cause a breach of the Conflicts of 
Interest of Policy.
(E).
Unless specified, the exercise of any Delegation is restricted —
(1).
by the Constitution, or to the portfolio or Business Unit, of the Delegate; and
(2).
the financial and resourcing limits of the portfolio or Business Unit of the Delegate.
(F).
Unless specified,  University Officers formally acting in a position may exercise any 
Delegation attached to that position.
(G).
Unless otherwise determined by the person or body who made the Delegation, if a 
committee or position is abolished or changed, the Delegation is inherited by the principal 
successor to the functions of that committee or position.
(H).
A Delegate is not obliged to exercise a Delegation if the Delegate considers that there are 
special or unusual circumstances which make it sensible for the matter to be considered at a 
more senior level.
(I).
The principles applying to Delegations also apply to sub-delegations.
