Originator and adaptor of Copyright work on whether the"", ""L025"": ""authorship is to be acknowledged and if so, the form of that acknowledgement."", ""L026"": ""(C). Authors of Copyright works consent to waive their moral rights when required to comply with"", ""L027"": ""the terms of an agreement, government tender or compulsion of law."", ""L028"": ""University of Western Australia"", ""L029"": ""uwa.edu.au/policy"", ""L030"": ""5""}","8
DVC determination and delegation
(A).
A Deputy Vice-Chancellor may determine the University’s position on issues of authorship, 
creation and invention of IP and may delegate these determinations.
(B).
A Deputy Vice-Chancellor may protect and Commercialise IP owned by or licensed to the 
University and may delegate as required.
(C).
A decision to initiate litigation under this Policy resides with the Vice-Chancellor.
9
IP disclosure
(A).
Originators and Contributors of IP are encouraged to:
(1).
inform the appropriate Deputy Vice-Chancellor;
(2).
identify any co-owners of the IP; and
(3).
advise whether the co-owners wish the University to assist in the Commercialisation of 
the IP.
(B).
A Deputy Vice-Chancellor may request that Originators and Contributors:
(1).
execute any document;
(2).
do anything reasonably required by the University,
(3).
to prove ownership of IP to third parties, secure IP protection or assist the University to 
Commercialise IP.
10
Commercialisation of IP
(A).
A Deputy Vice-Chancellor may Commercialise IP owned by or licensed to the University in a 
manner consistent with this Policy and the Intellectual Property Commercialisation Guidelines.
11
Moral rights
(A).
Where the University uses IP created by an Originator, it will take reasonable steps to consult 
with the Originator before modifying or adapting that IP.
(B).
The University will agree with the Originator and adaptor of Copyright work on whether the 
authorship is to be acknowledged and if so, the form of that acknowledgement.
(C).
Authors of Copyright works consent to waive their moral rights when required to comply with 
the terms of an agreement, government tender or compulsion of law.
