eted within a semester or trimester."", ""L061"": ""2""}","(3) foundational unit offered at the degree level only to enhance the depth of knowledge;
(4) elective, where a student may choose freely from among the units available in the University 
subject to the rules that govern the structure of their course and to any unit rules; 
(5) unattached elective, where it is not part of a major sequence of any undergraduate degree course; 
(6) a special unit, which is created in limited circumstances in an undergraduate or postgraduate 
coursework course for one teaching period only, is approved only through a ‘Fast Track’ process 
by the relevant board(s). The creation of a special unit may occur in one of the following 
exceptional circumstances:
(a) Category 1: to take advantage of an unforeseen circumstances/opportunity, such as the 
expertise of a visiting academic; or
(b) Category 2: to cater for the needs of an individual student or a group of students 
(i)
in a transition period where a unit previously offered is no longer available; 
(ii) where a student who is transferring from one course to another course may have 
completed work previously that is not in exact alignment with a required unit or units; or 
(iii) where incoming exchange or study abroad students need to undertake a research project.
4.2
A special unit, which must have comparable academic standards to a unit of an equivalent level 
approved for offering on an ongoing basis, must not be a core unit unless approved by the relevant 
board(s). The content of a Special unit may be entirely new or may include content from an existing 
approved unit or units, and the special unit must not have the same title as an active unit approved for 
offering on an ongoing basis.
4.3
A coursework unit created to serve in one of the functions set out in 4.1 may, where applicable:
(1) be a dissertation unit, in accordance with the University Policy on Courses: Coursework 
Dissertation; 
(2) be a thesis unit, in accordance with the University Policy on Courses: Research Thesis; 
(3) be designed as a project unit, which may also include folios;
(4) be designed for experiential learning purposes, in accordance with the University Policy on 
Courses: Experiential Learning;
(5) be offered as a substitution unit:
(a) only in limited situations where a student is enrolled before 2012 in a Postgraduate 
Coursework course which is being taught out; or under exceptional circumstances and 
assessed on a case-by-case basis by the school for a student enrolled in a Postgraduate 
Coursework course. The exceptional circumstances in which substitution of units may be 
permitted, but not limited to, the following: 
(i)
where the student has a specific interest in an area of study that would complement the 
course, but is not currently offered within it; 
(ii) the student has previously completed a unit of similar content; 
(iii) there is a limited range of units available; or 
(iv) a unit is not available.
(b) subject to the school ensuring that: 
(i)
the substitution of unit does not impact on the integrity and distinctiveness of the course; 
(ii) a student may be permitted to substitute units to a maximum value of 12 credit points in 
that course with another unit or units of equivalent value; and 
(iii) where relevant, a student may be permitted to substitute conversion units up to a 
maximum value of 24 credit points in that course for other units relevant to the course or 
equivalent as approved by the school. 
(6) be offered for micro-credentialing purposes in accordance with the University Policy on: Mico-
credentials.
4.4
The standard credit point value of a coursework unit is six points, which must be designed for a 
student to be able to complete within a teaching period in accordance with the University Policy on 
Teaching Periods. Units including thesis, dissertation, research project, clinical, or a folio component 
may have greater than six credit points. With the exception of units offered in micro-credential 
programs, these units are normally broken down into parts (multiples of six enrolled points) for 
administrative purposes to: 
(1) enable them to be undertaken over two (or more) semesters, where applicable; 
(2) allow student enrolment load to be distributed evenly over semesters, where appropriate; and 
(3) accommodate both full and part-time students. The results for parts of units are recorded as AC 
(Assessment Continuing) except for the final part, for which the result is the overall result for the 
unit. Other than the units specified above, approval must be sought from the Academic Council or 
delegated authority for any unit that is proposed to be offered with a credit point value greater than 
six points, provided the unit can be completed within a semester or trimester. 
