A coursework unit must be created in accordance with procedure 1 as set out in Appendix A. The 
creation of a coursework unit, which is approved by the relevant board(s) through the University 
Committee system,
(1) ensures that its content is developed by the relevant and recognised disciplinary expertise within 
the organisational entity with. Recognised disciplinary expertise is demonstrated by a school's: 
(a) historical specialisation in the discipline; 
(b) tradition of teaching in the discipline or closely related areas, at both introductory and 
advanced levels and/or 
(c) history of scholarly research with demonstrable outcomes in the discipline. School(s) with the 
appropriate disciplinary expertise normally contribute to the teaching of units in the discipline 
concerned;
(d) recruitment of new staff with distinct disciplinary expertise.
(2) includes an assessment of the viability of the unit. The efficient and effective use of resources 
requires that schools considering offering units that duplicate disciplinary expertise held elsewhere 
consider both academic and resource implications, such as: 
(a) whether there are existing units that are appropriate; 
(b) the relationship of the unit to the major; 
(c) the school's relationship to the discipline; 
(d) the advantages/disadvantages to students of a unit offering; 
(e) the potential costs of competing with other schools for students; 
(f)
the costs to staff within the school; 
(g) the potential student cohort size attracted to the unit;
(h) cost of newly required specialised teaching facilities; and 
(i)
implications for student enrolment in any similar pre-existing units. 
4.6
Responsibility for avoiding / minimising duplication of unit content and teaching effort lies with Schools 
delivering units and schools or boards administering courses. A proposer developing a unit must 
engage in and document appropriate consultation to minimise duplication of unit content (e.g. 
consultation with the unit coordinators or Heads of School wherein units with duplicative content 
reside).
4.7
Duplication of unit content may be minimised through:
(1) collaborative teaching which may occur where a unit has a focus that spans different disciplines 
and only when the highest pedagogical standards can be applied; and/or where it is recognised 
that more than one school has teaching expertise in a discipline, albeit with a different focus or 
context. Mutual responsibilities of schools in a collaborative teaching arrangement include:
(a) meeting all those obligations of a school teaching any unit;
(b) carrying out and sharing unit evaluations being responsive to feedback; and
(c) consulting with collaborators in relation to any proposed change to the unit; and/or
(2) service teaching which may be negotiated when the content of a new unit proposed by a school 
lies within the disciplinary expertise of another school. A service teaching arrangement must be 
negotiated in line with procedure 2 as set out in Appendix A and undertaken in a way that is 
beneficial to the schools, the course and the students affected. The University recognises that a 
service teaching arrangement can occur in several forms, which include but are not limited to the 
following: 
(a) a generic unit controlled, designed, and fully service taught by the provider; 
(b) a unit controlled, and fully service taught by the provider but customised with involvement from 
the receiver to provide a relevant and appropriate context of the discipline, examples, and 
applications. 
4.8
Where duplication of units is identified within a school, the Head of the School must consult with the 
disciplines concerned to resolve any issue(s). Where duplication of units is identified between schools, 
the Heads of Schools are encouraged to resolve inter-school disputes without recourse to intervention 
by the University. Intra-school or inter-school consultations must occur within the context of the 
University's academic and resource policies and processes (See Procedure 3 in Appendix A).
5
Part 3: Changes to Coursework Units 
5.1
An active coursework unit may be:
(1) made available, when a unit that was once made unavailable becomes available for new 
enrolments;
