a future date;
(3) rescinded, when it is no longer offered.
5.2
Changes to an active coursework unit must only be made where there are sound academic or 
administrative reasons. The proposal must ensure that potential impact on students is minimised; high 
standards of teaching are maintained; efficient delivery of academic teaching is observed; and 
relevant legislation is complied with. 
5.3
The following changes require consultation and approval by the relevant board(s); as such they must 
be planned and submitted on an annual basis in accordance with the University's specified procedures 
and associated deadlines:
5.3.1
Changes that must be approved by the relevant board through the University Committee 
system:
(1) Changing the level of a unit whereby: 
(a) a change to the level of a unit requires the submission of a new unit proposal for consideration 
and approval by the relevant board(s) through the University committee system, and a 
proposal to rescind the former unit;
(2) Rescission of a unit must be justified by circumstance on a case-by-case basis, including but not 
limited to: 
(a) the content of the unit is outdated; 
(b) the unit is no longer required; 
(c) the learning outcomes of the unit can be achieved by another unit; 
(d) there is no academic expertise available to teach the unit; 
(e) the unit is part of a course or major which is no longer being taught; or 
(f)
the relevant content of a unit has been subsumed into a new unit.
5.3.2
Changes that may be approved by the Head of School or delegate (as delegated by the 
Academic Board):
(1) Changing unit learning outcomes may be approved by the Head of School or delegate in the 
following situations: 
(a) Where units are part of an undergraduate major or a postgraduate course, any change to the 
learning outcomes of a unit must be aligned to the learning pedagogy and assessment of the 
undergraduate major or postgraduate course; and in the case of an undergraduate unit, 
consistent with the knowledge paradigm associated with the undergraduate degree in which 
the unit is made available. 
(b) Where a change to unit learning outcomes is proposed for a unit within an undergraduate 
major/course or a postgraduate coursework course, it must be mapped to the learning 
outcomes of the undergraduate major/course or postgraduate course; and the assessment 
must be tailored to the changed learning outcomes. 
(c) A change to the learning outcomes for a unit which is part of an undergraduate major or a 
postgraduate coursework course may be approved where there are proposed changes to the:
(i)
outcomes of the undergraduate major or postgraduate course to which the unit belongs;
(ii) structure of the undergraduate major or postgraduate course to which the unit belongs; 
(iii) academic content of the unit (approval delegated to School governenance 
arrangements);
(iv) status of a unit in the case of unattached electives.
(2) Making changes to mode of teaching that affect ESOS compliance 
(a) The Head of School or delegate may approve changes to the mode of teaching of a unit 
where there are sound academic or administrative reasons, such as: 
(i)
improving unit outcomes, 
(ii) more efficient or effective teaching; 
(iii) changes in student enrolment; 
(iv) availability of teaching expertise; or 
(v) unforeseen circumstances. 
(b) Approval for a change to the mode of teaching of a unit must be considered in light of the 
University's compliance with the ESOS Act and in particular the need to ensure that at least 
66% of the course is taught face-to-face in the case of international students holding student 
visas;
