(ii) Level 1 units must be available to all students for whom enrolment in the unit would not 
breach any rules concerning pre-requisite or co-requisite or incompatible units or any 
rules relating to the student’s course (for instance a maximum of 12 Level 1 units is 
permitted in an undergraduate course). This principle allows commencing undergraduate 
students to explore a range of disciplines before they have to nominate their degree-
specific major.
(iii) Except as provided under (iv) and (v), Level 2 and Level 3 units must be available to all 
students for whom enrolment in the unit does not breach any rules concerning pre-
requisite or co-requisite or incompatible units or any rules relating to the student’s course 
(for instance, a student must take at least four Level 3 units for meeting the requirements 
of an undergraduate degree course rules).
(iv) Where there are sound practical reasons to restrict enrolment in a unit, such as the need 
for specialised resources, the availability of facilities, or limited laboratory space, co-
requisite rules may be used to restrict enrolment in Level 2 or Level 3 units to students 
who are seeking to complete a particular major.
(v) In exceptional circumstances, with the approval of the Academic Council on the 
recommendation of the relevant board(s), enrolment in Level 2 or Level 3 units may be 
restricted by rules that require students to have completed a particular special 
requirement, have a special aptitude and/or have attained a particular standard, such as 
achieving a particular course Grade Point Average (GPA) or course Weighted Average 
mark (WAM).
(vi) Level 4 and Level 5 units offered in postgraduate coursework courses must be 
undertaken in accordance with the relevant course rules.
(5) Imposing a quota on a unit:
(a)
Entry to units is not restricted by way of quotas where avoidable. Where high numbers pose a 
challenge, Heads of School are expected to make flexible arrangements wherever possible, 
such as scheduling repeat classes and considering alternative modes of delivery.
(b)
Student quotas may only be imposed on enrolments in specific units when specific relevant 
conditions warrant their imposition. These conditions normally fall within one of the following 
categories:
(i)
constrained specialist learning spaces (e.g. laboratory or studio space);
(ii) restricted student placements (e.g. clinical training placements); 
(iii) supervision of dissertations, theses, or off-campus fieldwork; or 
(iv) occupational health and safety considerations.
(c)
Unit quotas are not normally applied to Level 1 units or core units in any course. The impact of 
imposing a quota must be considered and take into account students’ ability to progress 
through their course(s) in a timely manner, as well as the ability of students to meet pre-
requisites and co-requisites and to complete their courses and graduate without delay.
(d)
A quota cannot be imposed on a unit any later than the opening of enrolments for the relevant 
academic year.
(e)
The relevant board(s) may approve the introduction or change of a quota.
(6) Making units unavailable in a calendar year: 
(a) The Head of School may approve a request for a unit to be made unavailable in a calendar 
year where there are unforeseen circumstances (e.g. damage to facilities or equipment); the 
minimum enrolment numbers as advertised have not been reached; or the unit is only taught 
in alternate years. 
(b) A unit must not be made unavailable in a calendar year where the proposed unavailability 
unduly impacts students' ability to undertake the relevant requirements of an undergraduate 
