5.5
The following changes may be approved, as required, by the Head of School or delegate:
(1) changing the Unit Coordinator, where there are sound academic or administrative reasons for 
doing so; or
(2) changing the number of contact hours. Any proposed change to contact hours must not 
contravene the requirement that a six-credit unit represents 150 hours of student workload. The 
school may approve a change to the number of contact hours where it will result in improved 
teaching, more effective achievement of student learning outcomes, or greater efficiency. If the 
number of contact hours is to be reduced, the change must not result in any reduction in the 
standard of teaching or compromising of the learning outcomes.
6
Part 4: Selecting Teaching Modes 
6.1
The University recognises that there are different teaching modes which include but are not limited to 
the following: 
(1) face-to-face learning mode, including online learning component; 
(2) online learning where teaching and student learning occur entirely online to facilitate several 
circumstances including distance learning education. Where online delivery modes are used the 
students concerned must have access to the necessary technology. In modes that require the use 
of printed materials students must have the appropriate access. The communication of digital 
material is subject to the Commonwealth of Australia Copyright Act 1968 (and amendments);
6.2
All teaching modes are subject to equal application of the relevant University policy. An effective 
teaching mode considers how technologies are used in conjunction with other delivery methods to 
broaden the range of teaching tools to facilitate teaching in different learning situations. 
6.3
The choice of Teaching Modes is underpinned by principles that recognise that: 
(1) there is a plurality of student needs and circumstances; 
(2) student expectations may change in line with their diverse and transforming backgrounds; 
(3) technical systems and networks must be reliable and robust; 
(4) technology extends the range of tools available for teaching; 
(5) technology enables new, more effective ways of teaching and learning; and 
(6) the plurality of tools enables teaching that more closely matches the plurality of student needs and 
expectations.
6.4
The most effective teaching tool depends on an assessment of the characteristics and distinctive 
needs of the student group which includes but are not limited to: 
(1) live in-person (such as teacher-led classroom teaching, hands-on labs, coaching/mentoring, on-
the-job training, fieldwork); 
(2) synchronous virtual collaboration (such as live digital learning, digital chat sessions, conference 
calls, video-conferencing); 
(3) asynchronous virtual collaboration (such as digital discussion boards, and blogs); and (iv) self-
paced asynchronous (such as digital tutorials, simulations, podcasts). The effectiveness of a unit, 
its teaching mode, and any change to the teaching mode must be reviewed three years after its 
introduction.
6.5
The University encourages staff to make informed decisions when selecting from the full range of 
available teaching modes those that provide a learning experience that is student-centered, 
appropriate, and effective. A student-centered learning experience: 
(1) takes into account diverse student characteristics shaped by their learning attitude and outlook 
and study orientation; 
