6.6
Teaching modes must be selected appropriately according to: 
(1) the mode most appropriate to the learning experience to be delivered and selected from a wide 
range including visual, hearing, reading, writing, and kinaesthetic; and
(2) student locations whereby, while the majority of the UWA student body studies at the Crawley 
campus, significant numbers are at other sites within Perth, some are at remote locations in the 
State, and others are located in other States or overseas.
 
6.7
Consideration of appropriateness of teaching mode for a unit must be part of the unit approval 
process. Consideration must include, but not be limited to: 
(1) the learning objectives of the unit; 
(2) the appropriate mix of information transfer and active engagement in the unit; 
(3) the extent and nature of contact between staff and students; 
(4) the extent and nature of contact between students. A unit may be offered in more than one 
teaching mode in the same teaching period. 
6.8
Through its staff training and development programs the University supports its staff in the acquisition 
and development of relevant skills, including the use of emerging teaching and learning technologies, 
where appropriate. To ensure that teachers have the required knowledge on the use of technology-
assisted modes of teaching, the University must provide the necessary advice, training, and support. 
The provision of support can extend beyond the use of technologies to the teaching skills which must 
be specific to a particular mode (e.g. digital facilitation) and to the skills required in successfully 
constructing a blended course.
6.9
Through its provision of technical and physical infrastructure and of resources the University 
endeavours to facilitate the adoption of the full range of approved teaching modes. To ensure security, 
particularly for assessments appropriate identity and access management infrastructure must be in 
place.
6.10
Through its student support services, schools and the University support students to acquire and 
develop the relevant skills to engage fully with the learning modes that are employed. Students must:
(1) be informed of the modes by which units will be delivered in a course;  
(2) have a readily available source of advice and support academically and where digital technology 
is involved, technically; and 
(3) be given consistent and transparent guidance on how they are expected to engage with learning 
processes and activities.
