Authorship Agreements Procedure
(A).
It is good practice that members of a research team complete a Statement of Authorship 
Form for publications expected to arise from their research —
(1).
http://www.research.uwa.edu.au/staff/research-policy/?a=554227
 
   
(B).
That form documents which authors are included, excluded, or acknowledged, and in what 
order – these should be based on expected contributions. This agreement should be a living 
document, revisited over the life of the project. 
(C).
It is desirable that advice on authorship is sought from a Research Integrity Advisor of the 
relevant school in the first instance.  Contact details of all Research Integrity Advisors can be 
found at —
(1).
http://www.research.uwa.edu.au/staff/human-research/research-integrity-advisors
 
   
(D).
If one or more individuals do not meet the authorship criteria as set out in this Policy, then 
they must be removed as authors.
(E).
If one or more individuals do not meet the authorship criteria but their contribution to the 
work is relevant, then they must be fully acknowledged in the publication's 
Acknowledgements or footnotes.   However, it is important to note that data or work of others 
must not be published without their explicit permission, or properly cited when if it is already 
published. Academic work purchased or otherwise obtained from a third party, sometimes 
called ghost-written work, must clearly specify the real author.
(F).
If one or more individuals who were excluded from publication were deemed to have met the 
authorship criteria as set out in the Research Integrity Policy, then they must be included as 
authors before the work is published, or must state in writing that they do not wish to be 
included as authors.
(G).
If the valid authors cannot agree on authorship, then the paper cannot be published in its 
present form. In this circumstance it may be possible to break the paper down into discrete 
sections where there is agreement about the contribution of individuals. However, it should 
be noted that no person who is a valid author must be excluded from the publication without 
their explicit permission.
(H).
When all valid authors agree on authorship then the work can be published.
