Resolving Disputes Between Authors
(A).
Authorship disputes may include a disagreement about the inclusion, exclusion or order of 
authors, and should be as much as possible resolved at the local level among the project 
team.
(B).
If no agreement on authorship can be reached, then it is recommended that mediation led by 
a Research Integrity Advisor or Head of School be undertaken.
(C).
If no resolution is made after engagement with a Research Integrity Advisor or Head of School 
then the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Research) must be notified and will decide on an appropriate 
course of action.
(D).
In making a determination, the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Research) (or delegate) may ask for 
the following information —
(1).
a copy of the signed Statement of Authorship Form if the work is published;
(2).
a copy of any key documentation to show how each of the authors contributed to 
the key authorship criteria as defined in this Policy;
(3).
if relevant, a list of those believed to be valid authors of the publication(s) but were 
excluded, and why; and
(4).
if relevant, a statement explaining the rationale for the order of authors.
(E).
The Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Research) (or delegate) will then make a judgement on the 
authorship. The judgement might be that the work cannot be published in its current form.
End
