UWA Document (Public)
This is a UWA Official document authorised or intended for public access.
 
 
 
 
 
Information Breach Policy 
Policy UP 26/12 (F26/2009) 
 
Approver 
Senate 
Sponsor 
Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Operations) 
Owner 
Chief Risk Officer 
Secretary 
Corporate Secretary 
Policy Type 
Institutional 
Policy Category 
Corporate Governance 
 
1 
Purpose 
1.1 
The purpose of this Policy is to establish a consistent and compliant framework for the prevention, 
identification, assessment and management of University Information breaches to:   
 
(a) protect individuals from harm arising from the misuse, loss or unauthorised access to personal 
information; 
(b) ensure timely, effective and proportionate responses to information breaches; and 
(c) support the University’s compliance with applicable information privacy and information sharing 
laws and obligations. 
 
1.2 
This Policy is to be read in conjunction with the following — 
(a) Information Privacy Policy;  
(b) Information Breach Response Procedures (under development);  
(c) Information Protection Policy; 
(d) Information Retention Policy; 
(e) Acceptable Use of Information Technology Policy; and 
(f) UWA Code of Conduct. 
 
2 
Definitions 
 
Term 
Definition 
Affected Individual 
If personal information that relates to an individual is accessed, 
disclosed or lost in a notifiable information breach, the individual is an 
affected individual in relation to the breach. 
Information Breach 
An information breach is any incident involving the unauthorised access, 
unauthorised disclosure, alteration, or loss of University Information. 
An information breach may occur intentionally or unintentionally and 
includes situations where information is: 
• 
Accessed by unauthorised persons; 
• 
Disclosed inappropriately; 
• 
Lost, destroyed, misplaced, or stolen;  
• 
Compromised due to system or process failures. 
