This is a UWA Official document authorised or intended for public access.
Notifiable Information Breach 
A notifiable information breach occurs when unauthorised access, 
unauthorised disclosure, or loss of personal information or sensitive 
personal information held by the University is likely to result in serious 
harm to one or more individuals to whom the information relates. 
Information Users 
A member of the University Community that creates, captures, receives 
or uses University Information. 
Personal Information 
As defined in the UWA Information Privacy Policy. 
Sensitive Personal Information 
As defined in the UWA Information Privacy Policy. 
University 
The University as defined in Part 1 section 2 of the University of Western 
Australia Act 1911 (WA). 
University Community 
All individuals who engage in University activity and/or use University 
property. 
University Information 
All data, records and information created, captured, processed, stored, 
shared and/or disposed of by the University Community or on behalf of 
the University relating to University business in any format or medium.  
University Officer 
University Officer means any individual employed, appointed, engaged 
or otherwise authorised by the University to perform duties or exercise 
functions on behalf of the University, including, but not limited to: 
(a) Senate Members and Senate committee members; 
(b) Employees (full-time, part-time, fixed term and casual); 
(c) Contractors; 
(d) holders of honorary, adjunct or clinical appointments; and 
(e) holders of the title Emeritus or Emerita. 
3 
Scope 
3.1 
This Policy applies to all University Officers as well as affiliates, volunteers, students and other third 
parties when acting for, on behalf of, or under the control or direction of the University when 
collecting, holding, accessing, utilising, sharing, retaining or disclosing University Information as part 
of a University activity. 
3.2 
This Policy applies to all actual or suspected information breaches involving University Information, 
regardless of the format or location of the information. 
3.3 
Compliance with this Policy and any supporting guidance or subordinate documentation issued 
under it is mandatory. 
 
4 
Information Breach Principles   
The University manages information breaches in accordance with the following principles: 
4.1 
The University takes reasonable steps to prevent information breaches and maintains appropriate 
capabilities to identify and respond to breaches, including relevant procedures, clearly assigned 
roles and responsibilities, and training for University Officers commensurate with their roles and 
responsibilities. 
4.2 
Information Breaches, including suspected breaches, must be identified and reported as soon as 
practicable after they are detected to enable prompt assessment and response. 
(a) In the event of an actual or suspected information breach, members of the University Community 
(other than third parties engaged by the University under a contract for services) must 
immediately: 
• 
Report to University IT via (+61) 8 6488 1234 or emailing servicedesk@uwa.edu.au when 
immediate response and containment measures are required; and 
• 
Report the information breach to the UWA Privacy Office via the University’s Privacy 
website or by emailing privacy@uwa.edu.au. 
 
