ailability and the University's parking and permit by-laws, provide"", ""L022"": ""accessible parking facilities for people with Disability;"", ""L023"": ""(4). provide information regarding accessibility at the University by the provision of a"", ""L024"": ""Campus Access map and way-finding aids;"", ""L025"": ""(5). permit assistance animals as defined in section 9 of the DDA, on campus for the"", ""L026"": ""assistance of Employees, Students or visitors subject to the control, evidence,"", ""L027"": ""hygiene and health provisions set down in section 54A of the DDA."", ""L028"": ""3.6 Advocacy, Advice and Support"", ""L029"": ""(A). The University will provide pathways for support, and advice for people with Disability or"", ""L030"": ""medical conditions through services such as —"", ""L031"": ""(1). UniAccess,"", ""L032"": ""The University of Western uwa.edu.au/policy 5"", ""L033"": ""Australia"", ""L034"": ""Code of Conduct Inclusion and Diversity Administrative""}","3.4 Awareness
(A).
To foster awareness and encourage informed and non-discriminatory attitudes of Employees 
and Students towards people with Disability or medical condition, the University will —
(1).
facilitate workshops and provide online information for Students and Employees 
that focus on Disability issues;
(2).
provide information and specific orientation for prospective Students with Disability 
or medical conditions to introduce them to the policies, facilities, services and 
activities of the University;
(3).
ensure that promotional materials for University activities and courses include a 
request for notification of special requirements or access or communication needs.
(B).
It is the responsibility of school Supervisors and Line Managers to be aware of the 
legislation and policies relating to employment and people with disabilities.
(C).
Issues arising in this area should be dealt with by the relevant school Supervisor or Line 
Manager, using guidelines and assistance available through Human Resources
3.5 Physical Access
(A).
The University will wherever reasonably possible —
(1).
ensure that its buildings, facilities and services (including accommodation), are fully 
accessible and accept reports of obstacles to physical access;
(2).
make alternative arrangements, to allow participation in an activity or event that is 
inaccessible to a person with Disability;
(3).
subject to availability and the University's parking and permit by-laws, provide 
accessible parking facilities for people with Disability;
(4).
provide information regarding accessibility at the University by the provision of a 
Campus Access map and way-finding aids;
(5).
permit assistance animals as defined in section 9 of the DDA, on campus for the 
assistance of Employees, Students or visitors subject to the control, evidence, 
hygiene and health provisions set down in section 54A of the DDA.
3.6 Advocacy, Advice and Support
(A).
The University will provide pathways for support, and advice for people with Disability or 
medical conditions through services such as —
(1).
