Procedure —
A. The onus is on the University to prove objectively that inherent requirements exist in a 
course, based on substantive, defensible academic rationales not merely compulsory 
requirements or traditional practices.
B. Inherent requirements need to be determined in the circumstances of each job. They may 
include the ability to —
(2).
perform the tasks or functions which are a necessary part of the job productivity 
and quality requirements;
(3).
work effectively in the team or other type of work organisation concerned;
(4).
work safely.
C. If a person with disability can do the essential activities or ""inherent requirements"" of a job, 
they should have the same opportunity to perform that job as anyone else.
D. The University, is not obliged by the Disability Discrimination Act to change the inherent 
requirements of a job to suit an Employee.
E.
